              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Avoid trailing whitespace."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.49 | 0.83\n\nThis cop looks for trailing whitespace in the source code.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# The line in this example contains spaces after the 0.\n# bad\nx = 0\n\n# The line in this example ends directly after the 0.\n# good\nx = 0\n```\n#### AllowInHeredoc: false\n\n```ruby\n# The line in this example contains spaces after the 0.\n# bad\ncode = <<~RUBY\n  x = 0\nRUBY\n```\n#### AllowInHeredoc: true (default)\n\n```ruby\n# The line in this example contains spaces after the 0.\n# good\ncode = <<~RUBY\n  x = 0\nRUBY\n```\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nAllowInHeredoc | `true` | Boolean\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#no-trailing-whitespace](https://rubystyle.guide#no-trailing-whitespace)\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.49 | 0.83\n\nThis cop looks for trailing whitespace in the source code.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# The line in this example contains spaces after the 0.\n# bad\nx = 0\n\n# The line in this example ends directly after the 0.\n# good\nx = 0\n```\n#### AllowInHeredoc: false\n\n```ruby\n# The line in this example contains spaces after the 0.\n# bad\ncode = <<~RUBY\n  x = 0\nRUBY\n```\n#### AllowInHeredoc: true (default)\n\n```ruby\n# The line in this example contains spaces after the 0.\n# good\ncode = <<~RUBY\n  x = 0\nRUBY\n```\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nAllowInHeredoc | `true` | Boolean\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#no-trailing-whitespace](https://rubystyle.guide#no-trailing-whitespace)\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Naming/AccessorMethodName",
              "name": "Naming/AccessorMethodName",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Check the naming of accessor methods for get_/set_.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_naming/#namingaccessormethodname",
                "tags": [
                  "naming"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Check the naming of accessor methods for get_/set_."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Check the naming of accessor methods for get_/set_."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | No | 0.50 | -\n\nThis cop makes sure that accessor methods are named properly.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\ndef set_attribute(value)\nend\n\n# good\ndef attribute=(value)\nend\n\n# bad\ndef get_attribute\nend\n\n# good\ndef attribute\nend\n```\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#accessor_mutator_method_names](https://rubystyle.guide#accessor_mutator_method_names)\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | No | 0.50 | -\n\nThis cop makes sure that accessor methods are named properly.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\ndef set_attribute(value)\nend\n\n# good\ndef attribute=(value)\nend\n\n# bad\ndef get_attribute\nend\n\n# good\ndef attribute\nend\n```\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#accessor_mutator_method_names](https://rubystyle.guide#accessor_mutator_method_names)\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Naming/AsciiIdentifiers",
              "name": "Naming/AsciiIdentifiers",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Use only ascii symbols in identifiers.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_naming/#namingasciiidentifiers",
                "tags": [
                  "naming"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Use only ascii symbols in identifiers."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Use only ascii symbols in identifiers."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | No | 0.50 | -\n\nThis cop checks for non-ascii characters in identifier names.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\ndef καλημερα # Greek alphabet (non-ascii)\nend\n\n# bad\ndef こんにちはと言う # Japanese character (non-ascii)\nend\n\n# bad\ndef hello_🍣 # Emoji (non-ascii)\nend\n\n# good\ndef say_hello\nend\n\n# bad\n신장 = 10 # Hangul character (non-ascii)\n\n# good\nheight = 10\n\n# bad\nparams[:عرض_gteq] # Arabic character (non-ascii)\n\n# good\nparams[:width_gteq]\n```\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#english-identifiers](https://rubystyle.guide#english-identifiers)\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | No | 0.50 | -\n\nThis cop checks for non-ascii characters in identifier names.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\ndef καλημερα # Greek alphabet (non-ascii)\nend\n\n# bad\ndef こんにちはと言う # Japanese character (non-ascii)\nend\n\n# bad\ndef hello_🍣 # Emoji (non-ascii)\nend\n\n# good\ndef say_hello\nend\n\n# bad\n신장 = 10 # Hangul character (non-ascii)\n\n# good\nheight = 10\n\n# bad\nparams[:عرض_gteq] # Arabic character (non-ascii)\n\n# good\nparams[:width_gteq]\n```\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#english-identifiers](https://rubystyle.guide#english-identifiers)\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Naming/BinaryOperatorParameterName",
              "name": "Naming/BinaryOperatorParameterName",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "When defining binary operators, name the argument other.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_naming/#namingbinaryoperatorparametername",
                "tags": [
                  "naming"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "When defining binary operators, name the argument other."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "When defining binary operators, name the argument other."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | No | 0.50 | -\n\nThis cop makes sure that certain binary operator methods have their\nsole  parameter named `other`.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\ndef +(amount); end\n\n# good\ndef +(other); end\n```\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#other-arg](https://rubystyle.guide#other-arg)\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | No | 0.50 | -\n\nThis cop makes sure that certain binary operator methods have their\nsole  parameter named `other`.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\ndef +(amount); end\n\n# good\ndef +(other); end\n```\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#other-arg](https://rubystyle.guide#other-arg)\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Naming/BlockParameterName",
              "name": "Naming/BlockParameterName",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Checks for block parameter names that contain capital letters, end in numbers, or do not meet a minimal length.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_naming/#namingblockparametername",
                "tags": [
                  "naming"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Checks for block parameter names that contain capital letters, end in numbers, or do not meet a minimal length."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Checks for block parameter names that contain capital letters, end in numbers, or do not meet a minimal length."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | No | 0.53 | 0.77\n\nThis cop checks block parameter names for how descriptive they\nare. It is highly configurable.\n\nThe `MinNameLength` config option takes an integer. It represents\nthe minimum amount of characters the name must be. Its default is 1.\nThe `AllowNamesEndingInNumbers` config option takes a boolean. When\nset to false, this cop will register offenses for names ending with\nnumbers. Its default is false. The `AllowedNames` config option\ntakes an array of permitted names that will never register an\noffense. The `ForbiddenNames` config option takes an array of\nrestricted names that will always register an offense.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nbar do |varOne, varTwo|\n  varOne + varTwo\nend\n\n# With `AllowNamesEndingInNumbers` set to false\nfoo { |num1, num2| num1 * num2 }\n\n# With `MinParamNameLength` set to number greater than 1\nbaz { |a, b, c| do_stuff(a, b, c) }\n\n# good\nbar do |thud, fred|\n  thud + fred\nend\n\nfoo { |speed, distance| speed * distance }\n\nbaz { |age, height, gender| do_stuff(age, height, gender) }\n```\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nMinNameLength | `1` | Integer\nAllowNamesEndingInNumbers | `true` | Boolean\nAllowedNames | `[]` | Array\nForbiddenNames | `[]` | Array\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | No | 0.53 | 0.77\n\nThis cop checks block parameter names for how descriptive they\nare. It is highly configurable.\n\nThe `MinNameLength` config option takes an integer. It represents\nthe minimum amount of characters the name must be. Its default is 1.\nThe `AllowNamesEndingInNumbers` config option takes a boolean. When\nset to false, this cop will register offenses for names ending with\nnumbers. Its default is false. The `AllowedNames` config option\ntakes an array of permitted names that will never register an\noffense. The `ForbiddenNames` config option takes an array of\nrestricted names that will always register an offense.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nbar do |varOne, varTwo|\n  varOne + varTwo\nend\n\n# With `AllowNamesEndingInNumbers` set to false\nfoo { |num1, num2| num1 * num2 }\n\n# With `MinParamNameLength` set to number greater than 1\nbaz { |a, b, c| do_stuff(a, b, c) }\n\n# good\nbar do |thud, fred|\n  thud + fred\nend\n\nfoo { |speed, distance| speed * distance }\n\nbaz { |age, height, gender| do_stuff(age, height, gender) }\n```\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nMinNameLength | `1` | Integer\nAllowNamesEndingInNumbers | `true` | Boolean\nAllowedNames | `[]` | Array\nForbiddenNames | `[]` | Array\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Naming/ClassAndModuleCamelCase",
              "name": "Naming/ClassAndModuleCamelCase",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Use CamelCase for classes and modules.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_naming/#namingclassandmodulecamelcase",
                "tags": [
                  "naming"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Use CamelCase for classes and modules."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Use CamelCase for classes and modules."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | No | 0.50 | -\n\nThis cop checks for class and module names with\nan underscore in them.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nclass My_Class\nend\nmodule My_Module\nend\n\n# good\nclass MyClass\nend\nmodule MyModule\nend\n```\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#camelcase-classes](https://rubystyle.guide#camelcase-classes)\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | No | 0.50 | -\n\nThis cop checks for class and module names with\nan underscore in them.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nclass My_Class\nend\nmodule My_Module\nend\n\n# good\nclass MyClass\nend\nmodule MyModule\nend\n```\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#camelcase-classes](https://rubystyle.guide#camelcase-classes)\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Naming/ConstantName",
              "name": "Naming/ConstantName",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Constants should use SCREAMING_SNAKE_CASE.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_naming/#namingconstantname",
                "tags": [
                  "naming"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Constants should use SCREAMING_SNAKE_CASE."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Constants should use SCREAMING_SNAKE_CASE."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | No | 0.50 | -\n\nThis cop checks whether constant names are written using\nSCREAMING_SNAKE_CASE.\n\nTo avoid false positives, it ignores cases in which we cannot know\nfor certain the type of value that would be assigned to a constant.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nInchInCm = 2.54\nINCHinCM = 2.54\nInch_In_Cm = 2.54\n\n# good\nINCH_IN_CM = 2.54\n```\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#screaming-snake-case](https://rubystyle.guide#screaming-snake-case)\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | No | 0.50 | -\n\nThis cop checks whether constant names are written using\nSCREAMING_SNAKE_CASE.\n\nTo avoid false positives, it ignores cases in which we cannot know\nfor certain the type of value that would be assigned to a constant.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nInchInCm = 2.54\nINCHinCM = 2.54\nInch_In_Cm = 2.54\n\n# good\nINCH_IN_CM = 2.54\n```\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#screaming-snake-case](https://rubystyle.guide#screaming-snake-case)\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Naming/FileName",
              "name": "Naming/FileName",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Use snake_case for source file names.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_naming/#namingfilename",
                "tags": [
                  "naming"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Use snake_case for source file names."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Use snake_case for source file names."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | No | 0.50 | -\n\nThis cop makes sure that Ruby source files have snake_case\nnames. Ruby scripts (i.e. source files with a shebang in the\nfirst line) are ignored.\n\nThe cop also ignores `.gemspec` files, because Bundler\nrecommends using dashes to separate namespaces in nested gems\n(i.e. `bundler-console` becomes `Bundler::Console`). As such, the\ngemspec is supposed to be named `bundler-console.gemspec`.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nlib/layoutManager.rb\n\nanything/usingCamelCase\n\n# good\nlib/layout_manager.rb\n\nanything/using_snake_case.rake\n```\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nExclude | `[]` | Array\nExpectMatchingDefinition | `false` | Boolean\nRegex | `<none>` | \nIgnoreExecutableScripts | `true` | Boolean\nAllowedAcronyms | `CLI`, `DSL`, `ACL`, `API`, `ASCII`, `CPU`, `CSS`, `DNS`, `EOF`, `GUID`, `HTML`, `HTTP`, `HTTPS`, `ID`, `IP`, `JSON`, `LHS`, `QPS`, `RAM`, `RHS`, `RPC`, `SLA`, `SMTP`, `SQL`, `SSH`, `TCP`, `TLS`, `TTL`, `UDP`, `UI`, `UID`, `UUID`, `URI`, `URL`, `UTF8`, `VM`, `XML`, `XMPP`, `XSRF`, `XSS` | Array\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#snake-case-files](https://rubystyle.guide#snake-case-files)\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | No | 0.50 | -\n\nThis cop makes sure that Ruby source files have snake_case\nnames. Ruby scripts (i.e. source files with a shebang in the\nfirst line) are ignored.\n\nThe cop also ignores `.gemspec` files, because Bundler\nrecommends using dashes to separate namespaces in nested gems\n(i.e. `bundler-console` becomes `Bundler::Console`). As such, the\ngemspec is supposed to be named `bundler-console.gemspec`.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nlib/layoutManager.rb\n\nanything/usingCamelCase\n\n# good\nlib/layout_manager.rb\n\nanything/using_snake_case.rake\n```\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nExclude | `[]` | Array\nExpectMatchingDefinition | `false` | Boolean\nRegex | `<none>` | \nIgnoreExecutableScripts | `true` | Boolean\nAllowedAcronyms | `CLI`, `DSL`, `ACL`, `API`, `ASCII`, `CPU`, `CSS`, `DNS`, `EOF`, `GUID`, `HTML`, `HTTP`, `HTTPS`, `ID`, `IP`, `JSON`, `LHS`, `QPS`, `RAM`, `RHS`, `RPC`, `SLA`, `SMTP`, `SQL`, `SSH`, `TCP`, `TLS`, `TTL`, `UDP`, `UI`, `UID`, `UUID`, `URI`, `URL`, `UTF8`, `VM`, `XML`, `XMPP`, `XSRF`, `XSS` | Array\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#snake-case-files](https://rubystyle.guide#snake-case-files)\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Naming/HeredocDelimiterCase",
              "name": "Naming/HeredocDelimiterCase",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Use configured case for heredoc delimiters.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_naming/#namingheredocdelimitercase",
                "tags": [
                  "naming"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Use configured case for heredoc delimiters."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Use configured case for heredoc delimiters."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | No | 0.50 | -\n\nThis cop checks that your heredocs are using the configured case.\nBy default it is configured to enforce uppercase heredocs.\n\n### Examples\n\n#### EnforcedStyle: uppercase (default)\n\n```ruby\n# bad\n<<-sql\n  SELECT * FROM foo\nsql\n\n# good\n<<-SQL\n  SELECT * FROM foo\nSQL\n```\n#### EnforcedStyle: lowercase\n\n```ruby\n# bad\n<<-SQL\n  SELECT * FROM foo\nSQL\n\n# good\n<<-sql\n  SELECT * FROM foo\nsql\n```\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nEnforcedStyle | `uppercase` | `lowercase`, `uppercase`\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#heredoc-delimiters](https://rubystyle.guide#heredoc-delimiters)\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | No | 0.50 | -\n\nThis cop checks that your heredocs are using the configured case.\nBy default it is configured to enforce uppercase heredocs.\n\n### Examples\n\n#### EnforcedStyle: uppercase (default)\n\n```ruby\n# bad\n<<-sql\n  SELECT * FROM foo\nsql\n\n# good\n<<-SQL\n  SELECT * FROM foo\nSQL\n```\n#### EnforcedStyle: lowercase\n\n```ruby\n# bad\n<<-SQL\n  SELECT * FROM foo\nSQL\n\n# good\n<<-sql\n  SELECT * FROM foo\nsql\n```\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nEnforcedStyle | `uppercase` | `lowercase`, `uppercase`\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#heredoc-delimiters](https://rubystyle.guide#heredoc-delimiters)\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Naming/HeredocDelimiterNaming",
              "name": "Naming/HeredocDelimiterNaming",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Use descriptive heredoc delimiters.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_naming/#namingheredocdelimiternaming",
                "tags": [
                  "naming"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Use descriptive heredoc delimiters."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Use descriptive heredoc delimiters."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | No | 0.50 | -\n\nThis cop checks that your heredocs are using meaningful delimiters.\nBy default it disallows `END` and `EO*`, and can be configured through\nforbidden listing additional delimiters.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# good\n<<-SQL\n  SELECT * FROM foo\nSQL\n\n# bad\n<<-END\n  SELECT * FROM foo\nEND\n\n# bad\n<<-EOS\n  SELECT * FROM foo\nEOS\n```\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nForbiddenDelimiters | `(?-mix:(^|\\s)(EO[A-Z]{1}|END)(\\s|$))` | Array\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#heredoc-delimiters](https://rubystyle.guide#heredoc-delimiters)\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | No | 0.50 | -\n\nThis cop checks that your heredocs are using meaningful delimiters.\nBy default it disallows `END` and `EO*`, and can be configured through\nforbidden listing additional delimiters.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# good\n<<-SQL\n  SELECT * FROM foo\nSQL\n\n# bad\n<<-END\n  SELECT * FROM foo\nEND\n\n# bad\n<<-EOS\n  SELECT * FROM foo\nEOS\n```\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nForbiddenDelimiters | `(?-mix:(^|\\s)(EO[A-Z]{1}|END)(\\s|$))` | Array\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#heredoc-delimiters](https://rubystyle.guide#heredoc-delimiters)\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Naming/MemoizedInstanceVariableName",
              "name": "Naming/MemoizedInstanceVariableName",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Memoized method name should match memo instance variable name.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_naming/#namingmemoizedinstancevariablename",
                "tags": [
                  "naming"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Memoized method name should match memo instance variable name."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Memoized method name should match memo instance variable name."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | No | 0.53 | 0.58\n\nThis cop checks for memoized methods whose instance variable name\ndoes not match the method name.\n\nThis cop can be configured with the EnforcedStyleForLeadingUnderscores\ndirective. It can be configured to allow for memoized instance variables\nprefixed with an underscore. Prefixing ivars with an underscore is a\nconvention that is used to implicitly indicate that an ivar should not\nbe set or referenced outside of the memoization method.\n\n### Examples\n\n#### EnforcedStyleForLeadingUnderscores: disallowed (default)\n\n```ruby\n# bad\n# Method foo is memoized using an instance variable that is\n# not `@foo`. This can cause confusion and bugs.\ndef foo\n  @something ||= calculate_expensive_thing\nend\n\n# good\ndef _foo\n  @foo ||= calculate_expensive_thing\nend\n\n# good\ndef foo\n  @foo ||= calculate_expensive_thing\nend\n\n# good\ndef foo\n  @foo ||= begin\n    calculate_expensive_thing\n  end\nend\n\n# good\ndef foo\n  helper_variable = something_we_need_to_calculate_foo\n  @foo ||= calculate_expensive_thing(helper_variable)\nend\n```\n#### EnforcedStyleForLeadingUnderscores: required\n\n```ruby\n# bad\ndef foo\n  @something ||= calculate_expensive_thing\nend\n\n# bad\ndef foo\n  @foo ||= calculate_expensive_thing\nend\n\n# good\ndef foo\n  @_foo ||= calculate_expensive_thing\nend\n\n# good\ndef _foo\n  @_foo ||= calculate_expensive_thing\nend\n```\n#### EnforcedStyleForLeadingUnderscores :optional\n\n```ruby\n# bad\ndef foo\n  @something ||= calculate_expensive_thing\nend\n\n# good\ndef foo\n  @foo ||= calculate_expensive_thing\nend\n\n# good\ndef foo\n  @_foo ||= calculate_expensive_thing\nend\n\n# good\ndef _foo\n  @_foo ||= calculate_expensive_thing\nend\n```\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nEnforcedStyleForLeadingUnderscores | `disallowed` | `disallowed`, `required`, `optional`\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | No | 0.53 | 0.58\n\nThis cop checks for memoized methods whose instance variable name\ndoes not match the method name.\n\nThis cop can be configured with the EnforcedStyleForLeadingUnderscores\ndirective. It can be configured to allow for memoized instance variables\nprefixed with an underscore. Prefixing ivars with an underscore is a\nconvention that is used to implicitly indicate that an ivar should not\nbe set or referenced outside of the memoization method.\n\n### Examples\n\n#### EnforcedStyleForLeadingUnderscores: disallowed (default)\n\n```ruby\n# bad\n# Method foo is memoized using an instance variable that is\n# not `@foo`. This can cause confusion and bugs.\ndef foo\n  @something ||= calculate_expensive_thing\nend\n\n# good\ndef _foo\n  @foo ||= calculate_expensive_thing\nend\n\n# good\ndef foo\n  @foo ||= calculate_expensive_thing\nend\n\n# good\ndef foo\n  @foo ||= begin\n    calculate_expensive_thing\n  end\nend\n\n# good\ndef foo\n  helper_variable = something_we_need_to_calculate_foo\n  @foo ||= calculate_expensive_thing(helper_variable)\nend\n```\n#### EnforcedStyleForLeadingUnderscores: required\n\n```ruby\n# bad\ndef foo\n  @something ||= calculate_expensive_thing\nend\n\n# bad\ndef foo\n  @foo ||= calculate_expensive_thing\nend\n\n# good\ndef foo\n  @_foo ||= calculate_expensive_thing\nend\n\n# good\ndef _foo\n  @_foo ||= calculate_expensive_thing\nend\n```\n#### EnforcedStyleForLeadingUnderscores :optional\n\n```ruby\n# bad\ndef foo\n  @something ||= calculate_expensive_thing\nend\n\n# good\ndef foo\n  @foo ||= calculate_expensive_thing\nend\n\n# good\ndef foo\n  @_foo ||= calculate_expensive_thing\nend\n\n# good\ndef _foo\n  @_foo ||= calculate_expensive_thing\nend\n```\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nEnforcedStyleForLeadingUnderscores | `disallowed` | `disallowed`, `required`, `optional`\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Naming/MethodName",
              "name": "Naming/MethodName",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Use the configured style when naming methods.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_naming/#namingmethodname",
                "tags": [
                  "naming"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Use the configured style when naming methods."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Use the configured style when naming methods."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | No | 0.50 | -\n\nThis cop makes sure that all methods use the configured style,\nsnake_case or camelCase, for their names.\n\nThis cop has `IgnoredPatterns` configuration option.\n\n  Naming/MethodName:\n    IgnoredPatterns:\n      - '\\A\\s*onSelectionBulkChange\\s*'\n      - '\\A\\s*onSelectionCleared\\s*'\n\nMethod names matching patterns are always allowed.\n\n### Examples\n\n#### EnforcedStyle: snake_case (default)\n\n```ruby\n# bad\ndef fooBar; end\n\n# good\ndef foo_bar; end\n```\n#### EnforcedStyle: camelCase\n\n```ruby\n# bad\ndef foo_bar; end\n\n# good\ndef fooBar; end\n```\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nEnforcedStyle | `snake_case` | `snake_case`, `camelCase`\nIgnoredPatterns | `[]` | Array\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#snake-case-symbols-methods-vars](https://rubystyle.guide#snake-case-symbols-methods-vars)\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | No | 0.50 | -\n\nThis cop makes sure that all methods use the configured style,\nsnake_case or camelCase, for their names.\n\nThis cop has `IgnoredPatterns` configuration option.\n\n  Naming/MethodName:\n    IgnoredPatterns:\n      - '\\A\\s*onSelectionBulkChange\\s*'\n      - '\\A\\s*onSelectionCleared\\s*'\n\nMethod names matching patterns are always allowed.\n\n### Examples\n\n#### EnforcedStyle: snake_case (default)\n\n```ruby\n# bad\ndef fooBar; end\n\n# good\ndef foo_bar; end\n```\n#### EnforcedStyle: camelCase\n\n```ruby\n# bad\ndef foo_bar; end\n\n# good\ndef fooBar; end\n```\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nEnforcedStyle | `snake_case` | `snake_case`, `camelCase`\nIgnoredPatterns | `[]` | Array\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#snake-case-symbols-methods-vars](https://rubystyle.guide#snake-case-symbols-methods-vars)\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Naming/MethodParameterName",
              "name": "Naming/MethodParameterName",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Checks for method parameter names that contain capital letters, end in numbers, or do not meet a minimal length.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_naming/#namingmethodparametername",
                "tags": [
                  "naming"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Checks for method parameter names that contain capital letters, end in numbers, or do not meet a minimal length."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Checks for method parameter names that contain capital letters, end in numbers, or do not meet a minimal length."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | No | 0.53 | 0.77\n\nThis cop checks method parameter names for how descriptive they\nare. It is highly configurable.\n\nThe `MinNameLength` config option takes an integer. It represents\nthe minimum amount of characters the name must be. Its default is 3.\nThe `AllowNamesEndingInNumbers` config option takes a boolean. When\nset to false, this cop will register offenses for names ending with\nnumbers. Its default is false. The `AllowedNames` config option\ntakes an array of permitted names that will never register an\noffense. The `ForbiddenNames` config option takes an array of\nrestricted names that will always register an offense.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\ndef bar(varOne, varTwo)\n  varOne + varTwo\nend\n\n# With `AllowNamesEndingInNumbers` set to false\ndef foo(num1, num2)\n  num1 * num2\nend\n\n# With `MinArgNameLength` set to number greater than 1\ndef baz(a, b, c)\n  do_stuff(a, b, c)\nend\n\n# good\ndef bar(thud, fred)\n  thud + fred\nend\n\ndef foo(speed, distance)\n  speed * distance\nend\n\ndef baz(age_a, height_b, gender_c)\n  do_stuff(age_a, height_b, gender_c)\nend\n```\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nMinNameLength | `3` | Integer\nAllowNamesEndingInNumbers | `true` | Boolean\nAllowedNames | `io`, `id`, `to`, `by`, `on`, `in`, `at`, `ip`, `db`, `os`, `pp` | Array\nForbiddenNames | `[]` | Array\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | No | 0.53 | 0.77\n\nThis cop checks method parameter names for how descriptive they\nare. It is highly configurable.\n\nThe `MinNameLength` config option takes an integer. It represents\nthe minimum amount of characters the name must be. Its default is 3.\nThe `AllowNamesEndingInNumbers` config option takes a boolean. When\nset to false, this cop will register offenses for names ending with\nnumbers. Its default is false. The `AllowedNames` config option\ntakes an array of permitted names that will never register an\noffense. The `ForbiddenNames` config option takes an array of\nrestricted names that will always register an offense.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\ndef bar(varOne, varTwo)\n  varOne + varTwo\nend\n\n# With `AllowNamesEndingInNumbers` set to false\ndef foo(num1, num2)\n  num1 * num2\nend\n\n# With `MinArgNameLength` set to number greater than 1\ndef baz(a, b, c)\n  do_stuff(a, b, c)\nend\n\n# good\ndef bar(thud, fred)\n  thud + fred\nend\n\ndef foo(speed, distance)\n  speed * distance\nend\n\ndef baz(age_a, height_b, gender_c)\n  do_stuff(age_a, height_b, gender_c)\nend\n```\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nMinNameLength | `3` | Integer\nAllowNamesEndingInNumbers | `true` | Boolean\nAllowedNames | `io`, `id`, `to`, `by`, `on`, `in`, `at`, `ip`, `db`, `os`, `pp` | Array\nForbiddenNames | `[]` | Array\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Naming/PredicateName",
              "name": "Naming/PredicateName",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Check the names of predicate methods.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_naming/#namingpredicatename",
                "tags": [
                  "naming"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Check the names of predicate methods."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Check the names of predicate methods."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | No | 0.50 | 0.77\n\nThis cop makes sure that predicates are named properly.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\ndef is_even(value)\nend\n\ndef is_even?(value)\nend\n\n# good\ndef even?(value)\nend\n\n# bad\ndef has_value\nend\n\ndef has_value?\nend\n\n# good\ndef value?\nend\n```\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nNamePrefix | `is_`, `has_`, `have_` | Array\nForbiddenPrefixes | `is_`, `has_`, `have_` | Array\nAllowedMethods | `is_a?` | Array\nMethodDefinitionMacros | `define_method`, `define_singleton_method` | Array\nExclude | `spec/**/*` | Array\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#bool-methods-qmark](https://rubystyle.guide#bool-methods-qmark)\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | No | 0.50 | 0.77\n\nThis cop makes sure that predicates are named properly.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\ndef is_even(value)\nend\n\ndef is_even?(value)\nend\n\n# good\ndef even?(value)\nend\n\n# bad\ndef has_value\nend\n\ndef has_value?\nend\n\n# good\ndef value?\nend\n```\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nNamePrefix | `is_`, `has_`, `have_` | Array\nForbiddenPrefixes | `is_`, `has_`, `have_` | Array\nAllowedMethods | `is_a?` | Array\nMethodDefinitionMacros | `define_method`, `define_singleton_method` | Array\nExclude | `spec/**/*` | Array\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#bool-methods-qmark](https://rubystyle.guide#bool-methods-qmark)\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Naming/RescuedExceptionsVariableName",
              "name": "Naming/RescuedExceptionsVariableName",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Use consistent rescued exceptions variables naming.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_naming/#namingrescuedexceptionsvariablename",
                "tags": [
                  "naming"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Use consistent rescued exceptions variables naming."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Use consistent rescued exceptions variables naming."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.67 | 0.68\n\nThis cop makes sure that rescued exceptions variables are named as\nexpected.\n\nThe `PreferredName` config option takes a `String`. It represents\nthe required name of the variable. Its default is `e`.\n\n### Examples\n\n#### PreferredName: e (default)\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nbegin\n  # do something\nrescue MyException => exception\n  # do something\nend\n\n# good\nbegin\n  # do something\nrescue MyException => e\n  # do something\nend\n\n# good\nbegin\n  # do something\nrescue MyException => _e\n  # do something\nend\n```\n#### PreferredName: exception\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nbegin\n  # do something\nrescue MyException => e\n  # do something\nend\n\n# good\nbegin\n  # do something\nrescue MyException => exception\n  # do something\nend\n\n# good\nbegin\n  # do something\nrescue MyException => _exception\n  # do something\nend\n```\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nPreferredName | `e` | String\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.67 | 0.68\n\nThis cop makes sure that rescued exceptions variables are named as\nexpected.\n\nThe `PreferredName` config option takes a `String`. It represents\nthe required name of the variable. Its default is `e`.\n\n### Examples\n\n#### PreferredName: e (default)\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nbegin\n  # do something\nrescue MyException => exception\n  # do something\nend\n\n# good\nbegin\n  # do something\nrescue MyException => e\n  # do something\nend\n\n# good\nbegin\n  # do something\nrescue MyException => _e\n  # do something\nend\n```\n#### PreferredName: exception\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nbegin\n  # do something\nrescue MyException => e\n  # do something\nend\n\n# good\nbegin\n  # do something\nrescue MyException => exception\n  # do something\nend\n\n# good\nbegin\n  # do something\nrescue MyException => _exception\n  # do something\nend\n```\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nPreferredName | `e` | String\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Naming/VariableName",
              "name": "Naming/VariableName",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Use the configured style when naming variables.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_naming/#namingvariablename",
                "tags": [
                  "naming"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Use the configured style when naming variables."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Use the configured style when naming variables."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | No | 0.50 | -\n\nThis cop makes sure that all variables use the configured style,\nsnake_case or camelCase, for their names.\n\n### Examples\n\n#### EnforcedStyle: snake_case (default)\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nfooBar = 1\n\n# good\nfoo_bar = 1\n```\n#### EnforcedStyle: camelCase\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nfoo_bar = 1\n\n# good\nfooBar = 1\n```\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nEnforcedStyle | `snake_case` | `snake_case`, `camelCase`\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#snake-case-symbols-methods-vars](https://rubystyle.guide#snake-case-symbols-methods-vars)\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | No | 0.50 | -\n\nThis cop makes sure that all variables use the configured style,\nsnake_case or camelCase, for their names.\n\n### Examples\n\n#### EnforcedStyle: snake_case (default)\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nfooBar = 1\n\n# good\nfoo_bar = 1\n```\n#### EnforcedStyle: camelCase\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nfoo_bar = 1\n\n# good\nfooBar = 1\n```\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nEnforcedStyle | `snake_case` | `snake_case`, `camelCase`\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#snake-case-symbols-methods-vars](https://rubystyle.guide#snake-case-symbols-methods-vars)\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Naming/VariableNumber",
              "name": "Naming/VariableNumber",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Use the configured style when numbering variables.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_naming/#namingvariablenumber",
                "tags": [
                  "naming"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Use the configured style when numbering variables."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Use the configured style when numbering variables."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | No | 0.50 | -\n\nThis cop makes sure that all numbered variables use the\nconfigured style, snake_case, normalcase, or non_integer,\nfor their numbering.\n\n### Examples\n\n#### EnforcedStyle: snake_case\n\n```ruby\n# bad\n\nvariable1 = 1\n\n# good\n\nvariable_1 = 1\n```\n#### EnforcedStyle: normalcase (default)\n\n```ruby\n# bad\n\nvariable_1 = 1\n\n# good\n\nvariable1 = 1\n```\n#### EnforcedStyle: non_integer\n\n```ruby\n# bad\n\nvariable1 = 1\n\nvariable_1 = 1\n\n# good\n\nvariableone = 1\n\nvariable_one = 1\n```\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nEnforcedStyle | `normalcase` | `snake_case`, `normalcase`, `non_integer`\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | No | 0.50 | -\n\nThis cop makes sure that all numbered variables use the\nconfigured style, snake_case, normalcase, or non_integer,\nfor their numbering.\n\n### Examples\n\n#### EnforcedStyle: snake_case\n\n```ruby\n# bad\n\nvariable1 = 1\n\n# good\n\nvariable_1 = 1\n```\n#### EnforcedStyle: normalcase (default)\n\n```ruby\n# bad\n\nvariable_1 = 1\n\n# good\n\nvariable1 = 1\n```\n#### EnforcedStyle: non_integer\n\n```ruby\n# bad\n\nvariable1 = 1\n\nvariable_1 = 1\n\n# good\n\nvariableone = 1\n\nvariable_one = 1\n```\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nEnforcedStyle | `normalcase` | `snake_case`, `normalcase`, `non_integer`\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Metrics/AbcSize",
              "name": "Metrics/AbcSize",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "A calculated magnitude based on number of assignments, branches, and conditions.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_metrics/#metricsabcsize",
                "tags": [
                  "metrics"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "A calculated magnitude based on number of assignments, branches, and conditions."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A calculated magnitude based on number of assignments, branches, and conditions."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | No | 0.27 | 0.81\n\nThis cop checks that the ABC size of methods is not higher than the\nconfigured maximum. The ABC size is based on assignments, branches\n(method calls), and conditions. See http://c2.com/cgi/wiki?AbcMetric\nand https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/ABC_Software_Metric.\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nIgnoredMethods | `[]` | Array\nMax | `15` | Integer\n\n### References\n\n* [http://c2.com/cgi/wiki?AbcMetric](http://c2.com/cgi/wiki?AbcMetric)\n* [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/ABC_Software_Metric](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/ABC_Software_Metric)\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | No | 0.27 | 0.81\n\nThis cop checks that the ABC size of methods is not higher than the\nconfigured maximum. The ABC size is based on assignments, branches\n(method calls), and conditions. See http://c2.com/cgi/wiki?AbcMetric\nand https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/ABC_Software_Metric.\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nIgnoredMethods | `[]` | Array\nMax | `15` | Integer\n\n### References\n\n* [http://c2.com/cgi/wiki?AbcMetric](http://c2.com/cgi/wiki?AbcMetric)\n* [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/ABC_Software_Metric](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/ABC_Software_Metric)\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Metrics/BlockLength",
              "name": "Metrics/BlockLength",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Avoid long blocks with many lines.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_metrics/#metricsblocklength",
                "tags": [
                  "metrics"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Avoid long blocks with many lines."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Avoid long blocks with many lines."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | No | 0.44 | 0.66\n\nThis cop checks if the length of a block exceeds some maximum value.\nComment lines can optionally be ignored.\nThe maximum allowed length is configurable.\nThe cop can be configured to ignore blocks passed to certain methods.\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nCountComments | `false` | Boolean\nMax | `25` | Integer\nExcludedMethods | `refine` | Array\nExclude | `**/*.gemspec` | Array\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | No | 0.44 | 0.66\n\nThis cop checks if the length of a block exceeds some maximum value.\nComment lines can optionally be ignored.\nThe maximum allowed length is configurable.\nThe cop can be configured to ignore blocks passed to certain methods.\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nCountComments | `false` | Boolean\nMax | `25` | Integer\nExcludedMethods | `refine` | Array\nExclude | `**/*.gemspec` | Array\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Metrics/BlockNesting",
              "name": "Metrics/BlockNesting",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Avoid excessive block nesting.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_metrics/#metricsblocknesting",
                "tags": [
                  "metrics"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Avoid excessive block nesting."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Avoid excessive block nesting."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | No | 0.25 | 0.47\n\nThis cop checks for excessive nesting of conditional and looping\nconstructs.\n\nYou can configure if blocks are considered using the `CountBlocks`\noption. When set to `false` (the default) blocks are not counted\ntowards the nesting level. Set to `true` to count blocks as well.\n\nThe maximum level of nesting allowed is configurable.\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nCountBlocks | `false` | Boolean\nMax | `3` | Integer\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#three-is-the-number-thou-shalt-count](https://rubystyle.guide#three-is-the-number-thou-shalt-count)\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | No | 0.25 | 0.47\n\nThis cop checks for excessive nesting of conditional and looping\nconstructs.\n\nYou can configure if blocks are considered using the `CountBlocks`\noption. When set to `false` (the default) blocks are not counted\ntowards the nesting level. Set to `true` to count blocks as well.\n\nThe maximum level of nesting allowed is configurable.\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nCountBlocks | `false` | Boolean\nMax | `3` | Integer\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#three-is-the-number-thou-shalt-count](https://rubystyle.guide#three-is-the-number-thou-shalt-count)\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Metrics/ClassLength",
              "name": "Metrics/ClassLength",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Avoid classes longer than 100 lines of code.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_metrics/#metricsclasslength",
                "tags": [
                  "metrics"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Avoid classes longer than 100 lines of code."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Avoid classes longer than 100 lines of code."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | No | 0.25 | -\n\nThis cop checks if the length a class exceeds some maximum value.\nComment lines can optionally be ignored.\nThe maximum allowed length is configurable.\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nCountComments | `false` | Boolean\nMax | `100` | Integer\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | No | 0.25 | -\n\nThis cop checks if the length a class exceeds some maximum value.\nComment lines can optionally be ignored.\nThe maximum allowed length is configurable.\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nCountComments | `false` | Boolean\nMax | `100` | Integer\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Metrics/CyclomaticComplexity",
              "name": "Metrics/CyclomaticComplexity",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "A complexity metric that is strongly correlated to the number of test cases needed to validate a method.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_metrics/#metricscyclomaticcomplexity",
                "tags": [
                  "metrics"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "A complexity metric that is strongly correlated to the number of test cases needed to validate a method."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A complexity metric that is strongly correlated to the number of test cases needed to validate a method."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | No | 0.25 | 0.81\n\nThis cop checks that the cyclomatic complexity of methods is not higher\nthan the configured maximum. The cyclomatic complexity is the number of\nlinearly independent paths through a method. The algorithm counts\ndecision points and adds one.\n\nAn if statement (or unless or ?:) increases the complexity by one. An\nelse branch does not, since it doesn't add a decision point. The &&\noperator (or keyword and) can be converted to a nested if statement,\nand ||/or is shorthand for a sequence of ifs, so they also add one.\nLoops can be said to have an exit condition, so they add one.\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nIgnoredMethods | `[]` | Array\nMax | `6` | Integer\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | No | 0.25 | 0.81\n\nThis cop checks that the cyclomatic complexity of methods is not higher\nthan the configured maximum. The cyclomatic complexity is the number of\nlinearly independent paths through a method. The algorithm counts\ndecision points and adds one.\n\nAn if statement (or unless or ?:) increases the complexity by one. An\nelse branch does not, since it doesn't add a decision point. The &&\noperator (or keyword and) can be converted to a nested if statement,\nand ||/or is shorthand for a sequence of ifs, so they also add one.\nLoops can be said to have an exit condition, so they add one.\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nIgnoredMethods | `[]` | Array\nMax | `6` | Integer\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Metrics/MethodLength",
              "name": "Metrics/MethodLength",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Avoid methods longer than 10 lines of code.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_metrics/#metricsmethodlength",
                "tags": [
                  "metrics"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Avoid methods longer than 10 lines of code."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Avoid methods longer than 10 lines of code."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | No | 0.25 | 0.59.2\n\nThis cop checks if the length of a method exceeds some maximum value.\nComment lines can optionally be ignored.\nThe maximum allowed length is configurable.\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nCountComments | `false` | Boolean\nMax | `10` | Integer\nExcludedMethods | `[]` | Array\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#short-methods](https://rubystyle.guide#short-methods)\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | No | 0.25 | 0.59.2\n\nThis cop checks if the length of a method exceeds some maximum value.\nComment lines can optionally be ignored.\nThe maximum allowed length is configurable.\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nCountComments | `false` | Boolean\nMax | `10` | Integer\nExcludedMethods | `[]` | Array\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#short-methods](https://rubystyle.guide#short-methods)\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Metrics/ModuleLength",
              "name": "Metrics/ModuleLength",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Avoid modules longer than 100 lines of code.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_metrics/#metricsmodulelength",
                "tags": [
                  "metrics"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Avoid modules longer than 100 lines of code."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Avoid modules longer than 100 lines of code."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | No | 0.31 | -\n\nThis cop checks if the length a module exceeds some maximum value.\nComment lines can optionally be ignored.\nThe maximum allowed length is configurable.\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nCountComments | `false` | Boolean\nMax | `100` | Integer\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | No | 0.31 | -\n\nThis cop checks if the length a module exceeds some maximum value.\nComment lines can optionally be ignored.\nThe maximum allowed length is configurable.\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nCountComments | `false` | Boolean\nMax | `100` | Integer\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Metrics/ParameterLists",
              "name": "Metrics/ParameterLists",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Avoid parameter lists longer than three or four parameters.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_metrics/#metricsparameterlists",
                "tags": [
                  "metrics"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Avoid parameter lists longer than three or four parameters."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Avoid parameter lists longer than three or four parameters."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | No | 0.25 | -\n\nThis cop checks for methods with too many parameters.\nThe maximum number of parameters is configurable.\nKeyword arguments can optionally be excluded from the total count.\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nMax | `5` | Integer\nCountKeywordArgs | `true` | Boolean\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#too-many-params](https://rubystyle.guide#too-many-params)\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | No | 0.25 | -\n\nThis cop checks for methods with too many parameters.\nThe maximum number of parameters is configurable.\nKeyword arguments can optionally be excluded from the total count.\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nMax | `5` | Integer\nCountKeywordArgs | `true` | Boolean\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#too-many-params](https://rubystyle.guide#too-many-params)\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Metrics/PerceivedComplexity",
              "name": "Metrics/PerceivedComplexity",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "A complexity metric geared towards measuring complexity for a human reader.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_metrics/#metricsperceivedcomplexity",
                "tags": [
                  "metrics"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "A complexity metric geared towards measuring complexity for a human reader."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A complexity metric geared towards measuring complexity for a human reader."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | No | 0.25 | 0.81\n\nThis cop tries to produce a complexity score that's a measure of the\ncomplexity the reader experiences when looking at a method. For that\nreason it considers `when` nodes as something that doesn't add as much\ncomplexity as an `if` or a `&&`. Except if it's one of those special\n`case`/`when` constructs where there's no expression after `case`. Then\nthe cop treats it as an `if`/`elsif`/`elsif`... and lets all the `when`\nnodes count. In contrast to the CyclomaticComplexity cop, this cop\nconsiders `else` nodes as adding complexity.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\ndef my_method                   # 1\n  if cond                       # 1\n    case var                    # 2 (0.8 + 4 * 0.2, rounded)\n    when 1 then func_one\n    when 2 then func_two\n    when 3 then func_three\n    when 4..10 then func_other\n    end\n  else                          # 1\n    do_something until a && b   # 2\n  end                           # ===\nend                             # 7 complexity points\n```\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nIgnoredMethods | `[]` | Array\nMax | `7` | Integer\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | No | 0.25 | 0.81\n\nThis cop tries to produce a complexity score that's a measure of the\ncomplexity the reader experiences when looking at a method. For that\nreason it considers `when` nodes as something that doesn't add as much\ncomplexity as an `if` or a `&&`. Except if it's one of those special\n`case`/`when` constructs where there's no expression after `case`. Then\nthe cop treats it as an `if`/`elsif`/`elsif`... and lets all the `when`\nnodes count. In contrast to the CyclomaticComplexity cop, this cop\nconsiders `else` nodes as adding complexity.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\ndef my_method                   # 1\n  if cond                       # 1\n    case var                    # 2 (0.8 + 4 * 0.2, rounded)\n    when 1 then func_one\n    when 2 then func_two\n    when 3 then func_three\n    when 4..10 then func_other\n    end\n  else                          # 1\n    do_something until a && b   # 2\n  end                           # ===\nend                             # 7 complexity points\n```\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nIgnoredMethods | `[]` | Array\nMax | `7` | Integer\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Bundler/DuplicatedGem",
              "name": "Bundler/DuplicatedGem",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Checks for duplicate gem entries in Gemfile.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_bundler/#bundlerduplicatedgem",
                "tags": [
                  "bundler"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Checks for duplicate gem entries in Gemfile."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Checks for duplicate gem entries in Gemfile."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | No | 0.46 | -\n\nA Gem's requirements should be listed only once in a Gemfile.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\ngem 'rubocop'\ngem 'rubocop'\n\n# bad\ngroup :development do\n  gem 'rubocop'\nend\n\ngroup :test do\n  gem 'rubocop'\nend\n\n# good\ngroup :development, :test do\n  gem 'rubocop'\nend\n\n# good\ngem 'rubocop', groups: [:development, :test]\n```\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nInclude | `**/*.gemfile`, `**/Gemfile`, `**/gems.rb` | Array\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | No | 0.46 | -\n\nA Gem's requirements should be listed only once in a Gemfile.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\ngem 'rubocop'\ngem 'rubocop'\n\n# bad\ngroup :development do\n  gem 'rubocop'\nend\n\ngroup :test do\n  gem 'rubocop'\nend\n\n# good\ngroup :development, :test do\n  gem 'rubocop'\nend\n\n# good\ngem 'rubocop', groups: [:development, :test]\n```\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nInclude | `**/*.gemfile`, `**/Gemfile`, `**/gems.rb` | Array\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Bundler/GemComment",
              "name": "Bundler/GemComment",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Add a comment describing each gem.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_bundler/#bundlergemcomment",
                "tags": [
                  "bundler"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Add a comment describing each gem."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Add a comment describing each gem."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nDisabled | Yes | No | 0.59 | 0.77\n\nAdd a comment describing each gem in your Gemfile.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\n\ngem 'foo'\n\n# good\n\n# Helpers for the foo things.\ngem 'foo'\n```\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nInclude | `**/*.gemfile`, `**/Gemfile`, `**/gems.rb` | Array\nIgnoredGems | `[]` | Array\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nDisabled | Yes | No | 0.59 | 0.77\n\nAdd a comment describing each gem in your Gemfile.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\n\ngem 'foo'\n\n# good\n\n# Helpers for the foo things.\ngem 'foo'\n```\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nInclude | `**/*.gemfile`, `**/Gemfile`, `**/gems.rb` | Array\nIgnoredGems | `[]` | Array\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Bundler/InsecureProtocolSource",
              "name": "Bundler/InsecureProtocolSource",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "The source `:gemcutter`, `:rubygems` and `:rubyforge` are deprecated because HTTP requests are insecure. Please change your source to 'https://rubygems.org' if possible, or 'http://rubygems.org' if not.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_bundler/#bundlerinsecureprotocolsource",
                "tags": [
                  "bundler"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The source `:gemcutter`, `:rubygems` and `:rubyforge` are deprecated because HTTP requests are insecure. Please change your source to 'https://rubygems.org' if possible, or 'http://rubygems.org' if not."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The source `:gemcutter`, `:rubygems` and `:rubyforge` are deprecated because HTTP requests are insecure. Please change your source to 'https://rubygems.org' if possible, or 'http://rubygems.org' if not."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.50 | -\n\nThe symbol argument `:gemcutter`, `:rubygems`, and `:rubyforge`\nare deprecated. So please change your source to URL string that\n'https://rubygems.org' if possible, or 'http://rubygems.org' if not.\n\nThis autocorrect will replace these symbols with 'https://rubygems.org'.\nBecause it is secure, HTTPS request is strongly recommended. And in\nmost use cases HTTPS will be fine.\n\nHowever, it don't replace all `sources` of `http://` with `https://`.\nFor example, when specifying an internal gem server using HTTP on the\nintranet, a use case where HTTPS cannot be specified was considered.\nConsider using HTTP only if you cannot use HTTPS.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nsource :gemcutter\nsource :rubygems\nsource :rubyforge\n\n# good\nsource 'https://rubygems.org' # strongly recommended\nsource 'http://rubygems.org'\n```\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nInclude | `**/*.gemfile`, `**/Gemfile`, `**/gems.rb` | Array\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.50 | -\n\nThe symbol argument `:gemcutter`, `:rubygems`, and `:rubyforge`\nare deprecated. So please change your source to URL string that\n'https://rubygems.org' if possible, or 'http://rubygems.org' if not.\n\nThis autocorrect will replace these symbols with 'https://rubygems.org'.\nBecause it is secure, HTTPS request is strongly recommended. And in\nmost use cases HTTPS will be fine.\n\nHowever, it don't replace all `sources` of `http://` with `https://`.\nFor example, when specifying an internal gem server using HTTP on the\nintranet, a use case where HTTPS cannot be specified was considered.\nConsider using HTTP only if you cannot use HTTPS.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nsource :gemcutter\nsource :rubygems\nsource :rubyforge\n\n# good\nsource 'https://rubygems.org' # strongly recommended\nsource 'http://rubygems.org'\n```\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nInclude | `**/*.gemfile`, `**/Gemfile`, `**/gems.rb` | Array\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Bundler/OrderedGems",
              "name": "Bundler/OrderedGems",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Gems within groups in the Gemfile should be alphabetically sorted.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_bundler/#bundlerorderedgems",
                "tags": [
                  "bundler"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Gems within groups in the Gemfile should be alphabetically sorted."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Gems within groups in the Gemfile should be alphabetically sorted."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.46 | 0.47\n\nGems should be alphabetically sorted within groups.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\ngem 'rubocop'\ngem 'rspec'\n\n# good\ngem 'rspec'\ngem 'rubocop'\n\n# good\ngem 'rubocop'\n\ngem 'rspec'\n\n# good only if TreatCommentsAsGroupSeparators is true\n# For code quality\ngem 'rubocop'\n# For tests\ngem 'rspec'\n```\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nTreatCommentsAsGroupSeparators | `true` | Boolean\nInclude | `**/*.gemfile`, `**/Gemfile`, `**/gems.rb` | Array\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.46 | 0.47\n\nGems should be alphabetically sorted within groups.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\ngem 'rubocop'\ngem 'rspec'\n\n# good\ngem 'rspec'\ngem 'rubocop'\n\n# good\ngem 'rubocop'\n\ngem 'rspec'\n\n# good only if TreatCommentsAsGroupSeparators is true\n# For code quality\ngem 'rubocop'\n# For tests\ngem 'rspec'\n```\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nTreatCommentsAsGroupSeparators | `true` | Boolean\nInclude | `**/*.gemfile`, `**/Gemfile`, `**/gems.rb` | Array\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Security/Eval",
              "name": "Security/Eval",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "The use of eval represents a serious security risk.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_security/#securityeval",
                "tags": [
                  "security"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The use of eval represents a serious security risk."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The use of eval represents a serious security risk."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | No | 0.47 | -\n\nThis cop checks for the use of `Kernel#eval` and `Binding#eval`.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\n\neval(something)\nbinding.eval(something)\n```\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | No | 0.47 | -\n\nThis cop checks for the use of `Kernel#eval` and `Binding#eval`.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\n\neval(something)\nbinding.eval(something)\n```\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Security/JSONLoad",
              "name": "Security/JSONLoad",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Prefer usage of `JSON.parse` over `JSON.load` due to potential security issues. See reference for more information.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_security/#securityjsonload",
                "tags": [
                  "security"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Prefer usage of `JSON.parse` over `JSON.load` due to potential security issues. See reference for more information."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Prefer usage of `JSON.parse` over `JSON.load` due to potential security issues. See reference for more information."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes (Unsafe) | 0.43 | 0.44\n\nThis cop checks for the use of JSON class methods which have potential\nsecurity issues.\n\nAutocorrect is disabled by default because it's potentially dangerous.\nIf using a stream, like `JSON.load(open('file'))`, it will need to call\n`#read` manually, like `JSON.parse(open('file').read)`.\nIf reading single values (rather than proper JSON objects), like\n`JSON.load('false')`, it will need to pass the `quirks_mode: true`\noption, like `JSON.parse('false', quirks_mode: true)`.\nOther similar issues may apply.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nJSON.load(\"{}\")\nJSON.restore(\"{}\")\n\n# good\nJSON.parse(\"{}\")\n```\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nAutoCorrect | `false` | Boolean\n\n### References\n\n* [https://ruby-doc.org/stdlib-2.3.0/libdoc/json/rdoc/JSON.html#method-i-load](https://ruby-doc.org/stdlib-2.3.0/libdoc/json/rdoc/JSON.html#method-i-load)\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes (Unsafe) | 0.43 | 0.44\n\nThis cop checks for the use of JSON class methods which have potential\nsecurity issues.\n\nAutocorrect is disabled by default because it's potentially dangerous.\nIf using a stream, like `JSON.load(open('file'))`, it will need to call\n`#read` manually, like `JSON.parse(open('file').read)`.\nIf reading single values (rather than proper JSON objects), like\n`JSON.load('false')`, it will need to pass the `quirks_mode: true`\noption, like `JSON.parse('false', quirks_mode: true)`.\nOther similar issues may apply.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nJSON.load(\"{}\")\nJSON.restore(\"{}\")\n\n# good\nJSON.parse(\"{}\")\n```\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nAutoCorrect | `false` | Boolean\n\n### References\n\n* [https://ruby-doc.org/stdlib-2.3.0/libdoc/json/rdoc/JSON.html#method-i-load](https://ruby-doc.org/stdlib-2.3.0/libdoc/json/rdoc/JSON.html#method-i-load)\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Security/MarshalLoad",
              "name": "Security/MarshalLoad",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Avoid using of `Marshal.load` or `Marshal.restore` due to potential security issues. See reference for more information.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_security/#securitymarshalload",
                "tags": [
                  "security"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Avoid using of `Marshal.load` or `Marshal.restore` due to potential security issues. See reference for more information."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Avoid using of `Marshal.load` or `Marshal.restore` due to potential security issues. See reference for more information."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | No | 0.47 | -\n\nThis cop checks for the use of Marshal class methods which have\npotential security issues leading to remote code execution when\nloading from an untrusted source.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nMarshal.load(\"{}\")\nMarshal.restore(\"{}\")\n\n# good\nMarshal.dump(\"{}\")\n\n# okish - deep copy hack\nMarshal.load(Marshal.dump({}))\n```\n\n### References\n\n* [https://ruby-doc.org/core-2.3.3/Marshal.html#module-Marshal-label-Security+considerations](https://ruby-doc.org/core-2.3.3/Marshal.html#module-Marshal-label-Security+considerations)\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | No | 0.47 | -\n\nThis cop checks for the use of Marshal class methods which have\npotential security issues leading to remote code execution when\nloading from an untrusted source.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nMarshal.load(\"{}\")\nMarshal.restore(\"{}\")\n\n# good\nMarshal.dump(\"{}\")\n\n# okish - deep copy hack\nMarshal.load(Marshal.dump({}))\n```\n\n### References\n\n* [https://ruby-doc.org/core-2.3.3/Marshal.html#module-Marshal-label-Security+considerations](https://ruby-doc.org/core-2.3.3/Marshal.html#module-Marshal-label-Security+considerations)\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Security/Open",
              "name": "Security/Open",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "The use of Kernel#open represents a serious security risk.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_security/#securityopen",
                "tags": [
                  "security"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The use of Kernel#open represents a serious security risk."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The use of Kernel#open represents a serious security risk."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | No | No | 0.53 | -\n\nThis cop checks for the use of `Kernel#open`.\n\n`Kernel#open` enables not only file access but also process invocation\nby prefixing a pipe symbol (e.g., `open(\"| ls\")`). So, it may lead to\na serious security risk by using variable input to the argument of\n`Kernel#open`. It would be better to use `File.open`, `IO.popen` or\n`URI#open` explicitly.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nopen(something)\n\n# good\nFile.open(something)\nIO.popen(something)\nURI.parse(something).open\n```\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | No | No | 0.53 | -\n\nThis cop checks for the use of `Kernel#open`.\n\n`Kernel#open` enables not only file access but also process invocation\nby prefixing a pipe symbol (e.g., `open(\"| ls\")`). So, it may lead to\na serious security risk by using variable input to the argument of\n`Kernel#open`. It would be better to use `File.open`, `IO.popen` or\n`URI#open` explicitly.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nopen(something)\n\n# good\nFile.open(something)\nIO.popen(something)\nURI.parse(something).open\n```\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Security/YAMLLoad",
              "name": "Security/YAMLLoad",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Prefer usage of `YAML.safe_load` over `YAML.load` due to potential security issues. See reference for more information.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_security/#securityyamlload",
                "tags": [
                  "security"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Prefer usage of `YAML.safe_load` over `YAML.load` due to potential security issues. See reference for more information."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Prefer usage of `YAML.safe_load` over `YAML.load` due to potential security issues. See reference for more information."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes (Unsafe) | 0.47 | -\n\nThis cop checks for the use of YAML class methods which have\npotential security issues leading to remote code execution when\nloading from an untrusted source.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nYAML.load(\"--- foo\")\n\n# good\nYAML.safe_load(\"--- foo\")\nYAML.dump(\"foo\")\n```\n\n### References\n\n* [https://ruby-doc.org/stdlib-2.3.3/libdoc/yaml/rdoc/YAML.html#module-YAML-label-Security](https://ruby-doc.org/stdlib-2.3.3/libdoc/yaml/rdoc/YAML.html#module-YAML-label-Security)\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes (Unsafe) | 0.47 | -\n\nThis cop checks for the use of YAML class methods which have\npotential security issues leading to remote code execution when\nloading from an untrusted source.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nYAML.load(\"--- foo\")\n\n# good\nYAML.safe_load(\"--- foo\")\nYAML.dump(\"foo\")\n```\n\n### References\n\n* [https://ruby-doc.org/stdlib-2.3.3/libdoc/yaml/rdoc/YAML.html#module-YAML-label-Security](https://ruby-doc.org/stdlib-2.3.3/libdoc/yaml/rdoc/YAML.html#module-YAML-label-Security)\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Migration/DepartmentName",
