            },
            {
              "id": "Gemspec/DuplicatedAssignment",
              "name": "Gemspec/DuplicatedAssignment",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "An attribute assignment method calls should be listed only once in a gemspec.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_gemspec/#gemspecduplicatedassignment",
                "tags": [
                  "gemspec"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "An attribute assignment method calls should be listed only once in a gemspec."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "An attribute assignment method calls should be listed only once in a gemspec."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | No | 0.52 | -\n\nAn attribute assignment method calls should be listed only once\nin a gemspec.\n\nAssigning to an attribute with the same name using `spec.foo =` will be\nan unintended usage. On the other hand, duplication of methods such\nas `spec.requirements`, `spec.add_runtime_dependency`, and others are\npermitted because it is the intended use of appending values.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nGem::Specification.new do |spec|\n  spec.name = 'rubocop'\n  spec.name = 'rubocop2'\nend\n\n# good\nGem::Specification.new do |spec|\n  spec.name = 'rubocop'\nend\n\n# good\nGem::Specification.new do |spec|\n  spec.requirements << 'libmagick, v6.0'\n  spec.requirements << 'A good graphics card'\nend\n\n# good\nGem::Specification.new do |spec|\n  spec.add_runtime_dependency('parallel', '~> 1.10')\n  spec.add_runtime_dependency('parser', '>= 2.3.3.1', '< 3.0')\nend\n```\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nInclude | `**/*.gemspec` | Array\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | No | 0.52 | -\n\nAn attribute assignment method calls should be listed only once\nin a gemspec.\n\nAssigning to an attribute with the same name using `spec.foo =` will be\nan unintended usage. On the other hand, duplication of methods such\nas `spec.requirements`, `spec.add_runtime_dependency`, and others are\npermitted because it is the intended use of appending values.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nGem::Specification.new do |spec|\n  spec.name = 'rubocop'\n  spec.name = 'rubocop2'\nend\n\n# good\nGem::Specification.new do |spec|\n  spec.name = 'rubocop'\nend\n\n# good\nGem::Specification.new do |spec|\n  spec.requirements << 'libmagick, v6.0'\n  spec.requirements << 'A good graphics card'\nend\n\n# good\nGem::Specification.new do |spec|\n  spec.add_runtime_dependency('parallel', '~> 1.10')\n  spec.add_runtime_dependency('parser', '>= 2.3.3.1', '< 3.0')\nend\n```\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nInclude | `**/*.gemspec` | Array\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Gemspec/OrderedDependencies",
              "name": "Gemspec/OrderedDependencies",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Dependencies in the gemspec should be alphabetically sorted.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_gemspec/#gemspecordereddependencies",
                "tags": [
                  "gemspec"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Dependencies in the gemspec should be alphabetically sorted."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Dependencies in the gemspec should be alphabetically sorted."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.51 | -\n\nDependencies in the gemspec should be alphabetically sorted.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nspec.add_dependency 'rubocop'\nspec.add_dependency 'rspec'\n\n# good\nspec.add_dependency 'rspec'\nspec.add_dependency 'rubocop'\n\n# good\nspec.add_dependency 'rubocop'\n\nspec.add_dependency 'rspec'\n\n# bad\nspec.add_development_dependency 'rubocop'\nspec.add_development_dependency 'rspec'\n\n# good\nspec.add_development_dependency 'rspec'\nspec.add_development_dependency 'rubocop'\n\n# good\nspec.add_development_dependency 'rubocop'\n\nspec.add_development_dependency 'rspec'\n\n# bad\nspec.add_runtime_dependency 'rubocop'\nspec.add_runtime_dependency 'rspec'\n\n# good\nspec.add_runtime_dependency 'rspec'\nspec.add_runtime_dependency 'rubocop'\n\n# good\nspec.add_runtime_dependency 'rubocop'\n\nspec.add_runtime_dependency 'rspec'\n\n# good only if TreatCommentsAsGroupSeparators is true\n# For code quality\nspec.add_dependency 'rubocop'\n# For tests\nspec.add_dependency 'rspec'\n```\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nTreatCommentsAsGroupSeparators | `true` | Boolean\nInclude | `**/*.gemspec` | Array\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.51 | -\n\nDependencies in the gemspec should be alphabetically sorted.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nspec.add_dependency 'rubocop'\nspec.add_dependency 'rspec'\n\n# good\nspec.add_dependency 'rspec'\nspec.add_dependency 'rubocop'\n\n# good\nspec.add_dependency 'rubocop'\n\nspec.add_dependency 'rspec'\n\n# bad\nspec.add_development_dependency 'rubocop'\nspec.add_development_dependency 'rspec'\n\n# good\nspec.add_development_dependency 'rspec'\nspec.add_development_dependency 'rubocop'\n\n# good\nspec.add_development_dependency 'rubocop'\n\nspec.add_development_dependency 'rspec'\n\n# bad\nspec.add_runtime_dependency 'rubocop'\nspec.add_runtime_dependency 'rspec'\n\n# good\nspec.add_runtime_dependency 'rspec'\nspec.add_runtime_dependency 'rubocop'\n\n# good\nspec.add_runtime_dependency 'rubocop'\n\nspec.add_runtime_dependency 'rspec'\n\n# good only if TreatCommentsAsGroupSeparators is true\n# For code quality\nspec.add_dependency 'rubocop'\n# For tests\nspec.add_dependency 'rspec'\n```\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nTreatCommentsAsGroupSeparators | `true` | Boolean\nInclude | `**/*.gemspec` | Array\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Gemspec/RequiredRubyVersion",
              "name": "Gemspec/RequiredRubyVersion",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Checks that `required_ruby_version` of gemspec and `TargetRubyVersion` of .rubocop.yml are equal.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_gemspec/#gemspecrequiredrubyversion",
                "tags": [
                  "gemspec"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Checks that `required_ruby_version` of gemspec and `TargetRubyVersion` of .rubocop.yml are equal."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Checks that `required_ruby_version` of gemspec and `TargetRubyVersion` of .rubocop.yml are equal."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | No | 0.52 | -\n\nChecks that `required_ruby_version` of gemspec and `TargetRubyVersion`\nof .rubocop.yml are equal.\nThereby, RuboCop to perform static analysis working on the version\nrequired by gemspec.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# When `TargetRubyVersion` of .rubocop.yml is `2.5`.\n\n# bad\nGem::Specification.new do |spec|\n  spec.required_ruby_version = '>= 2.4.0'\nend\n\n# bad\nGem::Specification.new do |spec|\n  spec.required_ruby_version = '>= 2.6.0'\nend\n\n# good\nGem::Specification.new do |spec|\n  spec.required_ruby_version = '>= 2.5.0'\nend\n\n# good\nGem::Specification.new do |spec|\n  spec.required_ruby_version = '>= 2.5'\nend\n\n# good\nGem::Specification.new do |spec|\n  spec.required_ruby_version = ['>= 2.5.0', '< 2.7.0']\nend\n```\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nInclude | `**/*.gemspec` | Array\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | No | 0.52 | -\n\nChecks that `required_ruby_version` of gemspec and `TargetRubyVersion`\nof .rubocop.yml are equal.\nThereby, RuboCop to perform static analysis working on the version\nrequired by gemspec.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# When `TargetRubyVersion` of .rubocop.yml is `2.5`.\n\n# bad\nGem::Specification.new do |spec|\n  spec.required_ruby_version = '>= 2.4.0'\nend\n\n# bad\nGem::Specification.new do |spec|\n  spec.required_ruby_version = '>= 2.6.0'\nend\n\n# good\nGem::Specification.new do |spec|\n  spec.required_ruby_version = '>= 2.5.0'\nend\n\n# good\nGem::Specification.new do |spec|\n  spec.required_ruby_version = '>= 2.5'\nend\n\n# good\nGem::Specification.new do |spec|\n  spec.required_ruby_version = ['>= 2.5.0', '< 2.7.0']\nend\n```\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nInclude | `**/*.gemspec` | Array\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Gemspec/RubyVersionGlobalsUsage",
              "name": "Gemspec/RubyVersionGlobalsUsage",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Checks usage of RUBY_VERSION in gemspec.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_gemspec/#gemspecrubyversionglobalsusage",
                "tags": [
                  "gemspec"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Checks usage of RUBY_VERSION in gemspec."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Checks usage of RUBY_VERSION in gemspec."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | No | 0.72 | -\n\nChecks that `RUBY_VERSION` constant is not used in gemspec.\nUsing `RUBY_VERSION` is dangerous because value of the\nconstant is determined by `rake release`.\nIt's possible to have dependency based on ruby version used\nto execute `rake release` and not user's ruby version.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nGem::Specification.new do |spec|\n  if RUBY_VERSION >= '2.5'\n    spec.add_runtime_dependency 'gem_a'\n  else\n    spec.add_runtime_dependency 'gem_b'\n  end\nend\n\n# good\nGem::Specification.new do |spec|\n  spec.add_runtime_dependency 'gem_a'\nend\n```\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nInclude | `**/*.gemspec` | Array\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#no-ruby-version-in-the-gemspec](https://rubystyle.guide#no-ruby-version-in-the-gemspec)\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | No | 0.72 | -\n\nChecks that `RUBY_VERSION` constant is not used in gemspec.\nUsing `RUBY_VERSION` is dangerous because value of the\nconstant is determined by `rake release`.\nIt's possible to have dependency based on ruby version used\nto execute `rake release` and not user's ruby version.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nGem::Specification.new do |spec|\n  if RUBY_VERSION >= '2.5'\n    spec.add_runtime_dependency 'gem_a'\n  else\n    spec.add_runtime_dependency 'gem_b'\n  end\nend\n\n# good\nGem::Specification.new do |spec|\n  spec.add_runtime_dependency 'gem_a'\nend\n```\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nInclude | `**/*.gemspec` | Array\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#no-ruby-version-in-the-gemspec](https://rubystyle.guide#no-ruby-version-in-the-gemspec)\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Style/AccessModifierDeclarations",
              "name": "Style/AccessModifierDeclarations",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Checks style of how access modifiers are used.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_style/#styleaccessmodifierdeclarations",
                "tags": [
                  "style"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Checks style of how access modifiers are used."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Checks style of how access modifiers are used."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | No | 0.57 | 0.81\n\nAccess modifiers should be declared to apply to a group of methods\nor inline before each method, depending on configuration.\nEnforcedStyle config covers only method definitions.\nApplications of visibility methods to symbols can be controlled\nusing AllowModifiersOnSymbols config.\n\n### Examples\n\n#### EnforcedStyle: group (default)\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nclass Foo\n\n  private def bar; end\n  private def baz; end\n\nend\n\n# good\nclass Foo\n\n  private\n\n  def bar; end\n  def baz; end\n\nend\n```\n#### EnforcedStyle: inline\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nclass Foo\n\n  private\n\n  def bar; end\n  def baz; end\n\nend\n\n# good\nclass Foo\n\n  private def bar; end\n  private def baz; end\n\nend\n```\n#### AllowModifiersOnSymbols: true\n\n```ruby\n# good\nclass Foo\n\n  private :bar, :baz\n\nend\n```\n#### AllowModifiersOnSymbols: false\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nclass Foo\n\n  private :bar, :baz\n\nend\n```\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nEnforcedStyle | `group` | `inline`, `group`\nAllowModifiersOnSymbols | `true` | Boolean\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | No | 0.57 | 0.81\n\nAccess modifiers should be declared to apply to a group of methods\nor inline before each method, depending on configuration.\nEnforcedStyle config covers only method definitions.\nApplications of visibility methods to symbols can be controlled\nusing AllowModifiersOnSymbols config.\n\n### Examples\n\n#### EnforcedStyle: group (default)\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nclass Foo\n\n  private def bar; end\n  private def baz; end\n\nend\n\n# good\nclass Foo\n\n  private\n\n  def bar; end\n  def baz; end\n\nend\n```\n#### EnforcedStyle: inline\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nclass Foo\n\n  private\n\n  def bar; end\n  def baz; end\n\nend\n\n# good\nclass Foo\n\n  private def bar; end\n  private def baz; end\n\nend\n```\n#### AllowModifiersOnSymbols: true\n\n```ruby\n# good\nclass Foo\n\n  private :bar, :baz\n\nend\n```\n#### AllowModifiersOnSymbols: false\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nclass Foo\n\n  private :bar, :baz\n\nend\n```\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nEnforcedStyle | `group` | `inline`, `group`\nAllowModifiersOnSymbols | `true` | Boolean\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Style/Alias",
              "name": "Style/Alias",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Use alias instead of alias_method.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_style/#stylealias",
                "tags": [
                  "style"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Use alias instead of alias_method."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Use alias instead of alias_method."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.9 | 0.36\n\nThis cop enforces the use of either `#alias` or `#alias_method`\ndepending on configuration.\nIt also flags uses of `alias :symbol` rather than `alias bareword`.\n\n### Examples\n\n#### EnforcedStyle: prefer_alias (default)\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nalias_method :bar, :foo\nalias :bar :foo\n\n# good\nalias bar foo\n```\n#### EnforcedStyle: prefer_alias_method\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nalias :bar :foo\nalias bar foo\n\n# good\nalias_method :bar, :foo\n```\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nEnforcedStyle | `prefer_alias` | `prefer_alias`, `prefer_alias_method`\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#alias-method-lexically](https://rubystyle.guide#alias-method-lexically)\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.9 | 0.36\n\nThis cop enforces the use of either `#alias` or `#alias_method`\ndepending on configuration.\nIt also flags uses of `alias :symbol` rather than `alias bareword`.\n\n### Examples\n\n#### EnforcedStyle: prefer_alias (default)\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nalias_method :bar, :foo\nalias :bar :foo\n\n# good\nalias bar foo\n```\n#### EnforcedStyle: prefer_alias_method\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nalias :bar :foo\nalias bar foo\n\n# good\nalias_method :bar, :foo\n```\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nEnforcedStyle | `prefer_alias` | `prefer_alias`, `prefer_alias_method`\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#alias-method-lexically](https://rubystyle.guide#alias-method-lexically)\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Style/AndOr",
              "name": "Style/AndOr",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Use &&/|| instead of and/or.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_style/#styleandor",
                "tags": [
                  "style"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Use &&/|| instead of and/or."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Use &&/|| instead of and/or."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.9 | 0.25\n\nThis cop checks for uses of `and` and `or`, and suggests using `&&` and\n`||` instead. It can be configured to check only in conditions or in\nall contexts.\n\n### Examples\n\n#### EnforcedStyle: always (default)\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nfoo.save and return\n\n# bad\nif foo and bar\nend\n\n# good\nfoo.save && return\n\n# good\nif foo && bar\nend\n```\n#### EnforcedStyle: conditionals\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nif foo and bar\nend\n\n# good\nfoo.save && return\n\n# good\nfoo.save and return\n\n# good\nif foo && bar\nend\n```\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nEnforcedStyle | `always` | `always`, `conditionals`\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#no-and-or-or](https://rubystyle.guide#no-and-or-or)\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.9 | 0.25\n\nThis cop checks for uses of `and` and `or`, and suggests using `&&` and\n`||` instead. It can be configured to check only in conditions or in\nall contexts.\n\n### Examples\n\n#### EnforcedStyle: always (default)\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nfoo.save and return\n\n# bad\nif foo and bar\nend\n\n# good\nfoo.save && return\n\n# good\nif foo && bar\nend\n```\n#### EnforcedStyle: conditionals\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nif foo and bar\nend\n\n# good\nfoo.save && return\n\n# good\nfoo.save and return\n\n# good\nif foo && bar\nend\n```\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nEnforcedStyle | `always` | `always`, `conditionals`\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#no-and-or-or](https://rubystyle.guide#no-and-or-or)\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Style/ArrayJoin",
              "name": "Style/ArrayJoin",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Use Array#join instead of Array#*.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_style/#stylearrayjoin",
                "tags": [
                  "style"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Use Array#join instead of Array#*."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Use Array#join instead of Array#*."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.20 | 0.31\n\nThis cop checks for uses of \"\\*\" as a substitute for *join*.\n\nNot all cases can reliably checked, due to Ruby's dynamic\ntypes, so we consider only cases when the first argument is an\narray literal or the second is a string literal.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\n%w(foo bar baz) * \",\"\n\n# good\n%w(foo bar baz).join(\",\")\n```\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#array-join](https://rubystyle.guide#array-join)\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.20 | 0.31\n\nThis cop checks for uses of \"\\*\" as a substitute for *join*.\n\nNot all cases can reliably checked, due to Ruby's dynamic\ntypes, so we consider only cases when the first argument is an\narray literal or the second is a string literal.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\n%w(foo bar baz) * \",\"\n\n# good\n%w(foo bar baz).join(\",\")\n```\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#array-join](https://rubystyle.guide#array-join)\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Style/AsciiComments",
              "name": "Style/AsciiComments",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Use only ascii symbols in comments.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_style/#styleasciicomments",
                "tags": [
                  "style"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Use only ascii symbols in comments."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Use only ascii symbols in comments."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | No | 0.9 | 0.52\n\nThis cop checks for non-ascii (non-English) characters\nin comments. You could set an array of allowed non-ascii chars in\nAllowedChars attribute (empty by default).\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\n# Translates from English to 日本語。\n\n# good\n# Translates from English to Japanese\n```\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nAllowedChars | `[]` | Array\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#english-comments](https://rubystyle.guide#english-comments)\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | No | 0.9 | 0.52\n\nThis cop checks for non-ascii (non-English) characters\nin comments. You could set an array of allowed non-ascii chars in\nAllowedChars attribute (empty by default).\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\n# Translates from English to 日本語。\n\n# good\n# Translates from English to Japanese\n```\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nAllowedChars | `[]` | Array\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#english-comments](https://rubystyle.guide#english-comments)\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Style/Attr",
              "name": "Style/Attr",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Checks for uses of Module#attr.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_style/#styleattr",
                "tags": [
                  "style"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Checks for uses of Module#attr."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Checks for uses of Module#attr."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.9 | 0.12\n\nThis cop checks for uses of Module#attr.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad - creates a single attribute accessor (deprecated in Ruby 1.9)\nattr :something, true\nattr :one, :two, :three # behaves as attr_reader\n\n# good\nattr_accessor :something\nattr_reader :one, :two, :three\n```\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#attr](https://rubystyle.guide#attr)\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.9 | 0.12\n\nThis cop checks for uses of Module#attr.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad - creates a single attribute accessor (deprecated in Ruby 1.9)\nattr :something, true\nattr :one, :two, :three # behaves as attr_reader\n\n# good\nattr_accessor :something\nattr_reader :one, :two, :three\n```\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#attr](https://rubystyle.guide#attr)\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Style/AutoResourceCleanup",
              "name": "Style/AutoResourceCleanup",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Suggests the usage of an auto resource cleanup version of a method (if available).",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_style/#styleautoresourcecleanup",
                "tags": [
                  "style"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Suggests the usage of an auto resource cleanup version of a method (if available)."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Suggests the usage of an auto resource cleanup version of a method (if available)."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nDisabled | Yes | No | 0.30 | -\n\nThis cop checks for cases when you could use a block\naccepting version of a method that does automatic\nresource cleanup.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nf = File.open('file')\n\n# good\nFile.open('file') do |f|\n  # ...\nend\n```\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nDisabled | Yes | No | 0.30 | -\n\nThis cop checks for cases when you could use a block\naccepting version of a method that does automatic\nresource cleanup.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nf = File.open('file')\n\n# good\nFile.open('file') do |f|\n  # ...\nend\n```\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Style/BarePercentLiterals",
              "name": "Style/BarePercentLiterals",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Checks if usage of %() or %Q() matches configuration.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_style/#stylebarepercentliterals",
                "tags": [
                  "style"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Checks if usage of %() or %Q() matches configuration."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Checks if usage of %() or %Q() matches configuration."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.25 | -\n\nThis cop checks if usage of %() or %Q() matches configuration.\n\n### Examples\n\n#### EnforcedStyle: bare_percent (default)\n\n```ruby\n# bad\n%Q(He said: \"#{greeting}\")\n%q{She said: 'Hi'}\n\n# good\n%(He said: \"#{greeting}\")\n%{She said: 'Hi'}\n```\n#### EnforcedStyle: percent_q\n\n```ruby\n# bad\n%|He said: \"#{greeting}\"|\n%/She said: 'Hi'/\n\n# good\n%Q|He said: \"#{greeting}\"|\n%q/She said: 'Hi'/\n```\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nEnforcedStyle | `bare_percent` | `percent_q`, `bare_percent`\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#percent-q-shorthand](https://rubystyle.guide#percent-q-shorthand)\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.25 | -\n\nThis cop checks if usage of %() or %Q() matches configuration.\n\n### Examples\n\n#### EnforcedStyle: bare_percent (default)\n\n```ruby\n# bad\n%Q(He said: \"#{greeting}\")\n%q{She said: 'Hi'}\n\n# good\n%(He said: \"#{greeting}\")\n%{She said: 'Hi'}\n```\n#### EnforcedStyle: percent_q\n\n```ruby\n# bad\n%|He said: \"#{greeting}\"|\n%/She said: 'Hi'/\n\n# good\n%Q|He said: \"#{greeting}\"|\n%q/She said: 'Hi'/\n```\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nEnforcedStyle | `bare_percent` | `percent_q`, `bare_percent`\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#percent-q-shorthand](https://rubystyle.guide#percent-q-shorthand)\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Style/BeginBlock",
              "name": "Style/BeginBlock",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Avoid the use of BEGIN blocks.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_style/#stylebeginblock",
                "tags": [
                  "style"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Avoid the use of BEGIN blocks."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Avoid the use of BEGIN blocks."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | No | 0.9 | -\n\nThis cop checks for BEGIN blocks.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nBEGIN { test }\n```\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#no-BEGIN-blocks](https://rubystyle.guide#no-BEGIN-blocks)\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | No | 0.9 | -\n\nThis cop checks for BEGIN blocks.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nBEGIN { test }\n```\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#no-BEGIN-blocks](https://rubystyle.guide#no-BEGIN-blocks)\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Style/BlockComments",
              "name": "Style/BlockComments",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Do not use block comments.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_style/#styleblockcomments",
                "tags": [
                  "style"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Do not use block comments."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Do not use block comments."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.9 | 0.23\n\nThis cop looks for uses of block comments (=begin...=end).\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\n=begin\nMultiple lines\nof comments...\n=end\n\n# good\n# Multiple lines\n# of comments...\n```\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#no-block-comments](https://rubystyle.guide#no-block-comments)\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.9 | 0.23\n\nThis cop looks for uses of block comments (=begin...=end).\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\n=begin\nMultiple lines\nof comments...\n=end\n\n# good\n# Multiple lines\n# of comments...\n```\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#no-block-comments](https://rubystyle.guide#no-block-comments)\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Style/BlockDelimiters",
              "name": "Style/BlockDelimiters",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Avoid using {...} for multi-line blocks (multiline chaining is always ugly). Prefer {...} over do...end for single-line blocks.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_style/#styleblockdelimiters",
                "tags": [
                  "style"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Avoid using {...} for multi-line blocks (multiline chaining is always ugly). Prefer {...} over do...end for single-line blocks."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Avoid using {...} for multi-line blocks (multiline chaining is always ugly). Prefer {...} over do...end for single-line blocks."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.30 | 0.35\n\nCheck for uses of braces or do/end around single line or\nmulti-line blocks.\n\n### Examples\n\n#### EnforcedStyle: line_count_based (default)\n\n```ruby\n# bad - single line block\nitems.each do |item| item / 5 end\n\n# good - single line block\nitems.each { |item| item / 5 }\n\n# bad - multi-line block\nthings.map { |thing|\n  something = thing.some_method\n  process(something)\n}\n\n# good - multi-line block\nthings.map do |thing|\n  something = thing.some_method\n  process(something)\nend\n```\n#### EnforcedStyle: semantic\n\n```ruby\n# Prefer `do...end` over `{...}` for procedural blocks.\n\n# return value is used/assigned\n# bad\nfoo = map do |x|\n  x\nend\nputs (map do |x|\n  x\nend)\n\n# return value is not used out of scope\n# good\nmap do |x|\n  x\nend\n\n# Prefer `{...}` over `do...end` for functional blocks.\n\n# return value is not used out of scope\n# bad\neach { |x|\n  x\n}\n\n# return value is used/assigned\n# good\nfoo = map { |x|\n  x\n}\nmap { |x|\n  x\n}.inspect\n\n# The AllowBracesOnProceduralOneLiners option is ignored unless the\n# EnforcedStyle is set to `semantic`. If so:\n\n# If the AllowBracesOnProceduralOneLiners option is unspecified, or\n# set to `false` or any other falsey value, then semantic purity is\n# maintained, so one-line procedural blocks must use do-end, not\n# braces.\n\n# bad\ncollection.each { |element| puts element }\n\n# good\ncollection.each do |element| puts element end\n\n# If the AllowBracesOnProceduralOneLiners option is set to `true`, or\n# any other truthy value, then one-line procedural blocks may use\n# either style. (There is no setting for requiring braces on them.)\n\n# good\ncollection.each { |element| puts element }\n\n# also good\ncollection.each do |element| puts element end\n```\n#### EnforcedStyle: braces_for_chaining\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nwords.each do |word|\n  word.flip.flop\nend.join(\"-\")\n\n# good\nwords.each { |word|\n  word.flip.flop\n}.join(\"-\")\n```\n#### EnforcedStyle: always_braces\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nwords.each do |word|\n  word.flip.flop\nend\n\n# good\nwords.each { |word|\n  word.flip.flop\n}\n```\n#### BracesRequiredMethods: ['sig']\n\n```ruby\n# Methods listed in the BracesRequiredMethods list, such as 'sig'\n# in this example, will require `{...}` braces. This option takes\n# precedence over all other configurations except IgnoredMethods.\n\n# bad\nsig do\n  params(\n    foo: string,\n  ).void\nend\ndef bar(foo)\n  puts foo\nend\n\n# good\nsig {\n  params(\n    foo: string,\n  ).void\n}\ndef bar(foo)\n  puts foo\nend\n```\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nEnforcedStyle | `line_count_based` | `line_count_based`, `semantic`, `braces_for_chaining`, `always_braces`\nProceduralMethods | `benchmark`, `bm`, `bmbm`, `create`, `each_with_object`, `measure`, `new`, `realtime`, `tap`, `with_object` | Array\nFunctionalMethods | `let`, `let!`, `subject`, `watch` | Array\nIgnoredMethods | `lambda`, `proc`, `it` | Array\nAllowBracesOnProceduralOneLiners | `false` | Boolean\nBracesRequiredMethods | `[]` | Array\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#single-line-blocks](https://rubystyle.guide#single-line-blocks)\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.30 | 0.35\n\nCheck for uses of braces or do/end around single line or\nmulti-line blocks.\n\n### Examples\n\n#### EnforcedStyle: line_count_based (default)\n\n```ruby\n# bad - single line block\nitems.each do |item| item / 5 end\n\n# good - single line block\nitems.each { |item| item / 5 }\n\n# bad - multi-line block\nthings.map { |thing|\n  something = thing.some_method\n  process(something)\n}\n\n# good - multi-line block\nthings.map do |thing|\n  something = thing.some_method\n  process(something)\nend\n```\n#### EnforcedStyle: semantic\n\n```ruby\n# Prefer `do...end` over `{...}` for procedural blocks.\n\n# return value is used/assigned\n# bad\nfoo = map do |x|\n  x\nend\nputs (map do |x|\n  x\nend)\n\n# return value is not used out of scope\n# good\nmap do |x|\n  x\nend\n\n# Prefer `{...}` over `do...end` for functional blocks.\n\n# return value is not used out of scope\n# bad\neach { |x|\n  x\n}\n\n# return value is used/assigned\n# good\nfoo = map { |x|\n  x\n}\nmap { |x|\n  x\n}.inspect\n\n# The AllowBracesOnProceduralOneLiners option is ignored unless the\n# EnforcedStyle is set to `semantic`. If so:\n\n# If the AllowBracesOnProceduralOneLiners option is unspecified, or\n# set to `false` or any other falsey value, then semantic purity is\n# maintained, so one-line procedural blocks must use do-end, not\n# braces.\n\n# bad\ncollection.each { |element| puts element }\n\n# good\ncollection.each do |element| puts element end\n\n# If the AllowBracesOnProceduralOneLiners option is set to `true`, or\n# any other truthy value, then one-line procedural blocks may use\n# either style. (There is no setting for requiring braces on them.)\n\n# good\ncollection.each { |element| puts element }\n\n# also good\ncollection.each do |element| puts element end\n```\n#### EnforcedStyle: braces_for_chaining\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nwords.each do |word|\n  word.flip.flop\nend.join(\"-\")\n\n# good\nwords.each { |word|\n  word.flip.flop\n}.join(\"-\")\n```\n#### EnforcedStyle: always_braces\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nwords.each do |word|\n  word.flip.flop\nend\n\n# good\nwords.each { |word|\n  word.flip.flop\n}\n```\n#### BracesRequiredMethods: ['sig']\n\n```ruby\n# Methods listed in the BracesRequiredMethods list, such as 'sig'\n# in this example, will require `{...}` braces. This option takes\n# precedence over all other configurations except IgnoredMethods.\n\n# bad\nsig do\n  params(\n    foo: string,\n  ).void\nend\ndef bar(foo)\n  puts foo\nend\n\n# good\nsig {\n  params(\n    foo: string,\n  ).void\n}\ndef bar(foo)\n  puts foo\nend\n```\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nEnforcedStyle | `line_count_based` | `line_count_based`, `semantic`, `braces_for_chaining`, `always_braces`\nProceduralMethods | `benchmark`, `bm`, `bmbm`, `create`, `each_with_object`, `measure`, `new`, `realtime`, `tap`, `with_object` | Array\nFunctionalMethods | `let`, `let!`, `subject`, `watch` | Array\nIgnoredMethods | `lambda`, `proc`, `it` | Array\nAllowBracesOnProceduralOneLiners | `false` | Boolean\nBracesRequiredMethods | `[]` | Array\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#single-line-blocks](https://rubystyle.guide#single-line-blocks)\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Style/CaseEquality",
              "name": "Style/CaseEquality",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Avoid explicit use of the case equality operator(===).",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_style/#stylecaseequality",
                "tags": [
                  "style"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Avoid explicit use of the case equality operator(===)."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Avoid explicit use of the case equality operator(===)."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | No | 0.9 | -\n\nThis cop checks for uses of the case equality operator(===).\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nArray === something\n(1..100) === 7\n/something/ === some_string\n\n# good\nsomething.is_a?(Array)\n(1..100).include?(7)\nsome_string =~ /something/\n```\n#### AllowOnConstant\n\n```ruby\n# Style/CaseEquality:\n#   AllowOnConstant: true\n\n# bad\n(1..100) === 7\n/something/ === some_string\n\n# good\nArray === something\n(1..100).include?(7)\nsome_string =~ /something/\n```\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nAllowOnConstant | `false` | Boolean\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#no-case-equality](https://rubystyle.guide#no-case-equality)\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | No | 0.9 | -\n\nThis cop checks for uses of the case equality operator(===).\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nArray === something\n(1..100) === 7\n/something/ === some_string\n\n# good\nsomething.is_a?(Array)\n(1..100).include?(7)\nsome_string =~ /something/\n```\n#### AllowOnConstant\n\n```ruby\n# Style/CaseEquality:\n#   AllowOnConstant: true\n\n# bad\n(1..100) === 7\n/something/ === some_string\n\n# good\nArray === something\n(1..100).include?(7)\nsome_string =~ /something/\n```\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nAllowOnConstant | `false` | Boolean\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#no-case-equality](https://rubystyle.guide#no-case-equality)\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Style/CharacterLiteral",
              "name": "Style/CharacterLiteral",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Checks for uses of character literals.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_style/#stylecharacterliteral",
                "tags": [
                  "style"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Checks for uses of character literals."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Checks for uses of character literals."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.9 | -\n\nChecks for uses of the character literal ?x.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\n?x\n\n# good\n'x'\n\n# good\n?\\C-\\M-d\n```\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#no-character-literals](https://rubystyle.guide#no-character-literals)\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.9 | -\n\nChecks for uses of the character literal ?x.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\n?x\n\n# good\n'x'\n\n# good\n?\\C-\\M-d\n```\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#no-character-literals](https://rubystyle.guide#no-character-literals)\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Style/ClassAndModuleChildren",
              "name": "Style/ClassAndModuleChildren",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Checks style of children classes and modules.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_style/#styleclassandmodulechildren",
                "tags": [
                  "style"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Checks style of children classes and modules."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Checks style of children classes and modules."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes (Unsafe) | 0.19 | -\n\nThis cop checks the style of children definitions at classes and\nmodules. Basically there are two different styles:\n\nThe compact style is only forced for classes/modules with one child.\n\n### Examples\n\n#### EnforcedStyle: nested (default)\n\n```ruby\n# good\n# have each child on its own line\nclass Foo\n  class Bar\n  end\nend\n```\n#### EnforcedStyle: compact\n\n```ruby\n# good\n# combine definitions as much as possible\nclass Foo::Bar\nend\n```\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nAutoCorrect | `false` | Boolean\nEnforcedStyle | `nested` | `nested`, `compact`\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#namespace-definition](https://rubystyle.guide#namespace-definition)\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes (Unsafe) | 0.19 | -\n\nThis cop checks the style of children definitions at classes and\nmodules. Basically there are two different styles:\n\nThe compact style is only forced for classes/modules with one child.\n\n### Examples\n\n#### EnforcedStyle: nested (default)\n\n```ruby\n# good\n# have each child on its own line\nclass Foo\n  class Bar\n  end\nend\n```\n#### EnforcedStyle: compact\n\n```ruby\n# good\n# combine definitions as much as possible\nclass Foo::Bar\nend\n```\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nAutoCorrect | `false` | Boolean\nEnforcedStyle | `nested` | `nested`, `compact`\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#namespace-definition](https://rubystyle.guide#namespace-definition)\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Style/ClassCheck",
              "name": "Style/ClassCheck",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Enforces consistent use of `Object#is_a?` or `Object#kind_of?`.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_style/#styleclasscheck",
                "tags": [
                  "style"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Enforces consistent use of `Object#is_a?` or `Object#kind_of?`."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Enforces consistent use of `Object#is_a?` or `Object#kind_of?`."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.24 | -\n\nThis cop enforces consistent use of `Object#is_a?` or `Object#kind_of?`.\n\n### Examples\n\n#### EnforcedStyle: is_a? (default)\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nvar.kind_of?(Date)\nvar.kind_of?(Integer)\n\n# good\nvar.is_a?(Date)\nvar.is_a?(Integer)\n```\n#### EnforcedStyle: kind_of?\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nvar.is_a?(Time)\nvar.is_a?(String)\n\n# good\nvar.kind_of?(Time)\nvar.kind_of?(String)\n```\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nEnforcedStyle | `is_a?` | `is_a?`, `kind_of?`\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#is-a-vs-kind-of](https://rubystyle.guide#is-a-vs-kind-of)\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.24 | -\n\nThis cop enforces consistent use of `Object#is_a?` or `Object#kind_of?`.\n\n### Examples\n\n#### EnforcedStyle: is_a? (default)\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nvar.kind_of?(Date)\nvar.kind_of?(Integer)\n\n# good\nvar.is_a?(Date)\nvar.is_a?(Integer)\n```\n#### EnforcedStyle: kind_of?\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nvar.is_a?(Time)\nvar.is_a?(String)\n\n# good\nvar.kind_of?(Time)\nvar.kind_of?(String)\n```\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nEnforcedStyle | `is_a?` | `is_a?`, `kind_of?`\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#is-a-vs-kind-of](https://rubystyle.guide#is-a-vs-kind-of)\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Style/ClassMethods",
              "name": "Style/ClassMethods",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Use self when defining module/class methods.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_style/#styleclassmethods",
                "tags": [
                  "style"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Use self when defining module/class methods."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Use self when defining module/class methods."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.9 | 0.20\n\nThis cop checks for uses of the class/module name instead of\nself, when defining class/module methods.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nclass SomeClass\n  def SomeClass.class_method\n    # ...\n  end\nend\n\n# good\nclass SomeClass\n  def self.class_method\n    # ...\n  end\nend\n```\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#def-self-class-methods](https://rubystyle.guide#def-self-class-methods)\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.9 | 0.20\n\nThis cop checks for uses of the class/module name instead of\nself, when defining class/module methods.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nclass SomeClass\n  def SomeClass.class_method\n    # ...\n  end\nend\n\n# good\nclass SomeClass\n  def self.class_method\n    # ...\n  end\nend\n```\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#def-self-class-methods](https://rubystyle.guide#def-self-class-methods)\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Style/ClassVars",
              "name": "Style/ClassVars",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Avoid the use of class variables.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_style/#styleclassvars",
                "tags": [
                  "style"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Avoid the use of class variables."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Avoid the use of class variables."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | No | 0.13 | -\n\nThis cop checks for uses of class variables. Offenses\nare signaled only on assignment to class variables to\nreduce the number of offenses that would be reported.\n\nYou have to be careful when setting a value for a class\nvariable; if a class has been inherited, changing the\nvalue of a class variable also affects the inheriting\nclasses. This means that it's almost always better to\nuse a class instance variable instead.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nclass A\n  @@test = 10\nend\n\n# good\nclass A\n  @test = 10\nend\n\nclass A\n  def test\n    @@test # you can access class variable without offense\n  end\nend\n```\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#no-class-vars](https://rubystyle.guide#no-class-vars)\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | No | 0.13 | -\n\nThis cop checks for uses of class variables. Offenses\nare signaled only on assignment to class variables to\nreduce the number of offenses that would be reported.\n\nYou have to be careful when setting a value for a class\nvariable; if a class has been inherited, changing the\nvalue of a class variable also affects the inheriting\nclasses. This means that it's almost always better to\nuse a class instance variable instead.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nclass A\n  @@test = 10\nend\n\n# good\nclass A\n  @test = 10\nend\n\nclass A\n  def test\n    @@test # you can access class variable without offense\n  end\nend\n```\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#no-class-vars](https://rubystyle.guide#no-class-vars)\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Style/CollectionMethods",
              "name": "Style/CollectionMethods",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Preferred collection methods.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_style/#stylecollectionmethods",
                "tags": [
                  "style"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Preferred collection methods."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Preferred collection methods."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nDisabled | No | Yes (Unsafe) | 0.9 | 0.27\n\nThis cop enforces the use of consistent method names\nfrom the Enumerable module.\n\nUnfortunately we cannot actually know if a method is from\nEnumerable or not (static analysis limitation), so this cop\ncan yield some false positives.\n\nYou can customize the mapping from undesired method to desired method.\n\ne.g. to use `detect` over `find`:\n\n  Style/CollectionMethods:\n    PreferredMethods:\n      find: detect\n\nThe default mapping for `PreferredMethods` behaves as follows.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nitems.collect\nitems.collect!\nitems.inject\nitems.detect\nitems.find_all\nitems.member?\n\n# good\nitems.map\nitems.map!\nitems.reduce\nitems.find\nitems.select\nitems.include?\n```\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nPreferredMethods | `{\"collect\"=>\"map\", \"collect!\"=>\"map!\", \"inject\"=>\"reduce\", \"detect\"=>\"find\", \"find_all\"=>\"select\", \"member?\"=>\"include?\"}` | \n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#map-find-select-reduce-include-size](https://rubystyle.guide#map-find-select-reduce-include-size)\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nDisabled | No | Yes (Unsafe) | 0.9 | 0.27\n\nThis cop enforces the use of consistent method names\nfrom the Enumerable module.\n\nUnfortunately we cannot actually know if a method is from\nEnumerable or not (static analysis limitation), so this cop\ncan yield some false positives.\n\nYou can customize the mapping from undesired method to desired method.\n\ne.g. to use `detect` over `find`:\n\n  Style/CollectionMethods:\n    PreferredMethods:\n      find: detect\n\nThe default mapping for `PreferredMethods` behaves as follows.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nitems.collect\nitems.collect!\nitems.inject\nitems.detect\nitems.find_all\nitems.member?\n\n# good\nitems.map\nitems.map!\nitems.reduce\nitems.find\nitems.select\nitems.include?\n```\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nPreferredMethods | `{\"collect\"=>\"map\", \"collect!\"=>\"map!\", \"inject\"=>\"reduce\", \"detect\"=>\"find\", \"find_all\"=>\"select\", \"member?\"=>\"include?\"}` | \n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#map-find-select-reduce-include-size](https://rubystyle.guide#map-find-select-reduce-include-size)\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Style/ColonMethodCall",
              "name": "Style/ColonMethodCall",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Do not use :: for method call.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_style/#stylecolonmethodcall",
                "tags": [
                  "style"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Do not use :: for method call."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Do not use :: for method call."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.9 | -\n\nThis cop checks for methods invoked via the :: operator instead\nof the . operator (like FileUtils::rmdir instead of FileUtils.rmdir).\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nTimeout::timeout(500) { do_something }\nFileUtils::rmdir(dir)\nMarshal::dump(obj)\n\n# good\nTimeout.timeout(500) { do_something }\nFileUtils.rmdir(dir)\nMarshal.dump(obj)\n```\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#double-colons](https://rubystyle.guide#double-colons)\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.9 | -\n\nThis cop checks for methods invoked via the :: operator instead\nof the . operator (like FileUtils::rmdir instead of FileUtils.rmdir).\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nTimeout::timeout(500) { do_something }\nFileUtils::rmdir(dir)\nMarshal::dump(obj)\n\n# good\nTimeout.timeout(500) { do_something }\nFileUtils.rmdir(dir)\nMarshal.dump(obj)\n```\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#double-colons](https://rubystyle.guide#double-colons)\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Style/ColonMethodDefinition",
              "name": "Style/ColonMethodDefinition",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Do not use :: for defining class methods.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_style/#stylecolonmethoddefinition",
                "tags": [
                  "style"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Do not use :: for defining class methods."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Do not use :: for defining class methods."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.52 | -\n\nThis cop checks for class methods that are defined using the `::`\noperator instead of the `.` operator.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nclass Foo\n  def self::bar\n  end\nend\n\n# good\nclass Foo\n  def self.bar\n  end\nend\n```\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#colon-method-definition](https://rubystyle.guide#colon-method-definition)\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.52 | -\n\nThis cop checks for class methods that are defined using the `::`\noperator instead of the `.` operator.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nclass Foo\n  def self::bar\n  end\nend\n\n# good\nclass Foo\n  def self.bar\n  end\nend\n```\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#colon-method-definition](https://rubystyle.guide#colon-method-definition)\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Style/CommandLiteral",
              "name": "Style/CommandLiteral",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Use `` or %x around command literals.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_style/#stylecommandliteral",
                "tags": [
                  "style"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Use `` or %x around command literals."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Use `` or %x around command literals."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.30 | -\n\nThis cop enforces using `` or %x around command literals.\n\n### Examples\n\n#### EnforcedStyle: backticks (default)\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nfolders = %x(find . -type d).split\n\n# bad\n%x(\n  ln -s foo.example.yml foo.example\n  ln -s bar.example.yml bar.example\n)\n\n# good\nfolders = `find . -type d`.split\n\n# good\n`\n  ln -s foo.example.yml foo.example\n  ln -s bar.example.yml bar.example\n`\n```\n#### EnforcedStyle: mixed\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nfolders = %x(find . -type d).split\n\n# bad\n`\n  ln -s foo.example.yml foo.example\n  ln -s bar.example.yml bar.example\n`\n\n# good\nfolders = `find . -type d`.split\n\n# good\n%x(\n  ln -s foo.example.yml foo.example\n  ln -s bar.example.yml bar.example\n)\n```\n#### EnforcedStyle: percent_x\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nfolders = `find . -type d`.split\n\n# bad\n`\n  ln -s foo.example.yml foo.example\n  ln -s bar.example.yml bar.example\n`\n\n# good\nfolders = %x(find . -type d).split\n\n# good\n%x(\n  ln -s foo.example.yml foo.example\n  ln -s bar.example.yml bar.example\n)\n```\n#### AllowInnerBackticks: false (default)\n\n```ruby\n# If `false`, the cop will always recommend using `%x` if one or more\n# backticks are found in the command string.\n\n# bad\n`echo \\`ls\\``\n\n# good\n%x(echo `ls`)\n```\n#### AllowInnerBackticks: true\n\n```ruby\n# good\n`echo \\`ls\\``\n```\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nEnforcedStyle | `backticks` | `backticks`, `percent_x`, `mixed`\nAllowInnerBackticks | `false` | Boolean\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#percent-x](https://rubystyle.guide#percent-x)\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.30 | -\n\nThis cop enforces using `` or %x around command literals.\n\n### Examples\n\n#### EnforcedStyle: backticks (default)\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nfolders = %x(find . -type d).split\n\n# bad\n%x(\n  ln -s foo.example.yml foo.example\n  ln -s bar.example.yml bar.example\n)\n\n# good\nfolders = `find . -type d`.split\n\n# good\n`\n  ln -s foo.example.yml foo.example\n  ln -s bar.example.yml bar.example\n`\n```\n#### EnforcedStyle: mixed\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nfolders = %x(find . -type d).split\n\n# bad\n`\n  ln -s foo.example.yml foo.example\n  ln -s bar.example.yml bar.example\n`\n\n# good\nfolders = `find . -type d`.split\n\n# good\n%x(\n  ln -s foo.example.yml foo.example\n  ln -s bar.example.yml bar.example\n)\n```\n#### EnforcedStyle: percent_x\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nfolders = `find . -type d`.split\n\n# bad\n`\n  ln -s foo.example.yml foo.example\n  ln -s bar.example.yml bar.example\n`\n\n# good\nfolders = %x(find . -type d).split\n\n# good\n%x(\n  ln -s foo.example.yml foo.example\n  ln -s bar.example.yml bar.example\n)\n```\n#### AllowInnerBackticks: false (default)\n\n```ruby\n# If `false`, the cop will always recommend using `%x` if one or more\n# backticks are found in the command string.\n\n# bad\n`echo \\`ls\\``\n\n# good\n%x(echo `ls`)\n```\n#### AllowInnerBackticks: true\n\n```ruby\n# good\n`echo \\`ls\\``\n```\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nEnforcedStyle | `backticks` | `backticks`, `percent_x`, `mixed`\nAllowInnerBackticks | `false` | Boolean\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#percent-x](https://rubystyle.guide#percent-x)\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Style/CommentAnnotation",
              "name": "Style/CommentAnnotation",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Checks formatting of special comments (TODO, FIXME, OPTIMIZE, HACK, REVIEW).",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_style/#stylecommentannotation",
                "tags": [
                  "style"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Checks formatting of special comments (TODO, FIXME, OPTIMIZE, HACK, REVIEW)."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Checks formatting of special comments (TODO, FIXME, OPTIMIZE, HACK, REVIEW)."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.10 | 0.31\n\nThis cop checks that comment annotation keywords are written according\nto guidelines.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\n# TODO make better\n\n# good\n# TODO: make better\n\n# bad\n# TODO:make better\n\n# good\n# TODO: make better\n\n# bad\n# fixme: does not work\n\n# good\n# FIXME: does not work\n\n# bad\n# Optimize does not work\n\n# good\n# OPTIMIZE: does not work\n```\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nKeywords | `TODO`, `FIXME`, `OPTIMIZE`, `HACK`, `REVIEW` | Array\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#annotate-keywords](https://rubystyle.guide#annotate-keywords)\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.10 | 0.31\n\nThis cop checks that comment annotation keywords are written according\nto guidelines.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\n# TODO make better\n\n# good\n# TODO: make better\n\n# bad\n# TODO:make better\n\n# good\n# TODO: make better\n\n# bad\n# fixme: does not work\n\n# good\n# FIXME: does not work\n\n# bad\n# Optimize does not work\n\n# good\n# OPTIMIZE: does not work\n```\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nKeywords | `TODO`, `FIXME`, `OPTIMIZE`, `HACK`, `REVIEW` | Array\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#annotate-keywords](https://rubystyle.guide#annotate-keywords)\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Style/CommentedKeyword",
              "name": "Style/CommentedKeyword",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Do not place comments on the same line as certain keywords.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_style/#stylecommentedkeyword",
                "tags": [
                  "style"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Do not place comments on the same line as certain keywords."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Do not place comments on the same line as certain keywords."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | No | 0.51 | -\n\nThis cop checks for comments put on the same line as some keywords.\nThese keywords are: `begin`, `class`, `def`, `end`, `module`.\n\nNote that some comments\n(`:nodoc:`, `:yields:`, `rubocop:disable` and `rubocop:todo`)\nare allowed.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nif condition\n  statement\nend # end if\n\n# bad\nclass X # comment\n  statement\nend\n\n# bad\ndef x; end # comment\n\n# good\nif condition\n  statement\nend\n\n# good\nclass X # :nodoc:\n  y\nend\n```\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | No | 0.51 | -\n\nThis cop checks for comments put on the same line as some keywords.\nThese keywords are: `begin`, `class`, `def`, `end`, `module`.\n\nNote that some comments\n(`:nodoc:`, `:yields:`, `rubocop:disable` and `rubocop:todo`)\nare allowed.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nif condition\n  statement\nend # end if\n\n# bad\nclass X # comment\n  statement\nend\n\n# bad\ndef x; end # comment\n\n# good\nif condition\n  statement\nend\n\n# good\nclass X # :nodoc:\n  y\nend\n```\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Style/ConditionalAssignment",
              "name": "Style/ConditionalAssignment",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Use the return value of `if` and `case` statements for assignment to a variable and variable comparison instead of assigning that variable inside of each branch.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_style/#styleconditionalassignment",
                "tags": [
                  "style"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Use the return value of `if` and `case` statements for assignment to a variable and variable comparison instead of assigning that variable inside of each branch."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Use the return value of `if` and `case` statements for assignment to a variable and variable comparison instead of assigning that variable inside of each branch."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.36 | 0.47\n\nCheck for `if` and `case` statements where each branch is used for\nassignment to the same variable when using the return of the\ncondition can be used instead.\n\n### Examples\n\n#### EnforcedStyle: assign_to_condition (default)\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nif foo\n  bar = 1\nelse\n  bar = 2\nend\n\ncase foo\nwhen 'a'\n  bar += 1\nelse\n  bar += 2\nend\n\nif foo\n  some_method\n  bar = 1\nelse\n  some_other_method\n  bar = 2\nend\n\n# good\nbar = if foo\n        1\n      else\n        2\n      end\n\nbar += case foo\n       when 'a'\n         1\n       else\n         2\n       end\n\nbar << if foo\n         some_method\n         1\n       else\n         some_other_method\n         2\n       end\n```\n#### EnforcedStyle: assign_inside_condition\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nbar = if foo\n        1\n      else\n        2\n      end\n\nbar += case foo\n       when 'a'\n         1\n       else\n         2\n       end\n\nbar << if foo\n         some_method\n         1\n       else\n         some_other_method\n         2\n       end\n\n# good\nif foo\n  bar = 1\nelse\n  bar = 2\nend\n\ncase foo\nwhen 'a'\n  bar += 1\nelse\n  bar += 2\nend\n\nif foo\n  some_method\n  bar = 1\nelse\n  some_other_method\n  bar = 2\nend\n```\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nEnforcedStyle | `assign_to_condition` | `assign_to_condition`, `assign_inside_condition`\nSingleLineConditionsOnly | `true` | Boolean\nIncludeTernaryExpressions | `true` | Boolean\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.36 | 0.47\n\nCheck for `if` and `case` statements where each branch is used for\nassignment to the same variable when using the return of the\ncondition can be used instead.\n\n### Examples\n\n#### EnforcedStyle: assign_to_condition (default)\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nif foo\n  bar = 1\nelse\n  bar = 2\nend\n\ncase foo\nwhen 'a'\n  bar += 1\nelse\n  bar += 2\nend\n\nif foo\n  some_method\n  bar = 1\nelse\n  some_other_method\n  bar = 2\nend\n\n# good\nbar = if foo\n        1\n      else\n        2\n      end\n\nbar += case foo\n       when 'a'\n         1\n       else\n         2\n       end\n\nbar << if foo\n         some_method\n         1\n       else\n         some_other_method\n         2\n       end\n```\n#### EnforcedStyle: assign_inside_condition\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nbar = if foo\n        1\n      else\n        2\n      end\n\nbar += case foo\n       when 'a'\n         1\n       else\n         2\n       end\n\nbar << if foo\n         some_method\n         1\n       else\n         some_other_method\n         2\n       end\n\n# good\nif foo\n  bar = 1\nelse\n  bar = 2\nend\n\ncase foo\nwhen 'a'\n  bar += 1\nelse\n  bar += 2\nend\n\nif foo\n  some_method\n  bar = 1\nelse\n  some_other_method\n  bar = 2\nend\n```\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nEnforcedStyle | `assign_to_condition` | `assign_to_condition`, `assign_inside_condition`\nSingleLineConditionsOnly | `true` | Boolean\nIncludeTernaryExpressions | `true` | Boolean\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Style/ConstantVisibility",
              "name": "Style/ConstantVisibility",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Check that class- and module constants have visibility declarations.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_style/#styleconstantvisibility",
                "tags": [
                  "style"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Check that class- and module constants have visibility declarations."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Check that class- and module constants have visibility declarations."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nDisabled | Yes | No | 0.66 | -\n\nThis cop checks that constants defined in classes and modules have\nan explicit visibility declaration. By default, Ruby makes all class-\nand module constants public, which litters the public API of the\nclass or module. Explicitly declaring a visibility makes intent more\nclear, and prevents outside actors from touching private state.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nclass Foo\n  BAR = 42\n  BAZ = 43\nend\n\n# good\nclass Foo\n  BAR = 42\n  private_constant :BAR\n\n  BAZ = 43\n  public_constant :BAZ\nend\n```\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nDisabled | Yes | No | 0.66 | -\n\nThis cop checks that constants defined in classes and modules have\nan explicit visibility declaration. By default, Ruby makes all class-\nand module constants public, which litters the public API of the\nclass or module. Explicitly declaring a visibility makes intent more\nclear, and prevents outside actors from touching private state.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nclass Foo\n  BAR = 42\n  BAZ = 43\nend\n\n# good\nclass Foo\n  BAR = 42\n  private_constant :BAR\n\n  BAZ = 43\n  public_constant :BAZ\nend\n```\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Style/Copyright",
              "name": "Style/Copyright",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Include a copyright notice in each file before any code.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_style/#stylecopyright",
                "tags": [
                  "style"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Include a copyright notice in each file before any code."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Include a copyright notice in each file before any code."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nDisabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.30 | -\n\nCheck that a copyright notice was given in each source file.\n\nThe default regexp for an acceptable copyright notice can be found in\nconfig/default.yml. The default can be changed as follows:\n\n    Style/Copyright:\n      Notice: '^Copyright (\\(c\\) )?2\\d{3} Acme Inc'\n\nThis regex string is treated as an unanchored regex. For each file\nthat RuboCop scans, a comment that matches this regex must be found or\nan offense is reported.\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nNotice | `^Copyright (\\(c\\) )?2[0-9]{3} .+` | String\nAutocorrectNotice | `` | String\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nDisabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.30 | -\n\nCheck that a copyright notice was given in each source file.\n\nThe default regexp for an acceptable copyright notice can be found in\nconfig/default.yml. The default can be changed as follows:\n\n    Style/Copyright:\n      Notice: '^Copyright (\\(c\\) )?2\\d{3} Acme Inc'\n\nThis regex string is treated as an unanchored regex. For each file\nthat RuboCop scans, a comment that matches this regex must be found or\nan offense is reported.\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nNotice | `^Copyright (\\(c\\) )?2[0-9]{3} .+` | String\nAutocorrectNotice | `` | String\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Style/DateTime",
              "name": "Style/DateTime",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Use Time over DateTime.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_style/#styledatetime",
                "tags": [
                  "style"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Use Time over DateTime."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Use Time over DateTime."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nDisabled | Yes | No | 0.51 | 0.59\n\nThis cop checks for consistent usage of the `DateTime` class over the\n`Time` class. This cop is disabled by default since these classes,\nalthough highly overlapping, have particularities that make them not\nreplaceable in certain situations when dealing with multiple timezones\nand/or DST.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad - uses `DateTime` for current time\nDateTime.now\n\n# good - uses `Time` for current time\nTime.now\n\n# bad - uses `DateTime` for modern date\nDateTime.iso8601('2016-06-29')\n\n# good - uses `Time` for modern date\nTime.iso8601('2016-06-29')\n\n# good - uses `DateTime` with start argument for historical date\nDateTime.iso8601('1751-04-23', Date::ENGLAND)\n```\n#### AllowCoercion: false (default)\n\n```ruby\n# bad - coerces to `DateTime`\nsomething.to_datetime\n\n# good - coerces to `Time`\nsomething.to_time\n```\n#### AllowCoercion: true\n\n```ruby\n# good\nsomething.to_datetime\n\n# good\nsomething.to_time\n```\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nAllowCoercion | `false` | Boolean\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#date--time](https://rubystyle.guide#date--time)\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nDisabled | Yes | No | 0.51 | 0.59\n\nThis cop checks for consistent usage of the `DateTime` class over the\n`Time` class. This cop is disabled by default since these classes,\nalthough highly overlapping, have particularities that make them not\nreplaceable in certain situations when dealing with multiple timezones\nand/or DST.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad - uses `DateTime` for current time\nDateTime.now\n\n# good - uses `Time` for current time\nTime.now\n\n# bad - uses `DateTime` for modern date\nDateTime.iso8601('2016-06-29')\n\n# good - uses `Time` for modern date\nTime.iso8601('2016-06-29')\n\n# good - uses `DateTime` with start argument for historical date\nDateTime.iso8601('1751-04-23', Date::ENGLAND)\n```\n#### AllowCoercion: false (default)\n\n```ruby\n# bad - coerces to `DateTime`\nsomething.to_datetime\n\n# good - coerces to `Time`\nsomething.to_time\n```\n#### AllowCoercion: true\n\n```ruby\n# good\nsomething.to_datetime\n\n# good\nsomething.to_time\n```\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nAllowCoercion | `false` | Boolean\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#date--time](https://rubystyle.guide#date--time)\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Style/DefWithParentheses",
              "name": "Style/DefWithParentheses",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Use def with parentheses when there are arguments.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_style/#styledefwithparentheses",
                "tags": [
                  "style"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Use def with parentheses when there are arguments."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Use def with parentheses when there are arguments."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.9 | 0.12\n\nThis cop checks for parentheses in the definition of a method,\nthat does not take any arguments. Both instance and\nclass/singleton methods are checked.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\ndef foo()\n  # does a thing\nend\n\n# good\ndef foo\n  # does a thing\nend\n\n# also good\ndef foo() does_a_thing end\n```\n```ruby\n# bad\ndef Baz.foo()\n  # does a thing\nend\n\n# good\ndef Baz.foo\n  # does a thing\nend\n```\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#method-parens](https://rubystyle.guide#method-parens)\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.9 | 0.12\n\nThis cop checks for parentheses in the definition of a method,\nthat does not take any arguments. Both instance and\nclass/singleton methods are checked.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\ndef foo()\n  # does a thing\nend\n\n# good\ndef foo\n  # does a thing\nend\n\n# also good\ndef foo() does_a_thing end\n```\n```ruby\n# bad\ndef Baz.foo()\n  # does a thing\nend\n\n# good\ndef Baz.foo\n  # does a thing\nend\n```\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#method-parens](https://rubystyle.guide#method-parens)\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Style/Dir",
              "name": "Style/Dir",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Use the `__dir__` method to retrieve the canonicalized absolute path to the current file.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_style/#styledir",
                "tags": [
                  "style"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Use the `__dir__` method to retrieve the canonicalized absolute path to the current file."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Use the `__dir__` method to retrieve the canonicalized absolute path to the current file."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.50 | -\n\nThis cop checks for places where the `#__dir__` method can replace more\ncomplex constructs to retrieve a canonicalized absolute path to the\ncurrent file.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\npath = File.expand_path(File.dirname(__FILE__))\n\n# bad\npath = File.dirname(File.realpath(__FILE__))\n\n# good\npath = __dir__\n```\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.50 | -\n\nThis cop checks for places where the `#__dir__` method can replace more\ncomplex constructs to retrieve a canonicalized absolute path to the\ncurrent file.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\npath = File.expand_path(File.dirname(__FILE__))\n\n# bad\npath = File.dirname(File.realpath(__FILE__))\n\n# good\npath = __dir__\n```\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Style/DisableCopsWithinSourceCodeDirective",
              "name": "Style/DisableCopsWithinSourceCodeDirective",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Forbids disabling/enabling cops within source code.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_style/#styledisablecopswithinsourcecodedirective",
                "tags": [
                  "style"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Forbids disabling/enabling cops within source code."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Forbids disabling/enabling cops within source code."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nDisabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.82 | -\n\nDetects comments to enable/disable RuboCop.\nThis is useful if want to make sure that every RuboCop error gets fixed\nand not quickly disabled with a comment.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\n# rubocop:disable Metrics/AbcSize\ndef f\nend\n# rubocop:enable Metrics/AbcSize\n\n# good\ndef fixed_method_name_and_no_rubocop_comments\nend\n```\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nDisabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.82 | -\n\nDetects comments to enable/disable RuboCop.\nThis is useful if want to make sure that every RuboCop error gets fixed\nand not quickly disabled with a comment.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\n# rubocop:disable Metrics/AbcSize\ndef f\nend\n# rubocop:enable Metrics/AbcSize\n\n# good\ndef fixed_method_name_and_no_rubocop_comments\nend\n```\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Style/Documentation",
              "name": "Style/Documentation",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Document classes and non-namespace modules.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_style/#styledocumentation",
                "tags": [
                  "style"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Document classes and non-namespace modules."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Document classes and non-namespace modules."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | No | 0.9 | -\n\nThis cop checks for missing top-level documentation of classes and\nmodules. Classes with no body are exempt from the check and so are\nnamespace modules - modules that have nothing in their bodies except\nclasses, other modules, constant definitions or constant visibility\ndeclarations.\n\nThe documentation requirement is annulled if the class or module has\na \"#:nodoc:\" comment next to it. Likewise, \"#:nodoc: all\" does the\nsame for all its children.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nclass Person\n  # ...\nend\n\nmodule Math\nend\n\n# good\n# Description/Explanation of Person class\nclass Person\n  # ...\nend\n\n# allowed\n  # Class without body\n  class Person\n  end\n\n  # Namespace - A namespace can be a class or a module\n  # Containing a class\n  module Namespace\n    # Description/Explanation of Person class\n    class Person\n      # ...\n    end\n  end\n\n  # Containing constant visibility declaration\n  module Namespace\n    class Private\n    end\n\n    private_constant :Private\n  end\n\n  # Containing constant definition\n  module Namespace\n    Public = Class.new\n  end\n```\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nExclude | `spec/**/*`, `test/**/*` | Array\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | No | 0.9 | -\n\nThis cop checks for missing top-level documentation of classes and\nmodules. Classes with no body are exempt from the check and so are\nnamespace modules - modules that have nothing in their bodies except\nclasses, other modules, constant definitions or constant visibility\ndeclarations.\n\nThe documentation requirement is annulled if the class or module has\na \"#:nodoc:\" comment next to it. Likewise, \"#:nodoc: all\" does the\nsame for all its children.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nclass Person\n  # ...\nend\n\nmodule Math\nend\n\n# good\n# Description/Explanation of Person class\nclass Person\n  # ...\nend\n\n# allowed\n  # Class without body\n  class Person\n  end\n\n  # Namespace - A namespace can be a class or a module\n  # Containing a class\n  module Namespace\n    # Description/Explanation of Person class\n    class Person\n      # ...\n    end\n  end\n\n  # Containing constant visibility declaration\n  module Namespace\n    class Private\n    end\n\n    private_constant :Private\n  end\n\n  # Containing constant definition\n  module Namespace\n    Public = Class.new\n  end\n```\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nExclude | `spec/**/*`, `test/**/*` | Array\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Style/DocumentationMethod",
              "name": "Style/DocumentationMethod",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Checks for missing documentation comment for public methods.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_style/#styledocumentationmethod",
                "tags": [
                  "style"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Checks for missing documentation comment for public methods."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Checks for missing documentation comment for public methods."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nDisabled | Yes | No | 0.43 | -\n\nThis cop checks for missing documentation comment for public methods.\nIt can optionally be configured to also require documentation for\nnon-public methods.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\n\nclass Foo\n  def bar\n    puts baz\n  end\nend\n\nmodule Foo\n  def bar\n    puts baz\n  end\nend\n\ndef foo.bar\n  puts baz\nend\n\n# good\n\nclass Foo\n  # Documentation\n  def bar\n    puts baz\n  end\nend\n\nmodule Foo\n  # Documentation\n  def bar\n    puts baz\n  end\nend\n\n# Documentation\ndef foo.bar\n  puts baz\nend\n```\n#### RequireForNonPublicMethods: false (default)\n\n```ruby\n# good\nclass Foo\n  protected\n  def do_something\n  end\nend\n\nclass Foo\n  private\n  def do_something\n  end\nend\n```\n#### RequireForNonPublicMethods: true\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nclass Foo\n  protected\n  def do_something\n  end\nend\n\nclass Foo\n  private\n  def do_something\n  end\nend\n\n# good\nclass Foo\n  protected\n  # Documentation\n  def do_something\n  end\nend\n\nclass Foo\n  private\n  # Documentation\n  def do_something\n  end\nend\n```\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nExclude | `spec/**/*`, `test/**/*` | Array\nRequireForNonPublicMethods | `false` | Boolean\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nDisabled | Yes | No | 0.43 | -\n\nThis cop checks for missing documentation comment for public methods.\nIt can optionally be configured to also require documentation for\nnon-public methods.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\n\nclass Foo\n  def bar\n    puts baz\n  end\nend\n\nmodule Foo\n  def bar\n    puts baz\n  end\nend\n\ndef foo.bar\n  puts baz\nend\n\n# good\n\nclass Foo\n  # Documentation\n  def bar\n    puts baz\n  end\nend\n\nmodule Foo\n  # Documentation\n  def bar\n    puts baz\n  end\nend\n\n# Documentation\ndef foo.bar\n  puts baz\nend\n```\n#### RequireForNonPublicMethods: false (default)\n\n```ruby\n# good\nclass Foo\n  protected\n  def do_something\n  end\nend\n\nclass Foo\n  private\n  def do_something\n  end\nend\n```\n#### RequireForNonPublicMethods: true\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nclass Foo\n  protected\n  def do_something\n  end\nend\n\nclass Foo\n  private\n  def do_something\n  end\nend\n\n# good\nclass Foo\n  protected\n  # Documentation\n  def do_something\n  end\nend\n\nclass Foo\n  private\n  # Documentation\n  def do_something\n  end\nend\n```\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nExclude | `spec/**/*`, `test/**/*` | Array\nRequireForNonPublicMethods | `false` | Boolean\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Style/DoubleCopDisableDirective",
              "name": "Style/DoubleCopDisableDirective",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Checks for double rubocop:disable comments on a single line.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_style/#styledoublecopdisabledirective",
                "tags": [
                  "style"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Checks for double rubocop:disable comments on a single line."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Checks for double rubocop:disable comments on a single line."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.73 | -\n\nDetects double disable comments on one line. This is mostly to catch\nautomatically generated comments that need to be regenerated.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\ndef f # rubocop:disable Style/For # rubocop:disable Metrics/AbcSize\nend\n\n# good\n# rubocop:disable Metrics/AbcSize\ndef f # rubocop:disable Style/For\nend\n# rubocop:enable Metrics/AbcSize\n\n# if both fit on one line\ndef f # rubocop:disable Style/For, Metrics/AbcSize\nend\n```\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.73 | -\n\nDetects double disable comments on one line. This is mostly to catch\nautomatically generated comments that need to be regenerated.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\ndef f # rubocop:disable Style/For # rubocop:disable Metrics/AbcSize\nend\n\n# good\n# rubocop:disable Metrics/AbcSize\ndef f # rubocop:disable Style/For\nend\n# rubocop:enable Metrics/AbcSize\n\n# if both fit on one line\ndef f # rubocop:disable Style/For, Metrics/AbcSize\nend\n```\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Style/DoubleNegation",
              "name": "Style/DoubleNegation",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Checks for uses of double negation (!!).",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_style/#styledoublenegation",
                "tags": [
                  "style"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Checks for uses of double negation (!!)."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Checks for uses of double negation (!!)."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | No | 0.19 | -\n\nThis cop checks for uses of double negation (!!) to convert something\nto a boolean value. As this is both cryptic and usually redundant, it\nshould be avoided.\n\nPlease, note that when something is a boolean value\n!!something and !something.nil? are not the same thing.\nAs you're unlikely to write code that can accept values of any type\nthis is rarely a problem in practice.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\n!!something\n\n# good\n!something.nil?\n```\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#no-bang-bang](https://rubystyle.guide#no-bang-bang)\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | No | 0.19 | -\n\nThis cop checks for uses of double negation (!!) to convert something\nto a boolean value. As this is both cryptic and usually redundant, it\nshould be avoided.\n\nPlease, note that when something is a boolean value\n!!something and !something.nil? are not the same thing.\nAs you're unlikely to write code that can accept values of any type\nthis is rarely a problem in practice.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\n!!something\n\n# good\n!something.nil?\n```\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#no-bang-bang](https://rubystyle.guide#no-bang-bang)\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Style/EachForSimpleLoop",
              "name": "Style/EachForSimpleLoop",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Use `Integer#times` for a simple loop which iterates a fixed number of times.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_style/#styleeachforsimpleloop",
                "tags": [
                  "style"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Use `Integer#times` for a simple loop which iterates a fixed number of times."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Use `Integer#times` for a simple loop which iterates a fixed number of times."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.41 | -\n\nThis cop checks for loops which iterate a constant number of times,\nusing a Range literal and `#each`. This can be done more readably using\n`Integer#times`.\n\nThis check only applies if the block takes no parameters.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\n(1..5).each { }\n\n# good\n5.times { }\n```\n```ruby\n# bad\n(0...10).each {}\n\n# good\n10.times {}\n```\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.41 | -\n\nThis cop checks for loops which iterate a constant number of times,\nusing a Range literal and `#each`. This can be done more readably using\n`Integer#times`.\n\nThis check only applies if the block takes no parameters.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\n(1..5).each { }\n\n# good\n5.times { }\n```\n```ruby\n# bad\n(0...10).each {}\n\n# good\n10.times {}\n```\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Style/EachWithObject",
              "name": "Style/EachWithObject",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Prefer `each_with_object` over `inject` or `reduce`.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_style/#styleeachwithobject",
                "tags": [
                  "style"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Prefer `each_with_object` over `inject` or `reduce`."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Prefer `each_with_object` over `inject` or `reduce`."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.22 | 0.42\n\nThis cop looks for inject / reduce calls where the passed in object is\nreturned at the end and so could be replaced by each_with_object without\nthe need to return the object at the end.\n\nHowever, we can't replace with each_with_object if the accumulator\nparameter is assigned to within the block.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\n[1, 2].inject({}) { |a, e| a[e] = e; a }\n\n# good\n[1, 2].each_with_object({}) { |e, a| a[e] = e }\n```\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.22 | 0.42\n\nThis cop looks for inject / reduce calls where the passed in object is\nreturned at the end and so could be replaced by each_with_object without\nthe need to return the object at the end.\n\nHowever, we can't replace with each_with_object if the accumulator\nparameter is assigned to within the block.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\n[1, 2].inject({}) { |a, e| a[e] = e; a }\n\n# good\n[1, 2].each_with_object({}) { |e, a| a[e] = e }\n```\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Style/EmptyBlockParameter",
              "name": "Style/EmptyBlockParameter",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Omit pipes for empty block parameters.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_style/#styleemptyblockparameter",
                "tags": [
                  "style"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Omit pipes for empty block parameters."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Omit pipes for empty block parameters."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.52 | -\n\nThis cop checks for pipes for empty block parameters. Pipes for empty\nblock parameters do not cause syntax errors, but they are redundant.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\na do ||\n  do_something\nend\n\n# bad\na { || do_something }\n\n# good\na do\nend\n\n# good\na { do_something }\n```\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.52 | -\n\nThis cop checks for pipes for empty block parameters. Pipes for empty\nblock parameters do not cause syntax errors, but they are redundant.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\na do ||\n  do_something\nend\n\n# bad\na { || do_something }\n\n# good\na do\nend\n\n# good\na { do_something }\n```\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Style/EmptyCaseCondition",
              "name": "Style/EmptyCaseCondition",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Avoid empty condition in case statements.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_style/#styleemptycasecondition",
                "tags": [
                  "style"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Avoid empty condition in case statements."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Avoid empty condition in case statements."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.40 | -\n\nThis cop checks for case statements with an empty condition.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad:\ncase\nwhen x == 0\n  puts 'x is 0'\nwhen y == 0\n  puts 'y is 0'\nelse\n  puts 'neither is 0'\nend\n\n# good:\nif x == 0\n  puts 'x is 0'\nelsif y == 0\n  puts 'y is 0'\nelse\n  puts 'neither is 0'\nend\n\n# good: (the case condition node is not empty)\ncase n\nwhen 0\n  puts 'zero'\nwhen 1\n  puts 'one'\nelse\n  puts 'more'\nend\n```\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.40 | -\n\nThis cop checks for case statements with an empty condition.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad:\ncase\nwhen x == 0\n  puts 'x is 0'\nwhen y == 0\n  puts 'y is 0'\nelse\n  puts 'neither is 0'\nend\n\n# good:\nif x == 0\n  puts 'x is 0'\nelsif y == 0\n  puts 'y is 0'\nelse\n  puts 'neither is 0'\nend\n\n# good: (the case condition node is not empty)\ncase n\nwhen 0\n  puts 'zero'\nwhen 1\n  puts 'one'\nelse\n  puts 'more'\nend\n```\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Style/EmptyElse",
              "name": "Style/EmptyElse",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Avoid empty else-clauses.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_style/#styleemptyelse",
                "tags": [
                  "style"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Avoid empty else-clauses."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Avoid empty else-clauses."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.28 | 0.32\n\nChecks for empty else-clauses, possibly including comments and/or an\nexplicit `nil` depending on the EnforcedStyle.\n\n### Examples\n\n#### EnforcedStyle: empty\n\n```ruby\n# warn only on empty else\n\n# bad\nif condition\n  statement\nelse\nend\n\n# good\nif condition\n  statement\nelse\n  nil\nend\n\n# good\nif condition\n  statement\nelse\n  statement\nend\n\n# good\nif condition\n  statement\nend\n```\n#### EnforcedStyle: nil\n\n```ruby\n# warn on else with nil in it\n\n# bad\nif condition\n  statement\nelse\n  nil\nend\n\n# good\nif condition\n  statement\nelse\nend\n\n# good\nif condition\n  statement\nelse\n  statement\nend\n\n# good\nif condition\n  statement\nend\n```\n#### EnforcedStyle: both (default)\n\n```ruby\n# warn on empty else and else with nil in it\n\n# bad\nif condition\n  statement\nelse\n  nil\nend\n\n# bad\nif condition\n  statement\nelse\nend\n\n# good\nif condition\n  statement\nelse\n  statement\nend\n\n# good\nif condition\n  statement\nend\n```\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nEnforcedStyle | `both` | `empty`, `nil`, `both`\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.28 | 0.32\n\nChecks for empty else-clauses, possibly including comments and/or an\nexplicit `nil` depending on the EnforcedStyle.\n\n### Examples\n\n#### EnforcedStyle: empty\n\n```ruby\n# warn only on empty else\n\n# bad\nif condition\n  statement\nelse\nend\n\n# good\nif condition\n  statement\nelse\n  nil\nend\n\n# good\nif condition\n  statement\nelse\n  statement\nend\n\n# good\nif condition\n  statement\nend\n```\n#### EnforcedStyle: nil\n\n```ruby\n# warn on else with nil in it\n\n# bad\nif condition\n  statement\nelse\n  nil\nend\n\n# good\nif condition\n  statement\nelse\nend\n\n# good\nif condition\n  statement\nelse\n  statement\nend\n\n# good\nif condition\n  statement\nend\n```\n#### EnforcedStyle: both (default)\n\n```ruby\n# warn on empty else and else with nil in it\n\n# bad\nif condition\n  statement\nelse\n  nil\nend\n\n# bad\nif condition\n  statement\nelse\nend\n\n# good\nif condition\n  statement\nelse\n  statement\nend\n\n# good\nif condition\n  statement\nend\n```\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nEnforcedStyle | `both` | `empty`, `nil`, `both`\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Style/EmptyLambdaParameter",
              "name": "Style/EmptyLambdaParameter",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Omit parens for empty lambda parameters.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_style/#styleemptylambdaparameter",
                "tags": [
                  "style"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Omit parens for empty lambda parameters."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Omit parens for empty lambda parameters."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.52 | -\n\nThis cop checks for parentheses for empty lambda parameters. Parentheses\nfor empty lambda parameters do not cause syntax errors, but they are\nredundant.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\n-> () { do_something }\n\n# good\n-> { do_something }\n\n# good\n-> (arg) { do_something(arg) }\n```\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.52 | -\n\nThis cop checks for parentheses for empty lambda parameters. Parentheses\nfor empty lambda parameters do not cause syntax errors, but they are\nredundant.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\n-> () { do_something }\n\n# good\n-> { do_something }\n\n# good\n-> (arg) { do_something(arg) }\n```\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Style/EmptyLiteral",
              "name": "Style/EmptyLiteral",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Prefer literals to Array.new/Hash.new/String.new.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_style/#styleemptyliteral",
                "tags": [
                  "style"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Prefer literals to Array.new/Hash.new/String.new."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Prefer literals to Array.new/Hash.new/String.new."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.9 | 0.12\n\nThis cop checks for the use of a method, the result of which\nwould be a literal, like an empty array, hash, or string.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\na = Array.new\nh = Hash.new\ns = String.new\n\n# good\na = []\nh = {}\ns = ''\n```\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#literal-array-hash](https://rubystyle.guide#literal-array-hash)\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.9 | 0.12\n\nThis cop checks for the use of a method, the result of which\nwould be a literal, like an empty array, hash, or string.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\na = Array.new\nh = Hash.new\ns = String.new\n\n# good\na = []\nh = {}\ns = ''\n```\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#literal-array-hash](https://rubystyle.guide#literal-array-hash)\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Style/EmptyMethod",
              "name": "Style/EmptyMethod",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Checks the formatting of empty method definitions.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_style/#styleemptymethod",
                "tags": [
                  "style"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Checks the formatting of empty method definitions."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Checks the formatting of empty method definitions."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.46 | -\n\nThis cop checks for the formatting of empty method definitions.\nBy default it enforces empty method definitions to go on a single\nline (compact style), but it can be configured to enforce the `end`\nto go on its own line (expanded style).\n\nNote: A method definition is not considered empty if it contains\n      comments.\n\n### Examples\n\n#### EnforcedStyle: compact (default)\n\n```ruby\n# bad\ndef foo(bar)\nend\n\ndef self.foo(bar)\nend\n\n# good\ndef foo(bar); end\n\ndef foo(bar)\n  # baz\nend\n\ndef self.foo(bar); end\n```\n#### EnforcedStyle: expanded\n\n```ruby\n# bad\ndef foo(bar); end\n\ndef self.foo(bar); end\n\n# good\ndef foo(bar)\nend\n\ndef self.foo(bar)\nend\n```\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nEnforcedStyle | `compact` | `compact`, `expanded`\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#no-single-line-methods](https://rubystyle.guide#no-single-line-methods)\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.46 | -\n\nThis cop checks for the formatting of empty method definitions.\nBy default it enforces empty method definitions to go on a single\nline (compact style), but it can be configured to enforce the `end`\nto go on its own line (expanded style).\n\nNote: A method definition is not considered empty if it contains\n      comments.\n\n### Examples\n\n#### EnforcedStyle: compact (default)\n\n```ruby\n# bad\ndef foo(bar)\nend\n\ndef self.foo(bar)\nend\n\n# good\ndef foo(bar); end\n\ndef foo(bar)\n  # baz\nend\n\ndef self.foo(bar); end\n```\n#### EnforcedStyle: expanded\n\n```ruby\n# bad\ndef foo(bar); end\n\ndef self.foo(bar); end\n\n# good\ndef foo(bar)\nend\n\ndef self.foo(bar)\nend\n```\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nEnforcedStyle | `compact` | `compact`, `expanded`\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#no-single-line-methods](https://rubystyle.guide#no-single-line-methods)\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Style/Encoding",
              "name": "Style/Encoding",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Use UTF-8 as the source file encoding.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_style/#styleencoding",
                "tags": [
                  "style"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Use UTF-8 as the source file encoding."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Use UTF-8 as the source file encoding."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.9 | 0.50\n\nThis cop checks ensures source files have no utf-8 encoding comments.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\n# encoding: UTF-8\n# coding: UTF-8\n# -*- coding: UTF-8 -*-\n```\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#utf-8](https://rubystyle.guide#utf-8)\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.9 | 0.50\n\nThis cop checks ensures source files have no utf-8 encoding comments.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\n# encoding: UTF-8\n# coding: UTF-8\n# -*- coding: UTF-8 -*-\n```\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#utf-8](https://rubystyle.guide#utf-8)\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Style/EndBlock",
              "name": "Style/EndBlock",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Avoid the use of END blocks.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_style/#styleendblock",
                "tags": [
                  "style"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Avoid the use of END blocks."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Avoid the use of END blocks."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.9 | 0.81\n\nThis cop checks for END blocks.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nEND { puts 'Goodbye!' }\n\n# good\nat_exit { puts 'Goodbye!' }\n```\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#no-END-blocks](https://rubystyle.guide#no-END-blocks)\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.9 | 0.81\n\nThis cop checks for END blocks.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nEND { puts 'Goodbye!' }\n\n# good\nat_exit { puts 'Goodbye!' }\n```\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#no-END-blocks](https://rubystyle.guide#no-END-blocks)\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Style/EvalWithLocation",
              "name": "Style/EvalWithLocation",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Pass `__FILE__` and `__LINE__` to `eval` method, as they are used by backtraces.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_style/#styleevalwithlocation",
                "tags": [
                  "style"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Pass `__FILE__` and `__LINE__` to `eval` method, as they are used by backtraces."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Pass `__FILE__` and `__LINE__` to `eval` method, as they are used by backtraces."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | No | 0.52 | -\n\nThis cop checks `eval` method usage. `eval` can receive source location\nmetadata, that are filename and line number. The metadata is used by\nbacktraces. This cop recommends to pass the metadata to `eval` method.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\neval <<-RUBY\n  def do_something\n  end\nRUBY\n\n# bad\nC.class_eval <<-RUBY\n  def do_something\n  end\nRUBY\n\n# good\neval <<-RUBY, binding, __FILE__, __LINE__ + 1\n  def do_something\n  end\nRUBY\n\n# good\nC.class_eval <<-RUBY, __FILE__, __LINE__ + 1\n  def do_something\n  end\nRUBY\n```\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | No | 0.52 | -\n\nThis cop checks `eval` method usage. `eval` can receive source location\nmetadata, that are filename and line number. The metadata is used by\nbacktraces. This cop recommends to pass the metadata to `eval` method.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\neval <<-RUBY\n  def do_something\n  end\nRUBY\n\n# bad\nC.class_eval <<-RUBY\n  def do_something\n  end\nRUBY\n\n# good\neval <<-RUBY, binding, __FILE__, __LINE__ + 1\n  def do_something\n  end\nRUBY\n\n# good\nC.class_eval <<-RUBY, __FILE__, __LINE__ + 1\n  def do_something\n  end\nRUBY\n```\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Style/EvenOdd",
              "name": "Style/EvenOdd",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Favor the use of `Integer#even?` && `Integer#odd?`.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_style/#styleevenodd",
                "tags": [
                  "style"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Favor the use of `Integer#even?` && `Integer#odd?`."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Favor the use of `Integer#even?` && `Integer#odd?`."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.12 | 0.29\n\nThis cop checks for places where `Integer#even?` or `Integer#odd?`\ncan be used.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nif x % 2 == 0\nend\n\n# good\nif x.even?\nend\n```\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#predicate-methods](https://rubystyle.guide#predicate-methods)\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.12 | 0.29\n\nThis cop checks for places where `Integer#even?` or `Integer#odd?`\ncan be used.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nif x % 2 == 0\nend\n\n# good\nif x.even?\nend\n```\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#predicate-methods](https://rubystyle.guide#predicate-methods)\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Style/ExpandPathArguments",
              "name": "Style/ExpandPathArguments",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Use `expand_path(__dir__)` instead of `expand_path('..', __FILE__)`.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_style/#styleexpandpatharguments",
                "tags": [
                  "style"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Use `expand_path(__dir__)` instead of `expand_path('..', __FILE__)`."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Use `expand_path(__dir__)` instead of `expand_path('..', __FILE__)`."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.53 | -\n\nThis cop checks for use of the `File.expand_path` arguments.\nLikewise, it also checks for the `Pathname.new` argument.\n\nContrastive bad case and good case are alternately shown in\nthe following examples.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nFile.expand_path('..', __FILE__)\n\n# good\nFile.expand_path(__dir__)\n\n# bad\nFile.expand_path('../..', __FILE__)\n\n# good\nFile.expand_path('..', __dir__)\n\n# bad\nFile.expand_path('.', __FILE__)\n\n# good\nFile.expand_path(__FILE__)\n\n# bad\nPathname(__FILE__).parent.expand_path\n\n# good\nPathname(__dir__).expand_path\n\n# bad\nPathname.new(__FILE__).parent.expand_path\n\n# good\nPathname.new(__dir__).expand_path\n```\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.53 | -\n\nThis cop checks for use of the `File.expand_path` arguments.\nLikewise, it also checks for the `Pathname.new` argument.\n\nContrastive bad case and good case are alternately shown in\nthe following examples.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nFile.expand_path('..', __FILE__)\n\n# good\nFile.expand_path(__dir__)\n\n# bad\nFile.expand_path('../..', __FILE__)\n\n# good\nFile.expand_path('..', __dir__)\n\n# bad\nFile.expand_path('.', __FILE__)\n\n# good\nFile.expand_path(__FILE__)\n\n# bad\nPathname(__FILE__).parent.expand_path\n\n# good\nPathname(__dir__).expand_path\n\n# bad\nPathname.new(__FILE__).parent.expand_path\n\n# good\nPathname.new(__dir__).expand_path\n```\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Style/ExponentialNotation",
              "name": "Style/ExponentialNotation",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "When using exponential notation, favor a mantissa between 1 (inclusive) and 10 (exclusive).",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_style/#styleexponentialnotation",
                "tags": [
                  "style"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "When using exponential notation, favor a mantissa between 1 (inclusive) and 10 (exclusive)."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "When using exponential notation, favor a mantissa between 1 (inclusive) and 10 (exclusive)."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nPending | Yes | No | 0.82 | -\n\nThis cop enforces consistency when using exponential notation\nfor numbers in the code (eg 1.2e4). Different styles are supported:\n- `scientific` which enforces a mantissa between 1 (inclusive)\n               and 10 (exclusive).\n- `engineering` which enforces the exponent to be a multiple of 3\n                and the mantissa to be between 0.1 (inclusive)\n                and 10 (exclusive).\n- `integral` which enforces the mantissa to always be a whole number\n             without trailing zeroes.\n\n### Examples\n\n#### EnforcedStyle: scientific (default)\n\n```ruby\n# Enforces a mantissa between 1 (inclusive) and 10 (exclusive).\n\n# bad\n10e6\n0.3e4\n11.7e5\n3.14e0\n\n# good\n1e7\n3e3\n1.17e6\n3.14\n```\n#### EnforcedStyle: engineering\n\n```ruby\n# Enforces using multiple of 3 exponents,\n# mantissa should be between 0.1 (inclusive) and 1000 (exclusive)\n\n# bad\n3.2e7\n0.1e5\n12e5\n1232e6\n\n# good\n32e6\n10e3\n1.2e6\n1.232e9\n```\n#### EnforcedStyle: integral\n\n```ruby\n# Enforces the mantissa to have no decimal part and no\n# trailing zeroes.\n\n# bad\n3.2e7\n0.1e5\n120e4\n\n# good\n32e6\n1e4\n12e5\n```\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nEnforcedStyle | `scientific` | `scientific`, `engineering`, `integral`\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#exponential-notation](https://rubystyle.guide#exponential-notation)\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nPending | Yes | No | 0.82 | -\n\nThis cop enforces consistency when using exponential notation\nfor numbers in the code (eg 1.2e4). Different styles are supported:\n- `scientific` which enforces a mantissa between 1 (inclusive)\n               and 10 (exclusive).\n- `engineering` which enforces the exponent to be a multiple of 3\n                and the mantissa to be between 0.1 (inclusive)\n                and 10 (exclusive).\n- `integral` which enforces the mantissa to always be a whole number\n             without trailing zeroes.\n\n### Examples\n\n#### EnforcedStyle: scientific (default)\n\n```ruby\n# Enforces a mantissa between 1 (inclusive) and 10 (exclusive).\n\n# bad\n10e6\n0.3e4\n11.7e5\n3.14e0\n\n# good\n1e7\n3e3\n1.17e6\n3.14\n```\n#### EnforcedStyle: engineering\n\n```ruby\n# Enforces using multiple of 3 exponents,\n# mantissa should be between 0.1 (inclusive) and 1000 (exclusive)\n\n# bad\n3.2e7\n0.1e5\n12e5\n1232e6\n\n# good\n32e6\n10e3\n1.2e6\n1.232e9\n```\n#### EnforcedStyle: integral\n\n```ruby\n# Enforces the mantissa to have no decimal part and no\n# trailing zeroes.\n\n# bad\n3.2e7\n0.1e5\n120e4\n\n# good\n32e6\n1e4\n12e5\n```\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nEnforcedStyle | `scientific` | `scientific`, `engineering`, `integral`\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#exponential-notation](https://rubystyle.guide#exponential-notation)\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Style/FloatDivision",
              "name": "Style/FloatDivision",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "For performing float division, coerce one side only.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_style/#stylefloatdivision",
                "tags": [
                  "style"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "For performing float division, coerce one side only."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "For performing float division, coerce one side only."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | No | 0.72 | -\n\nThis cop checks for division with integers coerced to floats.\nIt is recommended to either always use `fdiv` or coerce one side only.\nThis cop also provides other options for code consistency.\n\n### Examples\n\n#### EnforcedStyle: single_coerce (default)\n\n```ruby\n# bad\na.to_f / b.to_f\n\n# good\na.to_f / b\na / b.to_f\n```\n#### EnforcedStyle: left_coerce\n\n```ruby\n# bad\na / b.to_f\na.to_f / b.to_f\n\n# good\na.to_f / b\n```\n#### EnforcedStyle: right_coerce\n\n```ruby\n# bad\na.to_f / b\na.to_f / b.to_f\n\n# good\na / b.to_f\n```\n#### EnforcedStyle: fdiv\n\n```ruby\n# bad\na / b.to_f\na.to_f / b\na.to_f / b.to_f\n\n# good\na.fdiv(b)\n```\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nEnforcedStyle | `single_coerce` | `left_coerce`, `right_coerce`, `single_coerce`, `fdiv`\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#float-division](https://rubystyle.guide#float-division)\n* [https://github.com/rubocop-hq/ruby-style-guide/issues/628](https://github.com/rubocop-hq/ruby-style-guide/issues/628)\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | No | 0.72 | -\n\nThis cop checks for division with integers coerced to floats.\nIt is recommended to either always use `fdiv` or coerce one side only.\nThis cop also provides other options for code consistency.\n\n### Examples\n\n#### EnforcedStyle: single_coerce (default)\n\n```ruby\n# bad\na.to_f / b.to_f\n\n# good\na.to_f / b\na / b.to_f\n```\n#### EnforcedStyle: left_coerce\n\n```ruby\n# bad\na / b.to_f\na.to_f / b.to_f\n\n# good\na.to_f / b\n```\n#### EnforcedStyle: right_coerce\n\n```ruby\n# bad\na.to_f / b\na.to_f / b.to_f\n\n# good\na / b.to_f\n```\n#### EnforcedStyle: fdiv\n\n```ruby\n# bad\na / b.to_f\na.to_f / b\na.to_f / b.to_f\n\n# good\na.fdiv(b)\n```\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nEnforcedStyle | `single_coerce` | `left_coerce`, `right_coerce`, `single_coerce`, `fdiv`\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#float-division](https://rubystyle.guide#float-division)\n* [https://github.com/rubocop-hq/ruby-style-guide/issues/628](https://github.com/rubocop-hq/ruby-style-guide/issues/628)\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Style/For",
              "name": "Style/For",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Checks use of for or each in multiline loops.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_style/#stylefor",
                "tags": [
                  "style"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Checks use of for or each in multiline loops."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Checks use of for or each in multiline loops."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.13 | 0.59\n\nThis cop looks for uses of the `for` keyword or `each` method. The\npreferred alternative is set in the EnforcedStyle configuration\nparameter. An `each` call with a block on a single line is always\nallowed.\n\n### Examples\n\n#### EnforcedStyle: each (default)\n\n```ruby\n# bad\ndef foo\n  for n in [1, 2, 3] do\n    puts n\n  end\nend\n\n# good\ndef foo\n  [1, 2, 3].each do |n|\n    puts n\n  end\nend\n```\n#### EnforcedStyle: for\n\n```ruby\n# bad\ndef foo\n  [1, 2, 3].each do |n|\n    puts n\n  end\nend\n\n# good\ndef foo\n  for n in [1, 2, 3] do\n    puts n\n  end\nend\n```\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nEnforcedStyle | `each` | `each`, `for`\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#no-for-loops](https://rubystyle.guide#no-for-loops)\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.13 | 0.59\n\nThis cop looks for uses of the `for` keyword or `each` method. The\npreferred alternative is set in the EnforcedStyle configuration\nparameter. An `each` call with a block on a single line is always\nallowed.\n\n### Examples\n\n#### EnforcedStyle: each (default)\n\n```ruby\n# bad\ndef foo\n  for n in [1, 2, 3] do\n    puts n\n  end\nend\n\n# good\ndef foo\n  [1, 2, 3].each do |n|\n    puts n\n  end\nend\n```\n#### EnforcedStyle: for\n\n```ruby\n# bad\ndef foo\n  [1, 2, 3].each do |n|\n    puts n\n  end\nend\n\n# good\ndef foo\n  for n in [1, 2, 3] do\n    puts n\n  end\nend\n```\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nEnforcedStyle | `each` | `each`, `for`\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#no-for-loops](https://rubystyle.guide#no-for-loops)\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Style/FormatString",
              "name": "Style/FormatString",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Enforce the use of Kernel#sprintf, Kernel#format or String#%.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_style/#styleformatstring",
                "tags": [
                  "style"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Enforce the use of Kernel#sprintf, Kernel#format or String#%."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Enforce the use of Kernel#sprintf, Kernel#format or String#%."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.19 | 0.49\n\nThis cop enforces the use of a single string formatting utility.\nValid options include Kernel#format, Kernel#sprintf and String#%.\n\nThe detection of String#% cannot be implemented in a reliable\nmanner for all cases, so only two scenarios are considered -\nif the first argument is a string literal and if the second\nargument is an array literal.\n\n### Examples\n\n#### EnforcedStyle: format (default)\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nputs sprintf('%10s', 'hoge')\nputs '%10s' % 'hoge'\n\n# good\nputs format('%10s', 'hoge')\n```\n#### EnforcedStyle: sprintf\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nputs format('%10s', 'hoge')\nputs '%10s' % 'hoge'\n\n# good\nputs sprintf('%10s', 'hoge')\n```\n#### EnforcedStyle: percent\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nputs format('%10s', 'hoge')\nputs sprintf('%10s', 'hoge')\n\n# good\nputs '%10s' % 'hoge'\n```\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nEnforcedStyle | `format` | `format`, `sprintf`, `percent`\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#sprintf](https://rubystyle.guide#sprintf)\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.19 | 0.49\n\nThis cop enforces the use of a single string formatting utility.\nValid options include Kernel#format, Kernel#sprintf and String#%.\n\nThe detection of String#% cannot be implemented in a reliable\nmanner for all cases, so only two scenarios are considered -\nif the first argument is a string literal and if the second\nargument is an array literal.\n\n### Examples\n\n#### EnforcedStyle: format (default)\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nputs sprintf('%10s', 'hoge')\nputs '%10s' % 'hoge'\n\n# good\nputs format('%10s', 'hoge')\n```\n#### EnforcedStyle: sprintf\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nputs format('%10s', 'hoge')\nputs '%10s' % 'hoge'\n\n# good\nputs sprintf('%10s', 'hoge')\n```\n#### EnforcedStyle: percent\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nputs format('%10s', 'hoge')\nputs sprintf('%10s', 'hoge')\n\n# good\nputs '%10s' % 'hoge'\n```\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nEnforcedStyle | `format` | `format`, `sprintf`, `percent`\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#sprintf](https://rubystyle.guide#sprintf)\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Style/FormatStringToken",
              "name": "Style/FormatStringToken",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Use a consistent style for format string tokens.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_style/#styleformatstringtoken",
                "tags": [
                  "style"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Use a consistent style for format string tokens."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Use a consistent style for format string tokens."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | No | 0.49 | 0.75\n\nUse a consistent style for named format string tokens.\n\n**Note:**\n`unannotated` style cop only works for strings\nwhich are passed as arguments to those methods:\n`printf`, `sprintf`, `format`, `%`.\nThe reason is that *unannotated* format is very similar\nto encoded URLs or Date/Time formatting strings.\n\n### Examples\n\n#### EnforcedStyle: annotated (default)\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nformat('%{greeting}', greeting: 'Hello')\nformat('%s', 'Hello')\n\n# good\nformat('%<greeting>s', greeting: 'Hello')\n```\n#### EnforcedStyle: template\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nformat('%<greeting>s', greeting: 'Hello')\nformat('%s', 'Hello')\n\n# good\nformat('%{greeting}', greeting: 'Hello')\n```\n#### EnforcedStyle: unannotated\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nformat('%<greeting>s', greeting: 'Hello')\nformat('%{greeting}', 'Hello')\n\n# good\nformat('%s', 'Hello')\n```\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nEnforcedStyle | `annotated` | `annotated`, `template`, `unannotated`\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | No | 0.49 | 0.75\n\nUse a consistent style for named format string tokens.\n\n**Note:**\n`unannotated` style cop only works for strings\nwhich are passed as arguments to those methods:\n`printf`, `sprintf`, `format`, `%`.\nThe reason is that *unannotated* format is very similar\nto encoded URLs or Date/Time formatting strings.\n\n### Examples\n\n#### EnforcedStyle: annotated (default)\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nformat('%{greeting}', greeting: 'Hello')\nformat('%s', 'Hello')\n\n# good\nformat('%<greeting>s', greeting: 'Hello')\n```\n#### EnforcedStyle: template\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nformat('%<greeting>s', greeting: 'Hello')\nformat('%s', 'Hello')\n\n# good\nformat('%{greeting}', greeting: 'Hello')\n```\n#### EnforcedStyle: unannotated\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nformat('%<greeting>s', greeting: 'Hello')\nformat('%{greeting}', 'Hello')\n\n# good\nformat('%s', 'Hello')\n```\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nEnforcedStyle | `annotated` | `annotated`, `template`, `unannotated`\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Style/FrozenStringLiteralComment",
              "name": "Style/FrozenStringLiteralComment",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Add the frozen_string_literal comment to the top of files to help transition to frozen string literals by default.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_style/#stylefrozenstringliteralcomment",
                "tags": [
                  "style"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Add the frozen_string_literal comment to the top of files to help transition to frozen string literals by default."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Add the frozen_string_literal comment to the top of files to help transition to frozen string literals by default."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | No | Yes (Unsafe) | 0.36 | 0.79\n\nThis cop is designed to help you transition from mutable string literals\nto frozen string literals.\nIt will add the comment `# frozen_string_literal: true` to the top of\nfiles to enable frozen string literals. Frozen string literals may be\ndefault in future Ruby. The comment will be added below a shebang and\nencoding comment. The frozen string literal comment is only valid in\nRuby 2.3+.\n\nNote that the cop will ignore files where the comment exists but is set\nto `false` instead of `true`.\n\n### Examples\n\n#### EnforcedStyle: always (default)\n\n```ruby\n# The `always` style will always add the frozen string literal comment\n# to a file, regardless of the Ruby version or if `freeze` or `<<` are\n# called on a string literal.\n# bad\nmodule Bar\n  # ...\nend\n\n# good\n# frozen_string_literal: true\n\nmodule Bar\n  # ...\nend\n\n# good\n# frozen_string_literal: false\n\nmodule Bar\n  # ...\nend\n```\n#### EnforcedStyle: never\n\n```ruby\n# The `never` will enforce that the frozen string literal comment does\n# not exist in a file.\n# bad\n# frozen_string_literal: true\n\nmodule Baz\n  # ...\nend\n\n# good\nmodule Baz\n  # ...\nend\n```\n#### EnforcedStyle: always_true\n\n```ruby\n# The `always_true` style enforces that the frozen string literal\n# comment is set to `true`. This is a stricter option than `always`\n# and forces projects to use frozen string literals.\n# bad\n# frozen_string_literal: false\n\nmodule Baz\n  # ...\nend\n\n# bad\nmodule Baz\n  # ...\nend\n\n# good\n# frozen_string_literal: true\n\nmodule Bar\n  # ...\nend\n```\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nEnforcedStyle | `always` | `always`, `always_true`, `never`\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | No | Yes (Unsafe) | 0.36 | 0.79\n\nThis cop is designed to help you transition from mutable string literals\nto frozen string literals.\nIt will add the comment `# frozen_string_literal: true` to the top of\nfiles to enable frozen string literals. Frozen string literals may be\ndefault in future Ruby. The comment will be added below a shebang and\nencoding comment. The frozen string literal comment is only valid in\nRuby 2.3+.\n\nNote that the cop will ignore files where the comment exists but is set\nto `false` instead of `true`.\n\n### Examples\n\n#### EnforcedStyle: always (default)\n\n```ruby\n# The `always` style will always add the frozen string literal comment\n# to a file, regardless of the Ruby version or if `freeze` or `<<` are\n# called on a string literal.\n# bad\nmodule Bar\n  # ...\nend\n\n# good\n# frozen_string_literal: true\n\nmodule Bar\n  # ...\nend\n\n# good\n# frozen_string_literal: false\n\nmodule Bar\n  # ...\nend\n```\n#### EnforcedStyle: never\n\n```ruby\n# The `never` will enforce that the frozen string literal comment does\n# not exist in a file.\n# bad\n# frozen_string_literal: true\n\nmodule Baz\n  # ...\nend\n\n# good\nmodule Baz\n  # ...\nend\n```\n#### EnforcedStyle: always_true\n\n```ruby\n# The `always_true` style enforces that the frozen string literal\n# comment is set to `true`. This is a stricter option than `always`\n# and forces projects to use frozen string literals.\n# bad\n# frozen_string_literal: false\n\nmodule Baz\n  # ...\nend\n\n# bad\nmodule Baz\n  # ...\nend\n\n# good\n# frozen_string_literal: true\n\nmodule Bar\n  # ...\nend\n```\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nEnforcedStyle | `always` | `always`, `always_true`, `never`\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Style/GlobalVars",
              "name": "Style/GlobalVars",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Do not introduce global variables.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_style/#styleglobalvars",
                "tags": [
                  "style"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Do not introduce global variables."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Do not introduce global variables."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | No | 0.13 | -\n\nThis cop looks for uses of global variables.\nIt does not report offenses for built-in global variables.\nBuilt-in global variables are allowed by default. Additionally\nusers can allow additional variables via the AllowedVariables option.\n\nNote that backreferences like $1, $2, etc are not global variables.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\n$foo = 2\nbar = $foo + 5\n\n# good\nFOO = 2\nfoo = 2\n$stdin.read\n```\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nAllowedVariables | `[]` | Array\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#instance-vars](https://rubystyle.guide#instance-vars)\n* [https://www.zenspider.com/ruby/quickref.html](https://www.zenspider.com/ruby/quickref.html)\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | No | 0.13 | -\n\nThis cop looks for uses of global variables.\nIt does not report offenses for built-in global variables.\nBuilt-in global variables are allowed by default. Additionally\nusers can allow additional variables via the AllowedVariables option.\n\nNote that backreferences like $1, $2, etc are not global variables.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\n$foo = 2\nbar = $foo + 5\n\n# good\nFOO = 2\nfoo = 2\n$stdin.read\n```\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nAllowedVariables | `[]` | Array\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#instance-vars](https://rubystyle.guide#instance-vars)\n* [https://www.zenspider.com/ruby/quickref.html](https://www.zenspider.com/ruby/quickref.html)\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Style/GuardClause",
              "name": "Style/GuardClause",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Check for conditionals that can be replaced with guard clauses.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_style/#styleguardclause",
                "tags": [
                  "style"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Check for conditionals that can be replaced with guard clauses."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Check for conditionals that can be replaced with guard clauses."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | No | 0.20 | 0.22\n\nUse a guard clause instead of wrapping the code inside a conditional\nexpression\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\ndef test\n  if something\n    work\n  end\nend\n\n# good\ndef test\n  return unless something\n  work\nend\n\n# also good\ndef test\n  work if something\nend\n\n# bad\nif something\n  raise 'exception'\nelse\n  ok\nend\n\n# good\nraise 'exception' if something\nok\n```\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nMinBodyLength | `1` | Integer\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#no-nested-conditionals](https://rubystyle.guide#no-nested-conditionals)\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | No | 0.20 | 0.22\n\nUse a guard clause instead of wrapping the code inside a conditional\nexpression\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\ndef test\n  if something\n    work\n  end\nend\n\n# good\ndef test\n  return unless something\n  work\nend\n\n# also good\ndef test\n  work if something\nend\n\n# bad\nif something\n  raise 'exception'\nelse\n  ok\nend\n\n# good\nraise 'exception' if something\nok\n```\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nMinBodyLength | `1` | Integer\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#no-nested-conditionals](https://rubystyle.guide#no-nested-conditionals)\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Style/HashEachMethods",
              "name": "Style/HashEachMethods",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Use Hash#each_key and Hash#each_value.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_style/#stylehasheachmethods",
                "tags": [
                  "style"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Use Hash#each_key and Hash#each_value."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Use Hash#each_key and Hash#each_value."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nPending | No | Yes (Unsafe) | 0.80 | -\n\nThis cop checks for uses of `each_key` and `each_value` Hash methods.\n\nNote: If you have an array of two-element arrays, you can put\n  parentheses around the block arguments to indicate that you're not\n  working with a hash, and suppress RuboCop offenses.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nhash.keys.each { |k| p k }\nhash.values.each { |v| p v }\n\n# good\nhash.each_key { |k| p k }\nhash.each_value { |v| p v }\n```\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#hash-each](https://rubystyle.guide#hash-each)\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nPending | No | Yes (Unsafe) | 0.80 | -\n\nThis cop checks for uses of `each_key` and `each_value` Hash methods.\n\nNote: If you have an array of two-element arrays, you can put\n  parentheses around the block arguments to indicate that you're not\n  working with a hash, and suppress RuboCop offenses.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nhash.keys.each { |k| p k }\nhash.values.each { |v| p v }\n\n# good\nhash.each_key { |k| p k }\nhash.each_value { |v| p v }\n```\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#hash-each](https://rubystyle.guide#hash-each)\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Style/HashSyntax",
              "name": "Style/HashSyntax",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Prefer Ruby 1.9 hash syntax { a: 1, b: 2 } over 1.8 syntax { :a => 1, :b => 2 }.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_style/#stylehashsyntax",
                "tags": [
                  "style"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Prefer Ruby 1.9 hash syntax { a: 1, b: 2 } over 1.8 syntax { :a => 1, :b => 2 }."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Prefer Ruby 1.9 hash syntax { a: 1, b: 2 } over 1.8 syntax { :a => 1, :b => 2 }."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.9 | 0.43\n\nThis cop checks hash literal syntax.\n\nIt can enforce either the use of the class hash rocket syntax or\nthe use of the newer Ruby 1.9 syntax (when applicable).\n\nA separate offense is registered for each problematic pair.\n\nThe supported styles are:\n\n* ruby19 - forces use of the 1.9 syntax (e.g. `{a: 1}`) when hashes have\n  all symbols for keys\n* hash_rockets - forces use of hash rockets for all hashes\n* no_mixed_keys - simply checks for hashes with mixed syntaxes\n* ruby19_no_mixed_keys - forces use of ruby 1.9 syntax and forbids mixed\n  syntax hashes\n\n### Examples\n\n#### EnforcedStyle: ruby19 (default)\n\n```ruby\n# bad\n{:a => 2}\n{b: 1, :c => 2}\n\n# good\n{a: 2, b: 1}\n{:c => 2, 'd' => 2} # acceptable since 'd' isn't a symbol\n{d: 1, 'e' => 2} # technically not forbidden\n```\n#### EnforcedStyle: hash_rockets\n\n```ruby\n# bad\n{a: 1, b: 2}\n{c: 1, 'd' => 5}\n\n# good\n{:a => 1, :b => 2}\n```\n#### EnforcedStyle: no_mixed_keys\n\n```ruby\n# bad\n{:a => 1, b: 2}\n{c: 1, 'd' => 2}\n\n# good\n{:a => 1, :b => 2}\n{c: 1, d: 2}\n```\n#### EnforcedStyle: ruby19_no_mixed_keys\n\n```ruby\n# bad\n{:a => 1, :b => 2}\n{c: 2, 'd' => 3} # should just use hash rockets\n\n# good\n{a: 1, b: 2}\n{:c => 3, 'd' => 4}\n```\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nEnforcedStyle | `ruby19` | `ruby19`, `hash_rockets`, `no_mixed_keys`, `ruby19_no_mixed_keys`\nUseHashRocketsWithSymbolValues | `false` | Boolean\nPreferHashRocketsForNonAlnumEndingSymbols | `false` | Boolean\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#hash-literals](https://rubystyle.guide#hash-literals)\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.9 | 0.43\n\nThis cop checks hash literal syntax.\n\nIt can enforce either the use of the class hash rocket syntax or\nthe use of the newer Ruby 1.9 syntax (when applicable).\n\nA separate offense is registered for each problematic pair.\n\nThe supported styles are:\n\n* ruby19 - forces use of the 1.9 syntax (e.g. `{a: 1}`) when hashes have\n  all symbols for keys\n* hash_rockets - forces use of hash rockets for all hashes\n* no_mixed_keys - simply checks for hashes with mixed syntaxes\n* ruby19_no_mixed_keys - forces use of ruby 1.9 syntax and forbids mixed\n  syntax hashes\n\n### Examples\n\n#### EnforcedStyle: ruby19 (default)\n\n```ruby\n# bad\n{:a => 2}\n{b: 1, :c => 2}\n\n# good\n{a: 2, b: 1}\n{:c => 2, 'd' => 2} # acceptable since 'd' isn't a symbol\n{d: 1, 'e' => 2} # technically not forbidden\n```\n#### EnforcedStyle: hash_rockets\n\n```ruby\n# bad\n{a: 1, b: 2}\n{c: 1, 'd' => 5}\n\n# good\n{:a => 1, :b => 2}\n```\n#### EnforcedStyle: no_mixed_keys\n\n```ruby\n# bad\n{:a => 1, b: 2}\n{c: 1, 'd' => 2}\n\n# good\n{:a => 1, :b => 2}\n{c: 1, d: 2}\n```\n#### EnforcedStyle: ruby19_no_mixed_keys\n\n```ruby\n# bad\n{:a => 1, :b => 2}\n{c: 2, 'd' => 3} # should just use hash rockets\n\n# good\n{a: 1, b: 2}\n{:c => 3, 'd' => 4}\n```\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nEnforcedStyle | `ruby19` | `ruby19`, `hash_rockets`, `no_mixed_keys`, `ruby19_no_mixed_keys`\nUseHashRocketsWithSymbolValues | `false` | Boolean\nPreferHashRocketsForNonAlnumEndingSymbols | `false` | Boolean\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#hash-literals](https://rubystyle.guide#hash-literals)\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Style/HashTransformKeys",
              "name": "Style/HashTransformKeys",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Prefer `transform_keys` over `each_with_object` and `map`.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_style/#stylehashtransformkeys",
                "tags": [
                  "style"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Prefer `transform_keys` over `each_with_object` and `map`."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Prefer `transform_keys` over `each_with_object` and `map`."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nPending | No | Yes (Unsafe) | 0.80 | -\n\nThis cop looks for uses of `_.each_with_object({}) {...}`,\n`_.map {...}.to_h`, and `Hash[_.map {...}]` that are actually just\ntransforming the keys of a hash, and tries to use a simpler & faster\ncall to `transform_keys` instead.\n\nThis can produce false positives if we are transforming an enumerable\nof key-value-like pairs that isn't actually a hash, e.g.:\n`[[k1, v1], [k2, v2], ...]`\n\nThis cop should only be enabled on Ruby version 2.5 or newer\n(`transform_keys` was added in Ruby 2.5.)\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\n{a: 1, b: 2}.each_with_object({}) { |(k, v), h| h[foo(k)] = v }\n{a: 1, b: 2}.map { |k, v| [k.to_s, v] }\n\n# good\n{a: 1, b: 2}.transform_keys { |k| foo(k) }\n{a: 1, b: 2}.transform_keys { |k| k.to_s }\n```\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nPending | No | Yes (Unsafe) | 0.80 | -\n\nThis cop looks for uses of `_.each_with_object({}) {...}`,\n`_.map {...}.to_h`, and `Hash[_.map {...}]` that are actually just\ntransforming the keys of a hash, and tries to use a simpler & faster\ncall to `transform_keys` instead.\n\nThis can produce false positives if we are transforming an enumerable\nof key-value-like pairs that isn't actually a hash, e.g.:\n`[[k1, v1], [k2, v2], ...]`\n\nThis cop should only be enabled on Ruby version 2.5 or newer\n(`transform_keys` was added in Ruby 2.5.)\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\n{a: 1, b: 2}.each_with_object({}) { |(k, v), h| h[foo(k)] = v }\n{a: 1, b: 2}.map { |k, v| [k.to_s, v] }\n\n# good\n{a: 1, b: 2}.transform_keys { |k| foo(k) }\n{a: 1, b: 2}.transform_keys { |k| k.to_s }\n```\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Style/HashTransformValues",
              "name": "Style/HashTransformValues",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Prefer `transform_values` over `each_with_object` and `map`.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_style/#stylehashtransformvalues",
                "tags": [
                  "style"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Prefer `transform_values` over `each_with_object` and `map`."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Prefer `transform_values` over `each_with_object` and `map`."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nPending | No | Yes (Unsafe) | 0.80 | -\n\nThis cop looks for uses of `_.each_with_object({}) {...}`,\n`_.map {...}.to_h`, and `Hash[_.map {...}]` that are actually just\ntransforming the values of a hash, and tries to use a simpler & faster\ncall to `transform_values` instead.\n\nThis can produce false positives if we are transforming an enumerable\nof key-value-like pairs that isn't actually a hash, e.g.:\n`[[k1, v1], [k2, v2], ...]`\n\nThis cop should only be enabled on Ruby version 2.4 or newer\n(`transform_values` was added in Ruby 2.4.)\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\n{a: 1, b: 2}.each_with_object({}) { |(k, v), h| h[k] = foo(v) }\n{a: 1, b: 2}.map { |k, v| [k, v * v] }\n\n# good\n{a: 1, b: 2}.transform_values { |v| foo(v) }\n{a: 1, b: 2}.transform_values { |v| v * v }\n```\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nPending | No | Yes (Unsafe) | 0.80 | -\n\nThis cop looks for uses of `_.each_with_object({}) {...}`,\n`_.map {...}.to_h`, and `Hash[_.map {...}]` that are actually just\ntransforming the values of a hash, and tries to use a simpler & faster\ncall to `transform_values` instead.\n\nThis can produce false positives if we are transforming an enumerable\nof key-value-like pairs that isn't actually a hash, e.g.:\n`[[k1, v1], [k2, v2], ...]`\n\nThis cop should only be enabled on Ruby version 2.4 or newer\n(`transform_values` was added in Ruby 2.4.)\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\n{a: 1, b: 2}.each_with_object({}) { |(k, v), h| h[k] = foo(v) }\n{a: 1, b: 2}.map { |k, v| [k, v * v] }\n\n# good\n{a: 1, b: 2}.transform_values { |v| foo(v) }\n{a: 1, b: 2}.transform_values { |v| v * v }\n```\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Style/IdenticalConditionalBranches",
              "name": "Style/IdenticalConditionalBranches",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Checks that conditional statements do not have an identical line at the end of each branch, which can validly be moved out of the conditional.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_style/#styleidenticalconditionalbranches",
                "tags": [
                  "style"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Checks that conditional statements do not have an identical line at the end of each branch, which can validly be moved out of the conditional."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Checks that conditional statements do not have an identical line at the end of each branch, which can validly be moved out of the conditional."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | No | 0.36 | -\n\nThis cop checks for identical lines at the beginning or end of\neach branch of a conditional statement.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nif condition\n  do_x\n  do_z\nelse\n  do_y\n  do_z\nend\n\n# good\nif condition\n  do_x\nelse\n  do_y\nend\ndo_z\n\n# bad\nif condition\n  do_z\n  do_x\nelse\n  do_z\n  do_y\nend\n\n# good\ndo_z\nif condition\n  do_x\nelse\n  do_y\nend\n\n# bad\ncase foo\nwhen 1\n  do_x\nwhen 2\n  do_x\nelse\n  do_x\nend\n\n# good\ncase foo\nwhen 1\n  do_x\n  do_y\nwhen 2\n  # nothing\nelse\n  do_x\n  do_z\nend\n```\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | No | 0.36 | -\n\nThis cop checks for identical lines at the beginning or end of\neach branch of a conditional statement.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nif condition\n  do_x\n  do_z\nelse\n  do_y\n  do_z\nend\n\n# good\nif condition\n  do_x\nelse\n  do_y\nend\ndo_z\n\n# bad\nif condition\n  do_z\n  do_x\nelse\n  do_z\n  do_y\nend\n\n# good\ndo_z\nif condition\n  do_x\nelse\n  do_y\nend\n\n# bad\ncase foo\nwhen 1\n  do_x\nwhen 2\n  do_x\nelse\n  do_x\nend\n\n# good\ncase foo\nwhen 1\n  do_x\n  do_y\nwhen 2\n  # nothing\nelse\n  do_x\n  do_z\nend\n```\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Style/IfInsideElse",
              "name": "Style/IfInsideElse",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Finds if nodes inside else, which can be converted to elsif.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_style/#styleifinsideelse",
                "tags": [
                  "style"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Finds if nodes inside else, which can be converted to elsif."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Finds if nodes inside else, which can be converted to elsif."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | No | 0.36 | -\n\nIf the `else` branch of a conditional consists solely of an `if` node,\nit can be combined with the `else` to become an `elsif`.\nThis helps to keep the nesting level from getting too deep.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nif condition_a\n  action_a\nelse\n  if condition_b\n    action_b\n  else\n    action_c\n  end\nend\n\n# good\nif condition_a\n  action_a\nelsif condition_b\n  action_b\nelse\n  action_c\nend\n```\n#### AllowIfModifier: false (default)\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nif condition_a\n  action_a\nelse\n  action_b if condition_b\nend\n\n# good\nif condition_a\n  action_a\nelsif condition_b\n  action_b\nend\n```\n#### AllowIfModifier: true\n\n```ruby\n# good\nif condition_a\n  action_a\nelse\n  action_b if condition_b\nend\n\n# good\nif condition_a\n  action_a\nelsif condition_b\n  action_b\nend\n```\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nAllowIfModifier | `false` | Boolean\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | No | 0.36 | -\n\nIf the `else` branch of a conditional consists solely of an `if` node,\nit can be combined with the `else` to become an `elsif`.\nThis helps to keep the nesting level from getting too deep.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nif condition_a\n  action_a\nelse\n  if condition_b\n    action_b\n  else\n    action_c\n  end\nend\n\n# good\nif condition_a\n  action_a\nelsif condition_b\n  action_b\nelse\n  action_c\nend\n```\n#### AllowIfModifier: false (default)\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nif condition_a\n  action_a\nelse\n  action_b if condition_b\nend\n\n# good\nif condition_a\n  action_a\nelsif condition_b\n  action_b\nend\n```\n#### AllowIfModifier: true\n\n```ruby\n# good\nif condition_a\n  action_a\nelse\n  action_b if condition_b\nend\n\n# good\nif condition_a\n  action_a\nelsif condition_b\n  action_b\nend\n```\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nAllowIfModifier | `false` | Boolean\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Style/IfUnlessModifier",
              "name": "Style/IfUnlessModifier",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Favor modifier if/unless usage when you have a single-line body.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_style/#styleifunlessmodifier",
                "tags": [
                  "style"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Favor modifier if/unless usage when you have a single-line body."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Favor modifier if/unless usage when you have a single-line body."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.9 | 0.30\n\nChecks for `if` and `unless` statements that would fit on one line if\nwritten as modifier `if`/`unless`. The cop also checks for modifier\n`if`/`unless` lines that exceed the maximum line length.\n\nThe maximum line length is configured in the `Layout/LineLength`\ncop. The tab size is configured in the `IndentationWidth` of the\n`Layout/IndentationStyle` cop.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nif condition\n  do_stuff(bar)\nend\n\nunless qux.empty?\n  Foo.do_something\nend\n\ndo_something_in_a_method_with_a_long_name(arg) if long_condition\n\n# good\ndo_stuff(bar) if condition\nFoo.do_something unless qux.empty?\n\nif long_condition\n  do_something_in_a_method_with_a_long_name(arg)\nend\n```\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#if-as-a-modifier](https://rubystyle.guide#if-as-a-modifier)\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.9 | 0.30\n\nChecks for `if` and `unless` statements that would fit on one line if\nwritten as modifier `if`/`unless`. The cop also checks for modifier\n`if`/`unless` lines that exceed the maximum line length.\n\nThe maximum line length is configured in the `Layout/LineLength`\ncop. The tab size is configured in the `IndentationWidth` of the\n`Layout/IndentationStyle` cop.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nif condition\n  do_stuff(bar)\nend\n\nunless qux.empty?\n  Foo.do_something\nend\n\ndo_something_in_a_method_with_a_long_name(arg) if long_condition\n\n# good\ndo_stuff(bar) if condition\nFoo.do_something unless qux.empty?\n\nif long_condition\n  do_something_in_a_method_with_a_long_name(arg)\nend\n```\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#if-as-a-modifier](https://rubystyle.guide#if-as-a-modifier)\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Style/IfUnlessModifierOfIfUnless",
              "name": "Style/IfUnlessModifierOfIfUnless",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Avoid modifier if/unless usage on conditionals.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_style/#styleifunlessmodifierofifunless",
                "tags": [
                  "style"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Avoid modifier if/unless usage on conditionals."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Avoid modifier if/unless usage on conditionals."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | No | 0.39 | -\n\nChecks for if and unless statements used as modifiers of other if or\nunless statements.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\ntired? ? 'stop' : 'go faster' if running?\n\n# bad\nif tired?\n  \"please stop\"\nelse\n  \"keep going\"\nend if running?\n\n# good\nif running?\n  tired? ? 'stop' : 'go faster'\nend\n```\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | No | 0.39 | -\n\nChecks for if and unless statements used as modifiers of other if or\nunless statements.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\ntired? ? 'stop' : 'go faster' if running?\n\n# bad\nif tired?\n  \"please stop\"\nelse\n  \"keep going\"\nend if running?\n\n# good\nif running?\n  tired? ? 'stop' : 'go faster'\nend\n```\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Style/IfWithSemicolon",
              "name": "Style/IfWithSemicolon",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Do not use if x; .... Use the ternary operator instead.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_style/#styleifwithsemicolon",
                "tags": [
                  "style"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Do not use if x; .... Use the ternary operator instead."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Do not use if x; .... Use the ternary operator instead."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | No | 0.9 | -\n\nChecks for uses of semicolon in if statements.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nresult = if some_condition; something else another_thing end\n\n# good\nresult = some_condition ? something : another_thing\n```\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#no-semicolon-ifs](https://rubystyle.guide#no-semicolon-ifs)\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | No | 0.9 | -\n\nChecks for uses of semicolon in if statements.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nresult = if some_condition; something else another_thing end\n\n# good\nresult = some_condition ? something : another_thing\n```\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#no-semicolon-ifs](https://rubystyle.guide#no-semicolon-ifs)\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Style/ImplicitRuntimeError",
              "name": "Style/ImplicitRuntimeError",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Use `raise` or `fail` with an explicit exception class and message, rather than just a message.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_style/#styleimplicitruntimeerror",
                "tags": [
                  "style"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Use `raise` or `fail` with an explicit exception class and message, rather than just a message."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Use `raise` or `fail` with an explicit exception class and message, rather than just a message."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nDisabled | Yes | No | 0.41 | -\n\nThis cop checks for `raise` or `fail` statements which do not specify an\nexplicit exception class. (This raises a `RuntimeError`. Some projects\nmight prefer to use exception classes which more precisely identify the\nnature of the error.)\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nraise 'Error message here'\n\n# good\nraise ArgumentError, 'Error message here'\n```\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nDisabled | Yes | No | 0.41 | -\n\nThis cop checks for `raise` or `fail` statements which do not specify an\nexplicit exception class. (This raises a `RuntimeError`. Some projects\nmight prefer to use exception classes which more precisely identify the\nnature of the error.)\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nraise 'Error message here'\n\n# good\nraise ArgumentError, 'Error message here'\n```\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Style/InfiniteLoop",
              "name": "Style/InfiniteLoop",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Use Kernel#loop for infinite loops.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_style/#styleinfiniteloop",
                "tags": [
                  "style"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Use Kernel#loop for infinite loops."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Use Kernel#loop for infinite loops."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.26 | 0.61\n\nUse `Kernel#loop` for infinite loops.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nwhile true\n  work\nend\n\n# good\nloop do\n  work\nend\n```\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#infinite-loop](https://rubystyle.guide#infinite-loop)\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.26 | 0.61\n\nUse `Kernel#loop` for infinite loops.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nwhile true\n  work\nend\n\n# good\nloop do\n  work\nend\n```\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#infinite-loop](https://rubystyle.guide#infinite-loop)\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Style/InlineComment",
              "name": "Style/InlineComment",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Avoid trailing inline comments.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_style/#styleinlinecomment",
                "tags": [
                  "style"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Avoid trailing inline comments."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Avoid trailing inline comments."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nDisabled | Yes | No | 0.23 | -\n\nThis cop checks for trailing inline comments.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# good\nfoo.each do |f|\n  # Standalone comment\n  f.bar\nend\n\n# bad\nfoo.each do |f|\n  f.bar # Trailing inline comment\nend\n```\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nDisabled | Yes | No | 0.23 | -\n\nThis cop checks for trailing inline comments.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# good\nfoo.each do |f|\n  # Standalone comment\n  f.bar\nend\n\n# bad\nfoo.each do |f|\n  f.bar # Trailing inline comment\nend\n```\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Style/InverseMethods",
              "name": "Style/InverseMethods",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Use the inverse method instead of `!.method` if an inverse method is defined.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_style/#styleinversemethods",
                "tags": [
                  "style"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Use the inverse method instead of `!.method` if an inverse method is defined."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Use the inverse method instead of `!.method` if an inverse method is defined."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | No | Yes (Unsafe) | 0.48 | -\n\nThis cop check for usages of not (`not` or `!`) called on a method\nwhen an inverse of that method can be used instead.\nMethods that can be inverted by a not (`not` or `!`) should be defined\nin `InverseMethods`\nMethods that are inverted by inverting the return\nof the block that is passed to the method should be defined in\n`InverseBlocks`\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\n!foo.none?\n!foo.any? { |f| f.even? }\n!foo.blank?\n!(foo == bar)\nfoo.select { |f| !f.even? }\nfoo.reject { |f| f != 7 }\n\n# good\nfoo.none?\nfoo.blank?\nfoo.any? { |f| f.even? }\nfoo != bar\nfoo == bar\n!!('foo' =~ /^\\w+$/)\n!(foo.class < Numeric) # Checking class hierarchy is allowed\n# Blocks with guard clauses are ignored:\nfoo.select do |f|\n  next if f.zero?\n  f != 1\nend\n```\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nInverseMethods | `{:any?=>:none?, :even?=>:odd?, :===>:!=, :=~=>:!~, :<=>:>=, :>=>:<=}` | \nInverseBlocks | `{:select=>:reject, :select!=>:reject!}` | \n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | No | Yes (Unsafe) | 0.48 | -\n\nThis cop check for usages of not (`not` or `!`) called on a method\nwhen an inverse of that method can be used instead.\nMethods that can be inverted by a not (`not` or `!`) should be defined\nin `InverseMethods`\nMethods that are inverted by inverting the return\nof the block that is passed to the method should be defined in\n`InverseBlocks`\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\n!foo.none?\n!foo.any? { |f| f.even? }\n!foo.blank?\n!(foo == bar)\nfoo.select { |f| !f.even? }\nfoo.reject { |f| f != 7 }\n\n# good\nfoo.none?\nfoo.blank?\nfoo.any? { |f| f.even? }\nfoo != bar\nfoo == bar\n!!('foo' =~ /^\\w+$/)\n!(foo.class < Numeric) # Checking class hierarchy is allowed\n# Blocks with guard clauses are ignored:\nfoo.select do |f|\n  next if f.zero?\n  f != 1\nend\n```\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nInverseMethods | `{:any?=>:none?, :even?=>:odd?, :===>:!=, :=~=>:!~, :<=>:>=, :>=>:<=}` | \nInverseBlocks | `{:select=>:reject, :select!=>:reject!}` | \n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Style/IpAddresses",
              "name": "Style/IpAddresses",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Don't include literal IP addresses in code.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_style/#styleipaddresses",
                "tags": [
                  "style"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Don't include literal IP addresses in code."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Don't include literal IP addresses in code."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nDisabled | Yes | No | 0.58 | 0.77\n\nThis cop checks for hardcoded IP addresses, which can make code\nbrittle. IP addresses are likely to need to be changed when code\nis deployed to a different server or environment, which may break\na deployment if forgotten. Prefer setting IP addresses in ENV or\nother configuration.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nip_address = '127.59.241.29'\n\n# good\nip_address = ENV['DEPLOYMENT_IP_ADDRESS']\n```\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nAllowedAddresses | `::` | Array\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nDisabled | Yes | No | 0.58 | 0.77\n\nThis cop checks for hardcoded IP addresses, which can make code\nbrittle. IP addresses are likely to need to be changed when code\nis deployed to a different server or environment, which may break\na deployment if forgotten. Prefer setting IP addresses in ENV or\nother configuration.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nip_address = '127.59.241.29'\n\n# good\nip_address = ENV['DEPLOYMENT_IP_ADDRESS']\n```\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nAllowedAddresses | `::` | Array\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Style/Lambda",
              "name": "Style/Lambda",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Use the new lambda literal syntax for single-line blocks.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_style/#stylelambda",
                "tags": [
                  "style"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Use the new lambda literal syntax for single-line blocks."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Use the new lambda literal syntax for single-line blocks."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.9 | 0.40\n\nThis cop (by default) checks for uses of the lambda literal syntax for\nsingle line lambdas, and the method call syntax for multiline lambdas.\nIt is configurable to enforce one of the styles for both single line\nand multiline lambdas as well.\n\n### Examples\n\n#### EnforcedStyle: line_count_dependent (default)\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nf = lambda { |x| x }\nf = ->(x) do\n      x\n    end\n\n# good\nf = ->(x) { x }\nf = lambda do |x|\n      x\n    end\n```\n#### EnforcedStyle: lambda\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nf = ->(x) { x }\nf = ->(x) do\n      x\n    end\n\n# good\nf = lambda { |x| x }\nf = lambda do |x|\n      x\n    end\n```\n#### EnforcedStyle: literal\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nf = lambda { |x| x }\nf = lambda do |x|\n      x\n    end\n\n# good\nf = ->(x) { x }\nf = ->(x) do\n      x\n    end\n```\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nEnforcedStyle | `line_count_dependent` | `line_count_dependent`, `lambda`, `literal`\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#lambda-multi-line](https://rubystyle.guide#lambda-multi-line)\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.9 | 0.40\n\nThis cop (by default) checks for uses of the lambda literal syntax for\nsingle line lambdas, and the method call syntax for multiline lambdas.\nIt is configurable to enforce one of the styles for both single line\nand multiline lambdas as well.\n\n### Examples\n\n#### EnforcedStyle: line_count_dependent (default)\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nf = lambda { |x| x }\nf = ->(x) do\n      x\n    end\n\n# good\nf = ->(x) { x }\nf = lambda do |x|\n      x\n    end\n```\n#### EnforcedStyle: lambda\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nf = ->(x) { x }\nf = ->(x) do\n      x\n    end\n\n# good\nf = lambda { |x| x }\nf = lambda do |x|\n      x\n    end\n```\n#### EnforcedStyle: literal\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nf = lambda { |x| x }\nf = lambda do |x|\n      x\n    end\n\n# good\nf = ->(x) { x }\nf = ->(x) do\n      x\n    end\n```\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nEnforcedStyle | `line_count_dependent` | `line_count_dependent`, `lambda`, `literal`\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#lambda-multi-line](https://rubystyle.guide#lambda-multi-line)\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Style/LambdaCall",
              "name": "Style/LambdaCall",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Use lambda.call(...) instead of lambda.(...).",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_style/#stylelambdacall",
                "tags": [
                  "style"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Use lambda.call(...) instead of lambda.(...)."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Use lambda.call(...) instead of lambda.(...)."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.13 | 0.14\n\nThis cop checks for use of the lambda.(args) syntax.\n\n### Examples\n\n#### EnforcedStyle: call (default)\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nlambda.(x, y)\n\n# good\nlambda.call(x, y)\n```\n#### EnforcedStyle: braces\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nlambda.call(x, y)\n\n# good\nlambda.(x, y)\n```\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nEnforcedStyle | `call` | `call`, `braces`\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#proc-call](https://rubystyle.guide#proc-call)\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.13 | 0.14\n\nThis cop checks for use of the lambda.(args) syntax.\n\n### Examples\n\n#### EnforcedStyle: call (default)\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nlambda.(x, y)\n\n# good\nlambda.call(x, y)\n```\n#### EnforcedStyle: braces\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nlambda.call(x, y)\n\n# good\nlambda.(x, y)\n```\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nEnforcedStyle | `call` | `call`, `braces`\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#proc-call](https://rubystyle.guide#proc-call)\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Style/LineEndConcatenation",
              "name": "Style/LineEndConcatenation",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Use \\ instead of + or << to concatenate two string literals at line end.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_style/#stylelineendconcatenation",
                "tags": [
                  "style"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Use \\ instead of + or << to concatenate two string literals at line end."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Use \\ instead of + or << to concatenate two string literals at line end."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes (Unsafe) | 0.18 | 0.64\n\nThis cop checks for string literal concatenation at\nthe end of a line.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nsome_str = 'ala' +\n           'bala'\n\nsome_str = 'ala' <<\n           'bala'\n\n# good\nsome_str = 'ala' \\\n           'bala'\n```\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes (Unsafe) | 0.18 | 0.64\n\nThis cop checks for string literal concatenation at\nthe end of a line.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nsome_str = 'ala' +\n           'bala'\n\nsome_str = 'ala' <<\n           'bala'\n\n# good\nsome_str = 'ala' \\\n           'bala'\n```\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Style/MethodCallWithArgsParentheses",
              "name": "Style/MethodCallWithArgsParentheses",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Use parentheses for method calls with arguments.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_style/#stylemethodcallwithargsparentheses",
                "tags": [
                  "style"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Use parentheses for method calls with arguments."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Use parentheses for method calls with arguments."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nDisabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.47 | 0.61\n\nThis cop enforces the presence (default) or absence of parentheses in\nmethod calls containing parameters.\n\nIn the default style (require_parentheses), macro methods are ignored.\nAdditional methods can be added to the `IgnoredMethods`\nor `IgnoredPatterns` list. These options are\nvalid only in the default style. Macros can be included by\neither setting `IgnoreMacros` to false or adding specific macros to\nthe `IncludedMacros` list.\n\nPrecedence of options is all follows:\n\n1. `IgnoredMethods`\n2. `IgnoredPatterns`\n3. `IncludedMacros`\n\neg. If a method is listed in both\n`IncludedMacros` and `IgnoredMethods`, then the latter takes\nprecedence (that is, the method is ignored).\n\nIn the alternative style (omit_parentheses), there are three additional\noptions.\n\n1. `AllowParenthesesInChaining` is `false` by default. Setting it to\n   `true` allows the presence of parentheses in the last call during\n   method chaining.\n\n2. `AllowParenthesesInMultilineCall` is `false` by default. Setting it\n    to `true` allows the presence of parentheses in multi-line method\n    calls.\n\n3. `AllowParenthesesInCamelCaseMethod` is `false` by default. This\n    allows the presence of parentheses when calling a method whose name\n    begins with a capital letter and which has no arguments. Setting it\n    to `true` allows the presence of parentheses in such a method call\n    even with arguments.\n\n### Examples\n\n#### EnforcedStyle: require_parentheses (default)\n\n```ruby\n# bad\narray.delete e\n\n# good\narray.delete(e)\n\n# good\n# Operators don't need parens\nfoo == bar\n\n# good\n# Setter methods don't need parens\nfoo.bar = baz\n\n# okay with `puts` listed in `IgnoredMethods`\nputs 'test'\n\n# okay with `^assert` listed in `IgnoredPatterns`\nassert_equal 'test', x\n```\n#### EnforcedStyle: omit_parentheses\n\n```ruby\n# bad\narray.delete(e)\n\n# good\narray.delete e\n\n# bad\nfoo.enforce(strict: true)\n\n# good\nfoo.enforce strict: true\n```\n#### IgnoreMacros: true (default)\n\n```ruby\n# good\nclass Foo\n  bar :baz\nend\n```\n#### IgnoreMacros: false\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nclass Foo\n  bar :baz\nend\n```\n#### AllowParenthesesInMultilineCall: false (default)\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nfoo.enforce(\n  strict: true\n)\n\n# good\nfoo.enforce \\\n  strict: true\n```\n#### AllowParenthesesInMultilineCall: true\n\n```ruby\n# good\nfoo.enforce(\n  strict: true\n)\n\n# good\nfoo.enforce \\\n  strict: true\n```\n#### AllowParenthesesInChaining: false (default)\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nfoo().bar(1)\n\n# good\nfoo().bar 1\n```\n#### AllowParenthesesInChaining: true\n\n```ruby\n# good\nfoo().bar(1)\n\n# good\nfoo().bar 1\n```\n#### AllowParenthesesInCamelCaseMethod: false (default)\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nArray(1)\n\n# good\nArray 1\n```\n#### AllowParenthesesInCamelCaseMethod: true\n\n```ruby\n# good\nArray(1)\n\n# good\nArray 1\n```\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nIgnoreMacros | `true` | Boolean\nIgnoredMethods | `[]` | Array\nIgnoredPatterns | `[]` | Array\nIncludedMacros | `[]` | Array\nAllowParenthesesInMultilineCall | `false` | Boolean\nAllowParenthesesInChaining | `false` | Boolean\nAllowParenthesesInCamelCaseMethod | `false` | Boolean\nEnforcedStyle | `require_parentheses` | `require_parentheses`, `omit_parentheses`\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#method-invocation-parens](https://rubystyle.guide#method-invocation-parens)\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nDisabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.47 | 0.61\n\nThis cop enforces the presence (default) or absence of parentheses in\nmethod calls containing parameters.\n\nIn the default style (require_parentheses), macro methods are ignored.\nAdditional methods can be added to the `IgnoredMethods`\nor `IgnoredPatterns` list. These options are\nvalid only in the default style. Macros can be included by\neither setting `IgnoreMacros` to false or adding specific macros to\nthe `IncludedMacros` list.\n\nPrecedence of options is all follows:\n\n1. `IgnoredMethods`\n2. `IgnoredPatterns`\n3. `IncludedMacros`\n\neg. If a method is listed in both\n`IncludedMacros` and `IgnoredMethods`, then the latter takes\nprecedence (that is, the method is ignored).\n\nIn the alternative style (omit_parentheses), there are three additional\noptions.\n\n1. `AllowParenthesesInChaining` is `false` by default. Setting it to\n   `true` allows the presence of parentheses in the last call during\n   method chaining.\n\n2. `AllowParenthesesInMultilineCall` is `false` by default. Setting it\n    to `true` allows the presence of parentheses in multi-line method\n    calls.\n\n3. `AllowParenthesesInCamelCaseMethod` is `false` by default. This\n    allows the presence of parentheses when calling a method whose name\n    begins with a capital letter and which has no arguments. Setting it\n    to `true` allows the presence of parentheses in such a method call\n    even with arguments.\n\n### Examples\n\n#### EnforcedStyle: require_parentheses (default)\n\n```ruby\n# bad\narray.delete e\n\n# good\narray.delete(e)\n\n# good\n# Operators don't need parens\nfoo == bar\n\n# good\n# Setter methods don't need parens\nfoo.bar = baz\n\n# okay with `puts` listed in `IgnoredMethods`\nputs 'test'\n\n# okay with `^assert` listed in `IgnoredPatterns`\nassert_equal 'test', x\n```\n#### EnforcedStyle: omit_parentheses\n\n```ruby\n# bad\narray.delete(e)\n\n# good\narray.delete e\n\n# bad\nfoo.enforce(strict: true)\n\n# good\nfoo.enforce strict: true\n```\n#### IgnoreMacros: true (default)\n\n```ruby\n# good\nclass Foo\n  bar :baz\nend\n```\n#### IgnoreMacros: false\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nclass Foo\n  bar :baz\nend\n```\n#### AllowParenthesesInMultilineCall: false (default)\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nfoo.enforce(\n  strict: true\n)\n\n# good\nfoo.enforce \\\n  strict: true\n```\n#### AllowParenthesesInMultilineCall: true\n\n```ruby\n# good\nfoo.enforce(\n  strict: true\n)\n\n# good\nfoo.enforce \\\n  strict: true\n```\n#### AllowParenthesesInChaining: false (default)\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nfoo().bar(1)\n\n# good\nfoo().bar 1\n```\n#### AllowParenthesesInChaining: true\n\n```ruby\n# good\nfoo().bar(1)\n\n# good\nfoo().bar 1\n```\n#### AllowParenthesesInCamelCaseMethod: false (default)\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nArray(1)\n\n# good\nArray 1\n```\n#### AllowParenthesesInCamelCaseMethod: true\n\n```ruby\n# good\nArray(1)\n\n# good\nArray 1\n```\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nIgnoreMacros | `true` | Boolean\nIgnoredMethods | `[]` | Array\nIgnoredPatterns | `[]` | Array\nIncludedMacros | `[]` | Array\nAllowParenthesesInMultilineCall | `false` | Boolean\nAllowParenthesesInChaining | `false` | Boolean\nAllowParenthesesInCamelCaseMethod | `false` | Boolean\nEnforcedStyle | `require_parentheses` | `require_parentheses`, `omit_parentheses`\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#method-invocation-parens](https://rubystyle.guide#method-invocation-parens)\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Style/MethodCallWithoutArgsParentheses",
              "name": "Style/MethodCallWithoutArgsParentheses",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Do not use parentheses for method calls with no arguments.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_style/#stylemethodcallwithoutargsparentheses",
                "tags": [
                  "style"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Do not use parentheses for method calls with no arguments."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Do not use parentheses for method calls with no arguments."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.47 | 0.55\n\nThis cop checks for unwanted parentheses in parameterless method calls.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nobject.some_method()\n\n# good\nobject.some_method\n```\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nIgnoredMethods | `[]` | Array\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#method-invocation-parens](https://rubystyle.guide#method-invocation-parens)\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.47 | 0.55\n\nThis cop checks for unwanted parentheses in parameterless method calls.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nobject.some_method()\n\n# good\nobject.some_method\n```\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nIgnoredMethods | `[]` | Array\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#method-invocation-parens](https://rubystyle.guide#method-invocation-parens)\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Style/MethodCalledOnDoEndBlock",
              "name": "Style/MethodCalledOnDoEndBlock",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Avoid chaining a method call on a do...end block.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_style/#stylemethodcalledondoendblock",
                "tags": [
                  "style"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Avoid chaining a method call on a do...end block."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Avoid chaining a method call on a do...end block."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nDisabled | Yes | No | 0.14 | -\n\nThis cop checks for methods called on a do...end block. The point of\nthis check is that it's easy to miss the call tacked on to the block\nwhen reading code.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\na do\n  b\nend.c\n```\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#single-line-blocks](https://rubystyle.guide#single-line-blocks)\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nDisabled | Yes | No | 0.14 | -\n\nThis cop checks for methods called on a do...end block. The point of\nthis check is that it's easy to miss the call tacked on to the block\nwhen reading code.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\na do\n  b\nend.c\n```\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#single-line-blocks](https://rubystyle.guide#single-line-blocks)\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Style/MethodDefParentheses",
              "name": "Style/MethodDefParentheses",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Checks if the method definitions have or don't have parentheses.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_style/#stylemethoddefparentheses",
                "tags": [
                  "style"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Checks if the method definitions have or don't have parentheses."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Checks if the method definitions have or don't have parentheses."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.16 | 0.35\n\nThis cop checks for parentheses around the arguments in method\ndefinitions. Both instance and class/singleton methods are checked.\n\n### Examples\n\n#### EnforcedStyle: require_parentheses (default)\n\n```ruby\n# The `require_parentheses` style requires method definitions\n# to always use parentheses\n\n# bad\ndef bar num1, num2\n  num1 + num2\nend\n\ndef foo descriptive_var_name,\n        another_descriptive_var_name,\n        last_descriptive_var_name\n  do_something\nend\n\n# good\ndef bar(num1, num2)\n  num1 + num2\nend\n\ndef foo(descriptive_var_name,\n        another_descriptive_var_name,\n        last_descriptive_var_name)\n  do_something\nend\n```\n#### EnforcedStyle: require_no_parentheses\n\n```ruby\n# The `require_no_parentheses` style requires method definitions\n# to never use parentheses\n\n# bad\ndef bar(num1, num2)\n  num1 + num2\nend\n\ndef foo(descriptive_var_name,\n        another_descriptive_var_name,\n        last_descriptive_var_name)\n  do_something\nend\n\n# good\ndef bar num1, num2\n  num1 + num2\nend\n\ndef foo descriptive_var_name,\n        another_descriptive_var_name,\n        last_descriptive_var_name\n  do_something\nend\n```\n#### EnforcedStyle: require_no_parentheses_except_multiline\n\n```ruby\n# The `require_no_parentheses_except_multiline` style prefers no\n# parentheses when method definition arguments fit on single line,\n# but prefers parentheses when arguments span multiple lines.\n\n# bad\ndef bar(num1, num2)\n  num1 + num2\nend\n\ndef foo descriptive_var_name,\n        another_descriptive_var_name,\n        last_descriptive_var_name\n  do_something\nend\n\n# good\ndef bar num1, num2\n  num1 + num2\nend\n\ndef foo(descriptive_var_name,\n        another_descriptive_var_name,\n        last_descriptive_var_name)\n  do_something\nend\n```\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nEnforcedStyle | `require_parentheses` | `require_parentheses`, `require_no_parentheses`, `require_no_parentheses_except_multiline`\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#method-parens](https://rubystyle.guide#method-parens)\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.16 | 0.35\n\nThis cop checks for parentheses around the arguments in method\ndefinitions. Both instance and class/singleton methods are checked.\n\n### Examples\n\n#### EnforcedStyle: require_parentheses (default)\n\n```ruby\n# The `require_parentheses` style requires method definitions\n# to always use parentheses\n\n# bad\ndef bar num1, num2\n  num1 + num2\nend\n\ndef foo descriptive_var_name,\n        another_descriptive_var_name,\n        last_descriptive_var_name\n  do_something\nend\n\n# good\ndef bar(num1, num2)\n  num1 + num2\nend\n\ndef foo(descriptive_var_name,\n        another_descriptive_var_name,\n        last_descriptive_var_name)\n  do_something\nend\n```\n#### EnforcedStyle: require_no_parentheses\n\n```ruby\n# The `require_no_parentheses` style requires method definitions\n# to never use parentheses\n\n# bad\ndef bar(num1, num2)\n  num1 + num2\nend\n\ndef foo(descriptive_var_name,\n        another_descriptive_var_name,\n        last_descriptive_var_name)\n  do_something\nend\n\n# good\ndef bar num1, num2\n  num1 + num2\nend\n\ndef foo descriptive_var_name,\n        another_descriptive_var_name,\n        last_descriptive_var_name\n  do_something\nend\n```\n#### EnforcedStyle: require_no_parentheses_except_multiline\n\n```ruby\n# The `require_no_parentheses_except_multiline` style prefers no\n# parentheses when method definition arguments fit on single line,\n# but prefers parentheses when arguments span multiple lines.\n\n# bad\ndef bar(num1, num2)\n  num1 + num2\nend\n\ndef foo descriptive_var_name,\n        another_descriptive_var_name,\n        last_descriptive_var_name\n  do_something\nend\n\n# good\ndef bar num1, num2\n  num1 + num2\nend\n\ndef foo(descriptive_var_name,\n        another_descriptive_var_name,\n        last_descriptive_var_name)\n  do_something\nend\n```\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nEnforcedStyle | `require_parentheses` | `require_parentheses`, `require_no_parentheses`, `require_no_parentheses_except_multiline`\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#method-parens](https://rubystyle.guide#method-parens)\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Style/MethodMissingSuper",
              "name": "Style/MethodMissingSuper",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Checks for `method_missing` to call `super`.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_style/#stylemethodmissingsuper",
                "tags": [
                  "style"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Checks for `method_missing` to call `super`."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Checks for `method_missing` to call `super`."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | No | 0.56 | -\n\nThis cop checks for the presence of `method_missing` without\nfalling back on `super`.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n#bad\ndef method_missing(name, *args)\n  # ...\nend\n\n#good\n\ndef method_missing(name, *args)\n  # ...\n  super\nend\n```\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#no-method-missing](https://rubystyle.guide#no-method-missing)\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | No | 0.56 | -\n\nThis cop checks for the presence of `method_missing` without\nfalling back on `super`.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n#bad\ndef method_missing(name, *args)\n  # ...\nend\n\n#good\n\ndef method_missing(name, *args)\n  # ...\n  super\nend\n```\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#no-method-missing](https://rubystyle.guide#no-method-missing)\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Style/MinMax",
              "name": "Style/MinMax",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Use `Enumerable#minmax` instead of `Enumerable#min` and `Enumerable#max` in conjunction.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_style/#styleminmax",
                "tags": [
                  "style"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Use `Enumerable#minmax` instead of `Enumerable#min` and `Enumerable#max` in conjunction."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Use `Enumerable#minmax` instead of `Enumerable#min` and `Enumerable#max` in conjunction."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.50 | -\n\nThis cop checks for potential uses of `Enumerable#minmax`.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nbar = [foo.min, foo.max]\nreturn foo.min, foo.max\n\n# good\nbar = foo.minmax\nreturn foo.minmax\n```\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.50 | -\n\nThis cop checks for potential uses of `Enumerable#minmax`.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nbar = [foo.min, foo.max]\nreturn foo.min, foo.max\n\n# good\nbar = foo.minmax\nreturn foo.minmax\n```\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Style/MissingElse",
              "name": "Style/MissingElse",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Require if/case expressions to have an else branches. If enabled, it is recommended that Style/UnlessElse and Style/EmptyElse be enabled. This will conflict with Style/EmptyElse if Style/EmptyElse is configured to style \"both\".",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_style/#stylemissingelse",
                "tags": [
                  "style"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Require if/case expressions to have an else branches. If enabled, it is recommended that Style/UnlessElse and Style/EmptyElse be enabled. This will conflict with Style/EmptyElse if Style/EmptyElse is configured to style \"both\"."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Require if/case expressions to have an else branches. If enabled, it is recommended that Style/UnlessElse and Style/EmptyElse be enabled. This will conflict with Style/EmptyElse if Style/EmptyElse is configured to style \"both\"."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nDisabled | Yes | No | 0.30 | 0.38\n\nChecks for `if` expressions that do not have an `else` branch.\n\nSupported styles are: if, case, both.\n\n### Examples\n\n#### EnforcedStyle: if\n\n```ruby\n# warn when an `if` expression is missing an `else` branch.\n\n# bad\nif condition\n  statement\nend\n\n# good\nif condition\n  statement\nelse\n  # the content of `else` branch will be determined by Style/EmptyElse\nend\n\n# good\ncase var\nwhen condition\n  statement\nend\n\n# good\ncase var\nwhen condition\n  statement\nelse\n  # the content of `else` branch will be determined by Style/EmptyElse\nend\n```\n#### EnforcedStyle: case\n\n```ruby\n# warn when a `case` expression is missing an `else` branch.\n\n# bad\ncase var\nwhen condition\n  statement\nend\n\n# good\ncase var\nwhen condition\n  statement\nelse\n  # the content of `else` branch will be determined by Style/EmptyElse\nend\n\n# good\nif condition\n  statement\nend\n\n# good\nif condition\n  statement\nelse\n  # the content of `else` branch will be determined by Style/EmptyElse\nend\n```\n#### EnforcedStyle: both (default)\n\n```ruby\n# warn when an `if` or `case` expression is missing an `else` branch.\n\n# bad\nif condition\n  statement\nend\n\n# bad\ncase var\nwhen condition\n  statement\nend\n\n# good\nif condition\n  statement\nelse\n  # the content of `else` branch will be determined by Style/EmptyElse\nend\n\n# good\ncase var\nwhen condition\n  statement\nelse\n  # the content of `else` branch will be determined by Style/EmptyElse\nend\n```\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nEnforcedStyle | `both` | `if`, `case`, `both`\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nDisabled | Yes | No | 0.30 | 0.38\n\nChecks for `if` expressions that do not have an `else` branch.\n\nSupported styles are: if, case, both.\n\n### Examples\n\n#### EnforcedStyle: if\n\n```ruby\n# warn when an `if` expression is missing an `else` branch.\n\n# bad\nif condition\n  statement\nend\n\n# good\nif condition\n  statement\nelse\n  # the content of `else` branch will be determined by Style/EmptyElse\nend\n\n# good\ncase var\nwhen condition\n  statement\nend\n\n# good\ncase var\nwhen condition\n  statement\nelse\n  # the content of `else` branch will be determined by Style/EmptyElse\nend\n```\n#### EnforcedStyle: case\n\n```ruby\n# warn when a `case` expression is missing an `else` branch.\n\n# bad\ncase var\nwhen condition\n  statement\nend\n\n# good\ncase var\nwhen condition\n  statement\nelse\n  # the content of `else` branch will be determined by Style/EmptyElse\nend\n\n# good\nif condition\n  statement\nend\n\n# good\nif condition\n  statement\nelse\n  # the content of `else` branch will be determined by Style/EmptyElse\nend\n```\n#### EnforcedStyle: both (default)\n\n```ruby\n# warn when an `if` or `case` expression is missing an `else` branch.\n\n# bad\nif condition\n  statement\nend\n\n# bad\ncase var\nwhen condition\n  statement\nend\n\n# good\nif condition\n  statement\nelse\n  # the content of `else` branch will be determined by Style/EmptyElse\nend\n\n# good\ncase var\nwhen condition\n  statement\nelse\n  # the content of `else` branch will be determined by Style/EmptyElse\nend\n```\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nEnforcedStyle | `both` | `if`, `case`, `both`\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Style/MissingRespondToMissing",
              "name": "Style/MissingRespondToMissing",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Checks if `method_missing` is implemented without implementing `respond_to_missing`.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_style/#stylemissingrespondtomissing",
                "tags": [
                  "style"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Checks if `method_missing` is implemented without implementing `respond_to_missing`."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Checks if `method_missing` is implemented without implementing `respond_to_missing`."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | No | 0.56 | -\n\nThis cop checks for the presence of `method_missing` without also\ndefining `respond_to_missing?`.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n#bad\ndef method_missing(name, *args)\n  # ...\nend\n\n#good\ndef respond_to_missing?(name, include_private)\n  # ...\nend\n\ndef method_missing(name, *args)\n  # ...\nend\n```\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#no-method-missing](https://rubystyle.guide#no-method-missing)\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | No | 0.56 | -\n\nThis cop checks for the presence of `method_missing` without also\ndefining `respond_to_missing?`.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n#bad\ndef method_missing(name, *args)\n  # ...\nend\n\n#good\ndef respond_to_missing?(name, include_private)\n  # ...\nend\n\ndef method_missing(name, *args)\n  # ...\nend\n```\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#no-method-missing](https://rubystyle.guide#no-method-missing)\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Style/MixinGrouping",
              "name": "Style/MixinGrouping",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Checks for grouping of mixins in `class` and `module` bodies.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_style/#stylemixingrouping",
                "tags": [
                  "style"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Checks for grouping of mixins in `class` and `module` bodies."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Checks for grouping of mixins in `class` and `module` bodies."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.48 | 0.49\n\nThis cop checks for grouping of mixins in `class` and `module` bodies.\nBy default it enforces mixins to be placed in separate declarations,\nbut it can be configured to enforce grouping them in one declaration.\n\n### Examples\n\n#### EnforcedStyle: separated (default)\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nclass Foo\n  include Bar, Qox\nend\n\n# good\nclass Foo\n  include Qox\n  include Bar\nend\n```\n#### EnforcedStyle: grouped\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nclass Foo\n  extend Bar\n  extend Qox\nend\n\n# good\nclass Foo\n  extend Qox, Bar\nend\n```\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nEnforcedStyle | `separated` | `separated`, `grouped`\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#mixin-grouping](https://rubystyle.guide#mixin-grouping)\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.48 | 0.49\n\nThis cop checks for grouping of mixins in `class` and `module` bodies.\nBy default it enforces mixins to be placed in separate declarations,\nbut it can be configured to enforce grouping them in one declaration.\n\n### Examples\n\n#### EnforcedStyle: separated (default)\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nclass Foo\n  include Bar, Qox\nend\n\n# good\nclass Foo\n  include Qox\n  include Bar\nend\n```\n#### EnforcedStyle: grouped\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nclass Foo\n  extend Bar\n  extend Qox\nend\n\n# good\nclass Foo\n  extend Qox, Bar\nend\n```\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nEnforcedStyle | `separated` | `separated`, `grouped`\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#mixin-grouping](https://rubystyle.guide#mixin-grouping)\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Style/MixinUsage",
              "name": "Style/MixinUsage",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Checks that `include`, `extend` and `prepend` exists at the top level.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_style/#stylemixinusage",
                "tags": [
                  "style"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Checks that `include`, `extend` and `prepend` exists at the top level."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Checks that `include`, `extend` and `prepend` exists at the top level."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | No | 0.51 | -\n\nThis cop checks that `include`, `extend` and `prepend` statements appear\ninside classes and modules, not at the top level, so as to not affect\nthe behavior of `Object`.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\ninclude M\n\nclass C\nend\n\n# bad\nextend M\n\nclass C\nend\n\n# bad\nprepend M\n\nclass C\nend\n\n# good\nclass C\n  include M\nend\n\n# good\nclass C\n  extend M\nend\n\n# good\nclass C\n  prepend M\nend\n```\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | No | 0.51 | -\n\nThis cop checks that `include`, `extend` and `prepend` statements appear\ninside classes and modules, not at the top level, so as to not affect\nthe behavior of `Object`.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\ninclude M\n\nclass C\nend\n\n# bad\nextend M\n\nclass C\nend\n\n# bad\nprepend M\n\nclass C\nend\n\n# good\nclass C\n  include M\nend\n\n# good\nclass C\n  extend M\nend\n\n# good\nclass C\n  prepend M\nend\n```\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Style/ModuleFunction",
              "name": "Style/ModuleFunction",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Checks for usage of `extend self` in modules.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_style/#stylemodulefunction",
                "tags": [
                  "style"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Checks for usage of `extend self` in modules."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Checks for usage of `extend self` in modules."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes (Unsafe) | 0.11 | 0.65\n\nThis cop checks for use of `extend self` or `module_function` in a\nmodule.\n\nSupported styles are: module_function, extend_self, forbidden.\n\nIn case there are private methods, the cop won't be activated.\nOtherwise, it forces to change the flow of the default code.\n\nThe option `forbidden` prohibits the usage of both styles.\n\nThese offenses are not safe to auto-correct since there are different\nimplications to each approach.\n\n### Examples\n\n#### EnforcedStyle: module_function (default)\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nmodule Test\n  extend self\n  # ...\nend\n\n# good\nmodule Test\n  module_function\n  # ...\nend\n```\n#### EnforcedStyle: module_function (default)\n\n```ruby\n# good\nmodule Test\n  extend self\n  # ...\n  private\n  # ...\nend\n```\n#### EnforcedStyle: extend_self\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nmodule Test\n  module_function\n  # ...\nend\n\n# good\nmodule Test\n  extend self\n  # ...\nend\n```\n#### EnforcedStyle: forbidden\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nmodule Test\n  module_function\n  # ...\nend\n\n# bad\nmodule Test\n  extend self\n  # ...\nend\n\n# bad\nmodule Test\n  extend self\n  # ...\n  private\n  # ...\nend\n```\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nEnforcedStyle | `module_function` | `module_function`, `extend_self`, `forbidden`\nAutocorrect | `false` | Boolean\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#module-function](https://rubystyle.guide#module-function)\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes (Unsafe) | 0.11 | 0.65\n\nThis cop checks for use of `extend self` or `module_function` in a\nmodule.\n\nSupported styles are: module_function, extend_self, forbidden.\n\nIn case there are private methods, the cop won't be activated.\nOtherwise, it forces to change the flow of the default code.\n\nThe option `forbidden` prohibits the usage of both styles.\n\nThese offenses are not safe to auto-correct since there are different\nimplications to each approach.\n\n### Examples\n\n#### EnforcedStyle: module_function (default)\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nmodule Test\n  extend self\n  # ...\nend\n\n# good\nmodule Test\n  module_function\n  # ...\nend\n```\n#### EnforcedStyle: module_function (default)\n\n```ruby\n# good\nmodule Test\n  extend self\n  # ...\n  private\n  # ...\nend\n```\n#### EnforcedStyle: extend_self\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nmodule Test\n  module_function\n  # ...\nend\n\n# good\nmodule Test\n  extend self\n  # ...\nend\n```\n#### EnforcedStyle: forbidden\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nmodule Test\n  module_function\n  # ...\nend\n\n# bad\nmodule Test\n  extend self\n  # ...\nend\n\n# bad\nmodule Test\n  extend self\n  # ...\n  private\n  # ...\nend\n```\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nEnforcedStyle | `module_function` | `module_function`, `extend_self`, `forbidden`\nAutocorrect | `false` | Boolean\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#module-function](https://rubystyle.guide#module-function)\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Style/MultilineBlockChain",
              "name": "Style/MultilineBlockChain",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Avoid multi-line chains of blocks.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_style/#stylemultilineblockchain",
                "tags": [
                  "style"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Avoid multi-line chains of blocks."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Avoid multi-line chains of blocks."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | No | 0.13 | -\n\nThis cop checks for chaining of a block after another block that spans\nmultiple lines.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\nThread.list.find_all do |t|\n  t.alive?\nend.map do |t|\n  t.object_id\nend\n```\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#single-line-blocks](https://rubystyle.guide#single-line-blocks)\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | No | 0.13 | -\n\nThis cop checks for chaining of a block after another block that spans\nmultiple lines.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\nThread.list.find_all do |t|\n  t.alive?\nend.map do |t|\n  t.object_id\nend\n```\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#single-line-blocks](https://rubystyle.guide#single-line-blocks)\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Style/MultilineIfModifier",
              "name": "Style/MultilineIfModifier",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Only use if/unless modifiers on single line statements.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_style/#stylemultilineifmodifier",
                "tags": [
                  "style"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Only use if/unless modifiers on single line statements."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Only use if/unless modifiers on single line statements."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.45 | -\n\nChecks for uses of if/unless modifiers with multiple-lines bodies.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\n{\n  result: 'this should not happen'\n} unless cond\n\n# good\n{ result: 'ok' } if cond\n```\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#no-multiline-if-modifiers](https://rubystyle.guide#no-multiline-if-modifiers)\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.45 | -\n\nChecks for uses of if/unless modifiers with multiple-lines bodies.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\n{\n  result: 'this should not happen'\n} unless cond\n\n# good\n{ result: 'ok' } if cond\n```\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#no-multiline-if-modifiers](https://rubystyle.guide#no-multiline-if-modifiers)\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Style/MultilineIfThen",
              "name": "Style/MultilineIfThen",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Do not use then for multi-line if/unless.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_style/#stylemultilineifthen",
                "tags": [
                  "style"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Do not use then for multi-line if/unless."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Do not use then for multi-line if/unless."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.9 | 0.26\n\nChecks for uses of the `then` keyword in multi-line if statements.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\n# This is considered bad practice.\nif cond then\nend\n\n# good\n# If statements can contain `then` on the same line.\nif cond then a\nelsif cond then b\nend\n```\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#no-then](https://rubystyle.guide#no-then)\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.9 | 0.26\n\nChecks for uses of the `then` keyword in multi-line if statements.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\n# This is considered bad practice.\nif cond then\nend\n\n# good\n# If statements can contain `then` on the same line.\nif cond then a\nelsif cond then b\nend\n```\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#no-then](https://rubystyle.guide#no-then)\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Style/MultilineMemoization",
              "name": "Style/MultilineMemoization",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Wrap multiline memoizations in a `begin` and `end` block.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_style/#stylemultilinememoization",
                "tags": [
                  "style"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Wrap multiline memoizations in a `begin` and `end` block."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Wrap multiline memoizations in a `begin` and `end` block."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.44 | 0.48\n\nThis cop checks expressions wrapping styles for multiline memoization.\n\n### Examples\n\n#### EnforcedStyle: keyword (default)\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nfoo ||= (\n  bar\n  baz\n)\n\n# good\nfoo ||= begin\n  bar\n  baz\nend\n```\n#### EnforcedStyle: braces\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nfoo ||= begin\n  bar\n  baz\nend\n\n# good\nfoo ||= (\n  bar\n  baz\n)\n```\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nEnforcedStyle | `keyword` | `keyword`, `braces`\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.44 | 0.48\n\nThis cop checks expressions wrapping styles for multiline memoization.\n\n### Examples\n\n#### EnforcedStyle: keyword (default)\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nfoo ||= (\n  bar\n  baz\n)\n\n# good\nfoo ||= begin\n  bar\n  baz\nend\n```\n#### EnforcedStyle: braces\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nfoo ||= begin\n  bar\n  baz\nend\n\n# good\nfoo ||= (\n  bar\n  baz\n)\n```\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nEnforcedStyle | `keyword` | `keyword`, `braces`\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Style/MultilineMethodSignature",
              "name": "Style/MultilineMethodSignature",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Avoid multi-line method signatures.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_style/#stylemultilinemethodsignature",
                "tags": [
                  "style"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Avoid multi-line method signatures."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Avoid multi-line method signatures."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nDisabled | Yes | No | 0.59 | -\n\nThis cop checks for method signatures that span multiple lines.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# good\n\ndef foo(bar, baz)\nend\n\n# bad\n\ndef foo(bar,\n        baz)\nend\n```\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nDisabled | Yes | No | 0.59 | -\n\nThis cop checks for method signatures that span multiple lines.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# good\n\ndef foo(bar, baz)\nend\n\n# bad\n\ndef foo(bar,\n        baz)\nend\n```\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Style/MultilineTernaryOperator",
              "name": "Style/MultilineTernaryOperator",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Avoid multi-line ?: (the ternary operator); use if/unless instead.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_style/#stylemultilineternaryoperator",
                "tags": [
                  "style"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Avoid multi-line ?: (the ternary operator); use if/unless instead."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Avoid multi-line ?: (the ternary operator); use if/unless instead."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | No | 0.9 | -\n\nThis cop checks for multi-line ternary op expressions.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\na = cond ?\n  b : c\na = cond ? b :\n    c\na = cond ?\n    b :\n    c\n\n# good\na = cond ? b : c\na =\n  if cond\n    b\n  else\n    c\n  end\n```\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#no-multiline-ternary](https://rubystyle.guide#no-multiline-ternary)\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | No | 0.9 | -\n\nThis cop checks for multi-line ternary op expressions.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\na = cond ?\n  b : c\na = cond ? b :\n    c\na = cond ?\n    b :\n    c\n\n# good\na = cond ? b : c\na =\n  if cond\n    b\n  else\n    c\n  end\n```\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#no-multiline-ternary](https://rubystyle.guide#no-multiline-ternary)\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Style/MultilineWhenThen",
              "name": "Style/MultilineWhenThen",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Do not use then for multi-line when statement.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_style/#stylemultilinewhenthen",
                "tags": [
                  "style"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Do not use then for multi-line when statement."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Do not use then for multi-line when statement."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.73 | -\n\nThis cop checks uses of the `then` keyword\nin multi-line when statements.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\ncase foo\nwhen bar then\nend\n\n# good\ncase foo\nwhen bar\nend\n\n# good\ncase foo\nwhen bar then do_something\nend\n\n# good\ncase foo\nwhen bar then do_something(arg1,\n                           arg2)\nend\n```\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#no-then](https://rubystyle.guide#no-then)\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.73 | -\n\nThis cop checks uses of the `then` keyword\nin multi-line when statements.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\ncase foo\nwhen bar then\nend\n\n# good\ncase foo\nwhen bar\nend\n\n# good\ncase foo\nwhen bar then do_something\nend\n\n# good\ncase foo\nwhen bar then do_something(arg1,\n                           arg2)\nend\n```\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#no-then](https://rubystyle.guide#no-then)\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Style/MultipleComparison",
              "name": "Style/MultipleComparison",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Avoid comparing a variable with multiple items in a conditional, use Array#include? instead.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_style/#stylemultiplecomparison",
                "tags": [
                  "style"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Avoid comparing a variable with multiple items in a conditional, use Array#include? instead."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Avoid comparing a variable with multiple items in a conditional, use Array#include? instead."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | No | 0.49 | -\n\nThis cop checks against comparing a variable with multiple items, where\n`Array#include?` could be used instead to avoid code repetition.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\na = 'a'\nfoo if a == 'a' || a == 'b' || a == 'c'\n\n# good\na = 'a'\nfoo if ['a', 'b', 'c'].include?(a)\n```\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | No | 0.49 | -\n\nThis cop checks against comparing a variable with multiple items, where\n`Array#include?` could be used instead to avoid code repetition.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\na = 'a'\nfoo if a == 'a' || a == 'b' || a == 'c'\n\n# good\na = 'a'\nfoo if ['a', 'b', 'c'].include?(a)\n```\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Style/MutableConstant",
              "name": "Style/MutableConstant",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Do not assign mutable objects to constants.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_style/#stylemutableconstant",
                "tags": [
                  "style"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Do not assign mutable objects to constants."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Do not assign mutable objects to constants."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.34 | 0.65\n\nThis cop checks whether some constant value isn't a\nmutable literal (e.g. array or hash).\n\nStrict mode can be used to freeze all constants, rather than\njust literals.\nStrict mode is considered an experimental feature. It has not been\nupdated with an exhaustive list of all methods that will produce\nfrozen objects so there is a decent chance of getting some false\npositives. Luckily, there is no harm in freezing an already\nfrozen object.\n\n### Examples\n\n#### EnforcedStyle: literals (default)\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nCONST = [1, 2, 3]\n\n# good\nCONST = [1, 2, 3].freeze\n\n# good\nCONST = <<~TESTING.freeze\n  This is a heredoc\nTESTING\n\n# good\nCONST = Something.new\n```\n#### EnforcedStyle: strict\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nCONST = Something.new\n\n# bad\nCONST = Struct.new do\n  def foo\n    puts 1\n  end\nend\n\n# good\nCONST = Something.new.freeze\n\n# good\nCONST = Struct.new do\n  def foo\n    puts 1\n  end\nend.freeze\n```\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nEnforcedStyle | `literals` | `literals`, `strict`\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.34 | 0.65\n\nThis cop checks whether some constant value isn't a\nmutable literal (e.g. array or hash).\n\nStrict mode can be used to freeze all constants, rather than\njust literals.\nStrict mode is considered an experimental feature. It has not been\nupdated with an exhaustive list of all methods that will produce\nfrozen objects so there is a decent chance of getting some false\npositives. Luckily, there is no harm in freezing an already\nfrozen object.\n\n### Examples\n\n#### EnforcedStyle: literals (default)\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nCONST = [1, 2, 3]\n\n# good\nCONST = [1, 2, 3].freeze\n\n# good\nCONST = <<~TESTING.freeze\n  This is a heredoc\nTESTING\n\n# good\nCONST = Something.new\n```\n#### EnforcedStyle: strict\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nCONST = Something.new\n\n# bad\nCONST = Struct.new do\n  def foo\n    puts 1\n  end\nend\n\n# good\nCONST = Something.new.freeze\n\n# good\nCONST = Struct.new do\n  def foo\n    puts 1\n  end\nend.freeze\n```\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nEnforcedStyle | `literals` | `literals`, `strict`\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Style/NegatedIf",
              "name": "Style/NegatedIf",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Favor unless over if for negative conditions (or control flow or).",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_style/#stylenegatedif",
                "tags": [
                  "style"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Favor unless over if for negative conditions (or control flow or)."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Favor unless over if for negative conditions (or control flow or)."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.20 | 0.48\n\nChecks for uses of if with a negated condition. Only ifs\nwithout else are considered. There are three different styles:\n\n  - both\n  - prefix\n  - postfix\n\n### Examples\n\n#### EnforcedStyle: both (default)\n\n```ruby\n# enforces `unless` for `prefix` and `postfix` conditionals\n\n# bad\n\nif !foo\n  bar\nend\n\n# good\n\nunless foo\n  bar\nend\n\n# bad\n\nbar if !foo\n\n# good\n\nbar unless foo\n```\n#### EnforcedStyle: prefix\n\n```ruby\n# enforces `unless` for just `prefix` conditionals\n\n# bad\n\nif !foo\n  bar\nend\n\n# good\n\nunless foo\n  bar\nend\n\n# good\n\nbar if !foo\n```\n#### EnforcedStyle: postfix\n\n```ruby\n# enforces `unless` for just `postfix` conditionals\n\n# bad\n\nbar if !foo\n\n# good\n\nbar unless foo\n\n# good\n\nif !foo\n  bar\nend\n```\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nEnforcedStyle | `both` | `both`, `prefix`, `postfix`\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#unless-for-negatives](https://rubystyle.guide#unless-for-negatives)\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.20 | 0.48\n\nChecks for uses of if with a negated condition. Only ifs\nwithout else are considered. There are three different styles:\n\n  - both\n  - prefix\n  - postfix\n\n### Examples\n\n#### EnforcedStyle: both (default)\n\n```ruby\n# enforces `unless` for `prefix` and `postfix` conditionals\n\n# bad\n\nif !foo\n  bar\nend\n\n# good\n\nunless foo\n  bar\nend\n\n# bad\n\nbar if !foo\n\n# good\n\nbar unless foo\n```\n#### EnforcedStyle: prefix\n\n```ruby\n# enforces `unless` for just `prefix` conditionals\n\n# bad\n\nif !foo\n  bar\nend\n\n# good\n\nunless foo\n  bar\nend\n\n# good\n\nbar if !foo\n```\n#### EnforcedStyle: postfix\n\n```ruby\n# enforces `unless` for just `postfix` conditionals\n\n# bad\n\nbar if !foo\n\n# good\n\nbar unless foo\n\n# good\n\nif !foo\n  bar\nend\n```\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nEnforcedStyle | `both` | `both`, `prefix`, `postfix`\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#unless-for-negatives](https://rubystyle.guide#unless-for-negatives)\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Style/NegatedUnless",
              "name": "Style/NegatedUnless",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Favor if over unless for negative conditions.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_style/#stylenegatedunless",
                "tags": [
                  "style"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Favor if over unless for negative conditions."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Favor if over unless for negative conditions."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.69 | -\n\nChecks for uses of unless with a negated condition. Only unless\nwithout else are considered. There are three different styles:\n\n  - both\n  - prefix\n  - postfix\n\n### Examples\n\n#### EnforcedStyle: both (default)\n\n```ruby\n# enforces `if` for `prefix` and `postfix` conditionals\n\n# bad\nunless !foo\n  bar\nend\n\n# good\nif foo\n  bar\nend\n\n# bad\nbar unless !foo\n\n# good\nbar if foo\n```\n#### EnforcedStyle: prefix\n\n```ruby\n# enforces `if` for just `prefix` conditionals\n\n# bad\nunless !foo\n  bar\nend\n\n# good\nif foo\n  bar\nend\n\n# good\nbar unless !foo\n```\n#### EnforcedStyle: postfix\n\n```ruby\n# enforces `if` for just `postfix` conditionals\n\n# bad\nbar unless !foo\n\n# good\nbar if foo\n\n# good\nunless !foo\n  bar\nend\n```\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nEnforcedStyle | `both` | `both`, `prefix`, `postfix`\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#if-for-negatives](https://rubystyle.guide#if-for-negatives)\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.69 | -\n\nChecks for uses of unless with a negated condition. Only unless\nwithout else are considered. There are three different styles:\n\n  - both\n  - prefix\n  - postfix\n\n### Examples\n\n#### EnforcedStyle: both (default)\n\n```ruby\n# enforces `if` for `prefix` and `postfix` conditionals\n\n# bad\nunless !foo\n  bar\nend\n\n# good\nif foo\n  bar\nend\n\n# bad\nbar unless !foo\n\n# good\nbar if foo\n```\n#### EnforcedStyle: prefix\n\n```ruby\n# enforces `if` for just `prefix` conditionals\n\n# bad\nunless !foo\n  bar\nend\n\n# good\nif foo\n  bar\nend\n\n# good\nbar unless !foo\n```\n#### EnforcedStyle: postfix\n\n```ruby\n# enforces `if` for just `postfix` conditionals\n\n# bad\nbar unless !foo\n\n# good\nbar if foo\n\n# good\nunless !foo\n  bar\nend\n```\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nEnforcedStyle | `both` | `both`, `prefix`, `postfix`\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#if-for-negatives](https://rubystyle.guide#if-for-negatives)\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Style/NegatedWhile",
              "name": "Style/NegatedWhile",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Favor until over while for negative conditions.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_style/#stylenegatedwhile",
                "tags": [
                  "style"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Favor until over while for negative conditions."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Favor until over while for negative conditions."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.20 | -\n\nChecks for uses of while with a negated condition.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nwhile !foo\n  bar\nend\n\n# good\nuntil foo\n  bar\nend\n\n# bad\nbar until !foo\n\n# good\nbar while foo\nbar while !foo && baz\n```\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#until-for-negatives](https://rubystyle.guide#until-for-negatives)\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.20 | -\n\nChecks for uses of while with a negated condition.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nwhile !foo\n  bar\nend\n\n# good\nuntil foo\n  bar\nend\n\n# bad\nbar until !foo\n\n# good\nbar while foo\nbar while !foo && baz\n```\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#until-for-negatives](https://rubystyle.guide#until-for-negatives)\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Style/NestedModifier",
              "name": "Style/NestedModifier",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Avoid using nested modifiers.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_style/#stylenestedmodifier",
                "tags": [
                  "style"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Avoid using nested modifiers."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Avoid using nested modifiers."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.35 | -\n\nThis cop checks for nested use of if, unless, while and until in their\nmodifier form.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nsomething if a if b\n\n# good\nsomething if b && a\n```\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#no-nested-modifiers](https://rubystyle.guide#no-nested-modifiers)\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.35 | -\n\nThis cop checks for nested use of if, unless, while and until in their\nmodifier form.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nsomething if a if b\n\n# good\nsomething if b && a\n```\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#no-nested-modifiers](https://rubystyle.guide#no-nested-modifiers)\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Style/NestedParenthesizedCalls",
              "name": "Style/NestedParenthesizedCalls",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Parenthesize method calls which are nested inside the argument list of another parenthesized method call.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_style/#stylenestedparenthesizedcalls",
                "tags": [
                  "style"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Parenthesize method calls which are nested inside the argument list of another parenthesized method call."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Parenthesize method calls which are nested inside the argument list of another parenthesized method call."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.36 | 0.77\n\nThis cop checks for unparenthesized method calls in the argument list\nof a parenthesized method call.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# good\nmethod1(method2(arg))\n\n# bad\nmethod1(method2 arg)\n```\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nAllowedMethods | `be`, `be_a`, `be_an`, `be_between`, `be_falsey`, `be_kind_of`, `be_instance_of`, `be_truthy`, `be_within`, `eq`, `eql`, `end_with`, `include`, `match`, `raise_error`, `respond_to`, `start_with` | Array\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.36 | 0.77\n\nThis cop checks for unparenthesized method calls in the argument list\nof a parenthesized method call.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# good\nmethod1(method2(arg))\n\n# bad\nmethod1(method2 arg)\n```\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nAllowedMethods | `be`, `be_a`, `be_an`, `be_between`, `be_falsey`, `be_kind_of`, `be_instance_of`, `be_truthy`, `be_within`, `eq`, `eql`, `end_with`, `include`, `match`, `raise_error`, `respond_to`, `start_with` | Array\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Style/NestedTernaryOperator",
              "name": "Style/NestedTernaryOperator",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Use one expression per branch in a ternary operator.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_style/#stylenestedternaryoperator",
                "tags": [
                  "style"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Use one expression per branch in a ternary operator."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Use one expression per branch in a ternary operator."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | No | 0.9 | -\n\nThis cop checks for nested ternary op expressions.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\na ? (b ? b1 : b2) : a2\n\n# good\nif a\n  b ? b1 : b2\nelse\n  a2\nend\n```\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#no-nested-ternary](https://rubystyle.guide#no-nested-ternary)\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | No | 0.9 | -\n\nThis cop checks for nested ternary op expressions.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\na ? (b ? b1 : b2) : a2\n\n# good\nif a\n  b ? b1 : b2\nelse\n  a2\nend\n```\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#no-nested-ternary](https://rubystyle.guide#no-nested-ternary)\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Style/Next",
              "name": "Style/Next",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Use `next` to skip iteration instead of a condition at the end.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_style/#stylenext",
                "tags": [
                  "style"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Use `next` to skip iteration instead of a condition at the end."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Use `next` to skip iteration instead of a condition at the end."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.22 | 0.35\n\nUse `next` to skip iteration instead of a condition at the end.\n\n### Examples\n\n#### EnforcedStyle: skip_modifier_ifs (default)\n\n```ruby\n# bad\n[1, 2].each do |a|\n  if a == 1\n    puts a\n  end\nend\n\n# good\n[1, 2].each do |a|\n  next unless a == 1\n  puts a\nend\n\n# good\n[1, 2].each do |a|\n  puts a if a == 1\nend\n```\n#### EnforcedStyle: always\n\n```ruby\n# With `always` all conditions at the end of an iteration needs to be\n# replaced by next - with `skip_modifier_ifs` the modifier if like\n# this one are ignored: `[1, 2].each { |a| puts a if a == 1 }`\n\n# bad\n[1, 2].each do |a|\n  puts a if a == 1\nend\n\n# bad\n[1, 2].each do |a|\n  if a == 1\n    puts a\n  end\nend\n\n# good\n[1, 2].each do |a|\n  next unless a == 1\n  puts a\nend\n```\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nEnforcedStyle | `skip_modifier_ifs` | `skip_modifier_ifs`, `always`\nMinBodyLength | `3` | Integer\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#no-nested-conditionals](https://rubystyle.guide#no-nested-conditionals)\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.22 | 0.35\n\nUse `next` to skip iteration instead of a condition at the end.\n\n### Examples\n\n#### EnforcedStyle: skip_modifier_ifs (default)\n\n```ruby\n# bad\n[1, 2].each do |a|\n  if a == 1\n    puts a\n  end\nend\n\n# good\n[1, 2].each do |a|\n  next unless a == 1\n  puts a\nend\n\n# good\n[1, 2].each do |a|\n  puts a if a == 1\nend\n```\n#### EnforcedStyle: always\n\n```ruby\n# With `always` all conditions at the end of an iteration needs to be\n# replaced by next - with `skip_modifier_ifs` the modifier if like\n# this one are ignored: `[1, 2].each { |a| puts a if a == 1 }`\n\n# bad\n[1, 2].each do |a|\n  puts a if a == 1\nend\n\n# bad\n[1, 2].each do |a|\n  if a == 1\n    puts a\n  end\nend\n\n# good\n[1, 2].each do |a|\n  next unless a == 1\n  puts a\nend\n```\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nEnforcedStyle | `skip_modifier_ifs` | `skip_modifier_ifs`, `always`\nMinBodyLength | `3` | Integer\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#no-nested-conditionals](https://rubystyle.guide#no-nested-conditionals)\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Style/NilComparison",
              "name": "Style/NilComparison",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Prefer x.nil? to x == nil.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_style/#stylenilcomparison",
                "tags": [
                  "style"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Prefer x.nil? to x == nil."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Prefer x.nil? to x == nil."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.12 | 0.59\n\nThis cop checks for comparison of something with nil using `==` and\n`nil?`.\n\nSupported styles are: predicate, comparison.\n\n### Examples\n\n#### EnforcedStyle: predicate (default)\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nif x == nil\nend\n\n# good\nif x.nil?\nend\n```\n#### EnforcedStyle: comparison\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nif x.nil?\nend\n\n# good\nif x == nil\nend\n```\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nEnforcedStyle | `predicate` | `predicate`, `comparison`\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#predicate-methods](https://rubystyle.guide#predicate-methods)\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.12 | 0.59\n\nThis cop checks for comparison of something with nil using `==` and\n`nil?`.\n\nSupported styles are: predicate, comparison.\n\n### Examples\n\n#### EnforcedStyle: predicate (default)\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nif x == nil\nend\n\n# good\nif x.nil?\nend\n```\n#### EnforcedStyle: comparison\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nif x.nil?\nend\n\n# good\nif x == nil\nend\n```\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nEnforcedStyle | `predicate` | `predicate`, `comparison`\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#predicate-methods](https://rubystyle.guide#predicate-methods)\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Style/NonNilCheck",
              "name": "Style/NonNilCheck",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Checks for redundant nil checks.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_style/#stylenonnilcheck",
                "tags": [
                  "style"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Checks for redundant nil checks."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Checks for redundant nil checks."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.20 | 0.22\n\nThis cop checks for non-nil checks, which are usually redundant.\n\nWith `IncludeSemanticChanges` set to `false` by default, this cop\ndoes not report offenses for `!x.nil?` and does no changes that might\nchange behavior.\n\nWith `IncludeSemanticChanges` set to `true`, this cop reports offenses\nfor `!x.nil?` and autocorrects that and `x != nil` to solely `x`, which\nis **usually** OK, but might change behavior.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nif x != nil\nend\n\n# good\nif x\nend\n\n# Non-nil checks are allowed if they are the final nodes of predicate.\n# good\ndef signed_in?\n  !current_user.nil?\nend\n```\n#### IncludeSemanticChanges: false (default)\n\n```ruby\n# good\nif !x.nil?\nend\n```\n#### IncludeSemanticChanges: true\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nif !x.nil?\nend\n```\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nIncludeSemanticChanges | `false` | Boolean\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#no-non-nil-checks](https://rubystyle.guide#no-non-nil-checks)\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.20 | 0.22\n\nThis cop checks for non-nil checks, which are usually redundant.\n\nWith `IncludeSemanticChanges` set to `false` by default, this cop\ndoes not report offenses for `!x.nil?` and does no changes that might\nchange behavior.\n\nWith `IncludeSemanticChanges` set to `true`, this cop reports offenses\nfor `!x.nil?` and autocorrects that and `x != nil` to solely `x`, which\nis **usually** OK, but might change behavior.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nif x != nil\nend\n\n# good\nif x\nend\n\n# Non-nil checks are allowed if they are the final nodes of predicate.\n# good\ndef signed_in?\n  !current_user.nil?\nend\n```\n#### IncludeSemanticChanges: false (default)\n\n```ruby\n# good\nif !x.nil?\nend\n```\n#### IncludeSemanticChanges: true\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nif !x.nil?\nend\n```\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nIncludeSemanticChanges | `false` | Boolean\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#no-non-nil-checks](https://rubystyle.guide#no-non-nil-checks)\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Style/Not",
              "name": "Style/Not",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Use ! instead of not.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_style/#stylenot",
                "tags": [
                  "style"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Use ! instead of not."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Use ! instead of not."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.9 | 0.20\n\nThis cop checks for uses of the keyword `not` instead of `!`.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad - parentheses are required because of op precedence\nx = (not something)\n\n# good\nx = !something\n```\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#bang-not-not](https://rubystyle.guide#bang-not-not)\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.9 | 0.20\n\nThis cop checks for uses of the keyword `not` instead of `!`.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad - parentheses are required because of op precedence\nx = (not something)\n\n# good\nx = !something\n```\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#bang-not-not](https://rubystyle.guide#bang-not-not)\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Style/NumericLiteralPrefix",
              "name": "Style/NumericLiteralPrefix",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Use smallcase prefixes for numeric literals.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_style/#stylenumericliteralprefix",
                "tags": [
                  "style"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Use smallcase prefixes for numeric literals."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Use smallcase prefixes for numeric literals."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.41 | -\n\nThis cop checks for octal, hex, binary, and decimal literals using\nuppercase prefixes and corrects them to lowercase prefix\nor no prefix (in case of decimals).\n\n### Examples\n\n#### EnforcedOctalStyle: zero_with_o (default)\n\n```ruby\n# bad - missing octal prefix\nnum = 01234\n\n# bad - uppercase prefix\nnum = 0O1234\nnum = 0X12AB\nnum = 0B10101\n\n# bad - redundant decimal prefix\nnum = 0D1234\nnum = 0d1234\n\n# good\nnum = 0o1234\nnum = 0x12AB\nnum = 0b10101\nnum = 1234\n```\n#### EnforcedOctalStyle: zero_only\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nnum = 0o1234\nnum = 0O1234\n\n# good\nnum = 01234\n```\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nEnforcedOctalStyle | `zero_with_o` | `zero_with_o`, `zero_only`\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#numeric-literal-prefixes](https://rubystyle.guide#numeric-literal-prefixes)\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.41 | -\n\nThis cop checks for octal, hex, binary, and decimal literals using\nuppercase prefixes and corrects them to lowercase prefix\nor no prefix (in case of decimals).\n\n### Examples\n\n#### EnforcedOctalStyle: zero_with_o (default)\n\n```ruby\n# bad - missing octal prefix\nnum = 01234\n\n# bad - uppercase prefix\nnum = 0O1234\nnum = 0X12AB\nnum = 0B10101\n\n# bad - redundant decimal prefix\nnum = 0D1234\nnum = 0d1234\n\n# good\nnum = 0o1234\nnum = 0x12AB\nnum = 0b10101\nnum = 1234\n```\n#### EnforcedOctalStyle: zero_only\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nnum = 0o1234\nnum = 0O1234\n\n# good\nnum = 01234\n```\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nEnforcedOctalStyle | `zero_with_o` | `zero_with_o`, `zero_only`\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#numeric-literal-prefixes](https://rubystyle.guide#numeric-literal-prefixes)\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Style/NumericLiterals",
              "name": "Style/NumericLiterals",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Add underscores to large numeric literals to improve their readability.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_style/#stylenumericliterals",
                "tags": [
                  "style"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Add underscores to large numeric literals to improve their readability."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Add underscores to large numeric literals to improve their readability."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.9 | 0.48\n\nThis cop checks for big numeric literals without _ between groups\nof digits in them.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\n1000000\n1_00_000\n1_0000\n\n# good\n1_000_000\n1000\n```\n#### Strict: false (default)\n\n```ruby\n# good\n10_000_00 # typical representation of $10,000 in cents\n```\n#### Strict: true\n\n```ruby\n# bad\n10_000_00 # typical representation of $10,000 in cents\n```\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nMinDigits | `5` | Integer\nStrict | `false` | Boolean\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#underscores-in-numerics](https://rubystyle.guide#underscores-in-numerics)\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.9 | 0.48\n\nThis cop checks for big numeric literals without _ between groups\nof digits in them.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\n1000000\n1_00_000\n1_0000\n\n# good\n1_000_000\n1000\n```\n#### Strict: false (default)\n\n```ruby\n# good\n10_000_00 # typical representation of $10,000 in cents\n```\n#### Strict: true\n\n```ruby\n# bad\n10_000_00 # typical representation of $10,000 in cents\n```\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nMinDigits | `5` | Integer\nStrict | `false` | Boolean\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#underscores-in-numerics](https://rubystyle.guide#underscores-in-numerics)\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Style/NumericPredicate",
              "name": "Style/NumericPredicate",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Checks for the use of predicate- or comparison methods for numeric comparisons.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_style/#stylenumericpredicate",
                "tags": [
                  "style"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Checks for the use of predicate- or comparison methods for numeric comparisons."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Checks for the use of predicate- or comparison methods for numeric comparisons."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | No | Yes (Unsafe) | 0.42 | 0.59\n\nThis cop checks for usage of comparison operators (`==`,\n`>`, `<`) to test numbers as zero, positive, or negative.\nThese can be replaced by their respective predicate methods.\nThe cop can also be configured to do the reverse.\n\nThe cop disregards `#nonzero?` as it its value is truthy or falsey,\nbut not `true` and `false`, and thus not always interchangeable with\n`!= 0`.\n\nThe cop ignores comparisons to global variables, since they are often\npopulated with objects which can be compared with integers, but are\nnot themselves `Integer` polymorphic.\n\n### Examples\n\n#### EnforcedStyle: predicate (default)\n\n```ruby\n# bad\n\nfoo == 0\n0 > foo\nbar.baz > 0\n\n# good\n\nfoo.zero?\nfoo.negative?\nbar.baz.positive?\n```\n#### EnforcedStyle: comparison\n\n```ruby\n# bad\n\nfoo.zero?\nfoo.negative?\nbar.baz.positive?\n\n# good\n\nfoo == 0\n0 > foo\nbar.baz > 0\n```\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nAutoCorrect | `false` | Boolean\nEnforcedStyle | `predicate` | `predicate`, `comparison`\nIgnoredMethods | `[]` | Array\nExclude | `spec/**/*` | Array\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#predicate-methods](https://rubystyle.guide#predicate-methods)\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | No | Yes (Unsafe) | 0.42 | 0.59\n\nThis cop checks for usage of comparison operators (`==`,\n`>`, `<`) to test numbers as zero, positive, or negative.\nThese can be replaced by their respective predicate methods.\nThe cop can also be configured to do the reverse.\n\nThe cop disregards `#nonzero?` as it its value is truthy or falsey,\nbut not `true` and `false`, and thus not always interchangeable with\n`!= 0`.\n\nThe cop ignores comparisons to global variables, since they are often\npopulated with objects which can be compared with integers, but are\nnot themselves `Integer` polymorphic.\n\n### Examples\n\n#### EnforcedStyle: predicate (default)\n\n```ruby\n# bad\n\nfoo == 0\n0 > foo\nbar.baz > 0\n\n# good\n\nfoo.zero?\nfoo.negative?\nbar.baz.positive?\n```\n#### EnforcedStyle: comparison\n\n```ruby\n# bad\n\nfoo.zero?\nfoo.negative?\nbar.baz.positive?\n\n# good\n\nfoo == 0\n0 > foo\nbar.baz > 0\n```\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nAutoCorrect | `false` | Boolean\nEnforcedStyle | `predicate` | `predicate`, `comparison`\nIgnoredMethods | `[]` | Array\nExclude | `spec/**/*` | Array\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#predicate-methods](https://rubystyle.guide#predicate-methods)\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Style/OneLineConditional",
              "name": "Style/OneLineConditional",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Favor the ternary operator(?:) over if/then/else/end constructs.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_style/#styleonelineconditional",
                "tags": [
                  "style"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Favor the ternary operator(?:) over if/then/else/end constructs."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Favor the ternary operator(?:) over if/then/else/end constructs."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.9 | 0.38\n\nTODO: Make configurable.\nChecks for uses of if/then/else/end on a single line.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nif foo then boo else doo end\nunless foo then boo else goo end\n\n# good\nfoo ? boo : doo\nboo if foo\nif foo then boo end\n\n# good\nif foo\n  boo\nelse\n  doo\nend\n```\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#ternary-operator](https://rubystyle.guide#ternary-operator)\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.9 | 0.38\n\nTODO: Make configurable.\nChecks for uses of if/then/else/end on a single line.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nif foo then boo else doo end\nunless foo then boo else goo end\n\n# good\nfoo ? boo : doo\nboo if foo\nif foo then boo end\n\n# good\nif foo\n  boo\nelse\n  doo\nend\n```\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#ternary-operator](https://rubystyle.guide#ternary-operator)\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Style/OptionHash",
              "name": "Style/OptionHash",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Don't use option hashes when you can use keyword arguments.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_style/#styleoptionhash",
                "tags": [
                  "style"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Don't use option hashes when you can use keyword arguments."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Don't use option hashes when you can use keyword arguments."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nDisabled | Yes | No | 0.33 | 0.34\n\nThis cop checks for options hashes and discourages them if the\ncurrent Ruby version supports keyword arguments.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\ndef fry(options = {})\n  temperature = options.fetch(:temperature, 300)\n  # ...\nend\n\n# good\ndef fry(temperature: 300)\n  # ...\nend\n```\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nSuspiciousParamNames | `options`, `opts`, `args`, `params`, `parameters` | Array\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nDisabled | Yes | No | 0.33 | 0.34\n\nThis cop checks for options hashes and discourages them if the\ncurrent Ruby version supports keyword arguments.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\ndef fry(options = {})\n  temperature = options.fetch(:temperature, 300)\n  # ...\nend\n\n# good\ndef fry(temperature: 300)\n  # ...\nend\n```\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nSuspiciousParamNames | `options`, `opts`, `args`, `params`, `parameters` | Array\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Style/OptionalArguments",
              "name": "Style/OptionalArguments",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Checks for optional arguments that do not appear at the end of the argument list.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_style/#styleoptionalarguments",
                "tags": [
                  "style"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Checks for optional arguments that do not appear at the end of the argument list."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Checks for optional arguments that do not appear at the end of the argument list."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | No | 0.33 | -\n\nThis cop checks for optional arguments to methods\nthat do not come at the end of the argument list\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\ndef foo(a = 1, b, c)\nend\n\n# good\ndef baz(a, b, c = 1)\nend\n\ndef foobar(a = 1, b = 2, c = 3)\nend\n```\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#optional-arguments](https://rubystyle.guide#optional-arguments)\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | No | 0.33 | -\n\nThis cop checks for optional arguments to methods\nthat do not come at the end of the argument list\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\ndef foo(a = 1, b, c)\nend\n\n# good\ndef baz(a, b, c = 1)\nend\n\ndef foobar(a = 1, b = 2, c = 3)\nend\n```\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#optional-arguments](https://rubystyle.guide#optional-arguments)\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Style/OrAssignment",
              "name": "Style/OrAssignment",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Recommend usage of double pipe equals (||=) where applicable.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_style/#styleorassignment",
                "tags": [
                  "style"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Recommend usage of double pipe equals (||=) where applicable."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Recommend usage of double pipe equals (||=) where applicable."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.50 | -\n\nThis cop checks for potential usage of the `||=` operator.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nname = name ? name : 'Bozhidar'\n\n# bad\nname = if name\n         name\n       else\n         'Bozhidar'\n       end\n\n# bad\nunless name\n  name = 'Bozhidar'\nend\n\n# bad\nname = 'Bozhidar' unless name\n\n# good - set name to 'Bozhidar', only if it's nil or false\nname ||= 'Bozhidar'\n```\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#double-pipe-for-uninit](https://rubystyle.guide#double-pipe-for-uninit)\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.50 | -\n\nThis cop checks for potential usage of the `||=` operator.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nname = name ? name : 'Bozhidar'\n\n# bad\nname = if name\n         name\n       else\n         'Bozhidar'\n       end\n\n# bad\nunless name\n  name = 'Bozhidar'\nend\n\n# bad\nname = 'Bozhidar' unless name\n\n# good - set name to 'Bozhidar', only if it's nil or false\nname ||= 'Bozhidar'\n```\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#double-pipe-for-uninit](https://rubystyle.guide#double-pipe-for-uninit)\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Style/ParallelAssignment",
              "name": "Style/ParallelAssignment",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Check for simple usages of parallel assignment. It will only warn when the number of variables matches on both sides of the assignment.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_style/#styleparallelassignment",
                "tags": [
                  "style"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Check for simple usages of parallel assignment. It will only warn when the number of variables matches on both sides of the assignment."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Check for simple usages of parallel assignment. It will only warn when the number of variables matches on both sides of the assignment."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.32 | -\n\nChecks for simple usages of parallel assignment.\nThis will only complain when the number of variables\nbeing assigned matched the number of assigning variables.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\na, b, c = 1, 2, 3\na, b, c = [1, 2, 3]\n\n# good\none, two = *foo\na, b = foo()\na, b = b, a\n\na = 1\nb = 2\nc = 3\n```\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#parallel-assignment](https://rubystyle.guide#parallel-assignment)\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.32 | -\n\nChecks for simple usages of parallel assignment.\nThis will only complain when the number of variables\nbeing assigned matched the number of assigning variables.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\na, b, c = 1, 2, 3\na, b, c = [1, 2, 3]\n\n# good\none, two = *foo\na, b = foo()\na, b = b, a\n\na = 1\nb = 2\nc = 3\n```\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#parallel-assignment](https://rubystyle.guide#parallel-assignment)\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Style/ParenthesesAroundCondition",
              "name": "Style/ParenthesesAroundCondition",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Don't use parentheses around the condition of an if/unless/while.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_style/#styleparenthesesaroundcondition",
                "tags": [
                  "style"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Don't use parentheses around the condition of an if/unless/while."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Don't use parentheses around the condition of an if/unless/while."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.9 | 0.56\n\nThis cop checks for the presence of superfluous parentheses around the\ncondition of if/unless/while/until.\n\n`AllowSafeAssignment` option for safe assignment.\nBy safe assignment we mean putting parentheses around\nan assignment to indicate \"I know I'm using an assignment\nas a condition. It's not a mistake.\"\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nx += 1 while (x < 10)\nfoo unless (bar || baz)\n\nif (x > 10)\nelsif (x < 3)\nend\n\n# good\nx += 1 while x < 10\nfoo unless bar || baz\n\nif x > 10\nelsif x < 3\nend\n```\n#### AllowSafeAssignment: true (default)\n\n```ruby\n# good\nfoo unless (bar = baz)\n```\n#### AllowSafeAssignment: false\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nfoo unless (bar = baz)\n```\n#### AllowInMultilineConditions: false (default)\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nif (x > 10 &&\n   y > 10)\nend\n\n# good\n if x > 10 &&\n    y > 10\n end\n```\n#### AllowInMultilineConditions: true\n\n```ruby\n# good\nif (x > 10 &&\n   y > 10)\nend\n```\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nAllowSafeAssignment | `true` | Boolean\nAllowInMultilineConditions | `false` | Boolean\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#no-parens-around-condition](https://rubystyle.guide#no-parens-around-condition)\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.9 | 0.56\n\nThis cop checks for the presence of superfluous parentheses around the\ncondition of if/unless/while/until.\n\n`AllowSafeAssignment` option for safe assignment.\nBy safe assignment we mean putting parentheses around\nan assignment to indicate \"I know I'm using an assignment\nas a condition. It's not a mistake.\"\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nx += 1 while (x < 10)\nfoo unless (bar || baz)\n\nif (x > 10)\nelsif (x < 3)\nend\n\n# good\nx += 1 while x < 10\nfoo unless bar || baz\n\nif x > 10\nelsif x < 3\nend\n```\n#### AllowSafeAssignment: true (default)\n\n```ruby\n# good\nfoo unless (bar = baz)\n```\n#### AllowSafeAssignment: false\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nfoo unless (bar = baz)\n```\n#### AllowInMultilineConditions: false (default)\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nif (x > 10 &&\n   y > 10)\nend\n\n# good\n if x > 10 &&\n    y > 10\n end\n```\n#### AllowInMultilineConditions: true\n\n```ruby\n# good\nif (x > 10 &&\n   y > 10)\nend\n```\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nAllowSafeAssignment | `true` | Boolean\nAllowInMultilineConditions | `false` | Boolean\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#no-parens-around-condition](https://rubystyle.guide#no-parens-around-condition)\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Style/PercentLiteralDelimiters",
              "name": "Style/PercentLiteralDelimiters",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Use `%`-literal delimiters consistently.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_style/#stylepercentliteraldelimiters",
                "tags": [
                  "style"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Use `%`-literal delimiters consistently."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Use `%`-literal delimiters consistently."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.19 | 0.48.1\n\nThis cop enforces the consistent usage of `%`-literal delimiters.\n\nSpecify the 'default' key to set all preferred delimiters at once. You\ncan continue to specify individual preferred delimiters to override the\ndefault.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# Style/PercentLiteralDelimiters:\n#   PreferredDelimiters:\n#     default: '[]'\n#     '%i':    '()'\n\n# good\n%w[alpha beta] + %i(gamma delta)\n\n# bad\n%W(alpha #{beta})\n\n# bad\n%I(alpha beta)\n```\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nPreferredDelimiters | `{\"default\"=>\"()\", \"%i\"=>\"[]\", \"%I\"=>\"[]\", \"%r\"=>\"{}\", \"%w\"=>\"[]\", \"%W\"=>\"[]\"}` | \n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#percent-literal-braces](https://rubystyle.guide#percent-literal-braces)\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.19 | 0.48.1\n\nThis cop enforces the consistent usage of `%`-literal delimiters.\n\nSpecify the 'default' key to set all preferred delimiters at once. You\ncan continue to specify individual preferred delimiters to override the\ndefault.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# Style/PercentLiteralDelimiters:\n#   PreferredDelimiters:\n#     default: '[]'\n#     '%i':    '()'\n\n# good\n%w[alpha beta] + %i(gamma delta)\n\n# bad\n%W(alpha #{beta})\n\n# bad\n%I(alpha beta)\n```\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nPreferredDelimiters | `{\"default\"=>\"()\", \"%i\"=>\"[]\", \"%I\"=>\"[]\", \"%r\"=>\"{}\", \"%w\"=>\"[]\", \"%W\"=>\"[]\"}` | \n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#percent-literal-braces](https://rubystyle.guide#percent-literal-braces)\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Style/PercentQLiterals",
              "name": "Style/PercentQLiterals",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Checks if uses of %Q/%q match the configured preference.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_style/#stylepercentqliterals",
                "tags": [
                  "style"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Checks if uses of %Q/%q match the configured preference."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Checks if uses of %Q/%q match the configured preference."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.25 | -\n\nThis cop checks for usage of the %Q() syntax when %q() would do.\n\n### Examples\n\n#### EnforcedStyle: lower_case_q (default)\n\n```ruby\n# The `lower_case_q` style prefers `%q` unless\n# interpolation is needed.\n# bad\n%Q[Mix the foo into the baz.]\n%Q(They all said: 'Hooray!')\n\n# good\n%q[Mix the foo into the baz]\n%q(They all said: 'Hooray!')\n```\n#### EnforcedStyle: upper_case_q\n\n```ruby\n# The `upper_case_q` style requires the sole use of `%Q`.\n# bad\n%q/Mix the foo into the baz./\n%q{They all said: 'Hooray!'}\n\n# good\n%Q/Mix the foo into the baz./\n%Q{They all said: 'Hooray!'}\n```\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nEnforcedStyle | `lower_case_q` | `lower_case_q`, `upper_case_q`\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.25 | -\n\nThis cop checks for usage of the %Q() syntax when %q() would do.\n\n### Examples\n\n#### EnforcedStyle: lower_case_q (default)\n\n```ruby\n# The `lower_case_q` style prefers `%q` unless\n# interpolation is needed.\n# bad\n%Q[Mix the foo into the baz.]\n%Q(They all said: 'Hooray!')\n\n# good\n%q[Mix the foo into the baz]\n%q(They all said: 'Hooray!')\n```\n#### EnforcedStyle: upper_case_q\n\n```ruby\n# The `upper_case_q` style requires the sole use of `%Q`.\n# bad\n%q/Mix the foo into the baz./\n%q{They all said: 'Hooray!'}\n\n# good\n%Q/Mix the foo into the baz./\n%Q{They all said: 'Hooray!'}\n```\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nEnforcedStyle | `lower_case_q` | `lower_case_q`, `upper_case_q`\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Style/PerlBackrefs",
              "name": "Style/PerlBackrefs",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Avoid Perl-style regex back references.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_style/#styleperlbackrefs",
                "tags": [
                  "style"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Avoid Perl-style regex back references."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Avoid Perl-style regex back references."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.13 | -\n\nThis cop looks for uses of Perl-style regexp match\nbackreferences like $1, $2, etc.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nputs $1\n\n# good\nputs Regexp.last_match(1)\n```\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#no-perl-regexp-last-matchers](https://rubystyle.guide#no-perl-regexp-last-matchers)\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.13 | -\n\nThis cop looks for uses of Perl-style regexp match\nbackreferences like $1, $2, etc.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nputs $1\n\n# good\nputs Regexp.last_match(1)\n```\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#no-perl-regexp-last-matchers](https://rubystyle.guide#no-perl-regexp-last-matchers)\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Style/PreferredHashMethods",
              "name": "Style/PreferredHashMethods",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Checks use of `has_key?` and `has_value?` Hash methods.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_style/#stylepreferredhashmethods",
                "tags": [
                  "style"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Checks use of `has_key?` and `has_value?` Hash methods."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Checks use of `has_key?` and `has_value?` Hash methods."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | No | Yes (Unsafe) | 0.41 | 0.70\n\nThis cop (by default) checks for uses of methods Hash#has_key? and\nHash#has_value? where it enforces Hash#key? and Hash#value?\nIt is configurable to enforce the inverse, using `verbose` method\nnames also.\n\n### Examples\n\n#### EnforcedStyle: short (default)\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nHash#has_key?\nHash#has_value?\n\n# good\nHash#key?\nHash#value?\n```\n#### EnforcedStyle: verbose\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nHash#key?\nHash#value?\n\n# good\nHash#has_key?\nHash#has_value?\n```\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nEnforcedStyle | `short` | `short`, `verbose`\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#hash-key](https://rubystyle.guide#hash-key)\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | No | Yes (Unsafe) | 0.41 | 0.70\n\nThis cop (by default) checks for uses of methods Hash#has_key? and\nHash#has_value? where it enforces Hash#key? and Hash#value?\nIt is configurable to enforce the inverse, using `verbose` method\nnames also.\n\n### Examples\n\n#### EnforcedStyle: short (default)\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nHash#has_key?\nHash#has_value?\n\n# good\nHash#key?\nHash#value?\n```\n#### EnforcedStyle: verbose\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nHash#key?\nHash#value?\n\n# good\nHash#has_key?\nHash#has_value?\n```\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nEnforcedStyle | `short` | `short`, `verbose`\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#hash-key](https://rubystyle.guide#hash-key)\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Style/Proc",
              "name": "Style/Proc",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Use proc instead of Proc.new.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_style/#styleproc",
                "tags": [
                  "style"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Use proc instead of Proc.new."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Use proc instead of Proc.new."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.9 | 0.18\n\nThis cop checks for uses of Proc.new where Kernel#proc\nwould be more appropriate.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\np = Proc.new { |n| puts n }\n\n# good\np = proc { |n| puts n }\n```\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#proc](https://rubystyle.guide#proc)\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.9 | 0.18\n\nThis cop checks for uses of Proc.new where Kernel#proc\nwould be more appropriate.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\np = Proc.new { |n| puts n }\n\n# good\np = proc { |n| puts n }\n```\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#proc](https://rubystyle.guide#proc)\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Style/RaiseArgs",
              "name": "Style/RaiseArgs",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Checks the arguments passed to raise/fail.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_style/#styleraiseargs",
                "tags": [
                  "style"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Checks the arguments passed to raise/fail."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Checks the arguments passed to raise/fail."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.14 | 0.40\n\nThis cop checks the args passed to `fail` and `raise`. For exploded\nstyle (default), it recommends passing the exception class and message\nto `raise`, rather than construct an instance of the error. It will\nstill allow passing just a message, or the construction of an error\nwith more than one argument.\n\nThe exploded style works identically, but with the addition that it\nwill also suggest constructing error objects when the exception is\npassed multiple arguments.\n\n### Examples\n\n#### EnforcedStyle: exploded (default)\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nraise StandardError.new(\"message\")\n\n# good\nraise StandardError, \"message\"\nfail \"message\"\nraise MyCustomError.new(arg1, arg2, arg3)\nraise MyKwArgError.new(key1: val1, key2: val2)\n```\n#### EnforcedStyle: compact\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nraise StandardError, \"message\"\nraise RuntimeError, arg1, arg2, arg3\n\n# good\nraise StandardError.new(\"message\")\nraise MyCustomError.new(arg1, arg2, arg3)\nfail \"message\"\n```\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nEnforcedStyle | `exploded` | `compact`, `exploded`\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#exception-class-messages](https://rubystyle.guide#exception-class-messages)\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.14 | 0.40\n\nThis cop checks the args passed to `fail` and `raise`. For exploded\nstyle (default), it recommends passing the exception class and message\nto `raise`, rather than construct an instance of the error. It will\nstill allow passing just a message, or the construction of an error\nwith more than one argument.\n\nThe exploded style works identically, but with the addition that it\nwill also suggest constructing error objects when the exception is\npassed multiple arguments.\n\n### Examples\n\n#### EnforcedStyle: exploded (default)\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nraise StandardError.new(\"message\")\n\n# good\nraise StandardError, \"message\"\nfail \"message\"\nraise MyCustomError.new(arg1, arg2, arg3)\nraise MyKwArgError.new(key1: val1, key2: val2)\n```\n#### EnforcedStyle: compact\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nraise StandardError, \"message\"\nraise RuntimeError, arg1, arg2, arg3\n\n# good\nraise StandardError.new(\"message\")\nraise MyCustomError.new(arg1, arg2, arg3)\nfail \"message\"\n```\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nEnforcedStyle | `exploded` | `compact`, `exploded`\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#exception-class-messages](https://rubystyle.guide#exception-class-messages)\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Style/RandomWithOffset",
              "name": "Style/RandomWithOffset",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Prefer to use ranges when generating random numbers instead of integers with offsets.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_style/#stylerandomwithoffset",
                "tags": [
                  "style"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Prefer to use ranges when generating random numbers instead of integers with offsets."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Prefer to use ranges when generating random numbers instead of integers with offsets."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.52 | -\n\nThis cop checks for the use of randomly generated numbers,\nadded/subtracted with integer literals, as well as those with\nInteger#succ and Integer#pred methods. Prefer using ranges instead,\nas it clearly states the intentions.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nrand(6) + 1\n1 + rand(6)\nrand(6) - 1\n1 - rand(6)\nrand(6).succ\nrand(6).pred\nRandom.rand(6) + 1\nKernel.rand(6) + 1\nrand(0..5) + 1\n\n# good\nrand(1..6)\nrand(1...7)\n```\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#random-numbers](https://rubystyle.guide#random-numbers)\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.52 | -\n\nThis cop checks for the use of randomly generated numbers,\nadded/subtracted with integer literals, as well as those with\nInteger#succ and Integer#pred methods. Prefer using ranges instead,\nas it clearly states the intentions.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nrand(6) + 1\n1 + rand(6)\nrand(6) - 1\n1 - rand(6)\nrand(6).succ\nrand(6).pred\nRandom.rand(6) + 1\nKernel.rand(6) + 1\nrand(0..5) + 1\n\n# good\nrand(1..6)\nrand(1...7)\n```\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#random-numbers](https://rubystyle.guide#random-numbers)\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Style/RedundantBegin",
              "name": "Style/RedundantBegin",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Don't use begin blocks when they are not needed.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_style/#styleredundantbegin",
                "tags": [
                  "style"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Don't use begin blocks when they are not needed."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Don't use begin blocks when they are not needed."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.10 | 0.21\n\nThis cop checks for redundant `begin` blocks.\n\nCurrently it checks for code like this:\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\ndef redundant\n  begin\n    ala\n    bala\n  rescue StandardError => e\n    something\n  end\nend\n\n# good\ndef preferred\n  ala\n  bala\nrescue StandardError => e\n  something\nend\n\n# bad\n# When using Ruby 2.5 or later.\ndo_something do\n  begin\n    something\n  rescue => ex\n    anything\n  end\nend\n\n# good\n# In Ruby 2.5 or later, you can omit `begin` in `do-end` block.\ndo_something do\n  something\nrescue => ex\n  anything\nend\n\n# good\n# Stabby lambdas don't support implicit `begin` in `do-end` blocks.\n-> do\n  begin\n    foo\n  rescue Bar\n    baz\n  end\nend\n```\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#begin-implicit](https://rubystyle.guide#begin-implicit)\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.10 | 0.21\n\nThis cop checks for redundant `begin` blocks.\n\nCurrently it checks for code like this:\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\ndef redundant\n  begin\n    ala\n    bala\n  rescue StandardError => e\n    something\n  end\nend\n\n# good\ndef preferred\n  ala\n  bala\nrescue StandardError => e\n  something\nend\n\n# bad\n# When using Ruby 2.5 or later.\ndo_something do\n  begin\n    something\n  rescue => ex\n    anything\n  end\nend\n\n# good\n# In Ruby 2.5 or later, you can omit `begin` in `do-end` block.\ndo_something do\n  something\nrescue => ex\n  anything\nend\n\n# good\n# Stabby lambdas don't support implicit `begin` in `do-end` blocks.\n-> do\n  begin\n    foo\n  rescue Bar\n    baz\n  end\nend\n```\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#begin-implicit](https://rubystyle.guide#begin-implicit)\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Style/RedundantCapitalW",
              "name": "Style/RedundantCapitalW",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Checks for %W when interpolation is not needed.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_style/#styleredundantcapitalw",
                "tags": [
                  "style"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Checks for %W when interpolation is not needed."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Checks for %W when interpolation is not needed."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.76 | -\n\nThis cop checks for usage of the %W() syntax when %w() would do.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\n%W(cat dog pig)\n%W[door wall floor]\n\n# good\n%w/swim run bike/\n%w[shirt pants shoes]\n%W(apple #{fruit} grape)\n```\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.76 | -\n\nThis cop checks for usage of the %W() syntax when %w() would do.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\n%W(cat dog pig)\n%W[door wall floor]\n\n# good\n%w/swim run bike/\n%w[shirt pants shoes]\n%W(apple #{fruit} grape)\n```\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Style/RedundantCondition",
              "name": "Style/RedundantCondition",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Checks for unnecessary conditional expressions.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_style/#styleredundantcondition",
                "tags": [
                  "style"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Checks for unnecessary conditional expressions."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Checks for unnecessary conditional expressions."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.76 | -\n\nThis cop checks for unnecessary conditional expressions.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\na = b ? b : c\n\n# good\na = b || c\n```\n```ruby\n# bad\nif b\n  b\nelse\n  c\nend\n\n# good\nb || c\n\n# good\nif b\n  b\nelsif cond\n  c\nend\n```\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.76 | -\n\nThis cop checks for unnecessary conditional expressions.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\na = b ? b : c\n\n# good\na = b || c\n```\n```ruby\n# bad\nif b\n  b\nelse\n  c\nend\n\n# good\nb || c\n\n# good\nif b\n  b\nelsif cond\n  c\nend\n```\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Style/RedundantConditional",
              "name": "Style/RedundantConditional",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Don't return true/false from a conditional.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_style/#styleredundantconditional",
                "tags": [
                  "style"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Don't return true/false from a conditional."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Don't return true/false from a conditional."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.50 | -\n\nThis cop checks for redundant returning of true/false in conditionals.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nx == y ? true : false\n\n# bad\nif x == y\n  true\nelse\n  false\nend\n\n# good\nx == y\n\n# bad\nx == y ? false : true\n\n# good\nx != y\n```\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.50 | -\n\nThis cop checks for redundant returning of true/false in conditionals.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nx == y ? true : false\n\n# bad\nif x == y\n  true\nelse\n  false\nend\n\n# good\nx == y\n\n# bad\nx == y ? false : true\n\n# good\nx != y\n```\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Style/RedundantException",
              "name": "Style/RedundantException",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Checks for an obsolete RuntimeException argument in raise/fail.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_style/#styleredundantexception",
                "tags": [
                  "style"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Checks for an obsolete RuntimeException argument in raise/fail."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Checks for an obsolete RuntimeException argument in raise/fail."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.14 | 0.29\n\nThis cop checks for RuntimeError as the argument of raise/fail.\n\nIt checks for code like this:\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# Bad\nraise RuntimeError, 'message'\n\n# Bad\nraise RuntimeError.new('message')\n\n# Good\nraise 'message'\n```\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#no-explicit-runtimeerror](https://rubystyle.guide#no-explicit-runtimeerror)\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.14 | 0.29\n\nThis cop checks for RuntimeError as the argument of raise/fail.\n\nIt checks for code like this:\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# Bad\nraise RuntimeError, 'message'\n\n# Bad\nraise RuntimeError.new('message')\n\n# Good\nraise 'message'\n```\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#no-explicit-runtimeerror](https://rubystyle.guide#no-explicit-runtimeerror)\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Style/RedundantFreeze",
              "name": "Style/RedundantFreeze",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Checks usages of Object#freeze on immutable objects.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_style/#styleredundantfreeze",
                "tags": [
                  "style"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Checks usages of Object#freeze on immutable objects."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Checks usages of Object#freeze on immutable objects."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.34 | 0.66\n\nThis cop check for uses of Object#freeze on immutable objects.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nCONST = 1.freeze\n\n# good\nCONST = 1\n```\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.34 | 0.66\n\nThis cop check for uses of Object#freeze on immutable objects.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nCONST = 1.freeze\n\n# good\nCONST = 1\n```\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Style/RedundantInterpolation",
              "name": "Style/RedundantInterpolation",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Checks for strings that are just an interpolated expression.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_style/#styleredundantinterpolation",
                "tags": [
                  "style"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Checks for strings that are just an interpolated expression."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Checks for strings that are just an interpolated expression."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.76 | -\n\nThis cop checks for strings that are just an interpolated expression.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\n\"#{@var}\"\n\n# good\n@var.to_s\n\n# good if @var is already a String\n@var\n```\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.76 | -\n\nThis cop checks for strings that are just an interpolated expression.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\n\"#{@var}\"\n\n# good\n@var.to_s\n\n# good if @var is already a String\n@var\n```\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Style/RedundantParentheses",
              "name": "Style/RedundantParentheses",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Checks for parentheses that seem not to serve any purpose.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_style/#styleredundantparentheses",
                "tags": [
                  "style"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Checks for parentheses that seem not to serve any purpose."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Checks for parentheses that seem not to serve any purpose."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.36 | -\n\nThis cop checks for redundant parentheses.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\n(x) if ((y.z).nil?)\n\n# good\nx if y.z.nil?\n```\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.36 | -\n\nThis cop checks for redundant parentheses.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\n(x) if ((y.z).nil?)\n\n# good\nx if y.z.nil?\n```\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Style/RedundantPercentQ",
              "name": "Style/RedundantPercentQ",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Checks for %q/%Q when single quotes or double quotes would do.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_style/#styleredundantpercentq",
                "tags": [
                  "style"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Checks for %q/%Q when single quotes or double quotes would do."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Checks for %q/%Q when single quotes or double quotes would do."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.76 | -\n\nThis cop checks for usage of the %q/%Q syntax when '' or \"\" would do.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nname = %q(Bruce Wayne)\ntime = %q(8 o'clock)\nquestion = %q(\"What did you say?\")\n\n# good\nname = 'Bruce Wayne'\ntime = \"8 o'clock\"\nquestion = '\"What did you say?\"'\n```\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#percent-q](https://rubystyle.guide#percent-q)\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.76 | -\n\nThis cop checks for usage of the %q/%Q syntax when '' or \"\" would do.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nname = %q(Bruce Wayne)\ntime = %q(8 o'clock)\nquestion = %q(\"What did you say?\")\n\n# good\nname = 'Bruce Wayne'\ntime = \"8 o'clock\"\nquestion = '\"What did you say?\"'\n```\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#percent-q](https://rubystyle.guide#percent-q)\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Style/RedundantReturn",
              "name": "Style/RedundantReturn",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Don't use return where it's not required.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_style/#styleredundantreturn",
                "tags": [
                  "style"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Don't use return where it's not required."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Don't use return where it's not required."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.10 | 0.14\n\nThis cop checks for redundant `return` expressions.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# These bad cases should be extended to handle methods whose body is\n# if/else or a case expression with a default branch.\n\n# bad\ndef test\n  return something\nend\n\n# bad\ndef test\n  one\n  two\n  three\n  return something\nend\n\n# good\ndef test\n  return something if something_else\nend\n\n# good\ndef test\n  if x\n  elsif y\n  else\n  end\nend\n```\n#### AllowMultipleReturnValues: false (default)\n\n```ruby\n# bad\ndef test\n  return x, y\nend\n```\n#### AllowMultipleReturnValues: true\n\n```ruby\n# good\ndef test\n  return x, y\nend\n```\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nAllowMultipleReturnValues | `false` | Boolean\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#no-explicit-return](https://rubystyle.guide#no-explicit-return)\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.10 | 0.14\n\nThis cop checks for redundant `return` expressions.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# These bad cases should be extended to handle methods whose body is\n# if/else or a case expression with a default branch.\n\n# bad\ndef test\n  return something\nend\n\n# bad\ndef test\n  one\n  two\n  three\n  return something\nend\n\n# good\ndef test\n  return something if something_else\nend\n\n# good\ndef test\n  if x\n  elsif y\n  else\n  end\nend\n```\n#### AllowMultipleReturnValues: false (default)\n\n```ruby\n# bad\ndef test\n  return x, y\nend\n```\n#### AllowMultipleReturnValues: true\n\n```ruby\n# good\ndef test\n  return x, y\nend\n```\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nAllowMultipleReturnValues | `false` | Boolean\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#no-explicit-return](https://rubystyle.guide#no-explicit-return)\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Style/RedundantSelf",
              "name": "Style/RedundantSelf",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Don't use self where it's not needed.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_style/#styleredundantself",
                "tags": [
                  "style"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Don't use self where it's not needed."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Don't use self where it's not needed."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.10 | 0.13\n\nThis cop checks for redundant uses of `self`.\n\nThe usage of `self` is only needed when:\n\n* Sending a message to same object with zero arguments in\n  presence of a method name clash with an argument or a local\n  variable.\n\n* Calling an attribute writer to prevent an local variable assignment.\n\nNote, with using explicit self you can only send messages with public or\nprotected scope, you cannot send private messages this way.\n\nNote we allow uses of `self` with operators because it would be awkward\notherwise.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\ndef foo(bar)\n  self.baz\nend\n\n# good\ndef foo(bar)\n  self.bar  # Resolves name clash with the argument.\nend\n\ndef foo\n  bar = 1\n  self.bar  # Resolves name clash with the local variable.\nend\n\ndef foo\n  %w[x y z].select do |bar|\n    self.bar == bar  # Resolves name clash with argument of the block.\n  end\nend\n```\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#no-self-unless-required](https://rubystyle.guide#no-self-unless-required)\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.10 | 0.13\n\nThis cop checks for redundant uses of `self`.\n\nThe usage of `self` is only needed when:\n\n* Sending a message to same object with zero arguments in\n  presence of a method name clash with an argument or a local\n  variable.\n\n* Calling an attribute writer to prevent an local variable assignment.\n\nNote, with using explicit self you can only send messages with public or\nprotected scope, you cannot send private messages this way.\n\nNote we allow uses of `self` with operators because it would be awkward\notherwise.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\ndef foo(bar)\n  self.baz\nend\n\n# good\ndef foo(bar)\n  self.bar  # Resolves name clash with the argument.\nend\n\ndef foo\n  bar = 1\n  self.bar  # Resolves name clash with the local variable.\nend\n\ndef foo\n  %w[x y z].select do |bar|\n    self.bar == bar  # Resolves name clash with argument of the block.\n  end\nend\n```\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#no-self-unless-required](https://rubystyle.guide#no-self-unless-required)\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Style/RedundantSort",
              "name": "Style/RedundantSort",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Use `min` instead of `sort.first`, `max_by` instead of `sort_by...last`, etc.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_style/#styleredundantsort",
                "tags": [
                  "style"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Use `min` instead of `sort.first`, `max_by` instead of `sort_by...last`, etc."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Use `min` instead of `sort.first`, `max_by` instead of `sort_by...last`, etc."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.76 | -\n\nThis cop is used to identify instances of sorting and then\ntaking only the first or last element. The same behavior can\nbe accomplished without a relatively expensive sort by using\n`Enumerable#min` instead of sorting and taking the first\nelement and `Enumerable#max` instead of sorting and taking the\nlast element. Similarly, `Enumerable#min_by` and\n`Enumerable#max_by` can replace `Enumerable#sort_by` calls\nafter which only the first or last element is used.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\n[2, 1, 3].sort.first\n[2, 1, 3].sort[0]\n[2, 1, 3].sort.at(0)\n[2, 1, 3].sort.slice(0)\n\n# good\n[2, 1, 3].min\n\n# bad\n[2, 1, 3].sort.last\n[2, 1, 3].sort[-1]\n[2, 1, 3].sort.at(-1)\n[2, 1, 3].sort.slice(-1)\n\n# good\n[2, 1, 3].max\n\n# bad\narr.sort_by(&:foo).first\narr.sort_by(&:foo)[0]\narr.sort_by(&:foo).at(0)\narr.sort_by(&:foo).slice(0)\n\n# good\narr.min_by(&:foo)\n\n# bad\narr.sort_by(&:foo).last\narr.sort_by(&:foo)[-1]\narr.sort_by(&:foo).at(-1)\narr.sort_by(&:foo).slice(-1)\n\n# good\narr.max_by(&:foo)\n```\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.76 | -\n\nThis cop is used to identify instances of sorting and then\ntaking only the first or last element. The same behavior can\nbe accomplished without a relatively expensive sort by using\n`Enumerable#min` instead of sorting and taking the first\nelement and `Enumerable#max` instead of sorting and taking the\nlast element. Similarly, `Enumerable#min_by` and\n`Enumerable#max_by` can replace `Enumerable#sort_by` calls\nafter which only the first or last element is used.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\n[2, 1, 3].sort.first\n[2, 1, 3].sort[0]\n[2, 1, 3].sort.at(0)\n[2, 1, 3].sort.slice(0)\n\n# good\n[2, 1, 3].min\n\n# bad\n[2, 1, 3].sort.last\n[2, 1, 3].sort[-1]\n[2, 1, 3].sort.at(-1)\n[2, 1, 3].sort.slice(-1)\n\n# good\n[2, 1, 3].max\n\n# bad\narr.sort_by(&:foo).first\narr.sort_by(&:foo)[0]\narr.sort_by(&:foo).at(0)\narr.sort_by(&:foo).slice(0)\n\n# good\narr.min_by(&:foo)\n\n# bad\narr.sort_by(&:foo).last\narr.sort_by(&:foo)[-1]\narr.sort_by(&:foo).at(-1)\narr.sort_by(&:foo).slice(-1)\n\n# good\narr.max_by(&:foo)\n```\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Style/RedundantSortBy",
              "name": "Style/RedundantSortBy",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Use `sort` instead of `sort_by { |x| x }`.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_style/#styleredundantsortby",
                "tags": [
                  "style"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Use `sort` instead of `sort_by { |x| x }`."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Use `sort` instead of `sort_by { |x| x }`."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.36 | -\n\nThis cop identifies places where `sort_by { ... }` can be replaced by\n`sort`.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\narray.sort_by { |x| x }\narray.sort_by do |var|\n  var\nend\n\n# good\narray.sort\n```\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.36 | -\n\nThis cop identifies places where `sort_by { ... }` can be replaced by\n`sort`.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\narray.sort_by { |x| x }\narray.sort_by do |var|\n  var\nend\n\n# good\narray.sort\n```\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Style/RegexpLiteral",
              "name": "Style/RegexpLiteral",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Use / or %r around regular expressions.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_style/#styleregexpliteral",
                "tags": [
                  "style"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Use / or %r around regular expressions."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Use / or %r around regular expressions."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.9 | 0.30\n\nThis cop enforces using // or %r around regular expressions.\n\n### Examples\n\n#### EnforcedStyle: slashes (default)\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nsnake_case = %r{^[\\dA-Z_]+$}\n\n# bad\nregex = %r{\n  foo\n  (bar)\n  (baz)\n}x\n\n# good\nsnake_case = /^[\\dA-Z_]+$/\n\n# good\nregex = /\n  foo\n  (bar)\n  (baz)\n/x\n```\n#### EnforcedStyle: percent_r\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nsnake_case = /^[\\dA-Z_]+$/\n\n# bad\nregex = /\n  foo\n  (bar)\n  (baz)\n/x\n\n# good\nsnake_case = %r{^[\\dA-Z_]+$}\n\n# good\nregex = %r{\n  foo\n  (bar)\n  (baz)\n}x\n```\n#### EnforcedStyle: mixed\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nsnake_case = %r{^[\\dA-Z_]+$}\n\n# bad\nregex = /\n  foo\n  (bar)\n  (baz)\n/x\n\n# good\nsnake_case = /^[\\dA-Z_]+$/\n\n# good\nregex = %r{\n  foo\n  (bar)\n  (baz)\n}x\n```\n#### AllowInnerSlashes: false (default)\n\n```ruby\n# If `false`, the cop will always recommend using `%r` if one or more\n# slashes are found in the regexp string.\n\n# bad\nx =~ /home\\//\n\n# good\nx =~ %r{home/}\n```\n#### AllowInnerSlashes: true\n\n```ruby\n# good\nx =~ /home\\//\n```\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nEnforcedStyle | `slashes` | `slashes`, `percent_r`, `mixed`\nAllowInnerSlashes | `false` | Boolean\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#percent-r](https://rubystyle.guide#percent-r)\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.9 | 0.30\n\nThis cop enforces using // or %r around regular expressions.\n\n### Examples\n\n#### EnforcedStyle: slashes (default)\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nsnake_case = %r{^[\\dA-Z_]+$}\n\n# bad\nregex = %r{\n  foo\n  (bar)\n  (baz)\n}x\n\n# good\nsnake_case = /^[\\dA-Z_]+$/\n\n# good\nregex = /\n  foo\n  (bar)\n  (baz)\n/x\n```\n#### EnforcedStyle: percent_r\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nsnake_case = /^[\\dA-Z_]+$/\n\n# bad\nregex = /\n  foo\n  (bar)\n  (baz)\n/x\n\n# good\nsnake_case = %r{^[\\dA-Z_]+$}\n\n# good\nregex = %r{\n  foo\n  (bar)\n  (baz)\n}x\n```\n#### EnforcedStyle: mixed\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nsnake_case = %r{^[\\dA-Z_]+$}\n\n# bad\nregex = /\n  foo\n  (bar)\n  (baz)\n/x\n\n# good\nsnake_case = /^[\\dA-Z_]+$/\n\n# good\nregex = %r{\n  foo\n  (bar)\n  (baz)\n}x\n```\n#### AllowInnerSlashes: false (default)\n\n```ruby\n# If `false`, the cop will always recommend using `%r` if one or more\n# slashes are found in the regexp string.\n\n# bad\nx =~ /home\\//\n\n# good\nx =~ %r{home/}\n```\n#### AllowInnerSlashes: true\n\n```ruby\n# good\nx =~ /home\\//\n```\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nEnforcedStyle | `slashes` | `slashes`, `percent_r`, `mixed`\nAllowInnerSlashes | `false` | Boolean\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#percent-r](https://rubystyle.guide#percent-r)\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Style/RescueModifier",
              "name": "Style/RescueModifier",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Avoid using rescue in its modifier form.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_style/#stylerescuemodifier",
                "tags": [
                  "style"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Avoid using rescue in its modifier form."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Avoid using rescue in its modifier form."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.9 | 0.34\n\nThis cop checks for uses of rescue in its modifier form.\n\nThe cop to check `rescue` in its modifier form is added for following\nreasons:\n\n* The syntax of modifier form `rescue` can be misleading because it\n  might led us to believe that `rescue` handles the given exception\n  but it actually rescue all exceptions to return the given rescue\n  block. In this case, value returned by handle_error or\n  SomeException.\n\n* Modifier form `rescue` would rescue all the exceptions. It would\n  silently skip all exception or errors and handle the error.\n  Example: If `NoMethodError` is raised, modifier form rescue would\n  handle the exception.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nsome_method rescue handle_error\n\n# bad\nsome_method rescue SomeException\n\n# good\nbegin\n  some_method\nrescue\n  handle_error\nend\n\n# good\nbegin\n  some_method\nrescue SomeException\n  handle_error\nend\n```\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#no-rescue-modifiers](https://rubystyle.guide#no-rescue-modifiers)\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.9 | 0.34\n\nThis cop checks for uses of rescue in its modifier form.\n\nThe cop to check `rescue` in its modifier form is added for following\nreasons:\n\n* The syntax of modifier form `rescue` can be misleading because it\n  might led us to believe that `rescue` handles the given exception\n  but it actually rescue all exceptions to return the given rescue\n  block. In this case, value returned by handle_error or\n  SomeException.\n\n* Modifier form `rescue` would rescue all the exceptions. It would\n  silently skip all exception or errors and handle the error.\n  Example: If `NoMethodError` is raised, modifier form rescue would\n  handle the exception.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nsome_method rescue handle_error\n\n# bad\nsome_method rescue SomeException\n\n# good\nbegin\n  some_method\nrescue\n  handle_error\nend\n\n# good\nbegin\n  some_method\nrescue SomeException\n  handle_error\nend\n```\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#no-rescue-modifiers](https://rubystyle.guide#no-rescue-modifiers)\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Style/RescueStandardError",
              "name": "Style/RescueStandardError",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Avoid rescuing without specifying an error class.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_style/#stylerescuestandarderror",
                "tags": [
                  "style"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Avoid rescuing without specifying an error class."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Avoid rescuing without specifying an error class."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.52 | -\n\nThis cop checks for rescuing `StandardError`. There are two supported\nstyles `implicit` and `explicit`. This cop will not register an offense\nif any error other than `StandardError` is specified.\n\n### Examples\n\n#### EnforcedStyle: implicit\n\n```ruby\n# `implicit` will enforce using `rescue` instead of\n# `rescue StandardError`.\n\n# bad\nbegin\n  foo\nrescue StandardError\n  bar\nend\n\n# good\nbegin\n  foo\nrescue\n  bar\nend\n\n# good\nbegin\n  foo\nrescue OtherError\n  bar\nend\n\n# good\nbegin\n  foo\nrescue StandardError, SecurityError\n  bar\nend\n```\n#### EnforcedStyle: explicit (default)\n\n```ruby\n# `explicit` will enforce using `rescue StandardError`\n# instead of `rescue`.\n\n# bad\nbegin\n  foo\nrescue\n  bar\nend\n\n# good\nbegin\n  foo\nrescue StandardError\n  bar\nend\n\n# good\nbegin\n  foo\nrescue OtherError\n  bar\nend\n\n# good\nbegin\n  foo\nrescue StandardError, SecurityError\n  bar\nend\n```\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nEnforcedStyle | `explicit` | `implicit`, `explicit`\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.52 | -\n\nThis cop checks for rescuing `StandardError`. There are two supported\nstyles `implicit` and `explicit`. This cop will not register an offense\nif any error other than `StandardError` is specified.\n\n### Examples\n\n#### EnforcedStyle: implicit\n\n```ruby\n# `implicit` will enforce using `rescue` instead of\n# `rescue StandardError`.\n\n# bad\nbegin\n  foo\nrescue StandardError\n  bar\nend\n\n# good\nbegin\n  foo\nrescue\n  bar\nend\n\n# good\nbegin\n  foo\nrescue OtherError\n  bar\nend\n\n# good\nbegin\n  foo\nrescue StandardError, SecurityError\n  bar\nend\n```\n#### EnforcedStyle: explicit (default)\n\n```ruby\n# `explicit` will enforce using `rescue StandardError`\n# instead of `rescue`.\n\n# bad\nbegin\n  foo\nrescue\n  bar\nend\n\n# good\nbegin\n  foo\nrescue StandardError\n  bar\nend\n\n# good\nbegin\n  foo\nrescue OtherError\n  bar\nend\n\n# good\nbegin\n  foo\nrescue StandardError, SecurityError\n  bar\nend\n```\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nEnforcedStyle | `explicit` | `implicit`, `explicit`\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Style/ReturnNil",
              "name": "Style/ReturnNil",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Use return instead of return nil.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_style/#stylereturnnil",
                "tags": [
                  "style"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Use return instead of return nil."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Use return instead of return nil."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nDisabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.50 | -\n\nThis cop enforces consistency between 'return nil' and 'return'.\n\nSupported styles are: return, return_nil.\n\n### Examples\n\n#### EnforcedStyle: return (default)\n\n```ruby\n# bad\ndef foo(arg)\n  return nil if arg\nend\n\n# good\ndef foo(arg)\n  return if arg\nend\n```\n#### EnforcedStyle: return_nil\n\n```ruby\n# bad\ndef foo(arg)\n  return if arg\nend\n\n# good\ndef foo(arg)\n  return nil if arg\nend\n```\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nEnforcedStyle | `return` | `return`, `return_nil`\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nDisabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.50 | -\n\nThis cop enforces consistency between 'return nil' and 'return'.\n\nSupported styles are: return, return_nil.\n\n### Examples\n\n#### EnforcedStyle: return (default)\n\n```ruby\n# bad\ndef foo(arg)\n  return nil if arg\nend\n\n# good\ndef foo(arg)\n  return if arg\nend\n```\n#### EnforcedStyle: return_nil\n\n```ruby\n# bad\ndef foo(arg)\n  return if arg\nend\n\n# good\ndef foo(arg)\n  return nil if arg\nend\n```\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nEnforcedStyle | `return` | `return`, `return_nil`\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Style/SafeNavigation",
              "name": "Style/SafeNavigation",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "This cop transforms usages of a method call safeguarded by a check for the existence of the object to safe navigation (`&.`).",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_style/#stylesafenavigation",
                "tags": [
                  "style"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "This cop transforms usages of a method call safeguarded by a check for the existence of the object to safe navigation (`&.`)."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "This cop transforms usages of a method call safeguarded by a check for the existence of the object to safe navigation (`&.`)."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.43 | 0.77\n\nThis cop transforms usages of a method call safeguarded by a non `nil`\ncheck for the variable whose method is being called to\nsafe navigation (`&.`). If there is a method chain, all of the methods\nin the chain need to be checked for safety, and all of the methods will\nneed to be changed to use safe navigation. We have limited the cop to\nnot register an offense for method chains that exceed 2 methods.\n\nConfiguration option: ConvertCodeThatCanStartToReturnNil\nThe default for this is `false`. When configured to `true`, this will\ncheck for code in the format `!foo.nil? && foo.bar`. As it is written,\nthe return of this code is limited to `false` and whatever the return\nof the method is. If this is converted to safe navigation,\n`foo&.bar` can start returning `nil` as well as what the method\nreturns.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nfoo.bar if foo\nfoo.bar.baz if foo\nfoo.bar(param1, param2) if foo\nfoo.bar { |e| e.something } if foo\nfoo.bar(param) { |e| e.something } if foo\n\nfoo.bar if !foo.nil?\nfoo.bar unless !foo\nfoo.bar unless foo.nil?\n\nfoo && foo.bar\nfoo && foo.bar.baz\nfoo && foo.bar(param1, param2)\nfoo && foo.bar { |e| e.something }\nfoo && foo.bar(param) { |e| e.something }\n\n# good\nfoo&.bar\nfoo&.bar&.baz\nfoo&.bar(param1, param2)\nfoo&.bar { |e| e.something }\nfoo&.bar(param) { |e| e.something }\nfoo && foo.bar.baz.qux # method chain with more than 2 methods\nfoo && foo.nil? # method that `nil` responds to\n\n# Method calls that do not use `.`\nfoo && foo < bar\nfoo < bar if foo\n\n# This could start returning `nil` as well as the return of the method\nfoo.nil? || foo.bar\n!foo || foo.bar\n\n# Methods that are used on assignment, arithmetic operation or\n# comparison should not be converted to use safe navigation\nfoo.baz = bar if foo\nfoo.baz + bar if foo\nfoo.bar > 2 if foo\n```\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nConvertCodeThatCanStartToReturnNil | `false` | Boolean\nAllowedMethods | `present?`, `blank?`, `presence`, `try`, `try!` | Array\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.43 | 0.77\n\nThis cop transforms usages of a method call safeguarded by a non `nil`\ncheck for the variable whose method is being called to\nsafe navigation (`&.`). If there is a method chain, all of the methods\nin the chain need to be checked for safety, and all of the methods will\nneed to be changed to use safe navigation. We have limited the cop to\nnot register an offense for method chains that exceed 2 methods.\n\nConfiguration option: ConvertCodeThatCanStartToReturnNil\nThe default for this is `false`. When configured to `true`, this will\ncheck for code in the format `!foo.nil? && foo.bar`. As it is written,\nthe return of this code is limited to `false` and whatever the return\nof the method is. If this is converted to safe navigation,\n`foo&.bar` can start returning `nil` as well as what the method\nreturns.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nfoo.bar if foo\nfoo.bar.baz if foo\nfoo.bar(param1, param2) if foo\nfoo.bar { |e| e.something } if foo\nfoo.bar(param) { |e| e.something } if foo\n\nfoo.bar if !foo.nil?\nfoo.bar unless !foo\nfoo.bar unless foo.nil?\n\nfoo && foo.bar\nfoo && foo.bar.baz\nfoo && foo.bar(param1, param2)\nfoo && foo.bar { |e| e.something }\nfoo && foo.bar(param) { |e| e.something }\n\n# good\nfoo&.bar\nfoo&.bar&.baz\nfoo&.bar(param1, param2)\nfoo&.bar { |e| e.something }\nfoo&.bar(param) { |e| e.something }\nfoo && foo.bar.baz.qux # method chain with more than 2 methods\nfoo && foo.nil? # method that `nil` responds to\n\n# Method calls that do not use `.`\nfoo && foo < bar\nfoo < bar if foo\n\n# This could start returning `nil` as well as the return of the method\nfoo.nil? || foo.bar\n!foo || foo.bar\n\n# Methods that are used on assignment, arithmetic operation or\n# comparison should not be converted to use safe navigation\nfoo.baz = bar if foo\nfoo.baz + bar if foo\nfoo.bar > 2 if foo\n```\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nConvertCodeThatCanStartToReturnNil | `false` | Boolean\nAllowedMethods | `present?`, `blank?`, `presence`, `try`, `try!` | Array\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Style/Sample",
              "name": "Style/Sample",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Use `sample` instead of `shuffle.first`, `shuffle.last`, and `shuffle[Integer]`.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_style/#stylesample",
                "tags": [
                  "style"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Use `sample` instead of `shuffle.first`, `shuffle.last`, and `shuffle[Integer]`."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Use `sample` instead of `shuffle.first`, `shuffle.last`, and `shuffle[Integer]`."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.30 | -\n\nThis cop is used to identify usages of `shuffle.first`,\n`shuffle.last`, and `shuffle[]` and change them to use\n`sample` instead.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\n[1, 2, 3].shuffle.first\n[1, 2, 3].shuffle.first(2)\n[1, 2, 3].shuffle.last\n[2, 1, 3].shuffle.at(0)\n[2, 1, 3].shuffle.slice(0)\n[1, 2, 3].shuffle[2]\n[1, 2, 3].shuffle[0, 2]    # sample(2) will do the same\n[1, 2, 3].shuffle[0..2]    # sample(3) will do the same\n[1, 2, 3].shuffle(random: Random.new).first\n\n# good\n[1, 2, 3].shuffle\n[1, 2, 3].sample\n[1, 2, 3].sample(3)\n[1, 2, 3].shuffle[1, 3]    # sample(3) might return a longer Array\n[1, 2, 3].shuffle[1..3]    # sample(3) might return a longer Array\n[1, 2, 3].shuffle[foo, bar]\n[1, 2, 3].shuffle(random: Random.new)\n```\n\n### References\n\n* [https://github.com/JuanitoFatas/fast-ruby#arrayshufflefirst-vs-arraysample-code](https://github.com/JuanitoFatas/fast-ruby#arrayshufflefirst-vs-arraysample-code)\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.30 | -\n\nThis cop is used to identify usages of `shuffle.first`,\n`shuffle.last`, and `shuffle[]` and change them to use\n`sample` instead.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\n[1, 2, 3].shuffle.first\n[1, 2, 3].shuffle.first(2)\n[1, 2, 3].shuffle.last\n[2, 1, 3].shuffle.at(0)\n[2, 1, 3].shuffle.slice(0)\n[1, 2, 3].shuffle[2]\n[1, 2, 3].shuffle[0, 2]    # sample(2) will do the same\n[1, 2, 3].shuffle[0..2]    # sample(3) will do the same\n[1, 2, 3].shuffle(random: Random.new).first\n\n# good\n[1, 2, 3].shuffle\n[1, 2, 3].sample\n[1, 2, 3].sample(3)\n[1, 2, 3].shuffle[1, 3]    # sample(3) might return a longer Array\n[1, 2, 3].shuffle[1..3]    # sample(3) might return a longer Array\n[1, 2, 3].shuffle[foo, bar]\n[1, 2, 3].shuffle(random: Random.new)\n```\n\n### References\n\n* [https://github.com/JuanitoFatas/fast-ruby#arrayshufflefirst-vs-arraysample-code](https://github.com/JuanitoFatas/fast-ruby#arrayshufflefirst-vs-arraysample-code)\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Style/SelfAssignment",
              "name": "Style/SelfAssignment",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Checks for places where self-assignment shorthand should have been used.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_style/#styleselfassignment",
                "tags": [
                  "style"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Checks for places where self-assignment shorthand should have been used."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Checks for places where self-assignment shorthand should have been used."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.19 | 0.29\n\nThis cop enforces the use the shorthand for self-assignment.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nx = x + 1\n\n# good\nx += 1\n```\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#self-assignment](https://rubystyle.guide#self-assignment)\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.19 | 0.29\n\nThis cop enforces the use the shorthand for self-assignment.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nx = x + 1\n\n# good\nx += 1\n```\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#self-assignment](https://rubystyle.guide#self-assignment)\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Style/Semicolon",
              "name": "Style/Semicolon",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Don't use semicolons to terminate expressions.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_style/#stylesemicolon",
                "tags": [
                  "style"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Don't use semicolons to terminate expressions."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Don't use semicolons to terminate expressions."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.9 | 0.19\n\nThis cop checks for multiple expressions placed on the same line.\nIt also checks for lines terminated with a semicolon.\n\nThis cop has `AllowAsExpressionSeparator` configuration option.\nIt allows `;` to separate several expressions on the same line.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nfoo = 1; bar = 2;\nbaz = 3;\n\n# good\nfoo = 1\nbar = 2\nbaz = 3\n```\n#### AllowAsExpressionSeparator: false (default)\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nfoo = 1; bar = 2\n```\n#### AllowAsExpressionSeparator: true\n\n```ruby\n# good\nfoo = 1; bar = 2\n```\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nAllowAsExpressionSeparator | `false` | Boolean\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#no-semicolon](https://rubystyle.guide#no-semicolon)\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.9 | 0.19\n\nThis cop checks for multiple expressions placed on the same line.\nIt also checks for lines terminated with a semicolon.\n\nThis cop has `AllowAsExpressionSeparator` configuration option.\nIt allows `;` to separate several expressions on the same line.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nfoo = 1; bar = 2;\nbaz = 3;\n\n# good\nfoo = 1\nbar = 2\nbaz = 3\n```\n#### AllowAsExpressionSeparator: false (default)\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nfoo = 1; bar = 2\n```\n#### AllowAsExpressionSeparator: true\n\n```ruby\n# good\nfoo = 1; bar = 2\n```\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nAllowAsExpressionSeparator | `false` | Boolean\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#no-semicolon](https://rubystyle.guide#no-semicolon)\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Style/Send",
              "name": "Style/Send",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Prefer `Object#__send__` or `Object#public_send` to `send`, as `send` may overlap with existing methods.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_style/#stylesend",
                "tags": [
                  "style"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Prefer `Object#__send__` or `Object#public_send` to `send`, as `send` may overlap with existing methods."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Prefer `Object#__send__` or `Object#public_send` to `send`, as `send` may overlap with existing methods."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nDisabled | Yes | No | 0.33 | -\n\nThis cop checks for the use of the send method.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nFoo.send(:bar)\nquuz.send(:fred)\n\n# good\nFoo.__send__(:bar)\nquuz.public_send(:fred)\n```\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#prefer-public-send](https://rubystyle.guide#prefer-public-send)\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nDisabled | Yes | No | 0.33 | -\n\nThis cop checks for the use of the send method.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nFoo.send(:bar)\nquuz.send(:fred)\n\n# good\nFoo.__send__(:bar)\nquuz.public_send(:fred)\n```\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#prefer-public-send](https://rubystyle.guide#prefer-public-send)\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Style/SignalException",
              "name": "Style/SignalException",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Checks for proper usage of fail and raise.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_style/#stylesignalexception",
                "tags": [
                  "style"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Checks for proper usage of fail and raise."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Checks for proper usage of fail and raise."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.11 | 0.37\n\nThis cop checks for uses of `fail` and `raise`.\n\n### Examples\n\n#### EnforcedStyle: only_raise (default)\n\n```ruby\n# The `only_raise` style enforces the sole use of `raise`.\n# bad\nbegin\n  fail\nrescue Exception\n  # handle it\nend\n\ndef watch_out\n  fail\nrescue Exception\n  # handle it\nend\n\nKernel.fail\n\n# good\nbegin\n  raise\nrescue Exception\n  # handle it\nend\n\ndef watch_out\n  raise\nrescue Exception\n  # handle it\nend\n\nKernel.raise\n```\n#### EnforcedStyle: only_fail\n\n```ruby\n# The `only_fail` style enforces the sole use of `fail`.\n# bad\nbegin\n  raise\nrescue Exception\n  # handle it\nend\n\ndef watch_out\n  raise\nrescue Exception\n  # handle it\nend\n\nKernel.raise\n\n# good\nbegin\n  fail\nrescue Exception\n  # handle it\nend\n\ndef watch_out\n  fail\nrescue Exception\n  # handle it\nend\n\nKernel.fail\n```\n#### EnforcedStyle: semantic\n\n```ruby\n# The `semantic` style enforces the use of `fail` to signal an\n# exception, then will use `raise` to trigger an offense after\n# it has been rescued.\n# bad\nbegin\n  raise\nrescue Exception\n  # handle it\nend\n\ndef watch_out\n  # Error thrown\nrescue Exception\n  fail\nend\n\nKernel.fail\nKernel.raise\n\n# good\nbegin\n  fail\nrescue Exception\n  # handle it\nend\n\ndef watch_out\n  fail\nrescue Exception\n  raise 'Preferably with descriptive message'\nend\n\nexplicit_receiver.fail\nexplicit_receiver.raise\n```\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nEnforcedStyle | `only_raise` | `only_raise`, `only_fail`, `semantic`\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#prefer-raise-over-fail](https://rubystyle.guide#prefer-raise-over-fail)\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.11 | 0.37\n\nThis cop checks for uses of `fail` and `raise`.\n\n### Examples\n\n#### EnforcedStyle: only_raise (default)\n\n```ruby\n# The `only_raise` style enforces the sole use of `raise`.\n# bad\nbegin\n  fail\nrescue Exception\n  # handle it\nend\n\ndef watch_out\n  fail\nrescue Exception\n  # handle it\nend\n\nKernel.fail\n\n# good\nbegin\n  raise\nrescue Exception\n  # handle it\nend\n\ndef watch_out\n  raise\nrescue Exception\n  # handle it\nend\n\nKernel.raise\n```\n#### EnforcedStyle: only_fail\n\n```ruby\n# The `only_fail` style enforces the sole use of `fail`.\n# bad\nbegin\n  raise\nrescue Exception\n  # handle it\nend\n\ndef watch_out\n  raise\nrescue Exception\n  # handle it\nend\n\nKernel.raise\n\n# good\nbegin\n  fail\nrescue Exception\n  # handle it\nend\n\ndef watch_out\n  fail\nrescue Exception\n  # handle it\nend\n\nKernel.fail\n```\n#### EnforcedStyle: semantic\n\n```ruby\n# The `semantic` style enforces the use of `fail` to signal an\n# exception, then will use `raise` to trigger an offense after\n# it has been rescued.\n# bad\nbegin\n  raise\nrescue Exception\n  # handle it\nend\n\ndef watch_out\n  # Error thrown\nrescue Exception\n  fail\nend\n\nKernel.fail\nKernel.raise\n\n# good\nbegin\n  fail\nrescue Exception\n  # handle it\nend\n\ndef watch_out\n  fail\nrescue Exception\n  raise 'Preferably with descriptive message'\nend\n\nexplicit_receiver.fail\nexplicit_receiver.raise\n```\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nEnforcedStyle | `only_raise` | `only_raise`, `only_fail`, `semantic`\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#prefer-raise-over-fail](https://rubystyle.guide#prefer-raise-over-fail)\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Style/SingleLineBlockParams",
              "name": "Style/SingleLineBlockParams",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Enforces the names of some block params.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_style/#stylesinglelineblockparams",
                "tags": [
                  "style"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Enforces the names of some block params."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Enforces the names of some block params."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nDisabled | Yes | No | 0.16 | 0.47\n\nThis cop checks whether the block parameters of a single-line\nmethod accepting a block match the names specified via configuration.\n\nFor instance one can configure `reduce`(`inject`) to use |a, e| as\nparameters.\n\nConfiguration option: Methods\nShould be set to use this cop. Array of hashes, where each key is the\nmethod name and value - array of argument names.\n\n### Examples\n\n#### Methods: [{reduce: %w[a b]}]\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nfoo.reduce { |c, d| c + d }\nfoo.reduce { |_, _d| 1 }\n\n# good\nfoo.reduce { |a, b| a + b }\nfoo.reduce { |a, _b| a }\nfoo.reduce { |a, (id, _)| a + id }\nfoo.reduce { true }\n\n# good\nfoo.reduce do |c, d|\n  c + d\nend\n```\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nMethods | `{\"reduce\"=>[\"acc\", \"elem\"]}`, `{\"inject\"=>[\"acc\", \"elem\"]}` | Array\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nDisabled | Yes | No | 0.16 | 0.47\n\nThis cop checks whether the block parameters of a single-line\nmethod accepting a block match the names specified via configuration.\n\nFor instance one can configure `reduce`(`inject`) to use |a, e| as\nparameters.\n\nConfiguration option: Methods\nShould be set to use this cop. Array of hashes, where each key is the\nmethod name and value - array of argument names.\n\n### Examples\n\n#### Methods: [{reduce: %w[a b]}]\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nfoo.reduce { |c, d| c + d }\nfoo.reduce { |_, _d| 1 }\n\n# good\nfoo.reduce { |a, b| a + b }\nfoo.reduce { |a, _b| a }\nfoo.reduce { |a, (id, _)| a + id }\nfoo.reduce { true }\n\n# good\nfoo.reduce do |c, d|\n  c + d\nend\n```\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nMethods | `{\"reduce\"=>[\"acc\", \"elem\"]}`, `{\"inject\"=>[\"acc\", \"elem\"]}` | Array\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Style/SingleLineMethods",
              "name": "Style/SingleLineMethods",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Avoid single-line methods.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_style/#stylesinglelinemethods",
                "tags": [
                  "style"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Avoid single-line methods."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Avoid single-line methods."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.9 | 0.19\n\nThis cop checks for single-line method definitions that contain a body.\nIt will accept single-line methods with no body.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\ndef some_method; body end\ndef link_to(url); {:name => url}; end\ndef @table.columns; super; end\n\n# good\ndef self.resource_class=(klass); end\ndef @table.columns; end\n```\n#### AllowIfMethodIsEmpty: true (default)\n\n```ruby\n# good\ndef no_op; end\n```\n#### AllowIfMethodIsEmpty: false\n\n```ruby\n# bad\ndef no_op; end\n```\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nAllowIfMethodIsEmpty | `true` | Boolean\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#no-single-line-methods](https://rubystyle.guide#no-single-line-methods)\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.9 | 0.19\n\nThis cop checks for single-line method definitions that contain a body.\nIt will accept single-line methods with no body.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\ndef some_method; body end\ndef link_to(url); {:name => url}; end\ndef @table.columns; super; end\n\n# good\ndef self.resource_class=(klass); end\ndef @table.columns; end\n```\n#### AllowIfMethodIsEmpty: true (default)\n\n```ruby\n# good\ndef no_op; end\n```\n#### AllowIfMethodIsEmpty: false\n\n```ruby\n# bad\ndef no_op; end\n```\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nAllowIfMethodIsEmpty | `true` | Boolean\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#no-single-line-methods](https://rubystyle.guide#no-single-line-methods)\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Style/SpecialGlobalVars",
              "name": "Style/SpecialGlobalVars",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Avoid Perl-style global variables.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_style/#stylespecialglobalvars",
                "tags": [
                  "style"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Avoid Perl-style global variables."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Avoid Perl-style global variables."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes (Unsafe) | 0.13 | 0.36\n\nThis cop looks for uses of Perl-style global variables.\n\n### Examples\n\n#### EnforcedStyle: use_english_names (default)\n\n```ruby\n# good\nputs $LOAD_PATH\nputs $LOADED_FEATURES\nputs $PROGRAM_NAME\nputs $ERROR_INFO\nputs $ERROR_POSITION\nputs $FIELD_SEPARATOR # or $FS\nputs $OUTPUT_FIELD_SEPARATOR # or $OFS\nputs $INPUT_RECORD_SEPARATOR # or $RS\nputs $OUTPUT_RECORD_SEPARATOR # or $ORS\nputs $INPUT_LINE_NUMBER # or $NR\nputs $LAST_READ_LINE\nputs $DEFAULT_OUTPUT\nputs $DEFAULT_INPUT\nputs $PROCESS_ID # or $PID\nputs $CHILD_STATUS\nputs $LAST_MATCH_INFO\nputs $IGNORECASE\nputs $ARGV # or ARGV\nputs $MATCH\nputs $PREMATCH\nputs $POSTMATCH\nputs $LAST_PAREN_MATCH\n```\n#### EnforcedStyle: use_perl_names\n\n```ruby\n# good\nputs $:\nputs $\"\nputs $0\nputs $!\nputs $@\nputs $;\nputs $,\nputs $/\nputs $\\\nputs $.\nputs $_\nputs $>\nputs $<\nputs $$\nputs $?\nputs $~\nputs $=\nputs $*\nputs $&\nputs $`\nputs $'\nputs $+\n```\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nEnforcedStyle | `use_english_names` | `use_perl_names`, `use_english_names`\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#no-cryptic-perlisms](https://rubystyle.guide#no-cryptic-perlisms)\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes (Unsafe) | 0.13 | 0.36\n\nThis cop looks for uses of Perl-style global variables.\n\n### Examples\n\n#### EnforcedStyle: use_english_names (default)\n\n```ruby\n# good\nputs $LOAD_PATH\nputs $LOADED_FEATURES\nputs $PROGRAM_NAME\nputs $ERROR_INFO\nputs $ERROR_POSITION\nputs $FIELD_SEPARATOR # or $FS\nputs $OUTPUT_FIELD_SEPARATOR # or $OFS\nputs $INPUT_RECORD_SEPARATOR # or $RS\nputs $OUTPUT_RECORD_SEPARATOR # or $ORS\nputs $INPUT_LINE_NUMBER # or $NR\nputs $LAST_READ_LINE\nputs $DEFAULT_OUTPUT\nputs $DEFAULT_INPUT\nputs $PROCESS_ID # or $PID\nputs $CHILD_STATUS\nputs $LAST_MATCH_INFO\nputs $IGNORECASE\nputs $ARGV # or ARGV\nputs $MATCH\nputs $PREMATCH\nputs $POSTMATCH\nputs $LAST_PAREN_MATCH\n```\n#### EnforcedStyle: use_perl_names\n\n```ruby\n# good\nputs $:\nputs $\"\nputs $0\nputs $!\nputs $@\nputs $;\nputs $,\nputs $/\nputs $\\\nputs $.\nputs $_\nputs $>\nputs $<\nputs $$\nputs $?\nputs $~\nputs $=\nputs $*\nputs $&\nputs $`\nputs $'\nputs $+\n```\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nEnforcedStyle | `use_english_names` | `use_perl_names`, `use_english_names`\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#no-cryptic-perlisms](https://rubystyle.guide#no-cryptic-perlisms)\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Style/StabbyLambdaParentheses",
              "name": "Style/StabbyLambdaParentheses",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Check for the usage of parentheses around stabby lambda arguments.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_style/#stylestabbylambdaparentheses",
                "tags": [
                  "style"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Check for the usage of parentheses around stabby lambda arguments."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Check for the usage of parentheses around stabby lambda arguments."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.35 | -\n\nCheck for parentheses around stabby lambda arguments.\nThere are two different styles. Defaults to `require_parentheses`.\n\n### Examples\n\n#### EnforcedStyle: require_parentheses (default)\n\n```ruby\n# bad\n->a,b,c { a + b + c }\n\n# good\n->(a,b,c) { a + b + c}\n```\n#### EnforcedStyle: require_no_parentheses\n\n```ruby\n# bad\n->(a,b,c) { a + b + c }\n\n# good\n->a,b,c { a + b + c}\n```\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nEnforcedStyle | `require_parentheses` | `require_parentheses`, `require_no_parentheses`\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#stabby-lambda-with-args](https://rubystyle.guide#stabby-lambda-with-args)\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.35 | -\n\nCheck for parentheses around stabby lambda arguments.\nThere are two different styles. Defaults to `require_parentheses`.\n\n### Examples\n\n#### EnforcedStyle: require_parentheses (default)\n\n```ruby\n# bad\n->a,b,c { a + b + c }\n\n# good\n->(a,b,c) { a + b + c}\n```\n#### EnforcedStyle: require_no_parentheses\n\n```ruby\n# bad\n->(a,b,c) { a + b + c }\n\n# good\n->a,b,c { a + b + c}\n```\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nEnforcedStyle | `require_parentheses` | `require_parentheses`, `require_no_parentheses`\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#stabby-lambda-with-args](https://rubystyle.guide#stabby-lambda-with-args)\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Style/StderrPuts",
              "name": "Style/StderrPuts",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Use `warn` instead of `$stderr.puts`.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_style/#stylestderrputs",
                "tags": [
                  "style"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Use `warn` instead of `$stderr.puts`."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Use `warn` instead of `$stderr.puts`."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.51 | -\n\nThis cop identifies places where `$stderr.puts` can be replaced by\n`warn`. The latter has the advantage of easily being disabled by,\nthe `-W0` interpreter flag or setting `$VERBOSE` to `nil`.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\n$stderr.puts('hello')\n\n# good\nwarn('hello')\n```\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#warn](https://rubystyle.guide#warn)\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.51 | -\n\nThis cop identifies places where `$stderr.puts` can be replaced by\n`warn`. The latter has the advantage of easily being disabled by,\nthe `-W0` interpreter flag or setting `$VERBOSE` to `nil`.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\n$stderr.puts('hello')\n\n# good\nwarn('hello')\n```\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#warn](https://rubystyle.guide#warn)\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Style/StringHashKeys",
              "name": "Style/StringHashKeys",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Prefer symbols instead of strings as hash keys.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_style/#stylestringhashkeys",
                "tags": [
                  "style"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Prefer symbols instead of strings as hash keys."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Prefer symbols instead of strings as hash keys."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nDisabled | No | Yes (Unsafe) | 0.52 | 0.75\n\nThis cop checks for the use of strings as keys in hashes. The use of\nsymbols is preferred instead.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\n{ 'one' => 1, 'two' => 2, 'three' => 3 }\n\n# good\n{ one: 1, two: 2, three: 3 }\n```\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#symbols-as-keys](https://rubystyle.guide#symbols-as-keys)\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nDisabled | No | Yes (Unsafe) | 0.52 | 0.75\n\nThis cop checks for the use of strings as keys in hashes. The use of\nsymbols is preferred instead.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\n{ 'one' => 1, 'two' => 2, 'three' => 3 }\n\n# good\n{ one: 1, two: 2, three: 3 }\n```\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#symbols-as-keys](https://rubystyle.guide#symbols-as-keys)\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Style/StringLiterals",
              "name": "Style/StringLiterals",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Checks if uses of quotes match the configured preference.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_style/#stylestringliterals",
                "tags": [
                  "style"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Checks if uses of quotes match the configured preference."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Checks if uses of quotes match the configured preference."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.9 | 0.36\n\nChecks if uses of quotes match the configured preference.\n\n### Examples\n\n#### EnforcedStyle: single_quotes (default)\n\n```ruby\n# bad\n\"No special symbols\"\n\"No string interpolation\"\n\"Just text\"\n\n# good\n'No special symbols'\n'No string interpolation'\n'Just text'\n\"Wait! What's #{this}!\"\n```\n#### EnforcedStyle: double_quotes\n\n```ruby\n# bad\n'Just some text'\n'No special chars or interpolation'\n\n# good\n\"Just some text\"\n\"No special chars or interpolation\"\n\"Every string in #{project} uses double_quotes\"\n```\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nEnforcedStyle | `single_quotes` | `single_quotes`, `double_quotes`\nConsistentQuotesInMultiline | `false` | Boolean\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#consistent-string-literals](https://rubystyle.guide#consistent-string-literals)\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.9 | 0.36\n\nChecks if uses of quotes match the configured preference.\n\n### Examples\n\n#### EnforcedStyle: single_quotes (default)\n\n```ruby\n# bad\n\"No special symbols\"\n\"No string interpolation\"\n\"Just text\"\n\n# good\n'No special symbols'\n'No string interpolation'\n'Just text'\n\"Wait! What's #{this}!\"\n```\n#### EnforcedStyle: double_quotes\n\n```ruby\n# bad\n'Just some text'\n'No special chars or interpolation'\n\n# good\n\"Just some text\"\n\"No special chars or interpolation\"\n\"Every string in #{project} uses double_quotes\"\n```\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nEnforcedStyle | `single_quotes` | `single_quotes`, `double_quotes`\nConsistentQuotesInMultiline | `false` | Boolean\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#consistent-string-literals](https://rubystyle.guide#consistent-string-literals)\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Style/StringLiteralsInInterpolation",
              "name": "Style/StringLiteralsInInterpolation",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Checks if uses of quotes inside expressions in interpolated strings match the configured preference.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_style/#stylestringliteralsininterpolation",
                "tags": [
                  "style"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Checks if uses of quotes inside expressions in interpolated strings match the configured preference."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Checks if uses of quotes inside expressions in interpolated strings match the configured preference."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.27 | -\n\nThis cop checks that quotes inside the string interpolation\nmatch the configured preference.\n\n### Examples\n\n#### EnforcedStyle: single_quotes (default)\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nresult = \"Tests #{success ? \"PASS\" : \"FAIL\"}\"\n\n# good\nresult = \"Tests #{success ? 'PASS' : 'FAIL'}\"\n```\n#### EnforcedStyle: double_quotes\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nresult = \"Tests #{success ? 'PASS' : 'FAIL'}\"\n\n# good\nresult = \"Tests #{success ? \"PASS\" : \"FAIL\"}\"\n```\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nEnforcedStyle | `single_quotes` | `single_quotes`, `double_quotes`\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.27 | -\n\nThis cop checks that quotes inside the string interpolation\nmatch the configured preference.\n\n### Examples\n\n#### EnforcedStyle: single_quotes (default)\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nresult = \"Tests #{success ? \"PASS\" : \"FAIL\"}\"\n\n# good\nresult = \"Tests #{success ? 'PASS' : 'FAIL'}\"\n```\n#### EnforcedStyle: double_quotes\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nresult = \"Tests #{success ? 'PASS' : 'FAIL'}\"\n\n# good\nresult = \"Tests #{success ? \"PASS\" : \"FAIL\"}\"\n```\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nEnforcedStyle | `single_quotes` | `single_quotes`, `double_quotes`\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Style/StringMethods",
              "name": "Style/StringMethods",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Checks if configured preferred methods are used over non-preferred.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_style/#stylestringmethods",
                "tags": [
                  "style"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Checks if configured preferred methods are used over non-preferred."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Checks if configured preferred methods are used over non-preferred."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nDisabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.34 | 0.34.2\n\nThis cop enforces the use of consistent method names\nfrom the String class.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\n'name'.intern\n'var'.unfavored_method\n\n# good\n'name'.to_sym\n'var'.preferred_method\n```\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nPreferredMethods | `{\"intern\"=>\"to_sym\"}` | \n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nDisabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.34 | 0.34.2\n\nThis cop enforces the use of consistent method names\nfrom the String class.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\n'name'.intern\n'var'.unfavored_method\n\n# good\n'name'.to_sym\n'var'.preferred_method\n```\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nPreferredMethods | `{\"intern\"=>\"to_sym\"}` | \n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Style/Strip",
              "name": "Style/Strip",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Use `strip` instead of `lstrip.rstrip`.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_style/#stylestrip",
                "tags": [
                  "style"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Use `strip` instead of `lstrip.rstrip`."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Use `strip` instead of `lstrip.rstrip`."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.36 | -\n\nThis cop identifies places where `lstrip.rstrip` can be replaced by\n`strip`.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\n'abc'.lstrip.rstrip\n'abc'.rstrip.lstrip\n\n# good\n'abc'.strip\n```\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.36 | -\n\nThis cop identifies places where `lstrip.rstrip` can be replaced by\n`strip`.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\n'abc'.lstrip.rstrip\n'abc'.rstrip.lstrip\n\n# good\n'abc'.strip\n```\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Style/StructInheritance",
              "name": "Style/StructInheritance",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Checks for inheritance from Struct.new.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_style/#stylestructinheritance",
                "tags": [
                  "style"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Checks for inheritance from Struct.new."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Checks for inheritance from Struct.new."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | No | 0.29 | -\n\nThis cop checks for inheritance from Struct.new.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nclass Person < Struct.new(:first_name, :last_name)\n  def age\n    42\n  end\nend\n\n# good\nPerson = Struct.new(:first_name, :last_name) do\n  def age\n    42\n  end\nend\n```\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#no-extend-struct-new](https://rubystyle.guide#no-extend-struct-new)\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | No | 0.29 | -\n\nThis cop checks for inheritance from Struct.new.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nclass Person < Struct.new(:first_name, :last_name)\n  def age\n    42\n  end\nend\n\n# good\nPerson = Struct.new(:first_name, :last_name) do\n  def age\n    42\n  end\nend\n```\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#no-extend-struct-new](https://rubystyle.guide#no-extend-struct-new)\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Style/SymbolArray",
              "name": "Style/SymbolArray",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Use %i or %I for arrays of symbols.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_style/#stylesymbolarray",
                "tags": [
                  "style"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Use %i or %I for arrays of symbols."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Use %i or %I for arrays of symbols."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.9 | 0.49\n\nThis cop can check for array literals made up of symbols that are not\nusing the %i() syntax.\n\nAlternatively, it checks for symbol arrays using the %i() syntax on\nprojects which do not want to use that syntax.\n\nConfiguration option: MinSize\nIf set, arrays with fewer elements than this value will not trigger the\ncop. For example, a `MinSize` of `3` will not enforce a style on an\narray of 2 or fewer elements.\n\n### Examples\n\n#### EnforcedStyle: percent (default)\n\n```ruby\n# good\n%i[foo bar baz]\n\n# bad\n[:foo, :bar, :baz]\n```\n#### EnforcedStyle: brackets\n\n```ruby\n# good\n[:foo, :bar, :baz]\n\n# bad\n%i[foo bar baz]\n```\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nEnforcedStyle | `percent` | `percent`, `brackets`\nMinSize | `2` | Integer\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#percent-i](https://rubystyle.guide#percent-i)\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.9 | 0.49\n\nThis cop can check for array literals made up of symbols that are not\nusing the %i() syntax.\n\nAlternatively, it checks for symbol arrays using the %i() syntax on\nprojects which do not want to use that syntax.\n\nConfiguration option: MinSize\nIf set, arrays with fewer elements than this value will not trigger the\ncop. For example, a `MinSize` of `3` will not enforce a style on an\narray of 2 or fewer elements.\n\n### Examples\n\n#### EnforcedStyle: percent (default)\n\n```ruby\n# good\n%i[foo bar baz]\n\n# bad\n[:foo, :bar, :baz]\n```\n#### EnforcedStyle: brackets\n\n```ruby\n# good\n[:foo, :bar, :baz]\n\n# bad\n%i[foo bar baz]\n```\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nEnforcedStyle | `percent` | `percent`, `brackets`\nMinSize | `2` | Integer\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#percent-i](https://rubystyle.guide#percent-i)\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Style/SymbolLiteral",
              "name": "Style/SymbolLiteral",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Use plain symbols instead of string symbols when possible.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_style/#stylesymbolliteral",
                "tags": [
                  "style"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Use plain symbols instead of string symbols when possible."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Use plain symbols instead of string symbols when possible."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.30 | -\n\nThis cop checks symbol literal syntax.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\n:\"symbol\"\n\n# good\n:symbol\n```\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.30 | -\n\nThis cop checks symbol literal syntax.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\n:\"symbol\"\n\n# good\n:symbol\n```\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Style/SymbolProc",
              "name": "Style/SymbolProc",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Use symbols as procs instead of blocks when possible.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_style/#stylesymbolproc",
                "tags": [
                  "style"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Use symbols as procs instead of blocks when possible."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Use symbols as procs instead of blocks when possible."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes (Unsafe) | 0.26 | 0.64\n\nUse symbols as procs when possible.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nsomething.map { |s| s.upcase }\n\n# good\nsomething.map(&:upcase)\n```\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nIgnoredMethods | `respond_to`, `define_method` | Array\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes (Unsafe) | 0.26 | 0.64\n\nUse symbols as procs when possible.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nsomething.map { |s| s.upcase }\n\n# good\nsomething.map(&:upcase)\n```\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nIgnoredMethods | `respond_to`, `define_method` | Array\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Style/TernaryParentheses",
              "name": "Style/TernaryParentheses",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Checks for use of parentheses around ternary conditions.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_style/#styleternaryparentheses",
                "tags": [
                  "style"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Checks for use of parentheses around ternary conditions."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Checks for use of parentheses around ternary conditions."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.42 | 0.46\n\nThis cop checks for the presence of parentheses around ternary\nconditions. It is configurable to enforce inclusion or omission of\nparentheses using `EnforcedStyle`. Omission is only enforced when\nremoving the parentheses won't cause a different behavior.\n\n`AllowSafeAssignment` option for safe assignment.\nBy safe assignment we mean putting parentheses around\nan assignment to indicate \"I know I'm using an assignment\nas a condition. It's not a mistake.\"\n\n### Examples\n\n#### EnforcedStyle: require_no_parentheses (default)\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nfoo = (bar?) ? a : b\nfoo = (bar.baz?) ? a : b\nfoo = (bar && baz) ? a : b\n\n# good\nfoo = bar? ? a : b\nfoo = bar.baz? ? a : b\nfoo = bar && baz ? a : b\n```\n#### EnforcedStyle: require_parentheses\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nfoo = bar? ? a : b\nfoo = bar.baz? ? a : b\nfoo = bar && baz ? a : b\n\n# good\nfoo = (bar?) ? a : b\nfoo = (bar.baz?) ? a : b\nfoo = (bar && baz) ? a : b\n```\n#### EnforcedStyle: require_parentheses_when_complex\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nfoo = (bar?) ? a : b\nfoo = (bar.baz?) ? a : b\nfoo = bar && baz ? a : b\n\n# good\nfoo = bar? ? a : b\nfoo = bar.baz? ? a : b\nfoo = (bar && baz) ? a : b\n```\n#### AllowSafeAssignment: true (default)\n\n```ruby\n# good\nfoo = (bar = baz) ? a : b\n```\n#### AllowSafeAssignment: false\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nfoo = (bar = baz) ? a : b\n```\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nEnforcedStyle | `require_no_parentheses` | `require_parentheses`, `require_no_parentheses`, `require_parentheses_when_complex`\nAllowSafeAssignment | `true` | Boolean\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.42 | 0.46\n\nThis cop checks for the presence of parentheses around ternary\nconditions. It is configurable to enforce inclusion or omission of\nparentheses using `EnforcedStyle`. Omission is only enforced when\nremoving the parentheses won't cause a different behavior.\n\n`AllowSafeAssignment` option for safe assignment.\nBy safe assignment we mean putting parentheses around\nan assignment to indicate \"I know I'm using an assignment\nas a condition. It's not a mistake.\"\n\n### Examples\n\n#### EnforcedStyle: require_no_parentheses (default)\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nfoo = (bar?) ? a : b\nfoo = (bar.baz?) ? a : b\nfoo = (bar && baz) ? a : b\n\n# good\nfoo = bar? ? a : b\nfoo = bar.baz? ? a : b\nfoo = bar && baz ? a : b\n```\n#### EnforcedStyle: require_parentheses\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nfoo = bar? ? a : b\nfoo = bar.baz? ? a : b\nfoo = bar && baz ? a : b\n\n# good\nfoo = (bar?) ? a : b\nfoo = (bar.baz?) ? a : b\nfoo = (bar && baz) ? a : b\n```\n#### EnforcedStyle: require_parentheses_when_complex\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nfoo = (bar?) ? a : b\nfoo = (bar.baz?) ? a : b\nfoo = bar && baz ? a : b\n\n# good\nfoo = bar? ? a : b\nfoo = bar.baz? ? a : b\nfoo = (bar && baz) ? a : b\n```\n#### AllowSafeAssignment: true (default)\n\n```ruby\n# good\nfoo = (bar = baz) ? a : b\n```\n#### AllowSafeAssignment: false\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nfoo = (bar = baz) ? a : b\n```\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nEnforcedStyle | `require_no_parentheses` | `require_parentheses`, `require_no_parentheses`, `require_parentheses_when_complex`\nAllowSafeAssignment | `true` | Boolean\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Style/TrailingBodyOnClass",
              "name": "Style/TrailingBodyOnClass",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Class body goes below class statement.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_style/#styletrailingbodyonclass",
                "tags": [
                  "style"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Class body goes below class statement."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Class body goes below class statement."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.53 | -\n\nThis cop checks for trailing code after the class definition.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nclass Foo; def foo; end\nend\n\n# good\nclass Foo\n  def foo; end\nend\n```\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.53 | -\n\nThis cop checks for trailing code after the class definition.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nclass Foo; def foo; end\nend\n\n# good\nclass Foo\n  def foo; end\nend\n```\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Style/TrailingBodyOnMethodDefinition",
              "name": "Style/TrailingBodyOnMethodDefinition",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Method body goes below definition.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_style/#styletrailingbodyonmethoddefinition",
                "tags": [
                  "style"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Method body goes below definition."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Method body goes below definition."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.52 | -\n\nThis cop checks for trailing code after the method definition.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\ndef some_method; do_stuff\nend\n\ndef f(x); b = foo\n  b[c: x]\nend\n\n# good\ndef some_method\n  do_stuff\nend\n\ndef f(x)\n  b = foo\n  b[c: x]\nend\n```\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.52 | -\n\nThis cop checks for trailing code after the method definition.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\ndef some_method; do_stuff\nend\n\ndef f(x); b = foo\n  b[c: x]\nend\n\n# good\ndef some_method\n  do_stuff\nend\n\ndef f(x)\n  b = foo\n  b[c: x]\nend\n```\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Style/TrailingBodyOnModule",
              "name": "Style/TrailingBodyOnModule",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Module body goes below module statement.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_style/#styletrailingbodyonmodule",
                "tags": [
                  "style"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Module body goes below module statement."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Module body goes below module statement."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.53 | -\n\nThis cop checks for trailing code after the module definition.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nmodule Foo extend self\nend\n\n# good\nmodule Foo\n  extend self\nend\n```\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.53 | -\n\nThis cop checks for trailing code after the module definition.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nmodule Foo extend self\nend\n\n# good\nmodule Foo\n  extend self\nend\n```\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Style/TrailingCommaInArguments",
              "name": "Style/TrailingCommaInArguments",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Checks for trailing comma in argument lists.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_style/#styletrailingcommainarguments",
                "tags": [
                  "style"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Checks for trailing comma in argument lists."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Checks for trailing comma in argument lists."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.36 | -\n\nThis cop checks for trailing comma in argument lists.\nThe supported styles are:\n\n- `consistent_comma`: Requires a comma after the last argument,\nfor all parenthesized method calls with arguments.\n- `comma`: Requires a comma after the last argument, but only for\nparenthesized method calls where each argument is on its own line.\n- `no_comma`: Requires that there is no comma after the last\nargument.\n\n### Examples\n\n#### EnforcedStyleForMultiline: consistent_comma\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nmethod(1, 2,)\n\n# good\nmethod(1, 2)\n\n# good\nmethod(\n  1, 2,\n  3,\n)\n\n# good\nmethod(\n  1, 2, 3,\n)\n\n# good\nmethod(\n  1,\n  2,\n)\n```\n#### EnforcedStyleForMultiline: comma\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nmethod(1, 2,)\n\n# good\nmethod(1, 2)\n\n# bad\nmethod(\n  1, 2,\n  3,\n)\n\n# good\nmethod(\n  1, 2,\n  3\n)\n\n# bad\nmethod(\n  1, 2, 3,\n)\n\n# good\nmethod(\n  1, 2, 3\n)\n\n# good\nmethod(\n  1,\n  2,\n)\n```\n#### EnforcedStyleForMultiline: no_comma (default)\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nmethod(1, 2,)\n\n# good\nmethod(1, 2)\n\n# good\nmethod(\n  1,\n  2\n)\n```\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nEnforcedStyleForMultiline | `no_comma` | `comma`, `consistent_comma`, `no_comma`\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#no-trailing-params-comma](https://rubystyle.guide#no-trailing-params-comma)\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.36 | -\n\nThis cop checks for trailing comma in argument lists.\nThe supported styles are:\n\n- `consistent_comma`: Requires a comma after the last argument,\nfor all parenthesized method calls with arguments.\n- `comma`: Requires a comma after the last argument, but only for\nparenthesized method calls where each argument is on its own line.\n- `no_comma`: Requires that there is no comma after the last\nargument.\n\n### Examples\n\n#### EnforcedStyleForMultiline: consistent_comma\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nmethod(1, 2,)\n\n# good\nmethod(1, 2)\n\n# good\nmethod(\n  1, 2,\n  3,\n)\n\n# good\nmethod(\n  1, 2, 3,\n)\n\n# good\nmethod(\n  1,\n  2,\n)\n```\n#### EnforcedStyleForMultiline: comma\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nmethod(1, 2,)\n\n# good\nmethod(1, 2)\n\n# bad\nmethod(\n  1, 2,\n  3,\n)\n\n# good\nmethod(\n  1, 2,\n  3\n)\n\n# bad\nmethod(\n  1, 2, 3,\n)\n\n# good\nmethod(\n  1, 2, 3\n)\n\n# good\nmethod(\n  1,\n  2,\n)\n```\n#### EnforcedStyleForMultiline: no_comma (default)\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nmethod(1, 2,)\n\n# good\nmethod(1, 2)\n\n# good\nmethod(\n  1,\n  2\n)\n```\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nEnforcedStyleForMultiline | `no_comma` | `comma`, `consistent_comma`, `no_comma`\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#no-trailing-params-comma](https://rubystyle.guide#no-trailing-params-comma)\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Style/TrailingCommaInArrayLiteral",
              "name": "Style/TrailingCommaInArrayLiteral",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Checks for trailing comma in array literals.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_style/#styletrailingcommainarrayliteral",
                "tags": [
                  "style"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Checks for trailing comma in array literals."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Checks for trailing comma in array literals."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.53 | -\n\nThis cop checks for trailing comma in array literals.\nThe configuration options are:\n\n- `consistent_comma`: Requires a comma after the\nlast item of all non-empty, multiline array literals.\n- `comma`: Requires a comma after last item in an array,\nbut only when each item is on its own line.\n- `no_comma`: Does not requires a comma after the\nlast item in an array\n\n### Examples\n\n#### EnforcedStyleForMultiline: consistent_comma\n\n```ruby\n# bad\na = [1, 2,]\n\n# good\na = [1, 2]\n\n# good\na = [\n  1, 2,\n  3,\n]\n\n# good\na = [\n  1, 2, 3,\n]\n\n# good\na = [\n  1,\n  2,\n]\n```\n#### EnforcedStyleForMultiline: comma\n\n```ruby\n# bad\na = [1, 2,]\n\n# good\na = [1, 2]\n\n# bad\na = [\n  1, 2,\n  3,\n]\n\n# good\na = [\n  1, 2,\n  3\n]\n\n# bad\na = [\n  1, 2, 3,\n]\n\n# good\na = [\n  1, 2, 3\n]\n\n# good\na = [\n  1,\n  2,\n]\n```\n#### EnforcedStyleForMultiline: no_comma (default)\n\n```ruby\n# bad\na = [1, 2,]\n\n# good\na = [\n  1,\n  2\n]\n```\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nEnforcedStyleForMultiline | `no_comma` | `comma`, `consistent_comma`, `no_comma`\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#no-trailing-array-commas](https://rubystyle.guide#no-trailing-array-commas)\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.53 | -\n\nThis cop checks for trailing comma in array literals.\nThe configuration options are:\n\n- `consistent_comma`: Requires a comma after the\nlast item of all non-empty, multiline array literals.\n- `comma`: Requires a comma after last item in an array,\nbut only when each item is on its own line.\n- `no_comma`: Does not requires a comma after the\nlast item in an array\n\n### Examples\n\n#### EnforcedStyleForMultiline: consistent_comma\n\n```ruby\n# bad\na = [1, 2,]\n\n# good\na = [1, 2]\n\n# good\na = [\n  1, 2,\n  3,\n]\n\n# good\na = [\n  1, 2, 3,\n]\n\n# good\na = [\n  1,\n  2,\n]\n```\n#### EnforcedStyleForMultiline: comma\n\n```ruby\n# bad\na = [1, 2,]\n\n# good\na = [1, 2]\n\n# bad\na = [\n  1, 2,\n  3,\n]\n\n# good\na = [\n  1, 2,\n  3\n]\n\n# bad\na = [\n  1, 2, 3,\n]\n\n# good\na = [\n  1, 2, 3\n]\n\n# good\na = [\n  1,\n  2,\n]\n```\n#### EnforcedStyleForMultiline: no_comma (default)\n\n```ruby\n# bad\na = [1, 2,]\n\n# good\na = [\n  1,\n  2\n]\n```\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nEnforcedStyleForMultiline | `no_comma` | `comma`, `consistent_comma`, `no_comma`\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#no-trailing-array-commas](https://rubystyle.guide#no-trailing-array-commas)\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Style/TrailingCommaInBlockArgs",
              "name": "Style/TrailingCommaInBlockArgs",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Checks for useless trailing commas in block arguments.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_style/#styletrailingcommainblockargs",
                "tags": [
                  "style"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Checks for useless trailing commas in block arguments."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Checks for useless trailing commas in block arguments."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nDisabled | No | Yes (Unsafe) | 0.81 | -\n\nThis cop checks whether trailing commas in block arguments are\nrequired. Blocks with only one argument and a trailing comma require\nthat comma to be present. Blocks with more than one argument never\nrequire a trailing comma.\n\n add do |foo, bar,|\n   foo + bar\n  end\n\n # good\n add do |foo, bar|\n   foo + bar\n end\n\n # good\n  add do |foo,|\n   foo\n end\n\n # good\n add do\n    foo + bar\n end\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nadd { |foo, bar,| foo + bar }\n\n # good\nadd { |foo, bar| foo + bar }\n\n# good\nadd { |foo,| foo }\n\n# good\nadd { foo }\n\n# bad\n```\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nDisabled | No | Yes (Unsafe) | 0.81 | -\n\nThis cop checks whether trailing commas in block arguments are\nrequired. Blocks with only one argument and a trailing comma require\nthat comma to be present. Blocks with more than one argument never\nrequire a trailing comma.\n\n add do |foo, bar,|\n   foo + bar\n  end\n\n # good\n add do |foo, bar|\n   foo + bar\n end\n\n # good\n  add do |foo,|\n   foo\n end\n\n # good\n add do\n    foo + bar\n end\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nadd { |foo, bar,| foo + bar }\n\n # good\nadd { |foo, bar| foo + bar }\n\n# good\nadd { |foo,| foo }\n\n# good\nadd { foo }\n\n# bad\n```\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Style/TrailingCommaInHashLiteral",
              "name": "Style/TrailingCommaInHashLiteral",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Checks for trailing comma in hash literals.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_style/#styletrailingcommainhashliteral",
                "tags": [
                  "style"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Checks for trailing comma in hash literals."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Checks for trailing comma in hash literals."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.53 | -\n\nThis cop checks for trailing comma in hash literals.\nThe configuration options are:\n\n- `consistent_comma`: Requires a comma after the\nlast item of all non-empty, multiline hash literals.\n- `comma`: Requires a comma after the last item in a hash,\nbut only when each item is on its own line.\n- `no_comma`: Does not requires a comma after the\nlast item in a hash\n\n### Examples\n\n#### EnforcedStyleForMultiline: consistent_comma\n\n```ruby\n# bad\na = { foo: 1, bar: 2, }\n\n# good\na = { foo: 1, bar: 2 }\n\n# good\na = {\n  foo: 1, bar: 2,\n  qux: 3,\n}\n\n# good\na = {\n  foo: 1, bar: 2, qux: 3,\n}\n\n# good\na = {\n  foo: 1,\n  bar: 2,\n}\n```\n#### EnforcedStyleForMultiline: comma\n\n```ruby\n# bad\na = { foo: 1, bar: 2, }\n\n# good\na = { foo: 1, bar: 2 }\n\n# bad\na = {\n  foo: 1, bar: 2,\n  qux: 3,\n}\n\n# good\na = {\n  foo: 1, bar: 2,\n  qux: 3\n}\n\n# bad\na = {\n  foo: 1, bar: 2, qux: 3,\n}\n\n# good\na = {\n  foo: 1, bar: 2, qux: 3\n}\n\n# good\na = {\n  foo: 1,\n  bar: 2,\n}\n```\n#### EnforcedStyleForMultiline: no_comma (default)\n\n```ruby\n# bad\na = { foo: 1, bar: 2, }\n\n# good\na = {\n  foo: 1,\n  bar: 2\n}\n```\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nEnforcedStyleForMultiline | `no_comma` | `comma`, `consistent_comma`, `no_comma`\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.53 | -\n\nThis cop checks for trailing comma in hash literals.\nThe configuration options are:\n\n- `consistent_comma`: Requires a comma after the\nlast item of all non-empty, multiline hash literals.\n- `comma`: Requires a comma after the last item in a hash,\nbut only when each item is on its own line.\n- `no_comma`: Does not requires a comma after the\nlast item in a hash\n\n### Examples\n\n#### EnforcedStyleForMultiline: consistent_comma\n\n```ruby\n# bad\na = { foo: 1, bar: 2, }\n\n# good\na = { foo: 1, bar: 2 }\n\n# good\na = {\n  foo: 1, bar: 2,\n  qux: 3,\n}\n\n# good\na = {\n  foo: 1, bar: 2, qux: 3,\n}\n\n# good\na = {\n  foo: 1,\n  bar: 2,\n}\n```\n#### EnforcedStyleForMultiline: comma\n\n```ruby\n# bad\na = { foo: 1, bar: 2, }\n\n# good\na = { foo: 1, bar: 2 }\n\n# bad\na = {\n  foo: 1, bar: 2,\n  qux: 3,\n}\n\n# good\na = {\n  foo: 1, bar: 2,\n  qux: 3\n}\n\n# bad\na = {\n  foo: 1, bar: 2, qux: 3,\n}\n\n# good\na = {\n  foo: 1, bar: 2, qux: 3\n}\n\n# good\na = {\n  foo: 1,\n  bar: 2,\n}\n```\n#### EnforcedStyleForMultiline: no_comma (default)\n\n```ruby\n# bad\na = { foo: 1, bar: 2, }\n\n# good\na = {\n  foo: 1,\n  bar: 2\n}\n```\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nEnforcedStyleForMultiline | `no_comma` | `comma`, `consistent_comma`, `no_comma`\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Style/TrailingMethodEndStatement",
              "name": "Style/TrailingMethodEndStatement",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Checks for trailing end statement on line of method body.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_style/#styletrailingmethodendstatement",
                "tags": [
                  "style"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Checks for trailing end statement on line of method body."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Checks for trailing end statement on line of method body."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.52 | -\n\nThis cop checks for trailing code after the method definition.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\ndef some_method\ndo_stuff; end\n\ndef do_this(x)\n  baz.map { |b| b.this(x) } end\n\ndef foo\n  block do\n    bar\n  end end\n\n# good\ndef some_method\n  do_stuff\nend\n\ndef do_this(x)\n  baz.map { |b| b.this(x) }\nend\n\ndef foo\n  block do\n    bar\n  end\nend\n```\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.52 | -\n\nThis cop checks for trailing code after the method definition.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\ndef some_method\ndo_stuff; end\n\ndef do_this(x)\n  baz.map { |b| b.this(x) } end\n\ndef foo\n  block do\n    bar\n  end end\n\n# good\ndef some_method\n  do_stuff\nend\n\ndef do_this(x)\n  baz.map { |b| b.this(x) }\nend\n\ndef foo\n  block do\n    bar\n  end\nend\n```\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Style/TrailingUnderscoreVariable",
              "name": "Style/TrailingUnderscoreVariable",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Checks for the usage of unneeded trailing underscores at the end of parallel variable assignment.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_style/#styletrailingunderscorevariable",
                "tags": [
                  "style"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Checks for the usage of unneeded trailing underscores at the end of parallel variable assignment."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Checks for the usage of unneeded trailing underscores at the end of parallel variable assignment."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.31 | 0.35\n\nThis cop checks for extra underscores in variable assignment.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\na, b, _ = foo()\na, b, _, = foo()\na, _, _ = foo()\na, _, _, = foo()\n\n# good\na, b, = foo()\na, = foo()\n*a, b, _ = foo()\n# => We need to know to not include 2 variables in a\na, *b, _ = foo()\n# => The correction `a, *b, = foo()` is a syntax error\n```\n#### AllowNamedUnderscoreVariables: true (default)\n\n```ruby\n# good\na, b, _something = foo()\n```\n#### AllowNamedUnderscoreVariables: false\n\n```ruby\n# bad\na, b, _something = foo()\n```\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nAllowNamedUnderscoreVariables | `true` | Boolean\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.31 | 0.35\n\nThis cop checks for extra underscores in variable assignment.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\na, b, _ = foo()\na, b, _, = foo()\na, _, _ = foo()\na, _, _, = foo()\n\n# good\na, b, = foo()\na, = foo()\n*a, b, _ = foo()\n# => We need to know to not include 2 variables in a\na, *b, _ = foo()\n# => The correction `a, *b, = foo()` is a syntax error\n```\n#### AllowNamedUnderscoreVariables: true (default)\n\n```ruby\n# good\na, b, _something = foo()\n```\n#### AllowNamedUnderscoreVariables: false\n\n```ruby\n# bad\na, b, _something = foo()\n```\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nAllowNamedUnderscoreVariables | `true` | Boolean\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Style/TrivialAccessors",
              "name": "Style/TrivialAccessors",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Prefer attr_* methods to trivial readers/writers.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_style/#styletrivialaccessors",
                "tags": [
                  "style"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Prefer attr_* methods to trivial readers/writers."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Prefer attr_* methods to trivial readers/writers."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.9 | 0.77\n\nThis cop looks for trivial reader/writer methods, that could\nhave been created with the attr_* family of functions automatically.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\ndef foo\n  @foo\nend\n\ndef bar=(val)\n  @bar = val\nend\n\ndef self.baz\n  @baz\nend\n\n# good\nattr_reader :foo\nattr_writer :bar\n\nclass << self\n  attr_reader :baz\nend\n```\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nExactNameMatch | `true` | Boolean\nAllowPredicates | `true` | Boolean\nAllowDSLWriters | `false` | Boolean\nIgnoreClassMethods | `false` | Boolean\nAllowedMethods | `to_ary`, `to_a`, `to_c`, `to_enum`, `to_h`, `to_hash`, `to_i`, `to_int`, `to_io`, `to_open`, `to_path`, `to_proc`, `to_r`, `to_regexp`, `to_str`, `to_s`, `to_sym` | Array\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#attr_family](https://rubystyle.guide#attr_family)\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.9 | 0.77\n\nThis cop looks for trivial reader/writer methods, that could\nhave been created with the attr_* family of functions automatically.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\ndef foo\n  @foo\nend\n\ndef bar=(val)\n  @bar = val\nend\n\ndef self.baz\n  @baz\nend\n\n# good\nattr_reader :foo\nattr_writer :bar\n\nclass << self\n  attr_reader :baz\nend\n```\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nExactNameMatch | `true` | Boolean\nAllowPredicates | `true` | Boolean\nAllowDSLWriters | `false` | Boolean\nIgnoreClassMethods | `false` | Boolean\nAllowedMethods | `to_ary`, `to_a`, `to_c`, `to_enum`, `to_h`, `to_hash`, `to_i`, `to_int`, `to_io`, `to_open`, `to_path`, `to_proc`, `to_r`, `to_regexp`, `to_str`, `to_s`, `to_sym` | Array\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#attr_family](https://rubystyle.guide#attr_family)\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Style/UnlessElse",
              "name": "Style/UnlessElse",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Do not use unless with else. Rewrite these with the positive case first.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_style/#styleunlesselse",
                "tags": [
                  "style"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Do not use unless with else. Rewrite these with the positive case first."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Do not use unless with else. Rewrite these with the positive case first."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.9 | -\n\nThis cop looks for *unless* expressions with *else* clauses.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nunless foo_bar.nil?\n  # do something...\nelse\n  # do a different thing...\nend\n\n# good\nif foo_bar.present?\n  # do something...\nelse\n  # do a different thing...\nend\n```\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#no-else-with-unless](https://rubystyle.guide#no-else-with-unless)\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.9 | -\n\nThis cop looks for *unless* expressions with *else* clauses.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nunless foo_bar.nil?\n  # do something...\nelse\n  # do a different thing...\nend\n\n# good\nif foo_bar.present?\n  # do something...\nelse\n  # do a different thing...\nend\n```\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#no-else-with-unless](https://rubystyle.guide#no-else-with-unless)\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Style/UnpackFirst",
              "name": "Style/UnpackFirst",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Checks for accessing the first element of `String#unpack` instead of using `unpack1`.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_style/#styleunpackfirst",
                "tags": [
                  "style"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Checks for accessing the first element of `String#unpack` instead of using `unpack1`."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Checks for accessing the first element of `String#unpack` instead of using `unpack1`."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.54 | -\n\nThis cop checks for accessing the first element of `String#unpack`\nwhich can be replaced with the shorter method `unpack1`.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\n'foo'.unpack('h*').first\n'foo'.unpack('h*')[0]\n'foo'.unpack('h*').slice(0)\n'foo'.unpack('h*').at(0)\n\n# good\n'foo'.unpack1('h*')\n```\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.54 | -\n\nThis cop checks for accessing the first element of `String#unpack`\nwhich can be replaced with the shorter method `unpack1`.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\n'foo'.unpack('h*').first\n'foo'.unpack('h*')[0]\n'foo'.unpack('h*').slice(0)\n'foo'.unpack('h*').at(0)\n\n# good\n'foo'.unpack1('h*')\n```\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Style/VariableInterpolation",
              "name": "Style/VariableInterpolation",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Don't interpolate global, instance and class variables directly in strings.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_style/#stylevariableinterpolation",
                "tags": [
                  "style"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Don't interpolate global, instance and class variables directly in strings."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Don't interpolate global, instance and class variables directly in strings."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.9 | 0.20\n\nThis cop checks for variable interpolation (like \"#@ivar\").\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\n\"His name is #$name\"\n/check #$pattern/\n\"Let's go to the #@store\"\n\n# good\n\"His name is #{$name}\"\n/check #{$pattern}/\n\"Let's go to the #{@store}\"\n```\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#curlies-interpolate](https://rubystyle.guide#curlies-interpolate)\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.9 | 0.20\n\nThis cop checks for variable interpolation (like \"#@ivar\").\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\n\"His name is #$name\"\n/check #$pattern/\n\"Let's go to the #@store\"\n\n# good\n\"His name is #{$name}\"\n/check #{$pattern}/\n\"Let's go to the #{@store}\"\n```\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#curlies-interpolate](https://rubystyle.guide#curlies-interpolate)\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Style/WhenThen",
              "name": "Style/WhenThen",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Use when x then ... for one-line cases.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_style/#stylewhenthen",
                "tags": [
                  "style"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Use when x then ... for one-line cases."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Use when x then ... for one-line cases."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.9 | -\n\nThis cop checks for *when;* uses in *case* expressions.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\ncase foo\nwhen 1; 'baz'\nwhen 2; 'bar'\nend\n\n# good\ncase foo\nwhen 1 then 'baz'\nwhen 2 then 'bar'\nend\n```\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#one-line-cases](https://rubystyle.guide#one-line-cases)\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.9 | -\n\nThis cop checks for *when;* uses in *case* expressions.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\ncase foo\nwhen 1; 'baz'\nwhen 2; 'bar'\nend\n\n# good\ncase foo\nwhen 1 then 'baz'\nwhen 2 then 'bar'\nend\n```\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#one-line-cases](https://rubystyle.guide#one-line-cases)\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Style/WhileUntilDo",
              "name": "Style/WhileUntilDo",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Checks for redundant do after while or until.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_style/#stylewhileuntildo",
                "tags": [
                  "style"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Checks for redundant do after while or until."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Checks for redundant do after while or until."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.9 | -\n\nChecks for uses of `do` in multi-line `while/until` statements.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nwhile x.any? do\n  do_something(x.pop)\nend\n\n# good\nwhile x.any?\n  do_something(x.pop)\nend\n```\n```ruby\n# bad\nuntil x.empty? do\n  do_something(x.pop)\nend\n\n# good\nuntil x.empty?\n  do_something(x.pop)\nend\n```\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#no-multiline-while-do](https://rubystyle.guide#no-multiline-while-do)\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.9 | -\n\nChecks for uses of `do` in multi-line `while/until` statements.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nwhile x.any? do\n  do_something(x.pop)\nend\n\n# good\nwhile x.any?\n  do_something(x.pop)\nend\n```\n```ruby\n# bad\nuntil x.empty? do\n  do_something(x.pop)\nend\n\n# good\nuntil x.empty?\n  do_something(x.pop)\nend\n```\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#no-multiline-while-do](https://rubystyle.guide#no-multiline-while-do)\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Style/WhileUntilModifier",
              "name": "Style/WhileUntilModifier",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Favor modifier while/until usage when you have a single-line body.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_style/#stylewhileuntilmodifier",
                "tags": [
                  "style"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Favor modifier while/until usage when you have a single-line body."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Favor modifier while/until usage when you have a single-line body."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.9 | 0.30\n\nChecks for while and until statements that would fit on one line\nif written as a modifier while/until. The maximum line length is\nconfigured in the `Layout/LineLength` cop.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nwhile x < 10\n  x += 1\nend\n\n# good\nx += 1 while x < 10\n```\n```ruby\n# bad\nuntil x > 10\n  x += 1\nend\n\n# good\nx += 1 until x > 10\n```\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#while-as-a-modifier](https://rubystyle.guide#while-as-a-modifier)\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.9 | 0.30\n\nChecks for while and until statements that would fit on one line\nif written as a modifier while/until. The maximum line length is\nconfigured in the `Layout/LineLength` cop.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nwhile x < 10\n  x += 1\nend\n\n# good\nx += 1 while x < 10\n```\n```ruby\n# bad\nuntil x > 10\n  x += 1\nend\n\n# good\nx += 1 until x > 10\n```\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#while-as-a-modifier](https://rubystyle.guide#while-as-a-modifier)\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Style/WordArray",
              "name": "Style/WordArray",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Use %w or %W for arrays of words.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_style/#stylewordarray",
                "tags": [
                  "style"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Use %w or %W for arrays of words."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Use %w or %W for arrays of words."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.9 | 0.36\n\nThis cop can check for array literals made up of word-like\nstrings, that are not using the %w() syntax.\n\nAlternatively, it can check for uses of the %w() syntax, in projects\nwhich do not want to include that syntax.\n\nConfiguration option: MinSize\nIf set, arrays with fewer elements than this value will not trigger the\ncop. For example, a `MinSize` of `3` will not enforce a style on an\narray of 2 or fewer elements.\n\n### Examples\n\n#### EnforcedStyle: percent (default)\n\n```ruby\n# good\n%w[foo bar baz]\n\n# bad\n['foo', 'bar', 'baz']\n```\n#### EnforcedStyle: brackets\n\n```ruby\n# good\n['foo', 'bar', 'baz']\n\n# bad\n%w[foo bar baz]\n```\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nEnforcedStyle | `percent` | `percent`, `brackets`\nMinSize | `2` | Integer\nWordRegex | `(?-mix:\\A(?:\\p{Word}|\\p{Word}-\\p{Word}|\\n|\\t)+\\z)` | \n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#percent-w](https://rubystyle.guide#percent-w)\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.9 | 0.36\n\nThis cop can check for array literals made up of word-like\nstrings, that are not using the %w() syntax.\n\nAlternatively, it can check for uses of the %w() syntax, in projects\nwhich do not want to include that syntax.\n\nConfiguration option: MinSize\nIf set, arrays with fewer elements than this value will not trigger the\ncop. For example, a `MinSize` of `3` will not enforce a style on an\narray of 2 or fewer elements.\n\n### Examples\n\n#### EnforcedStyle: percent (default)\n\n```ruby\n# good\n%w[foo bar baz]\n\n# bad\n['foo', 'bar', 'baz']\n```\n#### EnforcedStyle: brackets\n\n```ruby\n# good\n['foo', 'bar', 'baz']\n\n# bad\n%w[foo bar baz]\n```\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nEnforcedStyle | `percent` | `percent`, `brackets`\nMinSize | `2` | Integer\nWordRegex | `(?-mix:\\A(?:\\p{Word}|\\p{Word}-\\p{Word}|\\n|\\t)+\\z)` | \n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#percent-w](https://rubystyle.guide#percent-w)\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Style/YodaCondition",
              "name": "Style/YodaCondition",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Forbid or enforce yoda conditions.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_style/#styleyodacondition",
                "tags": [
                  "style"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Forbid or enforce yoda conditions."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Forbid or enforce yoda conditions."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | No | Yes (Unsafe) | 0.49 | 0.75\n\nThis cop can either enforce or forbid Yoda conditions,\ni.e. comparison operations where the order of expression is reversed.\neg. `5 == x`\n\n### Examples\n\n#### EnforcedStyle: forbid_for_all_comparison_operators (default)\n\n```ruby\n# bad\n99 == foo\n\"bar\" != foo\n42 >= foo\n10 < bar\n\n# good\nfoo == 99\nfoo == \"bar\"\nfoo <= 42\nbar > 10\n```\n#### EnforcedStyle: forbid_for_equality_operators_only\n\n```ruby\n# bad\n99 == foo\n\"bar\" != foo\n\n# good\n99 >= foo\n3 < a && a < 5\n```\n#### EnforcedStyle: require_for_all_comparison_operators\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nfoo == 99\nfoo == \"bar\"\nfoo <= 42\nbar > 10\n\n# good\n99 == foo\n\"bar\" != foo\n42 >= foo\n10 < bar\n```\n#### EnforcedStyle: require_for_equality_operators_only\n\n```ruby\n# bad\n99 >= foo\n3 < a && a < 5\n\n# good\n99 == foo\n\"bar\" != foo\n```\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nEnforcedStyle | `forbid_for_all_comparison_operators` | `forbid_for_all_comparison_operators`, `forbid_for_equality_operators_only`, `require_for_all_comparison_operators`, `require_for_equality_operators_only`\n\n### References\n\n* [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yoda_conditions](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yoda_conditions)\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | No | Yes (Unsafe) | 0.49 | 0.75\n\nThis cop can either enforce or forbid Yoda conditions,\ni.e. comparison operations where the order of expression is reversed.\neg. `5 == x`\n\n### Examples\n\n#### EnforcedStyle: forbid_for_all_comparison_operators (default)\n\n```ruby\n# bad\n99 == foo\n\"bar\" != foo\n42 >= foo\n10 < bar\n\n# good\nfoo == 99\nfoo == \"bar\"\nfoo <= 42\nbar > 10\n```\n#### EnforcedStyle: forbid_for_equality_operators_only\n\n```ruby\n# bad\n99 == foo\n\"bar\" != foo\n\n# good\n99 >= foo\n3 < a && a < 5\n```\n#### EnforcedStyle: require_for_all_comparison_operators\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nfoo == 99\nfoo == \"bar\"\nfoo <= 42\nbar > 10\n\n# good\n99 == foo\n\"bar\" != foo\n42 >= foo\n10 < bar\n```\n#### EnforcedStyle: require_for_equality_operators_only\n\n```ruby\n# bad\n99 >= foo\n3 < a && a < 5\n\n# good\n99 == foo\n\"bar\" != foo\n```\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nEnforcedStyle | `forbid_for_all_comparison_operators` | `forbid_for_all_comparison_operators`, `forbid_for_equality_operators_only`, `require_for_all_comparison_operators`, `require_for_equality_operators_only`\n\n### References\n\n* [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yoda_conditions](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yoda_conditions)\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Style/ZeroLengthPredicate",
              "name": "Style/ZeroLengthPredicate",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Use #empty? when testing for objects of length 0.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_style/#stylezerolengthpredicate",
                "tags": [
                  "style"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Use #empty? when testing for objects of length 0."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Use #empty? when testing for objects of length 0."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | No | Yes (Unsafe) | 0.37 | 0.39\n\nThis cop checks for numeric comparisons that can be replaced\nby a predicate method, such as receiver.length == 0,\nreceiver.length > 0, receiver.length != 0,\nreceiver.length < 1 and receiver.size == 0 that can be\nreplaced by receiver.empty? and !receiver.empty?.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\n[1, 2, 3].length == 0\n0 == \"foobar\".length\narray.length < 1\n{a: 1, b: 2}.length != 0\nstring.length > 0\nhash.size > 0\n\n# good\n[1, 2, 3].empty?\n\"foobar\".empty?\narray.empty?\n!{a: 1, b: 2}.empty?\n!string.empty?\n!hash.empty?\n```\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | No | Yes (Unsafe) | 0.37 | 0.39\n\nThis cop checks for numeric comparisons that can be replaced\nby a predicate method, such as receiver.length == 0,\nreceiver.length > 0, receiver.length != 0,\nreceiver.length < 1 and receiver.size == 0 that can be\nreplaced by receiver.empty? and !receiver.empty?.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\n[1, 2, 3].length == 0\n0 == \"foobar\".length\narray.length < 1\n{a: 1, b: 2}.length != 0\nstring.length > 0\nhash.size > 0\n\n# good\n[1, 2, 3].empty?\n\"foobar\".empty?\narray.empty?\n!{a: 1, b: 2}.empty?\n!string.empty?\n!hash.empty?\n```\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Lint/AmbiguousBlockAssociation",
              "name": "Lint/AmbiguousBlockAssociation",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Checks for ambiguous block association with method when param passed without parentheses.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_lint/#lintambiguousblockassociation",
                "tags": [
                  "lint"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Checks for ambiguous block association with method when param passed without parentheses."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Checks for ambiguous block association with method when param passed without parentheses."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | No | 0.48 | -\n\nThis cop checks for ambiguous block association with method\nwhen param passed without parentheses.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nsome_method a { |val| puts val }\n```\n```ruby\n# good\n# With parentheses, there's no ambiguity.\nsome_method(a) { |val| puts val }\n\n# good\n# Operator methods require no disambiguation\nfoo == bar { |b| b.baz }\n\n# good\n# Lambda arguments require no disambiguation\nfoo = ->(bar) { bar.baz }\n```\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#syntax](https://rubystyle.guide#syntax)\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | No | 0.48 | -\n\nThis cop checks for ambiguous block association with method\nwhen param passed without parentheses.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nsome_method a { |val| puts val }\n```\n```ruby\n# good\n# With parentheses, there's no ambiguity.\nsome_method(a) { |val| puts val }\n\n# good\n# Operator methods require no disambiguation\nfoo == bar { |b| b.baz }\n\n# good\n# Lambda arguments require no disambiguation\nfoo = ->(bar) { bar.baz }\n```\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#syntax](https://rubystyle.guide#syntax)\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Lint/AmbiguousOperator",
              "name": "Lint/AmbiguousOperator",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Checks for ambiguous operators in the first argument of a method invocation without parentheses.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_lint/#lintambiguousoperator",
                "tags": [
                  "lint"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Checks for ambiguous operators in the first argument of a method invocation without parentheses."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Checks for ambiguous operators in the first argument of a method invocation without parentheses."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.17 | 0.83\n\nThis cop checks for ambiguous operators in the first argument of a\nmethod invocation without parentheses.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\n\n# The `*` is interpreted as a splat operator but it could possibly be\n# a `*` method invocation (i.e. `do_something.*(some_array)`).\ndo_something *some_array\n```\n```ruby\n# good\n\n# With parentheses, there's no ambiguity.\ndo_something(*some_array)\n```\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#method-invocation-parens](https://rubystyle.guide#method-invocation-parens)\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.17 | 0.83\n\nThis cop checks for ambiguous operators in the first argument of a\nmethod invocation without parentheses.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\n\n# The `*` is interpreted as a splat operator but it could possibly be\n# a `*` method invocation (i.e. `do_something.*(some_array)`).\ndo_something *some_array\n```\n```ruby\n# good\n\n# With parentheses, there's no ambiguity.\ndo_something(*some_array)\n```\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#method-invocation-parens](https://rubystyle.guide#method-invocation-parens)\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Lint/AmbiguousRegexpLiteral",
              "name": "Lint/AmbiguousRegexpLiteral",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Checks for ambiguous regexp literals in the first argument of a method invocation without parentheses.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_lint/#lintambiguousregexpliteral",
                "tags": [
                  "lint"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Checks for ambiguous regexp literals in the first argument of a method invocation without parentheses."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Checks for ambiguous regexp literals in the first argument of a method invocation without parentheses."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.17 | 0.83\n\nThis cop checks for ambiguous regexp literals in the first argument of\na method invocation without parentheses.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\n\n# This is interpreted as a method invocation with a regexp literal,\n# but it could possibly be `/` method invocations.\n# (i.e. `do_something./(pattern)./(i)`)\ndo_something /pattern/i\n```\n```ruby\n# good\n\n# With parentheses, there's no ambiguity.\ndo_something(/pattern/i)\n```\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.17 | 0.83\n\nThis cop checks for ambiguous regexp literals in the first argument of\na method invocation without parentheses.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\n\n# This is interpreted as a method invocation with a regexp literal,\n# but it could possibly be `/` method invocations.\n# (i.e. `do_something./(pattern)./(i)`)\ndo_something /pattern/i\n```\n```ruby\n# good\n\n# With parentheses, there's no ambiguity.\ndo_something(/pattern/i)\n```\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Lint/AssignmentInCondition",
              "name": "Lint/AssignmentInCondition",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Don't use assignment in conditions.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_lint/#lintassignmentincondition",
                "tags": [
                  "lint"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Don't use assignment in conditions."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Don't use assignment in conditions."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | No | 0.9 | -\n\nThis cop checks for assignments in the conditions of\nif/while/until.\n\n`AllowSafeAssignment` option for safe assignment.\nBy safe assignment we mean putting parentheses around\nan assignment to indicate \"I know I'm using an assignment\nas a condition. It's not a mistake.\"\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nif some_var = true\n  do_something\nend\n\n# good\nif some_var == true\n  do_something\nend\n```\n#### AllowSafeAssignment: true (default)\n\n```ruby\n# good\nif (some_var = true)\n  do_something\nend\n```\n#### AllowSafeAssignment: false\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nif (some_var = true)\n  do_something\nend\n```\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nAllowSafeAssignment | `true` | Boolean\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#safe-assignment-in-condition](https://rubystyle.guide#safe-assignment-in-condition)\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | No | 0.9 | -\n\nThis cop checks for assignments in the conditions of\nif/while/until.\n\n`AllowSafeAssignment` option for safe assignment.\nBy safe assignment we mean putting parentheses around\nan assignment to indicate \"I know I'm using an assignment\nas a condition. It's not a mistake.\"\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nif some_var = true\n  do_something\nend\n\n# good\nif some_var == true\n  do_something\nend\n```\n#### AllowSafeAssignment: true (default)\n\n```ruby\n# good\nif (some_var = true)\n  do_something\nend\n```\n#### AllowSafeAssignment: false\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nif (some_var = true)\n  do_something\nend\n```\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nAllowSafeAssignment | `true` | Boolean\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#safe-assignment-in-condition](https://rubystyle.guide#safe-assignment-in-condition)\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Lint/BigDecimalNew",
              "name": "Lint/BigDecimalNew",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "`BigDecimal.new()` is deprecated. Use `BigDecimal()` instead.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_lint/#lintbigdecimalnew",
                "tags": [
                  "lint"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "`BigDecimal.new()` is deprecated. Use `BigDecimal()` instead."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "`BigDecimal.new()` is deprecated. Use `BigDecimal()` instead."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.53 | -\n\n`BigDecimal.new()` is deprecated since BigDecimal 1.3.3.\nThis cop identifies places where `BigDecimal.new()`\ncan be replaced by `BigDecimal()`.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nBigDecimal.new(123.456, 3)\n\n# good\nBigDecimal(123.456, 3)\n```\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.53 | -\n\n`BigDecimal.new()` is deprecated since BigDecimal 1.3.3.\nThis cop identifies places where `BigDecimal.new()`\ncan be replaced by `BigDecimal()`.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nBigDecimal.new(123.456, 3)\n\n# good\nBigDecimal(123.456, 3)\n```\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Lint/BooleanSymbol",
              "name": "Lint/BooleanSymbol",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Check for `:true` and `:false` symbols.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_lint/#lintbooleansymbol",
                "tags": [
                  "lint"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Check for `:true` and `:false` symbols."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Check for `:true` and `:false` symbols."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.50 | 0.81\n\nThis cop checks for `:true` and `:false` symbols.\nIn most cases it would be a typo.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\n:true\n\n# good\ntrue\n```\n```ruby\n# bad\n:false\n\n# good\nfalse\n```\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.50 | 0.81\n\nThis cop checks for `:true` and `:false` symbols.\nIn most cases it would be a typo.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\n:true\n\n# good\ntrue\n```\n```ruby\n# bad\n:false\n\n# good\nfalse\n```\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Lint/CircularArgumentReference",
              "name": "Lint/CircularArgumentReference",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Default values in optional keyword arguments and optional ordinal arguments should not refer back to the name of the argument.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_lint/#lintcircularargumentreference",
                "tags": [
                  "lint"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Default values in optional keyword arguments and optional ordinal arguments should not refer back to the name of the argument."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Default values in optional keyword arguments and optional ordinal arguments should not refer back to the name of the argument."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | No | 0.33 | -\n\nThis cop checks for circular argument references in optional keyword\narguments and optional ordinal arguments.\n\nThis cop mirrors a warning produced by MRI since 2.2.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\n\ndef bake(pie: pie)\n  pie.heat_up\nend\n```\n```ruby\n# good\n\ndef bake(pie:)\n  pie.refrigerate\nend\n```\n```ruby\n# good\n\ndef bake(pie: self.pie)\n  pie.feed_to(user)\nend\n```\n```ruby\n# bad\n\ndef cook(dry_ingredients = dry_ingredients)\n  dry_ingredients.reduce(&:+)\nend\n```\n```ruby\n# good\n\ndef cook(dry_ingredients = self.dry_ingredients)\n  dry_ingredients.combine\nend\n```\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | No | 0.33 | -\n\nThis cop checks for circular argument references in optional keyword\narguments and optional ordinal arguments.\n\nThis cop mirrors a warning produced by MRI since 2.2.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\n\ndef bake(pie: pie)\n  pie.heat_up\nend\n```\n```ruby\n# good\n\ndef bake(pie:)\n  pie.refrigerate\nend\n```\n```ruby\n# good\n\ndef bake(pie: self.pie)\n  pie.feed_to(user)\nend\n```\n```ruby\n# bad\n\ndef cook(dry_ingredients = dry_ingredients)\n  dry_ingredients.reduce(&:+)\nend\n```\n```ruby\n# good\n\ndef cook(dry_ingredients = self.dry_ingredients)\n  dry_ingredients.combine\nend\n```\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Lint/Debugger",
              "name": "Lint/Debugger",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Check for debugger calls.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_lint/#lintdebugger",
                "tags": [
                  "lint"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Check for debugger calls."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Check for debugger calls."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | No | 0.14 | 0.49\n\nThis cop checks for calls to debugger or pry.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad (ok during development)\n\n# using pry\ndef some_method\n  binding.pry\n  do_something\nend\n```\n```ruby\n# bad (ok during development)\n\n# using byebug\ndef some_method\n  byebug\n  do_something\nend\n```\n```ruby\n# good\n\ndef some_method\n  do_something\nend\n```\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | No | 0.14 | 0.49\n\nThis cop checks for calls to debugger or pry.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad (ok during development)\n\n# using pry\ndef some_method\n  binding.pry\n  do_something\nend\n```\n```ruby\n# bad (ok during development)\n\n# using byebug\ndef some_method\n  byebug\n  do_something\nend\n```\n```ruby\n# good\n\ndef some_method\n  do_something\nend\n```\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Lint/DeprecatedClassMethods",
              "name": "Lint/DeprecatedClassMethods",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Check for deprecated class method calls.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_lint/#lintdeprecatedclassmethods",
                "tags": [
                  "lint"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Check for deprecated class method calls."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Check for deprecated class method calls."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.19 | -\n\nThis cop checks for uses of the deprecated class method usages.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\n\nFile.exists?(some_path)\nDir.exists?(some_path)\niterator?\n```\n```ruby\n# good\n\nFile.exist?(some_path)\nDir.exist?(some_path)\nblock_given?\n```\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.19 | -\n\nThis cop checks for uses of the deprecated class method usages.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\n\nFile.exists?(some_path)\nDir.exists?(some_path)\niterator?\n```\n```ruby\n# good\n\nFile.exist?(some_path)\nDir.exist?(some_path)\nblock_given?\n```\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Lint/DisjunctiveAssignmentInConstructor",
              "name": "Lint/DisjunctiveAssignmentInConstructor",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "In constructor, plain assignment is preferred over disjunctive.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_lint/#lintdisjunctiveassignmentinconstructor",
                "tags": [
                  "lint"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "In constructor, plain assignment is preferred over disjunctive."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "In constructor, plain assignment is preferred over disjunctive."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | No | No | 0.62 | -\n\nThis cop checks constructors for disjunctive assignments that should\nbe plain assignments.\n\nSo far, this cop is only concerned with disjunctive assignment of\ninstance variables.\n\nIn ruby, an instance variable is nil until a value is assigned, so the\ndisjunction is unnecessary. A plain assignment has the same effect.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\ndef initialize\n  @x ||= 1\nend\n\n# good\ndef initialize\n  @x = 1\nend\n```\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | No | No | 0.62 | -\n\nThis cop checks constructors for disjunctive assignments that should\nbe plain assignments.\n\nSo far, this cop is only concerned with disjunctive assignment of\ninstance variables.\n\nIn ruby, an instance variable is nil until a value is assigned, so the\ndisjunction is unnecessary. A plain assignment has the same effect.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\ndef initialize\n  @x ||= 1\nend\n\n# good\ndef initialize\n  @x = 1\nend\n```\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Lint/DuplicateCaseCondition",
              "name": "Lint/DuplicateCaseCondition",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Do not repeat values in case conditionals.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_lint/#lintduplicatecasecondition",
                "tags": [
                  "lint"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Do not repeat values in case conditionals."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Do not repeat values in case conditionals."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | No | 0.45 | -\n\nThis cop checks that there are no repeated conditions\nused in case 'when' expressions.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\n\ncase x\nwhen 'first'\n  do_something\nwhen 'first'\n  do_something_else\nend\n```\n```ruby\n# good\n\ncase x\nwhen 'first'\n  do_something\nwhen 'second'\n  do_something_else\nend\n```\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | No | 0.45 | -\n\nThis cop checks that there are no repeated conditions\nused in case 'when' expressions.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\n\ncase x\nwhen 'first'\n  do_something\nwhen 'first'\n  do_something_else\nend\n```\n```ruby\n# good\n\ncase x\nwhen 'first'\n  do_something\nwhen 'second'\n  do_something_else\nend\n```\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Lint/DuplicateHashKey",
              "name": "Lint/DuplicateHashKey",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Check for duplicate keys in hash literals.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_lint/#lintduplicatehashkey",
                "tags": [
                  "lint"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Check for duplicate keys in hash literals."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Check for duplicate keys in hash literals."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | No | 0.34 | 0.77\n\nThis cop checks for duplicated keys in hash literals.\n\nThis cop mirrors a warning in Ruby 2.2.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\n\nhash = { food: 'apple', food: 'orange' }\n```\n```ruby\n# good\n\nhash = { food: 'apple', other_food: 'orange' }\n```\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | No | 0.34 | 0.77\n\nThis cop checks for duplicated keys in hash literals.\n\nThis cop mirrors a warning in Ruby 2.2.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\n\nhash = { food: 'apple', food: 'orange' }\n```\n```ruby\n# good\n\nhash = { food: 'apple', other_food: 'orange' }\n```\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Lint/DuplicateMethods",
              "name": "Lint/DuplicateMethods",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Check for duplicate method definitions.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_lint/#lintduplicatemethods",
                "tags": [
                  "lint"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Check for duplicate method definitions."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Check for duplicate method definitions."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | No | 0.29 | -\n\nThis cop checks for duplicated instance (or singleton) method\ndefinitions.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\n\ndef foo\n  1\nend\n\ndef foo\n  2\nend\n```\n```ruby\n# bad\n\ndef foo\n  1\nend\n\nalias foo bar\n```\n```ruby\n# good\n\ndef foo\n  1\nend\n\ndef bar\n  2\nend\n```\n```ruby\n# good\n\ndef foo\n  1\nend\n\nalias bar foo\n```\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | No | 0.29 | -\n\nThis cop checks for duplicated instance (or singleton) method\ndefinitions.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\n\ndef foo\n  1\nend\n\ndef foo\n  2\nend\n```\n```ruby\n# bad\n\ndef foo\n  1\nend\n\nalias foo bar\n```\n```ruby\n# good\n\ndef foo\n  1\nend\n\ndef bar\n  2\nend\n```\n```ruby\n# good\n\ndef foo\n  1\nend\n\nalias bar foo\n```\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Lint/EachWithObjectArgument",
              "name": "Lint/EachWithObjectArgument",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Check for immutable argument given to each_with_object.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_lint/#linteachwithobjectargument",
                "tags": [
                  "lint"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Check for immutable argument given to each_with_object."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Check for immutable argument given to each_with_object."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | No | 0.31 | -\n\nThis cop checks if each_with_object is called with an immutable\nargument. Since the argument is the object that the given block shall\nmake calls on to build something based on the enumerable that\neach_with_object iterates over, an immutable argument makes no sense.\nIt's definitely a bug.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\n\nsum = numbers.each_with_object(0) { |e, a| a += e }\n```\n```ruby\n# good\n\nnum = 0\nsum = numbers.each_with_object(num) { |e, a| a += e }\n```\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | No | 0.31 | -\n\nThis cop checks if each_with_object is called with an immutable\nargument. Since the argument is the object that the given block shall\nmake calls on to build something based on the enumerable that\neach_with_object iterates over, an immutable argument makes no sense.\nIt's definitely a bug.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\n\nsum = numbers.each_with_object(0) { |e, a| a += e }\n```\n```ruby\n# good\n\nnum = 0\nsum = numbers.each_with_object(num) { |e, a| a += e }\n```\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Lint/ElseLayout",
              "name": "Lint/ElseLayout",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Check for odd code arrangement in an else block.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_lint/#lintelselayout",
                "tags": [
                  "lint"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Check for odd code arrangement in an else block."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Check for odd code arrangement in an else block."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | No | 0.17 | -\n\nThis cop checks for odd else block layout - like\nhaving an expression on the same line as the else keyword,\nwhich is usually a mistake.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\n\nif something\n  # ...\nelse do_this\n  do_that\nend\n```\n```ruby\n# good\n\nif something\n  # ...\nelse\n  do_this\n  do_that\nend\n```\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | No | 0.17 | -\n\nThis cop checks for odd else block layout - like\nhaving an expression on the same line as the else keyword,\nwhich is usually a mistake.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\n\nif something\n  # ...\nelse do_this\n  do_that\nend\n```\n```ruby\n# good\n\nif something\n  # ...\nelse\n  do_this\n  do_that\nend\n```\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Lint/EmptyEnsure",
              "name": "Lint/EmptyEnsure",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Checks for empty ensure block.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_lint/#lintemptyensure",
                "tags": [
                  "lint"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Checks for empty ensure block."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Checks for empty ensure block."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.10 | 0.48\n\nThis cop checks for empty `ensure` blocks\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\n\ndef some_method\n  do_something\nensure\nend\n```\n```ruby\n# bad\n\nbegin\n  do_something\nensure\nend\n```\n```ruby\n# good\n\ndef some_method\n  do_something\nensure\n  do_something_else\nend\n```\n```ruby\n# good\n\nbegin\n  do_something\nensure\n  do_something_else\nend\n```\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nAutoCorrect | `false` | Boolean\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.10 | 0.48\n\nThis cop checks for empty `ensure` blocks\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\n\ndef some_method\n  do_something\nensure\nend\n```\n```ruby\n# bad\n\nbegin\n  do_something\nensure\nend\n```\n```ruby\n# good\n\ndef some_method\n  do_something\nensure\n  do_something_else\nend\n```\n```ruby\n# good\n\nbegin\n  do_something\nensure\n  do_something_else\nend\n```\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nAutoCorrect | `false` | Boolean\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Lint/EmptyExpression",
              "name": "Lint/EmptyExpression",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Checks for empty expressions.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_lint/#lintemptyexpression",
                "tags": [
                  "lint"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Checks for empty expressions."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Checks for empty expressions."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | No | 0.45 | -\n\nThis cop checks for the presence of empty expressions.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\n\nfoo = ()\nif ()\n  bar\nend\n```\n```ruby\n# good\n\nfoo = (some_expression)\nif (some_expression)\n  bar\nend\n```\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | No | 0.45 | -\n\nThis cop checks for the presence of empty expressions.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\n\nfoo = ()\nif ()\n  bar\nend\n```\n```ruby\n# good\n\nfoo = (some_expression)\nif (some_expression)\n  bar\nend\n```\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Lint/EmptyInterpolation",
              "name": "Lint/EmptyInterpolation",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Checks for empty string interpolation.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_lint/#lintemptyinterpolation",
                "tags": [
                  "lint"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Checks for empty string interpolation."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Checks for empty string interpolation."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.20 | 0.45\n\nThis cop checks for empty interpolation.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\n\n\"result is #{}\"\n```\n```ruby\n# good\n\n\"result is #{some_result}\"\n```\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.20 | 0.45\n\nThis cop checks for empty interpolation.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\n\n\"result is #{}\"\n```\n```ruby\n# good\n\n\"result is #{some_result}\"\n```\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Lint/EmptyWhen",
              "name": "Lint/EmptyWhen",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Checks for `when` branches with empty bodies.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_lint/#lintemptywhen",
                "tags": [
                  "lint"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Checks for `when` branches with empty bodies."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Checks for `when` branches with empty bodies."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | No | 0.45 | -\n\nThis cop checks for the presence of `when` branches without a body.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\n\ncase foo\nwhen bar then 1\nwhen baz then # nothing\nend\n```\n```ruby\n# good\n\ncase foo\nwhen bar then 1\nwhen baz then 2\nend\n```\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | No | 0.45 | -\n\nThis cop checks for the presence of `when` branches without a body.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\n\ncase foo\nwhen bar then 1\nwhen baz then # nothing\nend\n```\n```ruby\n# good\n\ncase foo\nwhen bar then 1\nwhen baz then 2\nend\n```\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Lint/EnsureReturn",
              "name": "Lint/EnsureReturn",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Do not use return in an ensure block.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_lint/#lintensurereturn",
                "tags": [
                  "lint"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Do not use return in an ensure block."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Do not use return in an ensure block."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | No | 0.9 | -\n\nThis cop checks for *return* from an *ensure* block.\nExplicit return from an ensure block alters the control flow\nas the return will take precedence over any exception being raised,\nand the exception will be silently thrown away as if it were rescued.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\n\nbegin\n  do_something\nensure\n  do_something_else\n  return\nend\n```\n```ruby\n# good\n\nbegin\n  do_something\nensure\n  do_something_else\nend\n```\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#no-return-ensure](https://rubystyle.guide#no-return-ensure)\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | No | 0.9 | -\n\nThis cop checks for *return* from an *ensure* block.\nExplicit return from an ensure block alters the control flow\nas the return will take precedence over any exception being raised,\nand the exception will be silently thrown away as if it were rescued.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\n\nbegin\n  do_something\nensure\n  do_something_else\n  return\nend\n```\n```ruby\n# good\n\nbegin\n  do_something\nensure\n  do_something_else\nend\n```\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#no-return-ensure](https://rubystyle.guide#no-return-ensure)\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Lint/ErbNewArguments",
              "name": "Lint/ErbNewArguments",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Use `:trim_mode` and `:eoutvar` keyword arguments to `ERB.new`.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_lint/#linterbnewarguments",
                "tags": [
                  "lint"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Use `:trim_mode` and `:eoutvar` keyword arguments to `ERB.new`."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Use `:trim_mode` and `:eoutvar` keyword arguments to `ERB.new`."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.56 | -\n\nThis cop emulates the following Ruby warnings in Ruby 2.6.\n\n% cat example.rb\nERB.new('hi', nil, '-', '@output_buffer')\n% ruby -rerb example.rb\nexample.rb:1: warning: Passing safe_level with the 2nd argument of\nERB.new is deprecated. Do not use it, and specify other arguments as\nkeyword arguments.\nexample.rb:1: warning: Passing trim_mode with the 3rd argument of\nERB.new is deprecated. Use keyword argument like\nERB.new(str, trim_mode:...) instead.\nexample.rb:1: warning: Passing eoutvar with the 4th argument of ERB.new\nis deprecated. Use keyword argument like ERB.new(str, eoutvar: ...)\ninstead.\n\nNow non-keyword arguments other than first one are softly deprecated\nand will be removed when Ruby 2.5 becomes EOL.\n`ERB.new` with non-keyword arguments is deprecated since ERB 2.2.0.\nUse `:trim_mode` and `:eoutvar` keyword arguments to `ERB.new`.\nThis cop identifies places where `ERB.new(str, trim_mode, eoutvar)` can\nbe replaced by `ERB.new(str, :trim_mode: trim_mode, eoutvar: eoutvar)`.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# Target codes supports Ruby 2.6 and higher only\n# bad\nERB.new(str, nil, '-', '@output_buffer')\n\n# good\nERB.new(str, trim_mode: '-', eoutvar: '@output_buffer')\n\n# Target codes supports Ruby 2.5 and lower only\n# good\nERB.new(str, nil, '-', '@output_buffer')\n\n# Target codes supports Ruby 2.6, 2.5 and lower\n# bad\nERB.new(str, nil, '-', '@output_buffer')\n\n# good\n# Ruby standard library style\n# https://github.com/ruby/ruby/commit/3406c5d\nif ERB.instance_method(:initialize).parameters.assoc(:key) # Ruby 2.6+\n  ERB.new(str, trim_mode: '-', eoutvar: '@output_buffer')\nelse\n  ERB.new(str, nil, '-', '@output_buffer')\nend\n\n# good\n# Use `RUBY_VERSION` style\nif RUBY_VERSION >= '2.6'\n  ERB.new(str, trim_mode: '-', eoutvar: '@output_buffer')\nelse\n  ERB.new(str, nil, '-', '@output_buffer')\nend\n```\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.56 | -\n\nThis cop emulates the following Ruby warnings in Ruby 2.6.\n\n% cat example.rb\nERB.new('hi', nil, '-', '@output_buffer')\n% ruby -rerb example.rb\nexample.rb:1: warning: Passing safe_level with the 2nd argument of\nERB.new is deprecated. Do not use it, and specify other arguments as\nkeyword arguments.\nexample.rb:1: warning: Passing trim_mode with the 3rd argument of\nERB.new is deprecated. Use keyword argument like\nERB.new(str, trim_mode:...) instead.\nexample.rb:1: warning: Passing eoutvar with the 4th argument of ERB.new\nis deprecated. Use keyword argument like ERB.new(str, eoutvar: ...)\ninstead.\n\nNow non-keyword arguments other than first one are softly deprecated\nand will be removed when Ruby 2.5 becomes EOL.\n`ERB.new` with non-keyword arguments is deprecated since ERB 2.2.0.\nUse `:trim_mode` and `:eoutvar` keyword arguments to `ERB.new`.\nThis cop identifies places where `ERB.new(str, trim_mode, eoutvar)` can\nbe replaced by `ERB.new(str, :trim_mode: trim_mode, eoutvar: eoutvar)`.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# Target codes supports Ruby 2.6 and higher only\n# bad\nERB.new(str, nil, '-', '@output_buffer')\n\n# good\nERB.new(str, trim_mode: '-', eoutvar: '@output_buffer')\n\n# Target codes supports Ruby 2.5 and lower only\n# good\nERB.new(str, nil, '-', '@output_buffer')\n\n# Target codes supports Ruby 2.6, 2.5 and lower\n# bad\nERB.new(str, nil, '-', '@output_buffer')\n\n# good\n# Ruby standard library style\n# https://github.com/ruby/ruby/commit/3406c5d\nif ERB.instance_method(:initialize).parameters.assoc(:key) # Ruby 2.6+\n  ERB.new(str, trim_mode: '-', eoutvar: '@output_buffer')\nelse\n  ERB.new(str, nil, '-', '@output_buffer')\nend\n\n# good\n# Use `RUBY_VERSION` style\nif RUBY_VERSION >= '2.6'\n  ERB.new(str, trim_mode: '-', eoutvar: '@output_buffer')\nelse\n  ERB.new(str, nil, '-', '@output_buffer')\nend\n```\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Lint/FlipFlop",
              "name": "Lint/FlipFlop",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Checks for flip-flops.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_lint/#lintflipflop",
                "tags": [
                  "lint"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Checks for flip-flops."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Checks for flip-flops."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | No | 0.16 | -\n\nThis cop looks for uses of flip-flop operator.\nflip-flop operator is deprecated since Ruby 2.6.0.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\n(1..20).each do |x|\n  puts x if (x == 5) .. (x == 10)\nend\n\n# good\n(1..20).each do |x|\n  puts x if (x >= 5) && (x <= 10)\nend\n```\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#no-flip-flops](https://rubystyle.guide#no-flip-flops)\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | No | 0.16 | -\n\nThis cop looks for uses of flip-flop operator.\nflip-flop operator is deprecated since Ruby 2.6.0.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\n(1..20).each do |x|\n  puts x if (x == 5) .. (x == 10)\nend\n\n# good\n(1..20).each do |x|\n  puts x if (x >= 5) && (x <= 10)\nend\n```\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#no-flip-flops](https://rubystyle.guide#no-flip-flops)\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Lint/FloatOutOfRange",
              "name": "Lint/FloatOutOfRange",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Catches floating-point literals too large or small for Ruby to represent.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_lint/#lintfloatoutofrange",
                "tags": [
                  "lint"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Catches floating-point literals too large or small for Ruby to represent."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Catches floating-point literals too large or small for Ruby to represent."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | No | 0.36 | -\n\nThis cop identifies Float literals which are, like, really really really\nreally really really really really big. Too big. No-one needs Floats\nthat big. If you need a float that big, something is wrong with you.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\n\nfloat = 3.0e400\n```\n```ruby\n# good\n\nfloat = 42.9\n```\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | No | 0.36 | -\n\nThis cop identifies Float literals which are, like, really really really\nreally really really really really big. Too big. No-one needs Floats\nthat big. If you need a float that big, something is wrong with you.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\n\nfloat = 3.0e400\n```\n```ruby\n# good\n\nfloat = 42.9\n```\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Lint/FormatParameterMismatch",
              "name": "Lint/FormatParameterMismatch",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "The number of parameters to format/sprint must match the fields.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_lint/#lintformatparametermismatch",
                "tags": [
                  "lint"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The number of parameters to format/sprint must match the fields."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The number of parameters to format/sprint must match the fields."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | No | 0.33 | -\n\nThis lint sees if there is a mismatch between the number of\nexpected fields for format/sprintf/#% and what is actually\npassed as arguments.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\n\nformat('A value: %s and another: %i', a_value)\n```\n```ruby\n# good\n\nformat('A value: %s and another: %i', a_value, another)\n```\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | No | 0.33 | -\n\nThis lint sees if there is a mismatch between the number of\nexpected fields for format/sprintf/#% and what is actually\npassed as arguments.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\n\nformat('A value: %s and another: %i', a_value)\n```\n```ruby\n# good\n\nformat('A value: %s and another: %i', a_value, another)\n```\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Lint/HeredocMethodCallPosition",
              "name": "Lint/HeredocMethodCallPosition",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Checks for the ordering of a method call where the receiver of the call is a HEREDOC.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_lint/#lintheredocmethodcallposition",
                "tags": [
                  "lint"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Checks for the ordering of a method call where the receiver of the call is a HEREDOC."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Checks for the ordering of a method call where the receiver of the call is a HEREDOC."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nDisabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.68 | -\n\nThis cop checks for the ordering of a method call where\nthe receiver of the call is a HEREDOC.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\n\n   <<-SQL\n     bar\n   SQL\n   .strip_indent\n\n   <<-SQL\n     bar\n   SQL\n   .strip_indent\n   .trim\n\n# good\n\n   <<~SQL\n     bar\n   SQL\n\n   <<~SQL.trim\n     bar\n   SQL\n```\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#heredoc-method-calls](https://rubystyle.guide#heredoc-method-calls)\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nDisabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.68 | -\n\nThis cop checks for the ordering of a method call where\nthe receiver of the call is a HEREDOC.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\n\n   <<-SQL\n     bar\n   SQL\n   .strip_indent\n\n   <<-SQL\n     bar\n   SQL\n   .strip_indent\n   .trim\n\n# good\n\n   <<~SQL\n     bar\n   SQL\n\n   <<~SQL.trim\n     bar\n   SQL\n```\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#heredoc-method-calls](https://rubystyle.guide#heredoc-method-calls)\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Lint/ImplicitStringConcatenation",
              "name": "Lint/ImplicitStringConcatenation",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Checks for adjacent string literals on the same line, which could better be represented as a single string literal.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_lint/#lintimplicitstringconcatenation",
                "tags": [
                  "lint"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Checks for adjacent string literals on the same line, which could better be represented as a single string literal."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Checks for adjacent string literals on the same line, which could better be represented as a single string literal."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | No | 0.36 | -\n\nThis cop checks for implicit string concatenation of string literals\nwhich are on the same line.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\n\narray = ['Item 1' 'Item 2']\n```\n```ruby\n# good\n\narray = ['Item 1Item 2']\narray = ['Item 1' + 'Item 2']\narray = [\n  'Item 1' \\\n  'Item 2'\n]\n```\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | No | 0.36 | -\n\nThis cop checks for implicit string concatenation of string literals\nwhich are on the same line.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\n\narray = ['Item 1' 'Item 2']\n```\n```ruby\n# good\n\narray = ['Item 1Item 2']\narray = ['Item 1' + 'Item 2']\narray = [\n  'Item 1' \\\n  'Item 2'\n]\n```\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Lint/IneffectiveAccessModifier",
              "name": "Lint/IneffectiveAccessModifier",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Checks for attempts to use `private` or `protected` to set the visibility of a class method, which does not work.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_lint/#lintineffectiveaccessmodifier",
                "tags": [
                  "lint"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Checks for attempts to use `private` or `protected` to set the visibility of a class method, which does not work."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Checks for attempts to use `private` or `protected` to set the visibility of a class method, which does not work."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | No | 0.36 | -\n\nThis cop checks for `private` or `protected` access modifiers which are\napplied to a singleton method. These access modifiers do not make\nsingleton methods private/protected. `private_class_method` can be\nused for that.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\n\nclass C\n  private\n\n  def self.method\n    puts 'hi'\n  end\nend\n```\n```ruby\n# good\n\nclass C\n  def self.method\n    puts 'hi'\n  end\n\n  private_class_method :method\nend\n```\n```ruby\n# good\n\nclass C\n  class << self\n    private\n\n    def method\n      puts 'hi'\n    end\n  end\nend\n```\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | No | 0.36 | -\n\nThis cop checks for `private` or `protected` access modifiers which are\napplied to a singleton method. These access modifiers do not make\nsingleton methods private/protected. `private_class_method` can be\nused for that.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\n\nclass C\n  private\n\n  def self.method\n    puts 'hi'\n  end\nend\n```\n```ruby\n# good\n\nclass C\n  def self.method\n    puts 'hi'\n  end\n\n  private_class_method :method\nend\n```\n```ruby\n# good\n\nclass C\n  class << self\n    private\n\n    def method\n      puts 'hi'\n    end\n  end\nend\n```\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Lint/InheritException",
              "name": "Lint/InheritException",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Avoid inheriting from the `Exception` class.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_lint/#lintinheritexception",
                "tags": [
                  "lint"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Avoid inheriting from the `Exception` class."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Avoid inheriting from the `Exception` class."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.41 | -\n\nThis cop looks for error classes inheriting from `Exception`\nand its standard library subclasses, excluding subclasses of\n`StandardError`. It is configurable to suggest using either\n`RuntimeError` (default) or `StandardError` instead.\n\n### Examples\n\n#### EnforcedStyle: runtime_error (default)\n\n```ruby\n# bad\n\nclass C < Exception; end\n\nC = Class.new(Exception)\n\n# good\n\nclass C < RuntimeError; end\n\nC = Class.new(RuntimeError)\n```\n#### EnforcedStyle: standard_error\n\n```ruby\n# bad\n\nclass C < Exception; end\n\nC = Class.new(Exception)\n\n# good\n\nclass C < StandardError; end\n\nC = Class.new(StandardError)\n```\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nEnforcedStyle | `runtime_error` | `runtime_error`, `standard_error`\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.41 | -\n\nThis cop looks for error classes inheriting from `Exception`\nand its standard library subclasses, excluding subclasses of\n`StandardError`. It is configurable to suggest using either\n`RuntimeError` (default) or `StandardError` instead.\n\n### Examples\n\n#### EnforcedStyle: runtime_error (default)\n\n```ruby\n# bad\n\nclass C < Exception; end\n\nC = Class.new(Exception)\n\n# good\n\nclass C < RuntimeError; end\n\nC = Class.new(RuntimeError)\n```\n#### EnforcedStyle: standard_error\n\n```ruby\n# bad\n\nclass C < Exception; end\n\nC = Class.new(Exception)\n\n# good\n\nclass C < StandardError; end\n\nC = Class.new(StandardError)\n```\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nEnforcedStyle | `runtime_error` | `runtime_error`, `standard_error`\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Lint/InterpolationCheck",
              "name": "Lint/InterpolationCheck",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Raise warning for interpolation in single q strs.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_lint/#lintinterpolationcheck",
                "tags": [
                  "lint"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Raise warning for interpolation in single q strs."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Raise warning for interpolation in single q strs."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | No | 0.50 | -\n\nThis cop checks for interpolation in a single quoted string.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\n\nfoo = 'something with #{interpolation} inside'\n```\n```ruby\n# good\n\nfoo = \"something with #{interpolation} inside\"\n```\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | No | 0.50 | -\n\nThis cop checks for interpolation in a single quoted string.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\n\nfoo = 'something with #{interpolation} inside'\n```\n```ruby\n# good\n\nfoo = \"something with #{interpolation} inside\"\n```\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Lint/LiteralAsCondition",
              "name": "Lint/LiteralAsCondition",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Checks of literals used in conditions.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_lint/#lintliteralascondition",
                "tags": [
                  "lint"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Checks of literals used in conditions."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Checks of literals used in conditions."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | No | 0.51 | -\n\nThis cop checks for literals used as the conditions or as\noperands in and/or expressions serving as the conditions of\nif/while/until.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\n\nif 20\n  do_something\nend\n```\n```ruby\n# bad\n\nif some_var && true\n  do_something\nend\n```\n```ruby\n# good\n\nif some_var && some_condition\n  do_something\nend\n```\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | No | 0.51 | -\n\nThis cop checks for literals used as the conditions or as\noperands in and/or expressions serving as the conditions of\nif/while/until.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\n\nif 20\n  do_something\nend\n```\n```ruby\n# bad\n\nif some_var && true\n  do_something\nend\n```\n```ruby\n# good\n\nif some_var && some_condition\n  do_something\nend\n```\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Lint/LiteralInInterpolation",
              "name": "Lint/LiteralInInterpolation",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Checks for literals used in interpolation.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_lint/#lintliteralininterpolation",
                "tags": [
                  "lint"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Checks for literals used in interpolation."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Checks for literals used in interpolation."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.19 | 0.32\n\nThis cop checks for interpolated literals.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\n\n\"result is #{10}\"\n```\n```ruby\n# good\n\n\"result is 10\"\n```\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.19 | 0.32\n\nThis cop checks for interpolated literals.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\n\n\"result is #{10}\"\n```\n```ruby\n# good\n\n\"result is 10\"\n```\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Lint/Loop",
              "name": "Lint/Loop",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Use Kernel#loop with break rather than begin/end/until or begin/end/while for post-loop tests.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_lint/#lintloop",
                "tags": [
                  "lint"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Use Kernel#loop with break rather than begin/end/until or begin/end/while for post-loop tests."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Use Kernel#loop with break rather than begin/end/until or begin/end/while for post-loop tests."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | No | 0.9 | -\n\nThis cop checks for uses of *begin...end while/until something*.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\n\n# using while\nbegin\n  do_something\nend while some_condition\n```\n```ruby\n# bad\n\n# using until\nbegin\n  do_something\nend until some_condition\n```\n```ruby\n# good\n\n# while replacement\nloop do\n  do_something\n  break unless some_condition\nend\n```\n```ruby\n# good\n\n# until replacement\nloop do\n  do_something\n  break if some_condition\nend\n```\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#loop-with-break](https://rubystyle.guide#loop-with-break)\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | No | 0.9 | -\n\nThis cop checks for uses of *begin...end while/until something*.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\n\n# using while\nbegin\n  do_something\nend while some_condition\n```\n```ruby\n# bad\n\n# using until\nbegin\n  do_something\nend until some_condition\n```\n```ruby\n# good\n\n# while replacement\nloop do\n  do_something\n  break unless some_condition\nend\n```\n```ruby\n# good\n\n# until replacement\nloop do\n  do_something\n  break if some_condition\nend\n```\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#loop-with-break](https://rubystyle.guide#loop-with-break)\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Lint/MissingCopEnableDirective",
              "name": "Lint/MissingCopEnableDirective",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Checks for a `# rubocop:enable` after `# rubocop:disable`.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_lint/#lintmissingcopenabledirective",
                "tags": [
                  "lint"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Checks for a `# rubocop:enable` after `# rubocop:disable`."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Checks for a `# rubocop:enable` after `# rubocop:disable`."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | No | 0.52 | -\n\nThis cop checks that there is an `# rubocop:enable ...` statement\nafter a `# rubocop:disable ...` statement. This will prevent leaving\ncop disables on wide ranges of code, that latter contributors to\na file wouldn't be aware of.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# Lint/MissingCopEnableDirective:\n#   MaximumRangeSize: .inf\n\n# good\n# rubocop:disable Layout/SpaceAroundOperators\nx= 0\n# rubocop:enable Layout/SpaceAroundOperators\n# y = 1\n# EOF\n\n# bad\n# rubocop:disable Layout/SpaceAroundOperators\nx= 0\n# EOF\n```\n```ruby\n# Lint/MissingCopEnableDirective:\n#   MaximumRangeSize: 2\n\n# good\n# rubocop:disable Layout/SpaceAroundOperators\nx= 0\n# With the previous, there are 2 lines on which cop is disabled.\n# rubocop:enable Layout/SpaceAroundOperators\n\n# bad\n# rubocop:disable Layout/SpaceAroundOperators\nx= 0\nx += 1\n# Including this, that's 3 lines on which the cop is disabled.\n# rubocop:enable Layout/SpaceAroundOperators\n```\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nMaximumRangeSize | `Infinity` | Float\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | No | 0.52 | -\n\nThis cop checks that there is an `# rubocop:enable ...` statement\nafter a `# rubocop:disable ...` statement. This will prevent leaving\ncop disables on wide ranges of code, that latter contributors to\na file wouldn't be aware of.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# Lint/MissingCopEnableDirective:\n#   MaximumRangeSize: .inf\n\n# good\n# rubocop:disable Layout/SpaceAroundOperators\nx= 0\n# rubocop:enable Layout/SpaceAroundOperators\n# y = 1\n# EOF\n\n# bad\n# rubocop:disable Layout/SpaceAroundOperators\nx= 0\n# EOF\n```\n```ruby\n# Lint/MissingCopEnableDirective:\n#   MaximumRangeSize: 2\n\n# good\n# rubocop:disable Layout/SpaceAroundOperators\nx= 0\n# With the previous, there are 2 lines on which cop is disabled.\n# rubocop:enable Layout/SpaceAroundOperators\n\n# bad\n# rubocop:disable Layout/SpaceAroundOperators\nx= 0\nx += 1\n# Including this, that's 3 lines on which the cop is disabled.\n# rubocop:enable Layout/SpaceAroundOperators\n```\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nMaximumRangeSize | `Infinity` | Float\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Lint/MultipleComparison",
              "name": "Lint/MultipleComparison",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Use `&&` operator to compare multiple values.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_lint/#lintmultiplecomparison",
                "tags": [
                  "lint"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Use `&&` operator to compare multiple values."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Use `&&` operator to compare multiple values."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.47 | 0.77\n\nIn math and Python, we can use `x < y < z` style comparison to compare\nmultiple value. However, we can't use the comparison in Ruby. However,\nthe comparison is not syntax error. This cop checks the bad usage of\ncomparison operators.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\n\nx < y < z\n10 <= x <= 20\n```\n```ruby\n# good\n\nx < y && y < z\n10 <= x && x <= 20\n```\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.47 | 0.77\n\nIn math and Python, we can use `x < y < z` style comparison to compare\nmultiple value. However, we can't use the comparison in Ruby. However,\nthe comparison is not syntax error. This cop checks the bad usage of\ncomparison operators.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\n\nx < y < z\n10 <= x <= 20\n```\n```ruby\n# good\n\nx < y && y < z\n10 <= x && x <= 20\n```\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Lint/NestedMethodDefinition",
              "name": "Lint/NestedMethodDefinition",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Do not use nested method definitions.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_lint/#lintnestedmethoddefinition",
                "tags": [
                  "lint"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Do not use nested method definitions."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Do not use nested method definitions."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | No | 0.32 | -\n\nThis cop checks for nested method definitions.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\n\n# `bar` definition actually produces methods in the same scope\n# as the outer `foo` method. Furthermore, the `bar` method\n# will be redefined every time `foo` is invoked.\ndef foo\n  def bar\n  end\nend\n```\n```ruby\n# good\n\ndef foo\n  bar = -> { puts 'hello' }\n  bar.call\nend\n```\n```ruby\n# good\n\ndef foo\n  self.class.class_eval do\n    def bar\n    end\n  end\nend\n\ndef foo\n  self.class.module_exec do\n    def bar\n    end\n  end\nend\n```\n```ruby\n# good\n\ndef foo\n  class << self\n    def bar\n    end\n  end\nend\n```\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#no-nested-methods](https://rubystyle.guide#no-nested-methods)\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | No | 0.32 | -\n\nThis cop checks for nested method definitions.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\n\n# `bar` definition actually produces methods in the same scope\n# as the outer `foo` method. Furthermore, the `bar` method\n# will be redefined every time `foo` is invoked.\ndef foo\n  def bar\n  end\nend\n```\n```ruby\n# good\n\ndef foo\n  bar = -> { puts 'hello' }\n  bar.call\nend\n```\n```ruby\n# good\n\ndef foo\n  self.class.class_eval do\n    def bar\n    end\n  end\nend\n\ndef foo\n  self.class.module_exec do\n    def bar\n    end\n  end\nend\n```\n```ruby\n# good\n\ndef foo\n  class << self\n    def bar\n    end\n  end\nend\n```\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#no-nested-methods](https://rubystyle.guide#no-nested-methods)\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Lint/NestedPercentLiteral",
              "name": "Lint/NestedPercentLiteral",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Checks for nested percent literals.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_lint/#lintnestedpercentliteral",
                "tags": [
                  "lint"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Checks for nested percent literals."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Checks for nested percent literals."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | No | 0.52 | -\n\nThis cop checks for nested percent literals.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\n\n# The percent literal for nested_attributes is parsed as four tokens,\n# yielding the array [:name, :content, :\"%i[incorrectly\", :\"nested]\"].\nattributes = {\n  valid_attributes: %i[name content],\n  nested_attributes: %i[name content %i[incorrectly nested]]\n}\n```\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | No | 0.52 | -\n\nThis cop checks for nested percent literals.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\n\n# The percent literal for nested_attributes is parsed as four tokens,\n# yielding the array [:name, :content, :\"%i[incorrectly\", :\"nested]\"].\nattributes = {\n  valid_attributes: %i[name content],\n  nested_attributes: %i[name content %i[incorrectly nested]]\n}\n```\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Lint/NextWithoutAccumulator",
              "name": "Lint/NextWithoutAccumulator",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Do not omit the accumulator when calling `next` in a `reduce`/`inject` block.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_lint/#lintnextwithoutaccumulator",
                "tags": [
                  "lint"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Do not omit the accumulator when calling `next` in a `reduce`/`inject` block."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Do not omit the accumulator when calling `next` in a `reduce`/`inject` block."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | No | 0.36 | -\n\nDon't omit the accumulator when calling `next` in a `reduce` block.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\n\nresult = (1..4).reduce(0) do |acc, i|\n  next if i.odd?\n  acc + i\nend\n```\n```ruby\n# good\n\nresult = (1..4).reduce(0) do |acc, i|\n  next acc if i.odd?\n  acc + i\nend\n```\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | No | 0.36 | -\n\nDon't omit the accumulator when calling `next` in a `reduce` block.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\n\nresult = (1..4).reduce(0) do |acc, i|\n  next if i.odd?\n  acc + i\nend\n```\n```ruby\n# good\n\nresult = (1..4).reduce(0) do |acc, i|\n  next acc if i.odd?\n  acc + i\nend\n```\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Lint/NonDeterministicRequireOrder",
              "name": "Lint/NonDeterministicRequireOrder",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Always sort arrays returned by Dir.glob when requiring files.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_lint/#lintnondeterministicrequireorder",
                "tags": [
                  "lint"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Always sort arrays returned by Dir.glob when requiring files."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Always sort arrays returned by Dir.glob when requiring files."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | No | Yes (Unsafe) | 0.78 | -\n\n`Dir[...]` and `Dir.glob(...)` do not make any guarantees about\nthe order in which files are returned. The final order is\ndetermined by the operating system and file system.\nThis means that using them in cases where the order matters,\nsuch as requiring files, can lead to intermittent failures\nthat are hard to debug. To ensure this doesn't happen,\nalways sort the list.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nDir[\"./lib/**/*.rb\"].each do |file|\n  require file\nend\n\n# good\nDir[\"./lib/**/*.rb\"].sort.each do |file|\n  require file\nend\n```\n```ruby\n# bad\nDir.glob(Rails.root.join(__dir__, 'test', '*.rb')) do |file|\n  require file\nend\n\n# good\nDir.glob(Rails.root.join(__dir__, 'test', '*.rb')).sort.each do |file|\n  require file\nend\n```\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | No | Yes (Unsafe) | 0.78 | -\n\n`Dir[...]` and `Dir.glob(...)` do not make any guarantees about\nthe order in which files are returned. The final order is\ndetermined by the operating system and file system.\nThis means that using them in cases where the order matters,\nsuch as requiring files, can lead to intermittent failures\nthat are hard to debug. To ensure this doesn't happen,\nalways sort the list.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nDir[\"./lib/**/*.rb\"].each do |file|\n  require file\nend\n\n# good\nDir[\"./lib/**/*.rb\"].sort.each do |file|\n  require file\nend\n```\n```ruby\n# bad\nDir.glob(Rails.root.join(__dir__, 'test', '*.rb')) do |file|\n  require file\nend\n\n# good\nDir.glob(Rails.root.join(__dir__, 'test', '*.rb')).sort.each do |file|\n  require file\nend\n```\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Lint/NonLocalExitFromIterator",
              "name": "Lint/NonLocalExitFromIterator",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Do not use return in iterator to cause non-local exit.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_lint/#lintnonlocalexitfromiterator",
                "tags": [
                  "lint"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Do not use return in iterator to cause non-local exit."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Do not use return in iterator to cause non-local exit."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | No | 0.30 | -\n\nThis cop checks for non-local exits from iterators without a return\nvalue. It registers an offense under these conditions:\n\n - No value is returned,\n - the block is preceded by a method chain,\n - the block has arguments,\n - the method which receives the block is not `define_method`\n   or `define_singleton_method`,\n - the return is not contained in an inner scope, e.g. a lambda or a\n   method definition.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\nclass ItemApi\n  rescue_from ValidationError do |e| # non-iteration block with arg\n    return { message: 'validation error' } unless e.errors # allowed\n    error_array = e.errors.map do |error| # block with method chain\n      return if error.suppress? # warned\n      return \"#{error.param}: invalid\" unless error.message # allowed\n      \"#{error.param}: #{error.message}\"\n    end\n    { message: 'validation error', errors: error_array }\n  end\n\n  def update_items\n    transaction do # block without arguments\n      return unless update_necessary? # allowed\n      find_each do |item| # block without method chain\n        return if item.stock == 0 # false-negative...\n        item.update!(foobar: true)\n      end\n    end\n  end\nend\n```\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | No | 0.30 | -\n\nThis cop checks for non-local exits from iterators without a return\nvalue. It registers an offense under these conditions:\n\n - No value is returned,\n - the block is preceded by a method chain,\n - the block has arguments,\n - the method which receives the block is not `define_method`\n   or `define_singleton_method`,\n - the return is not contained in an inner scope, e.g. a lambda or a\n   method definition.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\nclass ItemApi\n  rescue_from ValidationError do |e| # non-iteration block with arg\n    return { message: 'validation error' } unless e.errors # allowed\n    error_array = e.errors.map do |error| # block with method chain\n      return if error.suppress? # warned\n      return \"#{error.param}: invalid\" unless error.message # allowed\n      \"#{error.param}: #{error.message}\"\n    end\n    { message: 'validation error', errors: error_array }\n  end\n\n  def update_items\n    transaction do # block without arguments\n      return unless update_necessary? # allowed\n      find_each do |item| # block without method chain\n        return if item.stock == 0 # false-negative...\n        item.update!(foobar: true)\n      end\n    end\n  end\nend\n```\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Lint/NumberConversion",
              "name": "Lint/NumberConversion",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Checks unsafe usage of number conversion methods.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_lint/#lintnumberconversion",
                "tags": [
                  "lint"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Checks unsafe usage of number conversion methods."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Checks unsafe usage of number conversion methods."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nDisabled | Yes | Yes (Unsafe) | 0.53 | 0.70\n\nThis cop warns the usage of unsafe number conversions. Unsafe\nnumber conversion can cause unexpected error if auto type conversion\nfails. Cop prefer parsing with number class instead.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\n\n'10'.to_i\n'10.2'.to_f\n'10'.to_c\n\n# good\n\nInteger('10', 10)\nFloat('10.2')\nComplex('10')\n```\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nDisabled | Yes | Yes (Unsafe) | 0.53 | 0.70\n\nThis cop warns the usage of unsafe number conversions. Unsafe\nnumber conversion can cause unexpected error if auto type conversion\nfails. Cop prefer parsing with number class instead.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\n\n'10'.to_i\n'10.2'.to_f\n'10'.to_c\n\n# good\n\nInteger('10', 10)\nFloat('10.2')\nComplex('10')\n```\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Lint/OrderedMagicComments",
              "name": "Lint/OrderedMagicComments",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Checks the proper ordering of magic comments and whether a magic comment is not placed before a shebang.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_lint/#lintorderedmagiccomments",
                "tags": [
                  "lint"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Checks the proper ordering of magic comments and whether a magic comment is not placed before a shebang."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Checks the proper ordering of magic comments and whether a magic comment is not placed before a shebang."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.53 | -\n\nChecks the proper ordering of magic comments and whether\na magic comment is not placed before a shebang.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\n\n# frozen_string_literal: true\n# encoding: ascii\np [''.frozen?, ''.encoding] #=> [true, #<Encoding:UTF-8>]\n\n# good\n\n# encoding: ascii\n# frozen_string_literal: true\np [''.frozen?, ''.encoding] #=> [true, #<Encoding:US-ASCII>]\n\n# good\n\n#!/usr/bin/env ruby\n# encoding: ascii\n# frozen_string_literal: true\np [''.frozen?, ''.encoding] #=> [true, #<Encoding:US-ASCII>]\n```\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.53 | -\n\nChecks the proper ordering of magic comments and whether\na magic comment is not placed before a shebang.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\n\n# frozen_string_literal: true\n# encoding: ascii\np [''.frozen?, ''.encoding] #=> [true, #<Encoding:UTF-8>]\n\n# good\n\n# encoding: ascii\n# frozen_string_literal: true\np [''.frozen?, ''.encoding] #=> [true, #<Encoding:US-ASCII>]\n\n# good\n\n#!/usr/bin/env ruby\n# encoding: ascii\n# frozen_string_literal: true\np [''.frozen?, ''.encoding] #=> [true, #<Encoding:US-ASCII>]\n```\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Lint/ParenthesesAsGroupedExpression",
              "name": "Lint/ParenthesesAsGroupedExpression",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Checks for method calls with a space before the opening parenthesis.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_lint/#lintparenthesesasgroupedexpression",
                "tags": [
                  "lint"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Checks for method calls with a space before the opening parenthesis."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Checks for method calls with a space before the opening parenthesis."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | No | 0.12 | -\n\nChecks for space between the name of a called method and a left\nparenthesis.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\n\nputs (x + y)\n```\n```ruby\n# good\n\nputs(x + y)\n```\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#parens-no-spaces](https://rubystyle.guide#parens-no-spaces)\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | No | 0.12 | -\n\nChecks for space between the name of a called method and a left\nparenthesis.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\n\nputs (x + y)\n```\n```ruby\n# good\n\nputs(x + y)\n```\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#parens-no-spaces](https://rubystyle.guide#parens-no-spaces)\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Lint/PercentStringArray",
              "name": "Lint/PercentStringArray",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Checks for unwanted commas and quotes in %w/%W literals.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_lint/#lintpercentstringarray",
                "tags": [
                  "lint"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Checks for unwanted commas and quotes in %w/%W literals."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Checks for unwanted commas and quotes in %w/%W literals."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | No | Yes (Unsafe) | 0.41 | -\n\nThis cop checks for quotes and commas in %w, e.g. `%w('foo', \"bar\")`\n\nIt is more likely that the additional characters are unintended (for\nexample, mistranslating an array of literals to percent string notation)\nrather than meant to be part of the resulting strings.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\n\n%w('foo', \"bar\")\n```\n```ruby\n# good\n\n%w(foo bar)\n```\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | No | Yes (Unsafe) | 0.41 | -\n\nThis cop checks for quotes and commas in %w, e.g. `%w('foo', \"bar\")`\n\nIt is more likely that the additional characters are unintended (for\nexample, mistranslating an array of literals to percent string notation)\nrather than meant to be part of the resulting strings.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\n\n%w('foo', \"bar\")\n```\n```ruby\n# good\n\n%w(foo bar)\n```\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Lint/PercentSymbolArray",
              "name": "Lint/PercentSymbolArray",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Checks for unwanted commas and colons in %i/%I literals.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_lint/#lintpercentsymbolarray",
                "tags": [
                  "lint"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Checks for unwanted commas and colons in %i/%I literals."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Checks for unwanted commas and colons in %i/%I literals."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.41 | -\n\nThis cop checks for colons and commas in %i, e.g. `%i(:foo, :bar)`\n\nIt is more likely that the additional characters are unintended (for\nexample, mistranslating an array of literals to percent string notation)\nrather than meant to be part of the resulting symbols.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\n\n%i(:foo, :bar)\n```\n```ruby\n# good\n\n%i(foo bar)\n```\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.41 | -\n\nThis cop checks for colons and commas in %i, e.g. `%i(:foo, :bar)`\n\nIt is more likely that the additional characters are unintended (for\nexample, mistranslating an array of literals to percent string notation)\nrather than meant to be part of the resulting symbols.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\n\n%i(:foo, :bar)\n```\n```ruby\n# good\n\n%i(foo bar)\n```\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Lint/RaiseException",
              "name": "Lint/RaiseException",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Checks for `raise` or `fail` statements which are raising `Exception` class.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_lint/#lintraiseexception",
                "tags": [
                  "lint"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Checks for `raise` or `fail` statements which are raising `Exception` class."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Checks for `raise` or `fail` statements which are raising `Exception` class."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nPending | Yes | No | 0.81 | -\n\nThis cop checks for `raise` or `fail` statements which are\nraising `Exception` class.\n\nYou can specify a module name that will be an implicit namespace\nusing `AllowedImplicitNamespaces` option. The cop cause a false positive\nfor namespaced `Exception` when a namespace is omitted. This option can\nprevent the false positive by specifying a namespace to be omitted for\n`Exception`. Alternatively, make `Exception` a fully qualified class\nname with an explicit namespace.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nraise Exception, 'Error message here'\n\n# good\nraise StandardError, 'Error message here'\n```\n#### AllowedImplicitNamespaces: ['Gem']\n\n```ruby\n# good\nmodule Gem\n  def self.foo\n    raise Exception # This exception means `Gem::Exception`.\n  end\nend\n```\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nAllowedImplicitNamespaces | `Gem` | Array\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#raise-exception](https://rubystyle.guide#raise-exception)\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nPending | Yes | No | 0.81 | -\n\nThis cop checks for `raise` or `fail` statements which are\nraising `Exception` class.\n\nYou can specify a module name that will be an implicit namespace\nusing `AllowedImplicitNamespaces` option. The cop cause a false positive\nfor namespaced `Exception` when a namespace is omitted. This option can\nprevent the false positive by specifying a namespace to be omitted for\n`Exception`. Alternatively, make `Exception` a fully qualified class\nname with an explicit namespace.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nraise Exception, 'Error message here'\n\n# good\nraise StandardError, 'Error message here'\n```\n#### AllowedImplicitNamespaces: ['Gem']\n\n```ruby\n# good\nmodule Gem\n  def self.foo\n    raise Exception # This exception means `Gem::Exception`.\n  end\nend\n```\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nAllowedImplicitNamespaces | `Gem` | Array\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#raise-exception](https://rubystyle.guide#raise-exception)\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Lint/RandOne",
              "name": "Lint/RandOne",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Checks for `rand(1)` calls. Such calls always return `0` and most likely a mistake.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_lint/#lintrandone",
                "tags": [
                  "lint"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Checks for `rand(1)` calls. Such calls always return `0` and most likely a mistake."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Checks for `rand(1)` calls. Such calls always return `0` and most likely a mistake."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | No | 0.36 | -\n\nThis cop checks for `rand(1)` calls.\nSuch calls always return `0`.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\n\nrand 1\nKernel.rand(-1)\nrand 1.0\nrand(-1.0)\n```\n```ruby\n# good\n\n0 # just use 0 instead\n```\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | No | 0.36 | -\n\nThis cop checks for `rand(1)` calls.\nSuch calls always return `0`.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\n\nrand 1\nKernel.rand(-1)\nrand 1.0\nrand(-1.0)\n```\n```ruby\n# good\n\n0 # just use 0 instead\n```\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Lint/RedundantCopDisableDirective",
              "name": "Lint/RedundantCopDisableDirective",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Checks for rubocop:disable comments that can be removed. Note: this cop is not disabled when disabling all cops. It must be explicitly disabled.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_lint/#lintredundantcopdisabledirective",
                "tags": [
                  "lint"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Checks for rubocop:disable comments that can be removed. Note: this cop is not disabled when disabling all cops. It must be explicitly disabled."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Checks for rubocop:disable comments that can be removed. Note: this cop is not disabled when disabling all cops. It must be explicitly disabled."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.76 | -\n\nThis cop detects instances of rubocop:disable comments that can be\nremoved without causing any offenses to be reported. It's implemented\nas a cop in that it inherits from the Cop base class and calls\nadd_offense. The unusual part of its implementation is that it doesn't\nhave any on_* methods or an investigate method. This means that it\ndoesn't take part in the investigation phase when the other cops do\ntheir work. Instead, it waits until it's called in a later stage of the\nexecution. The reason it can't be implemented as a normal cop is that\nit depends on the results of all other cops to do its work.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\n# rubocop:disable Layout/LineLength\nx += 1\n# rubocop:enable Layout/LineLength\n\n# good\nx += 1\n```\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.76 | -\n\nThis cop detects instances of rubocop:disable comments that can be\nremoved without causing any offenses to be reported. It's implemented\nas a cop in that it inherits from the Cop base class and calls\nadd_offense. The unusual part of its implementation is that it doesn't\nhave any on_* methods or an investigate method. This means that it\ndoesn't take part in the investigation phase when the other cops do\ntheir work. Instead, it waits until it's called in a later stage of the\nexecution. The reason it can't be implemented as a normal cop is that\nit depends on the results of all other cops to do its work.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\n# rubocop:disable Layout/LineLength\nx += 1\n# rubocop:enable Layout/LineLength\n\n# good\nx += 1\n```\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Lint/RedundantCopEnableDirective",
              "name": "Lint/RedundantCopEnableDirective",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Checks for rubocop:enable comments that can be removed.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_lint/#lintredundantcopenabledirective",
                "tags": [
                  "lint"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Checks for rubocop:enable comments that can be removed."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Checks for rubocop:enable comments that can be removed."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.76 | -\n\nThis cop detects instances of rubocop:enable comments that can be\nremoved.\n\nWhen comment enables all cops at once `rubocop:enable all`\nthat cop checks whether any cop was actually enabled.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nfoo = 1\n# rubocop:enable Layout/LineLength\n\n# good\nfoo = 1\n```\n```ruby\n# bad\n# rubocop:disable Style/StringLiterals\nfoo = \"1\"\n# rubocop:enable Style/StringLiterals\nbaz\n# rubocop:enable all\n\n# good\n# rubocop:disable Style/StringLiterals\nfoo = \"1\"\n# rubocop:enable all\nbaz\n```\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.76 | -\n\nThis cop detects instances of rubocop:enable comments that can be\nremoved.\n\nWhen comment enables all cops at once `rubocop:enable all`\nthat cop checks whether any cop was actually enabled.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nfoo = 1\n# rubocop:enable Layout/LineLength\n\n# good\nfoo = 1\n```\n```ruby\n# bad\n# rubocop:disable Style/StringLiterals\nfoo = \"1\"\n# rubocop:enable Style/StringLiterals\nbaz\n# rubocop:enable all\n\n# good\n# rubocop:disable Style/StringLiterals\nfoo = \"1\"\n# rubocop:enable all\nbaz\n```\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Lint/RedundantRequireStatement",
              "name": "Lint/RedundantRequireStatement",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Checks for unnecessary `require` statement.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_lint/#lintredundantrequirestatement",
                "tags": [
                  "lint"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Checks for unnecessary `require` statement."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Checks for unnecessary `require` statement."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.76 | -\n\nChecks for unnecessary `require` statement.\n\nThe following features are unnecessary `require` statement because\nthey are already loaded.\n\nruby -ve 'p $LOADED_FEATURES.reject { |feature| %r|/| =~ feature }'\nruby 2.2.8p477 (2017-09-14 revision 59906) [x86_64-darwin13]\n[\"enumerator.so\", \"rational.so\", \"complex.so\", \"thread.rb\"]\n\nThis cop targets Ruby 2.2 or higher containing these 4 features.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nrequire 'unloaded_feature'\nrequire 'thread'\n\n# good\nrequire 'unloaded_feature'\n```\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.76 | -\n\nChecks for unnecessary `require` statement.\n\nThe following features are unnecessary `require` statement because\nthey are already loaded.\n\nruby -ve 'p $LOADED_FEATURES.reject { |feature| %r|/| =~ feature }'\nruby 2.2.8p477 (2017-09-14 revision 59906) [x86_64-darwin13]\n[\"enumerator.so\", \"rational.so\", \"complex.so\", \"thread.rb\"]\n\nThis cop targets Ruby 2.2 or higher containing these 4 features.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nrequire 'unloaded_feature'\nrequire 'thread'\n\n# good\nrequire 'unloaded_feature'\n```\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Lint/RedundantSplatExpansion",
              "name": "Lint/RedundantSplatExpansion",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Checks for splat unnecessarily being called on literals.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_lint/#lintredundantsplatexpansion",
                "tags": [
                  "lint"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Checks for splat unnecessarily being called on literals."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Checks for splat unnecessarily being called on literals."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.76 | -\n\nThis cop checks for unneeded usages of splat expansion\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\n\na = *[1, 2, 3]\na = *'a'\na = *1\n\nbegin\n  foo\nrescue *[StandardError, ApplicationError]\n  bar\nend\n\ncase foo\nwhen *[1, 2, 3]\n  bar\nelse\n  baz\nend\n```\n```ruby\n# good\n\nc = [1, 2, 3]\na = *c\na, b = *c\na, *b = *c\na = *1..10\na = ['a']\n\nbegin\n  foo\nrescue StandardError, ApplicationError\n  bar\nend\n\ncase foo\nwhen 1, 2, 3\n  bar\nelse\n  baz\nend\n```\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.76 | -\n\nThis cop checks for unneeded usages of splat expansion\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\n\na = *[1, 2, 3]\na = *'a'\na = *1\n\nbegin\n  foo\nrescue *[StandardError, ApplicationError]\n  bar\nend\n\ncase foo\nwhen *[1, 2, 3]\n  bar\nelse\n  baz\nend\n```\n```ruby\n# good\n\nc = [1, 2, 3]\na = *c\na, b = *c\na, *b = *c\na = *1..10\na = ['a']\n\nbegin\n  foo\nrescue StandardError, ApplicationError\n  bar\nend\n\ncase foo\nwhen 1, 2, 3\n  bar\nelse\n  baz\nend\n```\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Lint/RedundantStringCoercion",
              "name": "Lint/RedundantStringCoercion",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Checks for Object#to_s usage in string interpolation.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_lint/#lintredundantstringcoercion",
                "tags": [
                  "lint"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Checks for Object#to_s usage in string interpolation."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Checks for Object#to_s usage in string interpolation."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.19 | 0.77\n\nThis cop checks for string conversion in string interpolation,\nwhich is redundant.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\n\n\"result is #{something.to_s}\"\n```\n```ruby\n# good\n\n\"result is #{something}\"\n```\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#no-to-s](https://rubystyle.guide#no-to-s)\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.19 | 0.77\n\nThis cop checks for string conversion in string interpolation,\nwhich is redundant.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\n\n\"result is #{something.to_s}\"\n```\n```ruby\n# good\n\n\"result is #{something}\"\n```\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#no-to-s](https://rubystyle.guide#no-to-s)\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Lint/RedundantWithIndex",
              "name": "Lint/RedundantWithIndex",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Checks for redundant `with_index`.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_lint/#lintredundantwithindex",
                "tags": [
                  "lint"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Checks for redundant `with_index`."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Checks for redundant `with_index`."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.50 | -\n\nThis cop checks for redundant `with_index`.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nary.each_with_index do |v|\n  v\nend\n\n# good\nary.each do |v|\n  v\nend\n\n# bad\nary.each.with_index do |v|\n  v\nend\n\n# good\nary.each do |v|\n  v\nend\n```\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.50 | -\n\nThis cop checks for redundant `with_index`.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nary.each_with_index do |v|\n  v\nend\n\n# good\nary.each do |v|\n  v\nend\n\n# bad\nary.each.with_index do |v|\n  v\nend\n\n# good\nary.each do |v|\n  v\nend\n```\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Lint/RedundantWithObject",
              "name": "Lint/RedundantWithObject",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Checks for redundant `with_object`.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_lint/#lintredundantwithobject",
                "tags": [
                  "lint"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Checks for redundant `with_object`."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Checks for redundant `with_object`."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.51 | -\n\nThis cop checks for redundant `with_object`.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nary.each_with_object([]) do |v|\n  v\nend\n\n# good\nary.each do |v|\n  v\nend\n\n# bad\nary.each.with_object([]) do |v|\n  v\nend\n\n# good\nary.each do |v|\n  v\nend\n```\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.51 | -\n\nThis cop checks for redundant `with_object`.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nary.each_with_object([]) do |v|\n  v\nend\n\n# good\nary.each do |v|\n  v\nend\n\n# bad\nary.each.with_object([]) do |v|\n  v\nend\n\n# good\nary.each do |v|\n  v\nend\n```\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Lint/RegexpAsCondition",
              "name": "Lint/RegexpAsCondition",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Do not use regexp literal as a condition. The regexp literal matches `$_` implicitly.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_lint/#lintregexpascondition",
                "tags": [
                  "lint"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Do not use regexp literal as a condition. The regexp literal matches `$_` implicitly."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Do not use regexp literal as a condition. The regexp literal matches `$_` implicitly."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | No | 0.51 | -\n\nThis cop checks for regexp literals used as `match-current-line`.\nIf a regexp literal is in condition, the regexp matches `$_` implicitly.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nif /foo/\n  do_something\nend\n\n# good\nif /foo/ =~ $_\n  do_something\nend\n```\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | No | 0.51 | -\n\nThis cop checks for regexp literals used as `match-current-line`.\nIf a regexp literal is in condition, the regexp matches `$_` implicitly.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nif /foo/\n  do_something\nend\n\n# good\nif /foo/ =~ $_\n  do_something\nend\n```\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Lint/RequireParentheses",
              "name": "Lint/RequireParentheses",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Use parentheses in the method call to avoid confusion about precedence.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_lint/#lintrequireparentheses",
                "tags": [
                  "lint"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Use parentheses in the method call to avoid confusion about precedence."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Use parentheses in the method call to avoid confusion about precedence."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | No | 0.18 | -\n\nThis cop checks for expressions where there is a call to a predicate\nmethod with at least one argument, where no parentheses are used around\nthe parameter list, and a boolean operator, && or ||, is used in the\nlast argument.\n\nThe idea behind warning for these constructs is that the user might\nbe under the impression that the return value from the method call is\nan operand of &&/||.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\n\nif day.is? :tuesday && month == :jan\n  # ...\nend\n```\n```ruby\n# good\n\nif day.is?(:tuesday) && month == :jan\n  # ...\nend\n```\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | No | 0.18 | -\n\nThis cop checks for expressions where there is a call to a predicate\nmethod with at least one argument, where no parentheses are used around\nthe parameter list, and a boolean operator, && or ||, is used in the\nlast argument.\n\nThe idea behind warning for these constructs is that the user might\nbe under the impression that the return value from the method call is\nan operand of &&/||.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\n\nif day.is? :tuesday && month == :jan\n  # ...\nend\n```\n```ruby\n# good\n\nif day.is?(:tuesday) && month == :jan\n  # ...\nend\n```\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Lint/RescueException",
              "name": "Lint/RescueException",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Avoid rescuing the Exception class.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_lint/#lintrescueexception",
                "tags": [
                  "lint"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Avoid rescuing the Exception class."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Avoid rescuing the Exception class."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | No | 0.9 | 0.27.1\n\nThis cop checks for *rescue* blocks targeting the Exception class.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\n\nbegin\n  do_something\nrescue Exception\n  handle_exception\nend\n```\n```ruby\n# good\n\nbegin\n  do_something\nrescue ArgumentError\n  handle_exception\nend\n```\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#no-blind-rescues](https://rubystyle.guide#no-blind-rescues)\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | No | 0.9 | 0.27.1\n\nThis cop checks for *rescue* blocks targeting the Exception class.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\n\nbegin\n  do_something\nrescue Exception\n  handle_exception\nend\n```\n```ruby\n# good\n\nbegin\n  do_something\nrescue ArgumentError\n  handle_exception\nend\n```\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#no-blind-rescues](https://rubystyle.guide#no-blind-rescues)\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Lint/RescueType",
              "name": "Lint/RescueType",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Avoid rescuing from non constants that could result in a `TypeError`.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_lint/#lintrescuetype",
                "tags": [
                  "lint"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Avoid rescuing from non constants that could result in a `TypeError`."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Avoid rescuing from non constants that could result in a `TypeError`."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.49 | -\n\nCheck for arguments to `rescue` that will result in a `TypeError`\nif an exception is raised.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nbegin\n  bar\nrescue nil\n  baz\nend\n\n# bad\ndef foo\n  bar\nrescue 1, 'a', \"#{b}\", 0.0, [], {}\n  baz\nend\n\n# good\nbegin\n  bar\nrescue\n  baz\nend\n\n# good\ndef foo\n  bar\nrescue NameError\n  baz\nend\n```\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.49 | -\n\nCheck for arguments to `rescue` that will result in a `TypeError`\nif an exception is raised.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nbegin\n  bar\nrescue nil\n  baz\nend\n\n# bad\ndef foo\n  bar\nrescue 1, 'a', \"#{b}\", 0.0, [], {}\n  baz\nend\n\n# good\nbegin\n  bar\nrescue\n  baz\nend\n\n# good\ndef foo\n  bar\nrescue NameError\n  baz\nend\n```\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Lint/ReturnInVoidContext",
              "name": "Lint/ReturnInVoidContext",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Checks for return in void context.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_lint/#lintreturninvoidcontext",
                "tags": [
                  "lint"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Checks for return in void context."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Checks for return in void context."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | No | 0.50 | -\n\nThis cop checks for the use of a return with a value in a context\nwhere the value will be ignored. (initialize and setter methods)\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\ndef initialize\n  foo\n  return :qux if bar?\n  baz\nend\n\ndef foo=(bar)\n  return 42\nend\n```\n```ruby\n# good\ndef initialize\n  foo\n  return if bar?\n  baz\nend\n\ndef foo=(bar)\n  return\nend\n```\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | No | 0.50 | -\n\nThis cop checks for the use of a return with a value in a context\nwhere the value will be ignored. (initialize and setter methods)\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\ndef initialize\n  foo\n  return :qux if bar?\n  baz\nend\n\ndef foo=(bar)\n  return 42\nend\n```\n```ruby\n# good\ndef initialize\n  foo\n  return if bar?\n  baz\nend\n\ndef foo=(bar)\n  return\nend\n```\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Lint/SafeNavigationChain",
              "name": "Lint/SafeNavigationChain",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Do not chain ordinary method call after safe navigation operator.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_lint/#lintsafenavigationchain",
                "tags": [
                  "lint"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Do not chain ordinary method call after safe navigation operator."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Do not chain ordinary method call after safe navigation operator."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | No | 0.47 | 0.77\n\nThe safe navigation operator returns nil if the receiver is\nnil. If you chain an ordinary method call after a safe\nnavigation operator, it raises NoMethodError. We should use a\nsafe navigation operator after a safe navigation operator.\nThis cop checks for the problem outlined above.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\n\nx&.foo.bar\nx&.foo + bar\nx&.foo[bar]\n```\n```ruby\n# good\n\nx&.foo&.bar\nx&.foo || bar\n```\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nAllowedMethods | `present?`, `blank?`, `presence`, `try`, `try!` | Array\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | No | 0.47 | 0.77\n\nThe safe navigation operator returns nil if the receiver is\nnil. If you chain an ordinary method call after a safe\nnavigation operator, it raises NoMethodError. We should use a\nsafe navigation operator after a safe navigation operator.\nThis cop checks for the problem outlined above.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\n\nx&.foo.bar\nx&.foo + bar\nx&.foo[bar]\n```\n```ruby\n# good\n\nx&.foo&.bar\nx&.foo || bar\n```\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nAllowedMethods | `present?`, `blank?`, `presence`, `try`, `try!` | Array\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Lint/SafeNavigationConsistency",
              "name": "Lint/SafeNavigationConsistency",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Check to make sure that if safe navigation is used for a method call in an `&&` or `||` condition that safe navigation is used for all method calls on that same object.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_lint/#lintsafenavigationconsistency",
                "tags": [
                  "lint"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Check to make sure that if safe navigation is used for a method call in an `&&` or `||` condition that safe navigation is used for all method calls on that same object."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Check to make sure that if safe navigation is used for a method call in an `&&` or `||` condition that safe navigation is used for all method calls on that same object."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.55 | 0.77\n\nThis cop check to make sure that if safe navigation is used for a method\ncall in an `&&` or `||` condition that safe navigation is used for all\nmethod calls on that same object.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nfoo&.bar && foo.baz\n\n# bad\nfoo.bar || foo&.baz\n\n# bad\nfoo&.bar && (foobar.baz || foo.baz)\n\n# good\nfoo.bar && foo.baz\n\n# good\nfoo&.bar || foo&.baz\n\n# good\nfoo&.bar && (foobar.baz || foo&.baz)\n```\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nAllowedMethods | `present?`, `blank?`, `presence`, `try`, `try!` | Array\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.55 | 0.77\n\nThis cop check to make sure that if safe navigation is used for a method\ncall in an `&&` or `||` condition that safe navigation is used for all\nmethod calls on that same object.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nfoo&.bar && foo.baz\n\n# bad\nfoo.bar || foo&.baz\n\n# bad\nfoo&.bar && (foobar.baz || foo.baz)\n\n# good\nfoo.bar && foo.baz\n\n# good\nfoo&.bar || foo&.baz\n\n# good\nfoo&.bar && (foobar.baz || foo&.baz)\n```\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nAllowedMethods | `present?`, `blank?`, `presence`, `try`, `try!` | Array\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Lint/SafeNavigationWithEmpty",
              "name": "Lint/SafeNavigationWithEmpty",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Avoid `foo&.empty?` in conditionals.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_lint/#lintsafenavigationwithempty",
                "tags": [
                  "lint"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Avoid `foo&.empty?` in conditionals."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Avoid `foo&.empty?` in conditionals."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | No | 0.62 | -\n\nThis cop checks to make sure safe navigation isn't used with `empty?` in\na conditional.\n\nWhile the safe navigation operator is generally a good idea, when\nchecking `foo&.empty?` in a conditional, `foo` being `nil` will actually\ndo the opposite of what the author intends.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nreturn if foo&.empty?\nreturn unless foo&.empty?\n\n# good\nreturn if foo && foo.empty?\nreturn unless foo && foo.empty?\n```\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | No | 0.62 | -\n\nThis cop checks to make sure safe navigation isn't used with `empty?` in\na conditional.\n\nWhile the safe navigation operator is generally a good idea, when\nchecking `foo&.empty?` in a conditional, `foo` being `nil` will actually\ndo the opposite of what the author intends.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nreturn if foo&.empty?\nreturn unless foo&.empty?\n\n# good\nreturn if foo && foo.empty?\nreturn unless foo && foo.empty?\n```\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Lint/ScriptPermission",
              "name": "Lint/ScriptPermission",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Grant script file execute permission.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_lint/#lintscriptpermission",
                "tags": [
                  "lint"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Grant script file execute permission."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Grant script file execute permission."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.49 | 0.50\n\nThis cop checks if a file which has a shebang line as\nits first line is granted execute permission.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\n\n# A file which has a shebang line as its first line is not\n# granted execute permission.\n\n#!/usr/bin/env ruby\nputs 'hello, world'\n\n# good\n\n# A file which has a shebang line as its first line is\n# granted execute permission.\n\n#!/usr/bin/env ruby\nputs 'hello, world'\n\n# good\n\n# A file which has not a shebang line as its first line is not\n# granted execute permission.\n\nputs 'hello, world'\n```\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.49 | 0.50\n\nThis cop checks if a file which has a shebang line as\nits first line is granted execute permission.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\n\n# A file which has a shebang line as its first line is not\n# granted execute permission.\n\n#!/usr/bin/env ruby\nputs 'hello, world'\n\n# good\n\n# A file which has a shebang line as its first line is\n# granted execute permission.\n\n#!/usr/bin/env ruby\nputs 'hello, world'\n\n# good\n\n# A file which has not a shebang line as its first line is not\n# granted execute permission.\n\nputs 'hello, world'\n```\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Lint/SendWithMixinArgument",
              "name": "Lint/SendWithMixinArgument",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Checks for `send` method when using mixin.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_lint/#lintsendwithmixinargument",
                "tags": [
                  "lint"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Checks for `send` method when using mixin."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Checks for `send` method when using mixin."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.75 | -\n\nThis cop checks for `send`, `public_send`, and `__send__` methods\nwhen using mix-in.\n\n`include` and `prepend` methods were private methods until Ruby 2.0,\nthey were mixed-in via `send` method. This cop uses Ruby 2.1 or\nhigher style that can be called by public methods.\nAnd `extend` method that was originally a public method is also targeted\nfor style unification.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nFoo.send(:include, Bar)\nFoo.send(:prepend, Bar)\nFoo.send(:extend, Bar)\n\n# bad\nFoo.public_send(:include, Bar)\nFoo.public_send(:prepend, Bar)\nFoo.public_send(:extend, Bar)\n\n# bad\nFoo.__send__(:include, Bar)\nFoo.__send__(:prepend, Bar)\nFoo.__send__(:extend, Bar)\n\n# good\nFoo.include Bar\nFoo.prepend Bar\nFoo.extend Bar\n```\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | Yes  | 0.75 | -\n\nThis cop checks for `send`, `public_send`, and `__send__` methods\nwhen using mix-in.\n\n`include` and `prepend` methods were private methods until Ruby 2.0,\nthey were mixed-in via `send` method. This cop uses Ruby 2.1 or\nhigher style that can be called by public methods.\nAnd `extend` method that was originally a public method is also targeted\nfor style unification.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nFoo.send(:include, Bar)\nFoo.send(:prepend, Bar)\nFoo.send(:extend, Bar)\n\n# bad\nFoo.public_send(:include, Bar)\nFoo.public_send(:prepend, Bar)\nFoo.public_send(:extend, Bar)\n\n# bad\nFoo.__send__(:include, Bar)\nFoo.__send__(:prepend, Bar)\nFoo.__send__(:extend, Bar)\n\n# good\nFoo.include Bar\nFoo.prepend Bar\nFoo.extend Bar\n```\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Lint/ShadowedArgument",
              "name": "Lint/ShadowedArgument",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Avoid reassigning arguments before they were used.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_lint/#lintshadowedargument",
                "tags": [
                  "lint"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Avoid reassigning arguments before they were used."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Avoid reassigning arguments before they were used."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | No | 0.52 | -\n\nThis cop checks for shadowed arguments.\n\nThis cop has `IgnoreImplicitReferences` configuration option.\nIt means argument shadowing is used in order to pass parameters\nto zero arity `super` when `IgnoreImplicitReferences` is `true`.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\ndo_something do |foo|\n  foo = 42\n  puts foo\nend\n\ndef do_something(foo)\n  foo = 42\n  puts foo\nend\n\n# good\ndo_something do |foo|\n  foo = foo + 42\n  puts foo\nend\n\ndef do_something(foo)\n  foo = foo + 42\n  puts foo\nend\n\ndef do_something(foo)\n  puts foo\nend\n```\n#### IgnoreImplicitReferences: false (default)\n\n```ruby\n# bad\ndef do_something(foo)\n  foo = 42\n  super\nend\n\ndef do_something(foo)\n  foo = super\n  bar\nend\n```\n#### IgnoreImplicitReferences: true\n\n```ruby\n# good\ndef do_something(foo)\n  foo = 42\n  super\nend\n\ndef do_something(foo)\n  foo = super\n  bar\nend\n```\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nIgnoreImplicitReferences | `false` | Boolean\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | No | 0.52 | -\n\nThis cop checks for shadowed arguments.\n\nThis cop has `IgnoreImplicitReferences` configuration option.\nIt means argument shadowing is used in order to pass parameters\nto zero arity `super` when `IgnoreImplicitReferences` is `true`.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\ndo_something do |foo|\n  foo = 42\n  puts foo\nend\n\ndef do_something(foo)\n  foo = 42\n  puts foo\nend\n\n# good\ndo_something do |foo|\n  foo = foo + 42\n  puts foo\nend\n\ndef do_something(foo)\n  foo = foo + 42\n  puts foo\nend\n\ndef do_something(foo)\n  puts foo\nend\n```\n#### IgnoreImplicitReferences: false (default)\n\n```ruby\n# bad\ndef do_something(foo)\n  foo = 42\n  super\nend\n\ndef do_something(foo)\n  foo = super\n  bar\nend\n```\n#### IgnoreImplicitReferences: true\n\n```ruby\n# good\ndef do_something(foo)\n  foo = 42\n  super\nend\n\ndef do_something(foo)\n  foo = super\n  bar\nend\n```\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nIgnoreImplicitReferences | `false` | Boolean\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Lint/ShadowedException",
              "name": "Lint/ShadowedException",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Avoid rescuing a higher level exception before a lower level exception.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_lint/#lintshadowedexception",
                "tags": [
                  "lint"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Avoid rescuing a higher level exception before a lower level exception."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Avoid rescuing a higher level exception before a lower level exception."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | No | 0.41 | -\n\nThis cop checks for a rescued exception that get shadowed by a\nless specific exception being rescued before a more specific\nexception is rescued.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\n\nbegin\n  something\nrescue Exception\n  handle_exception\nrescue StandardError\n  handle_standard_error\nend\n\n# good\n\nbegin\n  something\nrescue StandardError\n  handle_standard_error\nrescue Exception\n  handle_exception\nend\n\n# good, however depending on runtime environment.\n#\n# This is a special case for system call errors.\n# System dependent error code depends on runtime environment.\n# For example, whether `Errno::EAGAIN` and `Errno::EWOULDBLOCK` are\n# the same error code or different error code depends on environment.\n# This good case is for `Errno::EAGAIN` and `Errno::EWOULDBLOCK` with\n# the same error code.\nbegin\n  something\nrescue Errno::EAGAIN, Errno::EWOULDBLOCK\n  handle_standard_error\nend\n```\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | No | 0.41 | -\n\nThis cop checks for a rescued exception that get shadowed by a\nless specific exception being rescued before a more specific\nexception is rescued.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\n\nbegin\n  something\nrescue Exception\n  handle_exception\nrescue StandardError\n  handle_standard_error\nend\n\n# good\n\nbegin\n  something\nrescue StandardError\n  handle_standard_error\nrescue Exception\n  handle_exception\nend\n\n# good, however depending on runtime environment.\n#\n# This is a special case for system call errors.\n# System dependent error code depends on runtime environment.\n# For example, whether `Errno::EAGAIN` and `Errno::EWOULDBLOCK` are\n# the same error code or different error code depends on environment.\n# This good case is for `Errno::EAGAIN` and `Errno::EWOULDBLOCK` with\n# the same error code.\nbegin\n  something\nrescue Errno::EAGAIN, Errno::EWOULDBLOCK\n  handle_standard_error\nend\n```\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Lint/ShadowingOuterLocalVariable",
              "name": "Lint/ShadowingOuterLocalVariable",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Do not use the same name as outer local variable for block arguments or block local variables.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_lint/#lintshadowingouterlocalvariable",
                "tags": [
                  "lint"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Do not use the same name as outer local variable for block arguments or block local variables."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Do not use the same name as outer local variable for block arguments or block local variables."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | No | 0.9 | -\n\nThis cop looks for use of the same name as outer local variables\nfor block arguments or block local variables.\nThis is a mimic of the warning\n\"shadowing outer local variable - foo\" from `ruby -cw`.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\n\ndef some_method\n  foo = 1\n\n  2.times do |foo| # shadowing outer `foo`\n    do_something(foo)\n  end\nend\n```\n```ruby\n# good\n\ndef some_method\n  foo = 1\n\n  2.times do |bar|\n    do_something(bar)\n  end\nend\n```\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | No | 0.9 | -\n\nThis cop looks for use of the same name as outer local variables\nfor block arguments or block local variables.\nThis is a mimic of the warning\n\"shadowing outer local variable - foo\" from `ruby -cw`.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\n\ndef some_method\n  foo = 1\n\n  2.times do |foo| # shadowing outer `foo`\n    do_something(foo)\n  end\nend\n```\n```ruby\n# good\n\ndef some_method\n  foo = 1\n\n  2.times do |bar|\n    do_something(bar)\n  end\nend\n```\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Lint/StructNewOverride",
              "name": "Lint/StructNewOverride",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Disallow overriding the `Struct` built-in methods via `Struct.new`.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_lint/#lintstructnewoverride",
                "tags": [
                  "lint"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Disallow overriding the `Struct` built-in methods via `Struct.new`."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Disallow overriding the `Struct` built-in methods via `Struct.new`."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nPending | Yes | No | 0.81 | -\n\nThis cop checks unexpected overrides of the `Struct` built-in methods\nvia `Struct.new`.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nBad = Struct.new(:members, :clone, :count)\nb = Bad.new([], true, 1)\nb.members #=> [] (overriding `Struct#members`)\nb.clone #=> true (overriding `Object#clone`)\nb.count #=> 1 (overriding `Enumerable#count`)\n\n# good\nGood = Struct.new(:id, :name)\ng = Good.new(1, \"foo\")\ng.members #=> [:id, :name]\ng.clone #=> #<struct Good id=1, name=\"foo\">\ng.count #=> 2\n```\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nPending | Yes | No | 0.81 | -\n\nThis cop checks unexpected overrides of the `Struct` built-in methods\nvia `Struct.new`.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nBad = Struct.new(:members, :clone, :count)\nb = Bad.new([], true, 1)\nb.members #=> [] (overriding `Struct#members`)\nb.clone #=> true (overriding `Object#clone`)\nb.count #=> 1 (overriding `Enumerable#count`)\n\n# good\nGood = Struct.new(:id, :name)\ng = Good.new(1, \"foo\")\ng.members #=> [:id, :name]\ng.clone #=> #<struct Good id=1, name=\"foo\">\ng.count #=> 2\n```\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Lint/SuppressedException",
              "name": "Lint/SuppressedException",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "none"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "description": "Don't suppress exceptions.",
                "queryURI": "https://docs.rubocop.org/en/stable/cops_lint/#lintsuppressedexception",
                "tags": [
                  "lint"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Don't suppress exceptions."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Don't suppress exceptions."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | No | 0.9 | 0.81\n\nThis cop checks for *rescue* blocks with no body.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\ndef some_method\n  do_something\nrescue\nend\n\n# bad\nbegin\n  do_something\nrescue\nend\n\n# good\ndef some_method\n  do_something\nrescue\n  handle_exception\nend\n\n# good\nbegin\n  do_something\nrescue\n  handle_exception\nend\n```\n#### AllowComments: true (default)\n\n```ruby\n# good\ndef some_method\n  do_something\nrescue\n  # do nothing\nend\n\n# good\nbegin\n  do_something\nrescue\n  # do nothing\nend\n```\n#### AllowComments: false\n\n```ruby\n# bad\ndef some_method\n  do_something\nrescue\n  # do nothing\nend\n\n# bad\nbegin\n  do_something\nrescue\n  # do nothing\nend\n```\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nAllowComments | `true` | Boolean\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#dont-hide-exceptions](https://rubystyle.guide#dont-hide-exceptions)\n\n",
                "markdown": "Enabled by default | Safe | Supports autocorrection | VersionAdded | VersionChanged\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEnabled | Yes | No | 0.9 | 0.81\n\nThis cop checks for *rescue* blocks with no body.\n\n### Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\ndef some_method\n  do_something\nrescue\nend\n\n# bad\nbegin\n  do_something\nrescue\nend\n\n# good\ndef some_method\n  do_something\nrescue\n  handle_exception\nend\n\n# good\nbegin\n  do_something\nrescue\n  handle_exception\nend\n```\n#### AllowComments: true (default)\n\n```ruby\n# good\ndef some_method\n  do_something\nrescue\n  # do nothing\nend\n\n# good\nbegin\n  do_something\nrescue\n  # do nothing\nend\n```\n#### AllowComments: false\n\n```ruby\n# bad\ndef some_method\n  do_something\nrescue\n  # do nothing\nend\n\n# bad\nbegin\n  do_something\nrescue\n  # do nothing\nend\n```\n\n### Configurable attributes\n\nName | Default value | Configurable values\n--- | --- | ---\nAllowComments | `true` | Boolean\n\n### References\n\n* [https://rubystyle.guide#dont-hide-exceptions](https://rubystyle.guide#dont-hide-exceptions)\n\n"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "Lint/Syntax",
              "name": "Lint/Syntax",
